1 NOTE {geni:about_me} # ''Irish Kings and High-Kings'' by Francis John Byrne pp. 151-152, 215, 289. [https://archive.org/details/irishkingshighki00byrn]  
2 CONT # ''Fáelán mac Colmáin'' [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%A1el%C3%A1n_mac_Colm%C3%A1in]   
2 CONT # ''Annals of Ulste''r [https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T100001A.html]. Enter 628.1 then 633.2 into browser search. 
2 CONT # ''Annals of Tigernach'' [https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T100002A/index.html]. Click CELT editions as a single file first, then enter 630. 1 then 636.2 into browser search. 
2 CONT # ''Annals of Inisfallen'' [https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T100004.html]. Enter 637.2 into browser search. 
2 CONT # MacKillop, James, A Dictionary of Celtic Mythology. Oxford University Press, Oxford, 1998. (Kenneth Shelley, 27 Nov 2021)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see List of Kings of Leinster; [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_kings_of_Leinster#Kings_of_Leinster] (Steven Ferry, April 13, 2019.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conall_Guthbinn]. (Steven Ferry, April 13, 2019.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Fáelán mac Colmáin (died 666) was a King of Leinster from the Uí Dúnlainge branch of the Laigin. He was the son of Colmán Már mac Coirpri, a previous king.[1] Faelan was the founder of the true fortunes of the Ui Dunlainge dynasty
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT His son by Sarnat was Conall mac Fáelán, who was father, by Conandil ingen Crundmáel of the Ui Dunlainge, of Bran Mut mac Conaill (died 693), a king of Leinster.
