1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Cellach (d. 658), son of Máel-Cobo and high-king of Ireland, belonged to the dynasty of Cenél Conaill. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His father Máel-Cobo (d. 615), one of the five sons of Áed (qv) son of Ainmere (qv), was high-king according to some sources; his father's only recorded 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother, Cróinseach, daughter of Áed Finn king of Osraige (Lec. 390b). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cellach's immediate predecessor in the kingship of Cenél Conaill and in the high-kingship was his uncle, Domnall (qv) (d. 642) son of Áed. Although Cellach appears in Middle Irish regnal lists (e.g. Rawl. B. 502, 137a), his absenc
2 CONC e from the earlier ‘Baile Chuinn’ raises questions about the reality of his achieving the high-kingship. In any case, it would appear that for most of his reign Cellach shared power with his brother Conall Cóel (qv) (d. 654). In 6
2 CONC 50 (AU) the two brothers faced a challenge from their first cousin, Óengus son of the above-mentioned Domnall. They defeated and slew Óengus at the battle of Dún Cremthainn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cellach was married to Dathnat or Damnat, whose dynastic origin is unknown (Lec. 390b; RIA, D.2.1, 96rb). They had a daughter Cacht, who married Máel-dúin, king of Cenél nÉogain, and became the mother of a future high-king, Fergal
2 CONC  (qv) son of Máel-dúin. It seems likely that Cellach was also the father of Findelb, wife of the later high-king Sechnassach son of Blathmac (qv) and grandson of Áed Sláine (qv) (Lec. 391; RIA, D.2.1, 97). There is no record, howe
2 CONC ver, of Cellach having left male issue; on his death, the high-kingship passed to Blathmac son of Áed Sláine, while the kingship of Cenél Conaill continued in the line of his uncle Domnall.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
