1 OCCU aka Flaherty (Flaithbheartach Flaugherty) MacLOINGSIG (O'NEILL); 159th MONARCH of IRELAND
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Flaithbertach mac Loíngsigh
2 CONT
2 CONT High King of Ireland
2 CONT Died 733–735 CE
2 CONT Cenél Conaill, Northern Uí Néill
2 CONT
2 CONT Summary
2 CONT
2 CONT Flaithbertach mac Loíngsigh of the Cenél Conaill branch of the Northern Uí Néill succeeded to the High Kingship of Ireland after the death of Cináed mac Írgalaig. He ruled for seven years, then abdicated and entered monastic life 
2 CONC at Armagh, where he died shortly afterward. He must be distinguished from another contemporary dynasty of a similar name who died in AU 765; that individual belonged to Cenél nEógain and is not the same man.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parentage
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Loingsech mac Óengusso (d. 703), High King of Ireland
2 CONT Mother: Not preserved in the annals
2 CONT
2 CONT Accession
2 CONT
2 CONT Annals of Tigernach (AT 728.8):
2 CONT “Flaithbertach mac Loíngsigh do gabail rígi nÉrenn.”
2 CONT Translation: “Flaithbertach son of Loingsech took the kingship of Ireland.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Flaithbertach reigned for seven years. His kingship is characterized by conflict with rival branches of the Northern Uí Néill, particularly the Cenél nEógain under Áed Allán.
2 CONT
2 CONT Recorded Events
2 CONT
2 CONT Annals of Ulster (AU 734.8):
2 CONT An encounter at Mag Itha between Flaithbertach son of Loingsech and Áed Allán, demonstrating that Flaithbertach was still active during AU’s year 734.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdication and Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Annals of the Four Masters (AFM 729.1):
2 CONT “After Flaithbheartach, son of Loingseach, son of Aenghus, had been seven years in the sovereignty of Ireland, he died at Ard Macha (Armagh), having resigned his kingdom for a monastic life.”
2 CONT
2 CONT The Four Masters use a year-numbering system four years behind AU in this period. AFM 729 corresponds to AU 733–734. Modern historical reconstruction places his death between 733 and 735 CE, conventionally listed as 734.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children (Partially Attested)
2 CONT
2 CONT • Loingsech mac Flaithbertaig (d. 754)
2 CONT • Áed mac Muinderg
2 CONT • Dúnlaith ingen Flaithbertaig (ancestor of Áed Oirdnide)
2 CONT
2 CONT Disambiguation
2 CONT
2 CONT This Flaithbertach mac Loíngsigh is not the same individual as the Flaithbertach recorded in Annals of Ulster 765.2 (“Flaithbertach son of Loingsech, king of Temair, in religious life”).
2 CONT The man in AU 765 belonged to the Cenél nEógain and was the son of a different Loingsech (Loingsech mac Fergusa).
2 CONT The two figures are distinct and should not be merged.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chronology Note
2 CONT
2 CONT Early Irish annals do not use synchronized year numbering.
2 CONT For the eighth century:
2 CONT
2 CONT • AU 734 ≈ AT 735 ≈ AFM 729
2 CONT • All refer to the same approximate historical year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The absence of Flaithbertach’s death in AU is not unusual. AT preserves the accession, AU preserves his last military activity, and AFM preserves the obituary. Combined, these sources give a consistent reign and death window of 73
2 CONC 3–735 CE.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT • Annals of Ulster (AU 734.8)
2 CONT • Annals of Tigernach (AT 728.8)
2 CONT • Annals of the Four Masters (AFM 729.1)
2 CONT • Rawlinson B502 (recension of Tigernach material)
2 CONT • Synchronistic King Lists
2 CONT • Francis John Byrne, Irish Kings and High-Kings, Oxford, 1973
2 CONT • T. M. Charles-Edwards, Early Christian Ireland, Cambridge, 2000
2 CONT • Gearóid Mac Niocaill, Ireland Before the Vikings
2 CONT • Edel Bhreathnach, Kings, Saints and Sagas
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT CURATOR NOTE: Chronology and Identity Clarification
2 CONT
2 CONT This profile represents Flaithbertach mac Loíngsigh of Cenél Conaill, High King of Ireland, who reigned for seven years and later abdicated to enter monastic life at Armagh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early Irish annals do not use synchronized year-numbering systems.
2 CONT For the eighth century:
2 CONT
2 CONT • AU 734 ≈ AT 735 ≈ AFM 729
2 CONT All refer to the same historical year despite differing labels.
2 CONT
2 CONT Flaithbertach’s death appears not in the Annals of Ulster but in the Annals of the Four Masters (AFM 729.1), whose chronology runs approximately four years behind AU for this period. AT 728.8 records his accession; AU 734.8 record
2 CONC s his last appearance alive. Together, these sources place his death between 733–735 CE, conventionally standardized as 734 CE.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is important not to confuse this individual with Flaithbertach mac Loingsig of Cenél nEógain, who appears in the Annals of Ulster under AU 765.2 (“Flaithbertach son of Loingsech, king of Temair, in religious life”). The AU 765 
2 CONC obituary refers to a different dynasty and to a different Loingsech; the two individuals have been mistakenly merged in some secondary genealogies and early online imports.
2 CONT
2 CONT This profile should remain distinct and aligned with the Cenél Conaill High King whose father was Loingsech mac Óengusso (d. 703).
