2 CONT '''Dúngal mac Fergaile'''
2 CONT '''Konge over Osraige, Irland  fra 802 til 842''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * f, ca 769 i Osraige sønn av Fergal mac Anmchada, moren er ukjent 
2 CONT * Hans kone er ukjent, de hadde barna Cerball mac Dúnlainge, Riacan mac Dúnlainge og Land ingen Dúngaile
2 CONT
2 CONT Kong  Dúngal overtok trona i Osraige sin far på begynnelsen av vikingtiden,et kritisk punkt i Irlands historie   Dúngal mac Fergaile sin lange regjeringstid var en del av en lengre periode med jevn royal rekkefølge og politisk sta
2 CONC bilitet i nnenfor kongeriket Osraige, Han ble far til 2 andre konger i Osraige; Cerball mac Dúnlainge og Riacan mac Dúnlainge; Samt Deres søster, prinsesse Land ingen Dúngaile, kona til den høye kongen máel sechnaill mac maíl ruan
2 CONC aid.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%BAngal_mac_Fergaile
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT Name: Dunghal MACFEARGHAL , King Of Osraige 
2 CONT Sex: M 
2 CONT Name: Dungal Of OSRAIGE , King Of Osraige 
2 CONT Birth: ABT 760 in Ossory, Ireland 
2 CONT Death: UNKNOWN
2 CONT Dúngal mac Fergaile King of Osraige Reign 802–842 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dúngal mac Fergaile was of the old Ossorian lineage called the Dál Birn, who traced their descent from Óengus Osrithe the first king of Osraige through his son and successor Loegaire Birn Buadach, of the second century.[2] King Dú
2 CONC ngal took the throne of Osraige after the death of his father, king Fergal mac Anmchada (K.O., d. 802) at a critical point in its history, just at the dawn of the Viking age in Ireland.[3] Dúngal mac Fergaile's long reign was part
2 CONC  of a longer period of steady royal succession and political stability within the kingdom of Osraige.[4] He is also noteworthy as being the father of two other kings of Osraige; Cerball mac Dúnlainge (K.O. 842-888), his successor 
2 CONC whose fame is recounted in the Fragmentary Annals of Ireland and Riacan mac Dúnlainge (K.O. 888-894); as well as their sister, princess Land ingen Dúngaile, wife of the high king Máel Sechnaill mac Máele Ruanaid.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%BAngal_mac_Fergaile
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The Kingdom of Osraighe (older spelling: Osraige, reformed spelling: Osraí), anglicized as Ossory, was an ancient kingdom of Ireland. It formed the easternmost part of the kingdom and province of Munster until the middle of the 9t
2 CONC h century, after which it attached itself to Leinster. Ossory was allied to the powerful Corcu Loígde or Dáirine of Munster for several centuries before the rise of the Eóganachta in the 7th century, and is said to have provided a
2 CONC  number of prehistoric kings of Munster under the alliance. The new political configuration, probably the result of an Uí Néill-Eóganachta alliance against the Corcu Loígde, caused a reduction in Ossory's relative status, and its 
2 CONC kings to thus seek their fortunes in Leinster.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dúngal mac Fergaile was of the old Ossorian lineage called the Dál Birn, who traced their descent from Óengus Osrithe the first king of Osraige through his son and successor Loegaire Birn Buadach, of the second century.[2] King Dú
2 CONC ngal took the throne of Osraige after the death of his father, king Fergal mac Anmchada (K.O., d. 802) at a critical point in its history, just at the dawn of the Viking age in Ireland.[3] Dúngal mac Fergaile's long reign was part
2 CONC  of a longer period of steady royal succession and political stability within the kingdom of Osraige.[4] He is also noteworthy as being the father of two other kings of Osraige; Cerball mac Dúnlainge (K.O. 842-888), his successor 
2 CONC whose fame is recounted in the Fragmentary Annals of Ireland and Riacan mac Dúnlainge (K.O. 888-894); as well as their sister, princess Land ingen Dúngaile, wife of the high king Máel Sechnaill mac Máele Ruanaid.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes'''
2 CONT * U842.13 ''Book of Leinster,' formerly Lebar na Núachongbála'' (Author:unknown) Section 29; folio 40e "Reges Ossairge" [https://celt.ucc.ie/published/G800011A/]
2 CONT * ''Early Irish Regnal Succession: A Case Study'', by Jim Reid. [https://www.academia.edu/6401329/Early_Irish_Regnal_Succession_A_Case_Study] FA 246 
2 CONT '''Link Gone Bad'''
