1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==GORMFLAITH 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a known poet  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gormflaith was the daughter of Flann Sinna, High King of Ireland from 879 to 916. Her mother was Gormlaith ingen Flann mac Conaing of Brega. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Gormflaith was notable for being the successive queen consort of Munster, Leinster and Tara and the  The Dessi Muman in Ireland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gormflaith was married first to King Cormac mac Cuilennáin of Munster, who had taken vows of celibacy as a bishop. The marriage was not said to be cosumated. MacShamhran writes "Difficulties relating to this marriage leave it prob
2 CONC able that it is a fiction - created when memory of Gormlaith became assimilated to the "sovereignty goddess" who had three husbands."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Cormac was killed at the battle of Bealach Mugna in 908 by an alliance of Flann Sinna of Tara and Cerball mac Muirecáin, King of Leinster. Flann afterwards married Gormflaith to Cerball, who is alleged (according to a text in the 
2 CONC Book of Leinster) to have abused her so much that she was forced to return to her father at least once.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT MacShamhran writes (p.203) "... the case for accepting as historical her marriage to Cerball is strenthened by a dindshenchas poem in the Book of Leinster, which also presents a different view of their relationship, implying that 
2 CONC she was involved in intrigue on his behalf. She is blamed for the deaths of Cellach Carmain, who was an Ui Muirdaig dynast, and his wife Aillenn - apparently rivals of her husband. This circumstance, along with the fact that Cerba
2 CONC ll had the support of Flann Sinna at Belach Mugna, fits well with a Clan Cholmain-Ui Faelain alliance in the years prior to that battle."
2 CONT
2 CONT Ó Cróinín (pp.219), citing the poem Cell Chorbbáin (composed shortly after 909), writes: "It states quite categorically that Gormlaith was responsible for the death of Cellach of Carmun and his wife Aillend - 'she laid them in the
2 CONC  church ground' (dos-fuc i talmain cilli) and by these actions 'she wrought terrible deeds' (do-ringni gnimu grana). This is clearly referring to a double-murder, and equally clearly, it is implicit that Gormlaith - and, by extens
2 CONC ion, her husband Cerball? - were involved together in a conspiracy to remove the reigning king of Leinster (here named as Cellach Carmun) and presumably replace him with Cerball." Ó Cróinín goes on to compare the data in the poem 
2 CONC with that of Cóic ríg tríchat to show that "there is something wrong with the Ui Dunlainge succession at precisely this point. ... It looks very much as though the struggle for succession ... saw several of the Ui Muiredaig line e
2 CONC liminated in the first half of the ninth century, and their names were simply expunged from the record:"
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT After Cerball's death in 909 Gormlaith married her stepbrother Niall Glúndub, who died in 919. By him she had Muirchertach mac Néill, ancestor to the O'Neill dynasty of the north of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annals of Clonmacnoise have her becoming poverty-striken after the death of Niall, reduced to wandering from place to place as a poet to survive. This literary tradition, which appears over a century after her death, may be ba
2 CONC sed upon a misreading of her obit in the Annals of Ulster, which instead indicate she died in a convent.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A number of poems of later date are ascribed to Gormflaith in Middle Irish sources, including laments for Cerball and Niall, but not for Cormac.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There are conflicting stories of the end of her life. Some say she was destitute others she was in a convent.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in 947, after accidentally falling on the sharp-pointed post of her bed
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
