2 CONT f) [HLODVIR Torfinnsson ([945/50]-[988], bur Hofn, Caithness). Orkneyinga Saga names (in order) “Arnfinn…Havard the Fecund…Hlodvir…Ljot…Skuli” as the five sons of Thorfinn[223]. Snorre names "the Earls Hlodver, Arnfid, Ljot and Sk
2 CONC ule, the sons of Thorfin Hausakljufer" as rulers in Orkney when Queen Gunhild and her sons returned to Orkney[224]. In a later saga, Snorre names (in order) "Arnfin, Havard, Hlodver, Liot and Skule" as sons of Thorfin "Hausakljufe
2 CONC r"[225].
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that “after Ljot´s death, Hlodvir took charge of the earldom”, presumably indicating Ljot´s brother but this is not explicitly stated in the text, recording that “he died in his bed” and was buried “in a bu
2 CONC rial mound at Ham in Caithness”[226].
2 CONT
2 CONT m EITHNE, daughter of KIARVAL King of Ireland & his wife ---. Orkneyinga Saga records that “Hlodvir” married “Eithne, the daughter of King Kjarval of Ireland”, adding in a later passage that “Sigurd´s mother was a sorceress”[227].
2 CONC  Hlodve´s supposed father-in-law has not been identified among known Irish kings. Hlodve & his wife had [three] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT i) [SIGURD "Digri/the Stout" ([965/70]-killed in battle Clontarf 23 Apr 1014). Orkneyinga Saga names “Sigurd the Stout” as the son of Hlodvir and his wife, adding that he “took over the earldom” after his father died[228].
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre names "Sigurd the Thick" as the son of Hlodver[229]. He succeeded his father [988] as Jarl of Orkney and Caithness.]
2 CONT
2 CONT ii) [SVANLAUG or Nereid. m (Orkney 990) GILLI Jarl of the Hebrides 988-1014.]
2 CONT
2 CONT iii) [daughter . m HAVARD (-winter 988/89). Steward of Caithness for Jarl Sigurd. Killed by Jarls Melsnati and Hundi, who were defeated when Jarl Sigurd attacked them in revenge.]
2 CONT
2 CONT g) [SKULI Torfinnsson (-killed in battle). Orkneyinga Saga names (in order) “Arnfinn…Havard the Fecund…Hlodvir…Ljot…Skuli” as the five sons of Thorfinn[230]. Snorre names "the Earls Hlodver, Arnfid, Ljot and Skule, the sons of Tho
2 CONC rfin Hausakljufer" as rulers in Orkney when Queen Gunhild and her sons returned to Orkney[231]. In a later saga, Snorre names (in order) "Arnfin, Havard, Hlodver, Liot and Skule" as sons of Thorfin "Hausakljufer"[232].
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that “Skuli, Ljot´s brother” was given “the title earl by the King of Scots”, before going north to Caithness from where he sailed to Orkney to claim the islands, but was defeated and fled “first over to Ca
2 CONC ithness and then south to Scotland” and later killed[233].
2 CONT
2 CONT The uncertain chronology of events recorded in Orkneyinga Saga make it difficult to be certain about the identity of the Scottish king in question, although Kenneth II (who ruled from 971 to 995) appears the most likely possibilit
2 CONC y. It is interesting to note that the text does not specify that Skuli was made “Earl of Caithness” just earl without a territorial epithet, reminiscent of the position in many other European countries at the time where the title 
2 CONC was not linked to the landholding.] 
