2 CONC øks/Blood-axe" King of Norway & his wife Gunhild Gormsdatter.  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Ragnhild Eirik´s daughter plotted the death of her husband Arnfinn at Murkle in Caithness” before marrying “his brother Havard the Fecund
2 CONC  who succeeded to the earldom”, but that she plotted her second husband´s death with his “nephew…Einar Buttered-Bread” (whom she promised to marry), that she then plotted with “Einar Hardmouth, the son of another of Havard´s siste
2 CONC rs” to kill Einar Buttered-Bread, but that she finally married “Ljot the brother of Arnfinn and Havard”[838].  She married thirdly her brother-in-law, Ljot Jarl of Orkney.]  [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm
2 CONC #_Toc268450047 (Cawley's Medlands)]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>e)         [http://www.geni.com/people/Ljot/6000000003645977990   LJOT] Torfinnsson (-killed in battle).  Orkneyinga Saga names (in order) “Arnfinn…Havard the Fecund…Hlodvir…[http://www.geni.com/people/Ljot/6000000003645977990 
2 CONC Ljot]…Skuli” as the five sons of Thorfinn[839].  Snorre names "the Earls Hlodver, Arnfid, Ljot and Skule, the sons of Thorfin Hausakljufer" as rulers in Orkney when Queen Gunhild and her sons returned to Orkney[840].  In a later s
2 CONC aga, Snorre names (in order) "Arnfin, Havard, Hlodver, Liot and Skule" as sons of Thorfin "Hausakljufer"[841].  He succeeded his brother as Jarl of Orkney.  Orkneyinga Saga records that, after the death of “Skuli, Ljot´s brother” 
2 CONC whose invasion of Orkney was repelled, “Earl Ljot took over in Caithness” and defeated “Earl Macbeth [who] came north from Scotland with a large army…at Skitten in Caithness” but died from his wounds after returning to Orkney[842]
2 CONC .  It is assumed that “Earl Macbeth” was not the future Scottish King Macbeth, who was Mormaer of Moray in the early 1030s, but an earlier earl of the same name who has not yet been identified in other primary sources.  Otherwise 
2 CONC the chronology of Orkneyinga Saga must be even more shaky than appears, and the family relationships of the Jarls of Orkney as described therein even less likely to be correct.  
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>m as her third husband, [http://www.geni.com/people/Ragnhild-The-Wicked-Eiriksdotter/6000000003645825602 RAGNHILD] Eiriksdatter, widow firstly of ARNFINN Torfinnsson Jarl of Orkney and secondly of HAVARD Torfinnsson Jarl of Ork
2 CONC ney, daughter of ERIK I "Blodøks/Blood-axe" King of Norway & his wife [http://www.geni.com/people/Gunhild-Ossursdotter/6000000005049052310  Gunhild] Gormsdatter.  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Ragnhild Eirik´s daughter plotted the
2 CONC  death of her husband Arnfinn at Murkle in Caithness” before marrying “his brother Havard the Fecund who succeeded to the earldom”, but that she plotted her second husband´s death with his “nephew…Einar Buttered-Bread” (whom she p
2 CONC romised to marry), that she then plotted with “Einar Hardmouth, the son of another of Havard´s sisters” to kill Einar Buttered-Bread, but that she finally married “Ljot the brother of Arnfinn and Havard”[843].]  [http://fmg.ac/Pro
2 CONC jects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc268450047 (Cawley's Medlands)]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>f)          '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Hlodver-Thorfinnsson/6000000001045422816  HLODVIR Torfinnsson] ([945/50]-[988], bur Hofn, Caithness).'''  Orkneyinga Saga names (in order) “Arnfinn…Havard the Fecund…Hlodvir…Ljot…Skuli
2 CONC ” as the five sons of Thorfinn[844].  Snorre names "the Earls Hlodver, Arnfid, Ljot and Skule, the sons of Thorfin Hausakljufer" as rulers in Orkney when [http://www.geni.com/people/Gunhild-Ossursdotter/6000000005049052310 Queen G
2 CONC unhild]  and her sons returned to Orkney[845].  In a later saga, Snorre names (in order) "Arnfin, Havard, Hlodver, Liot and Skule" as sons of Thorfin "Hausakljufer"[846].  Orkneyinga Saga records that “after Ljot´s death, Hlodvir 
2 CONC took charge of the earldom”, presumably indicating Ljot´s brother but this is not explicitly stated in the text, recording that “he died in his bed” and was buried “in a burial mound at Ham in Caithness”[847]. Ross, David: (‘Ancie
2 CONC nt Scotland’, Geddes & Grosset, Scotland 2004) places his death at c 980
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>m [http://www.geni.com/people/Audna-of-Orkney/6000000000160947334?through=6000000001045422816  EITHNE], daughter of KIARVAL King of Ireland & his wife ---.  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Hlodvir” married “Eithne, the daughter o
2 CONC f King Kjarval of Ireland”, adding in a later passage that “Sigurd´s mother was a sorceress”[848].  Hlodve´s supposed father-in-law has not been identified among known Irish kings.  
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>Hlodve & his wife had [three] children:  [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc268450047 (Cawley's Medlands)]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>i)          [http://www.geni.com/people/Sigurd-II-Hlodvesson/6000000001041474621?through=6000000001045422816  SIGURD] "Digri/the Stout", son of [HLODVIR Torfinnsson Jarl of Orkney & his wife Eithne of Ireland] ([965/70]-killed
2 CONC  in battle Clontarf 23 Apr 1014).  Orkneyinga Saga names “Sigurd the Stout” as the son of Hlodvir and his wife, adding that he “took over the earldom” after his father died[856].  Snorre names "Sigurd the Thick" as the son of Hlod
2 CONC ver[857].  His birth date is estimated from the fact that his older sons were already sufficiently mature in 1014 to be placed in charge of Orkney by their father when he left for war in Ireland.  He succeeded his father in [988] 
2 CONC as Jarl of Orkney and Caithness.  He defeated Findlaech Mormaer of Moray before 995 at the second battle of Skidmoor in Caithness, consolidating his power on the Scottish mainland.  Orkneyinga Saga records Sigurd´s battle against 
2 CONC “a Scottish earl called Finnleik”[858].  Orkneyinga Saga records that “[http://www.geni.com/people/Olav-Tryggvason/6000000003867333182 Olaf Tryggvason]” pressured Sigurd to accept baptism and returned to Norway with Sigurd´s son a
2 CONC s a hostage[859].  Orkneyinga Saga records that Sigurd went to Ireland “five years after the Battle of Svoldur” to support “King [http://www.geni.com/people/Sitric-I-King-of-Dublin/6000000002043182790?through=6000000003645985234 S
2 CONC igtrygg Silk-Beard]” against “[http://www.geni.com/people/Brian-B%C3%B3ruma-mac-Cenn%C3%A9tig/6000000002043263223  King Brian] of Ireland”, and left “his elder sons in charge of the earldom”, but was killed in the battle in which 
2 CONC King Brian was killed[860].  Snorre records that "Sigurd the Thick" went to Ireland and fell "in Brian's battle"[861].  [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc268450047 (Cawley's Medlands)]
2 CONT
