1 OCCU Egilssaga siger at han var meget smuk, Nobleman, chieftain, travels to Ireland to meet his grandfather, King Myrkjartan, to Norway, retainer of King Harald Grey-Cloak, later of Earl Hakon, inherits Goddastead, builds Hjardaholt
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf the Peacock (Old Norse: Ólafr Pái) or Olaf Hoskuldsson (Ólafr Höskuldsson) (c. 938–1006)[1] was a merchant and chieftain of the early Icelandic Commonwealth, who was nicknamed "the Peacock" because of his proud bearing and ma
2 CONC gnificent wardrobe. He is a major character in the Laxdaela Saga and is mentioned in a number of other Icelandic sources. The son of a slave woman, Olaf became one of the wealthiest landowners in Iceland and played a major role in
2 CONC  its politics and society during the latter half of the tenth century. In addition to the Laxdaela Saga in which he takes a leading role, Olaf also is mentioned in Egil's Saga, Njal's Saga, Gunnlaugs saga, Kormaks saga, Grettir's 
2 CONC Saga and the Landnámabók, among others. Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT     1 Birth and upbringing
2 CONT     2 Career abroad
2 CONT     3 Return to Iceland
2 CONT     4 Second expedition to Norway and aftermath
2 CONT     5 Later years
2 CONT     6 Notes
2 CONT     7 References
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth and upbringing
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf was the son of Hoskuld Dala-Kollsson, a chieftain who lived in the Laxardal region.[2] According to Laxdaela Saga, Hoskuld purchased a mute thrall-woman from a Rus' merchant on Brännö while on a trading expedition to Norway, 
2 CONC and made her his concubine while away from his wife Jorunn Bjarnadottir.[3] When Hoskuld returned home to Iceland, he took the concubine with him. Despite Jorunn's irritation, the concubine was accepted into Hoskuld's household, t
2 CONC hough he remained faithful to Jorunn while in Iceland. The following winter the concubine gave birth to a son, to whom they gave the name Olaf after Hoskuld's uncle, Olaf Feilan, who had recently died.[4] Landnámabók mentions that
2 CONC  Hoskuld and Melkorka had another son, Helgi, but he does not appear in Laxdaela.[5] According to Laxdaela Saga, Olaf was a precocious child, and could speak and walk perfectly by the age of two. One day Hoskuld discovered Olaf's 
2 CONC mother speaking to her son; she was not, in fact, mute.[6] When he confronted her she told him that she was an Irish princess named Melkorka carried off in a viking raid, and that her father was an Irish king named "Myrkjartan" (M
2 CONC uirchertach).[7] Shortly thereafter squabbling between Jorunn and Melkorka forced Hoskuld to move his concubine and his son by her to a different farm, which thereafter was known as Melkorkustead.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of seven, over his mother's objections, Olaf became the foster son and heir of a wealthy but childless goði named Thord, who was at the time engaged in complex litigation with the kinsmen of his ex-wife Vigdis Ingjaldsd
2 CONC ottir (another descendent of Thorstein the Red). Olaf's adoption complicated the issues in the suit and threatened to lead to a blood feud, but Hoskuld arranged a settlement and compensated Vigdis' kinsmen with gifts.[9] By foster
2 CONC ing Olaf Thord gained the protection of the powerful Hoskuld, and Hoskuld secured an inheritance for his illegitimate son beyond the limited amount he was permitted to leave to Olaf under Icelandic law.[10] Olaf accompanied Thord 
2 CONC to the Althing when he was twelve years old, and his fancy clothing earned him the admiring nickname "the Peacock."[11] Career abroad
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 956, Olaf, at Melkorka's urging, decided to go abroad to seek his fortune. Hoskuld was opposed and would not provide trade wares, and the property of Olaf's foster-father Thord was mostly in immobile goods and land. In part
2 CONC  to arrange financing for his expedition, his mother Melkorka married Thorbjorn the Feeble, a farmer who had previously assisted her in the management of Melkorkustead. Melkorka and Thorbjorn had a son named Lambi.[12] Olaf sailed
2 CONC  to Norway with Orn, a sea-captain and hirdman of King Harald Greycloak. He gained great honor at Harald's court, and was a favorite of the king's mother Gunnhild, who had, according to Icelandic sources, been the lover of Olaf's 
2 CONC uncle Hrut Herjolfsson.[13] When Olaf expressed a desire to find his mother's people in Ireland, Gunnhild financed his voyage.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf set sail for Ireland with Orm to find his mother's people, taking with him tokens and gifts from Melkorka to her father and her nursemaid.[15] During the voyage, their ship became lost in a fog; upon arriving in Ireland they 
2 CONC were stranded far outside the protection of the Norse-Gaelic longphorts.[16] The ship was attacked by local Irishmen, despite the efforts of Olaf, who spoke the Gaelic language, to negotiate safe passage with them.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT As luck would have it, the local king arrived on the scene, and proved to be Olaf's alleged grandfather Myrkjartan. Olaf remained with Myrkjartan for a time, and the king, according to Laxdaela Saga, even offered to make Olaf his 
2 CONC heir. Olaf, however, ultimately returned to Norway, afraid of provoking Myrkjartan's sons.[18] Olaf returned to the court of King Harald, where he was greatly honored by both the king and his mother Gunnhild.[19] Return to Iceland
2 CONC  Olaf's father in law Egill Skallagrímsson, from a 17th century manuscript of Egils Saga
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf returned home around 957 with great wealth. Upon his return, his father Hoskuld arranged a marriage for him with Thorgerd Egilsdottir, the daughter of Egil Skallagrimsson.[20] Thorgerd was initially reluctant to marry the son
2 CONC  of a slave, refusing to believe that Olaf's mother was a princess. However, she ultimately agreed to the match after an hours-long private conversation with Olaf. At the wedding Olaf gave Egil an ornate sword from Ireland.[21] Ol
2 CONC af and Thorgerd lived happily together at Hoskuldstead for some time. Around 962 Olaf's foster father Thord died, leaving Olaf his property and goðorð Olaf bought land and built a new homestead at Hjardarholt, which, according to 
2 CONC the saga, he had to cleanse of the draugr of its former owner, Killer-Hrapp. As time went on people began to settle near Olaf's hall and regarded him as their goði. Olaf's ever-increasing wealth caused jealousy from Hoskuld's wife
2 CONC  Jorunn. Around the same time Olaf and Thorgerd had a daughter, Thurid.[22] Hjardarholt was renowned for its rich decorations; some two decades later, the skald Úlfr Uggason composed the famous poem Húsdrápa, about certain mytholo
2 CONC gical scenes illustrated on the walls of Olaf's hall.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf's half-sister Hallgerd Hoskuldsdottir married Gunnar Hámundarson, a chieftain who lived at Hlíðarendi in southern Iceland, during this period.[24] Olaf and Gunnar became close friends.[25]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hoskuld died around 965, leaving Olaf a full mark of gold, causing tension between Olaf and Hoskuld's legitimate sons, Bard and Thorleik.[26] As an illegitimate son, Olaf was entitled to one mark of his father's wealth; this was, 
2 CONC however, customarily understood to be a mark of silver and not gold. Olaf eased the tension by paying one-third of the communal funeral feast for Hoskuld.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf and Thorgerd had a number of children after Thurid, the sons Kjartan (named after King Myrkjartan),[27] Steinthor, Halldor, Helgi, and Hoskuld and the daughters Thorbjorg, Thorgerd and Berghora. The ill-fated Kjartan would be
2 CONC  his father's favorite.[28] Second expedition to Norway and aftermath Jarl Haakon Sigurdsson. Illustration by Christian Krohg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 975, over his wife's objections, Olaf went on a second expedition to Norway. There he stayed with a viking named Geirmund the Noisy and visited Haakon Jarl, the latter of whom gave him a cargo of timber to take home as a gi
2 CONC ft. On his return Olaf reluctantly brought Geirmund with him. and Geirmund fell in love with Olaf's daughter Thurid. Though Olaf was opposed to the match, Geirmund bribed Thorgerd to be his advocate, and Olaf relented. The marriag
2 CONC e was an unhappy one, and after three years Geirmund decided to return home without leaving any money for the support of his ex-wife and daughter. Enraged, Thurid boarded his ship before he departed, stole his famous sword "Leg-Bi
2 CONC ter," and left their infant daughter Groa on the ship. Geirmund cursed the sword, and on his return to Norway he and all of his shipmates, including little Groa, were drowned.[29] Later years
2 CONT
2 CONT During the closing years of the tenth century, Olaf's kinsman and friend Gunnar became embroiled in a blood feud with several neighboring landowners. A settlement was reached whereby Gunnar would accept "lesser outlawry," a three-
2 CONC year exile, but after agreeing to the settlement Gunnar refused to leave Iceland. Olaf tried to protect his kinsman but was unsuccessful, and Gunnar was killed by his enemies.[30] Olaf's favorite son Kjartan traveled abroad with h
2 CONC is beloved cousin Bolli Thorleiksson. The two were very close. Ultimately, however, they grew apart when Bolli married Kjartan's lover Guðrún Ósvífursdóttir. According to Oddr Snorrason, Olaf had predicted that strife would ensue 
2 CONC between Kjartan and Bolli.[31] Tensions between the cousins grew until a full-blown blood feud ended with Kjartan being killed by Bolli in 1003. Ironically, Bolli killed Kjartan with the sword "Leg-biter," which had been given to 
2 CONC him as a gift by his cousin Thurid, Kjartan's sister.[32] Olaf refused to prosecute Bolli for the killing, and arranged for him to pay a fine instead; by sharp contrast, he had Gudrun's brothers, who had goaded Bolli to fight his 
2 CONC cousin, driven into exile.[33] Jesse Byock contrasted Olaf's magnanimity towards Bolli with the blood feud mentality of his wife Thorgerd:
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf knows that Kjartan, who was involved in a love triangle with Bolli and Bolli's wife Gudrun Osvifrsdottir, caused his own downfall by acting aggressively. In Icelandic terms, Kjartan had surpassed the acceptable limits of immo
2 CONC deration. Whereas Olaf wants to maintain the solidarity of the larger family, keeping workable relations with his siblings and their children, Thorgerd's concerns are different. She focuses more narrowly on the honour of her nucle
2 CONC ar family.[34]
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf died in 1006, and Olaf's widow Thorgerd subsequently directed a number of revenge-killings herself, including that of Thorkel, a man who had witnessed Kjartan's death but been indifferent to it and had not intervened.[35] Bol
2 CONC li was killed by Olaf's sons and their allies in a raid led by Thorgerd.[36] Some twelve years later, Gudrun, with the help of her friend Snorri Goði, had a number of Bolli's murderers killed in revenge.[37]
