1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sigtrygg Silkbeard ''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sigtrygg_Silkbeard#Issue_and_legacy '''Wikipedia], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sigtrygg II Silkbeard Olafsson''' (also Sihtric, Sitric[1] and Sitrick in Irish texts; or Sigtryg[2] and Sigtryggr[3] in Scandinavian texts) was a Hiberno-Norse king of Dublin (possibly AD 989–994; restored or began 995–1000; r
2 CONC estored 1000 and abdicated 1036) of the Uí Ímair dynasty. He was caught up in the abortive Leinster revolt of 999–1000, after which he was forced to submit to the King of Munster, Brian Boru. His family also conducted a double mar
2 CONC riage alliance with Boru, although he later realigned himself with the main leaders of the Leinster revolt of 1012–1014. He has a prominent role in the 12th-century Irish Cogadh Gaedhil re Gallaibh and the 13th century Icelandic N
2 CONC jal's Saga, as the main Norse leader at the Battle of Clontarf in 1014.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigtrygg's long reign spanned 46 years, until his abdication in 1036.[4] During that period, his armies saw action in four of the five Irish provinces of the time. In particular, he conducted a long series of raids into territorie
2 CONC s such as Meath, Wicklow, Ulster, and perhaps even the coast of Wales. He also came into conflict with rival Norse kings, especially in Cork and Waterford.
2 CONT
2 CONT He went on pilgrimage to Rome in 1028 and is associated with the foundation of Christ Church Cathedral in Dublin. Although Dublin underwent several reversals of fortune during his reign, on the whole trade in the city flourished. 
2 CONC He died in 1042.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family===
2 CONT Sigtrygg was of Norse and Irish ancestry.[2] He was a son of Olaf Cuarán (also called Kváran), King of York and of Dublin, and Gormflaith ingen Murchada.[3] Gormflaith was the daughter of the King of Leinster, Murchad mac Finn,[5]
2 CONC  and the sister of his successor, King Máel Mórda of Leinster.[3] She had previously been married to the King of Meath and High King of Ireland, Máel Sechnaill[3] — the first of her three husbands. She was a beautiful, powerful an
2 CONC d intriguing Irish woman,[3] who according to the 13th-century Icelandic Njál's saga, was "the fairest of all women, and best gifted in everything that was not in her own power, but it was the talk of men that she did all things i
2 CONC ll over which she had any power".[3][6] Sigtrygg's paternal half-brother was Glúniairn, "Iron-knee", who ruled as King of Dublin from 980–989.
2 CONT
2 CONT An incident involving the ransom of one of Sigtrygg's sons late in his reign, in which "seven score British horse" were mentioned in the list of demands,[7] suggests that Dublin was one of the main ports for importing horses into 
2 CONC 11th century Ireland, and that Sigtrygg and his family may have been personally involved in animal husbandry.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ''From [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/IRELAND.htm#OlafSihtricsondied981 '''Medlands] v3.2 Updated 21 April 2017
2 CONT
