1 OCCU King of Aileach and Prince of Ulster and /Tyrone/
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Aedh Athlamhan Ó Néill (955 – 1033), universally recorded as Aodh Athlamh ("Aodh the Dexterous"), was a powerful high-medieval sovereign, King of Ailech, and a premier prince of the Northern Uí Néill. As the chosen
2 CONC  heir of the Cenél nÉogain dynasty, his career was defined by fierce territorial conflicts, joint raiding campaigns alongside his father, and crucial structural alliances that linked his family directly to the royal household of H
2 CONC igh King Brian Boru.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aedh earned his distinctive historical epithet Athlamh (the Dexterous or Agile) due to his exceptional reputation for physical skill and rapid tactical movement during cattle-tribute raids. He operated as the primary enforcement a
2 CONC rm of his father, King Flaithbertach "An Trostáin". Together, they fiercely defended the northern province against the rising expansion of southern dynasties. In 1030, when his father abdicated the crown to undertake a highly publ
2 CONC icized religious pilgrimage to Rome, Aedh formally ascended the throne as the undisputed King of Ailech, holding total regional authority over western Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aedh’s political authority was immensely fortified by his maternal lineage. His mother was Bé Binn ingen Briain, a royal princess and daughter of the iconic High King Brian Boru. This marriage alliance created a complex political 
2 CONC dynamic, positioning Aedh as both a competitor and a blood-relative to the southern O'Brien (Dál gCais) dynasty. To further secure his maritime horizons, historical clan records indicate a strategic marital union connecting him to
2 CONC  the prominent MacKenneth (Lamont) lineages of western Scotland, establishing deep cross-channel networks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Political Succession. Aedh’s reign at the absolute peak of his power was abruptly cut short. On November 30, 1033, he was killed following an intense regional clash or sudden illness. His death was a devastating blow to 
2 CONC his aging father, Flaithbertach, who was forced to return from his pilgrimage, cut his retirement short, and resume the crown of Ailech for three more years to stabilize the heavily fractured kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aedh's direct lineages split into two highly distinct branches that fundamentally reshaped both Irish and Scottish history:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Domnall an Togdhamh Ó Néill – His eldest son, who remained in Ireland to contest the regional lordship of Tyrone and anchor the core O'Neill lineages.
2 CONT #Ánrothán Uí Néill (Aodh Anrachan) – His younger son, who following a bitter dynastic dispute, migrated across the Irish Sea to Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the official, unyielding traditions of Clan MacNeil of Scotland, Aedh Athlamhan is formally venerated as the 19th Ancestral Chief. He represents the absolute final patriarch of the lineage to rule entirely from Irish soil. 
2 CONC He is the direct patrilineal root from whom both Clan MacNeil and the MacSweeneys spring, acting as the historic genealogical anchor that binds the prehistoric royal dynasties of Ireland directly to the medieval seafaring lords of
2 CONC  the Hebrides.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
