2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT WIKIPEDIA
2 CONT
2 CONT Rognvald Brusason (died 1046), son of Brusi Sigurdsson, was Earl of Orkney jointly with Thorfinn Sigurdsson from about 1037 onwards. His life is recorded in the Orkneyinga Saga.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rognvald was taken by his father to Norway, to the court of Olaf Haraldsson, when Brusi and Thorfinn went there to have the inheritance of Einar Wry-mouth's third-share of the Earldom settled. Olaf kept Einar's share for himself, 
2 CONC appointing Brusi to administer it, and kept Rognvald at his court.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Orkneyinga Saga says of Rognvald:
2 CONT     Rognvald was one of the handsomest of men, with a fine head of golden hair, smooth as silk. At an early age he grew to be tall and strong, earning a great reputation for his shrewdness and courtesy ...[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rognvald was a supporter of Olaf Haraldsson, later Saint Olaf, sharing his exile in Kievan Rus, and helping his brother Harald Sigurdsson, better known as Harald Hardraade, escape after the Battle of Stiklestad in 1030. While Hara
2 CONC ld went on to Constantinople, Rognvald and other exiles remained in Rus, in the service of Yaroslav the Wise. Rognvald returned to Norway with Olaf's son Magnus the Good in 1035.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT While Rognvald was abroad, his father had died and Thorfinn Sigurdsson was ruling all of the Earldom of Orkney. Rognvald asked King Magnus for his third part of the Earldom, and Magnus agreed, giving him three ships and granting h
2 CONC im the stewardship of Magnus's own third share. When Rognvald arrived in Orkney, he sent to his uncle Thorfinn asking him for the two thirds of the Earldom which Magnus had given him. Thorfinn agreed to give Rognvald his father's 
2 CONC third, and the third which Magnus claimed into the bargain, although he claimed not to recognise Magnus's claim and presented this as a gift in return for Rognvald's assistance. and Rognvald worked closely together for eight years
2 CONC , fighting against enemies in the Hebrides and raiding Scotland and England.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the Earls eventually fell out. The proximate cause of their quarrel, according to the saga, was the arrival of Kalf Arnesson, the uncle of Thorfinn's wife Ingibiorg Finnsdottir.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Kalf had a large following which placed a heavy burden on the Earl's finances. Plenty of people told him that he shouldn't let Rognvald have two-thirds of the islands, considering his heavy outlay.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rognvald and Kalf Arnesson were not friends. The Orkneyinga Saga reports that Rognvald, a staunch supporter of Saint Olaf, came close to attacking Kalf in Rus, who alone among the Arnessons had betrayed Olaf, when he came to pledg
2 CONC e his support to Magnus.[6] For that reason, if for no other, Rognvald refused to hand over the third which Thorfinn asked for. From then onwards, relations deteriorated. Rognvald was defeated in a sea-battle and sought refuge in 
2 CONC Norway with Magnus while Thorfinn took control of the earldom.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT With a single ship, and a crew of picked men, Rognvald returned to Orkney hoping that surprise would enable him to retake the earldom. He succeeded, but not entirely as Thorfinn was able to flee to Caithness. However, soon afterwa
2 CONC rds, Rognvald was surprised in his turn, but was killed by Thorkell the Fosterer while escaping, given away by the barking of his lap dog.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rognvald was buried on Papa Westray. The Orkneyinga Saga offers this assessment of Rognvald:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Everyone agrees that of all the Earls of Orkney he was the most popular and gifted, and his death was mourned by many.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Twelfth-century Earl Kali Kolsson was given the name Rognvald "because Kali's mother claimed that Rognvald Brusason had been the most able of all the Earls of Orkney, and people saw this as a sign of good luck.[10]"
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT    1. ^ Orkneyinga Saga, cc. 17–19; Saint Olaf's Saga, cc. 100–102.
2 CONT    2. ^ Orkneyinga Saga, c. 19; Saint Olaf's Saga, c. 100.
2 CONT    3. ^ Orkneyinga Saga, cc. 19 &21; Saga of Harald Sigurtharson, c. 1.
2 CONT    4. ^ Orkneyinga Saga, cc. 21–22; Crawford, pp. 77–78.
2 CONT    5. ^ Orkneyinga Saga, c. 25.
2 CONT    6. ^ Orkneyinga Saga, c. 21; Crawford, pp. 77–78.
2 CONT    7. ^ Orkneyinga Saga, cc. 25–27.
2 CONT    8. ^ Orkneyinga Saga, cc. 27–29.
2 CONT    9. ^ Orkneyinga Saga, c. 29.
2 CONT   10. ^ Orkneyinga Saga, c. 61.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT     * Anderson, Alan Orr, Early Sources of Scottish History A.D 500–1286, volume 1. Reprinted with corrections. Paul Watkins, Stamford, 1990. ISBN 1-871615-03-8
2 CONT     * Anon., Orkneyinga Saga: The History of the Earls of Orkney, tr. Hermann Pálsson and Paul Edwards. Penguin, London, 1978. ISBN 0-14-044383-5
2 CONT     * Crawford, Barbara, Scandinavian Scotland. Leicester University Press, Leicester, 1987. ISBN 0-7185-1282-0
2 CONT     * Sturluson, Snorri, Heimskringla: History of the Kings of Norway, tr. Lee M. Hollander. Reprinted University of Texas Press, Austin, 1992. ISBN 0-292-73061-6
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT NOTE Ragnvald Orkneyjarl er mest truleg identisk med Ragnvald Jarl som nemnast som far til Tora, mor til Magnus Berrføtt i Sunnmørsættleggen, kjelde frå ca 1220. Ragnvald Orkneyjarl var i lange tider i Noreg. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Den grundigste kilden for Toras opphav er norsk, Sunnmørsættleggen fra omkring 1220/1350 (senere gjengitt i Adversaria Huitfeldiana), og oppgir Tora Magnus' mor som datter av Ragnvald, jarl på Godøy og Orkney. Historiker P.A.Munch
2 CONC  skrev en oversikt over denne i 1839 (tavlene fins i Photos): http://www.soga.no/artiklar/pamunch/index.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT P.A. Munch skriver bl a: "En annen omstendighet, hvori nærværende slegtregistre avvige fra sagaerne, eller nøyere oplyse dem, er den, at Kong Magnus Barfods moder her siges at være datter av en Ragnvald Jarl i Godø, (ikke som i sa
2 CONC gaerne Thora Jonsdatter eller Arnesdatter), og siden gift med Ljot Jarl."
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT Rognvald was taken by his father to Norway, to the court of Olaf Haraldsson, when Brusi and Thorfinn went there to have the inheritance of Einar Wry-mouth's third-share of the Earldom settled. Olaf kept Einar's share for himself, 
2 CONC appointing Brusi to administer it, and kept Rognvald at his court.
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT FROM THE SAGAS "Ragnvald, sønn til Bruse, ble igjen østpå hos kong Olav; han var så vakker som få, hadde stort hår, gult som silke; han ble tidlig stor og sterk og var en usedvanlig kjekk kar, både når det gjaldt å ha vett og å fø
2 CONC re seg høvisk. Han ble hos kong Olav i lang tid siden. Ottar svarte nevner dette i den dråpa han diktet om kong Olav:
2 CONT
2 CONT Fast og strengt du holder på storkongens velde; hjaltlendinger teller du blant dine tegner Før deg ingen stridsdjerv yngling her på jorda vant under seg øst fra øyer vest for havet."
2 CONT
2 CONT fra Heimskringla, Olaf den helliges saga, Snorri Sturluson
2 CONT -----------------------------------------
2 CONT Earl Bruse remained behind, and took his time to get ready. Before his departure the king sent for him, and said, " It appears to me, earl, that in thee I have a man on the west side of the sea on whose fidelity I can depend; ther
2 CONC efore I intend to give thee the two parts of the country which thou formerly hadst to rule over; for I will not that thou shouldst be a less powerful man after entering into my service than before: but I will secure thy fidelity b
2 CONC y keeping thy son Ragnvald with me. I see well enough that with two parts of the country and my help, thou wilt be able to defend what is thy own against thy brother Thorfin." Bruse was thankful for getting two thirds instead of o
2 CONC ne third of the country, and soon after he set out, and came about autumn to Orkney; but Ragnvald, Bruse's son, remained behind in the East with King Olaf. Ragnvald was one of the handsomest men that could be seen, -- his hair lon
2 CONC g, and yellow as silk; and he soon grew up, stout and tall, and he was a very able and superb man, both of great understanding and polite manners. He was long with King Olaf. Otter Svarte speaks of these affairs in the poem he com
2 CONC posed about King Olaf: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "From Shetland, far off in the cold North Sea, Come chiefs who desire to be subject to thee: No king so well known for his will, and his might, To defend his own people from scaith or unright. These isles of the West midst the oce
2 CONC an's wild roar, Scarcely heard the voice of their sovereign before; Our bravest of sovereigns before could scarce bring These islemen so proud to acknowledge their king."
2 CONT
2 CONT Full text:  http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=&emne=&vis=s_e_olav_haraldsson3
2 CONT
