2 CONT =Paul and Erlend Thorfinnsson=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul Thorfinnsson (died after 1098) and Erlend Thorfinnsson (died after 1098) were the sons of Thorfinn Sigurdsson and Ingibiorg Finnsdottir, and ruled as together as Earls of Orkney after the death of their father. Their lives an
2 CONC d times are recounted in the Orkneyinga Saga.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first mention of the brothers is when they accompanied the Norwegian king Harald Hardrade and Tostig Godwinson on the ill-fated expedition to England in 1066. Paul and Erlend were with Harald's son Olaf Kyrre, guarding the shi
2 CONC ps, when the battle of Stamford Bridge was fought. Along with Olaf they were allowed to leave by the English king Harold Godwinson. Olaf overwintered on Orkney with them and left on good terms with the Thorfinssons.
2 CONT
2 CONT The saga says that Paul and Erlend were on good terms until their children grew to adulthood, after which the disputes between their sons led to a quarrel and open hostility between the brothers. As the disputes between the descen
2 CONC dants of Paul and Erlend loomed large in the affairs of 12th century Orkney, the saga goes into some detail on their family relationships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Paul was married to an unnamed daughter of Norwegian earl Hakon Ivarsson. Two sons and four daughters are named. Of these, Hakon played the greatest part in events. Paul's daughter Gunnhild's was marred to Kol Kalison and Rog
2 CONC nvald Kali Kolsson was their son. Earl Erlend married Thora, daughter of one Sumarlidi Ospaksson, and they had two sons and two daughters, while Erlend had a third, illegitimate daughter as well. Erlend's son Magnus appears in the
2 CONC  saga as earl, martyr and saint, but the troubles between the earls began with rivalry between Hakon Paulsson and Magnus's brother Erling. Both are described as quarrelsome, arrogant men, and talented too.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Thorfinnson, Paul I of Orkney, Earl of Orkney. Acceded: 1057 Died: 1099, Bergen. Father: Sigurdsdon, Thorfinn the Black of Orkney, Jarl of Orkney, b. 1009. Mother: Finnsdottir, Ingibiorg Earls Mother. Married to Hakondottir, Daugh
2 CONC ter. Child 1: Paulsson, Hakon, Earl of Orkney. Child 2: Pauldottir, Thora. Child 3: Paulson, Brynjolf. Child 4: Pauldottir, Ingirid. Child 5: Pauldottir, Herbjorg. Child 6: Pauldottir, Ragnhild. (Directory of Royal Genealogical Da
2 CONC ta, Hull, England)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1098 drar Magnus Barfot till Orkneyöarna, som de facto är ett oavhängigt jarladöme nu. Han lyckas infånga två bröder som heter Pål Jarl och Erlend Jarl och skickar dem till Norge. I deras ställe sätter han ett norskt regeringskoll
2 CONC egium med sin åttaårige son Sigurd som toppfigur. På Hebriderna och Man härskar en hövding som heter Gudröd Crovan. Magnus Barfot underlägger sig utan svårighet dennes rike. (En nordisk kronologi, sid 92, Alf Henrikson)
2 CONT Bar
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.espell.se/saga/p866eb593.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Paul Thorfinnsson (died after 1098) and Erlend Thorfinnsson (died after 1098) ruled together as Earls of Orkney.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul and Erlend Thorfinnsson were the sons of Thorfinn Sigurdsson and Ingibiorg Finnsdottir. Through Ingibiorg's father Finn Arnesson and his wife, the family was related to the Norwegian Kings Olav II and Harald II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their lives and times are recounted in the Orkneyinga Saga. The first mention of the brothers is when they accompanied the Norwegian king Harald Hardrade and Tostig Godwinson on the ill-fated expedition to England in 1066. Paul an
2 CONC d Erlend were with Harald's son Olaf Kyrre, guarding the ships, when the battle of Stamford Bridge was fought. Along with Olaf they were allowed to leave by the English king Harold Godwinson. Olaf overwintered on Orkney with them 
2 CONC and left on good terms with the Thorfinssons.
2 CONT
2 CONT The saga says that Paul and Erlend were on good terms until their children grew to adulthood, after which the disputes between their sons led to a quarrel and open hostility between the brothers. As the disputes between the descen
2 CONC dants of Paul and Erlend loomed large in the affairs of 12th century Orkney, the saga goes into some detail on their family relationships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul was married to an unnamed daughter of Norwegian earl Hakon Ivarsson. Two sons and four daughters are named. Of these, Hakon played the greatest part in events. Paul's daughter Gunnhild's was marred to Kol Kalison and Rognvald
2 CONC  Kali Kolsson was their son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erlend married Thora, daughter of one Sumarlidi Ospaksson, and they had two sons and two daughters, while Erlend had a third, illegitimate daughter as well. Erlend's son Magnus appears in the saga as earl, martyr and saint. The tr
2 CONC oubles between the earls began with rivalry between Hakon Paulsson and Magnus's brother Erling. Both are described as quarrelsome, arrogant men, and talented too.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus III of Norway took possession of the islands in 1098, deposing Erlend and Paul. Paul's son, Haakon Paulsson, then became regent on behalf of the Norwegian prince, the future King Sigurd I of Norway, who made Haakon earl in 
2 CONC 1105.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  son of Thorfinn
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT PAUL Thorfinnsson, son of THORFINN II "the Black" Jarl of Orkney & his wife Ingibjörg Finnsdatter (-in prison Bergen 1098, bur Bergen).  Orkneyinga Saga names “Paul and…Erlend” as the two sons of Jarl Thorfinn and his wife who sur
2 CONC vived childhood[Orkneyinga Saga 33, p. 76].  He succeeded his father [1065 (or earlier)] as Joint Jarl of Orkney, jointly with his brother.  Morkinskinna records that Harald III King of Norway sailed to Orkney en route for his inv
2 CONC asion of England in 1066 and “recruited the aid of Jarl Thorfinn’s sons Páll and Erlendr”[Morkinskinna, 49, p. 264].  He may have fought with his father at Stamford Bridge 1066[ William of Malmesbury, ii, 311]. ''This is profoundl
2 CONC y unlikely, as Thorfinn was certainly out of the picture  before then and the Thorfinssons are reported by Norse sources to have been left guarding the baggage - or *as* baggage. Maven.]''
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that Magnus "Barelegs" King of Norway “seized the Earls, Paul and Erlend” on his arrival in Orkney, “sent them east to Norway, and made his own son Sigurd overlord of the islands with regents to govern the 
2 CONC earldom”, adding in a later passage that Paul “was buried at Bergen”[Orkneyinga Saga 39 and 42, pp. 84 and 88].  He was deposed and imprisoned in 1098 by Magnus "Barelegs" King of Norway, and died in Bergen according to Snorre[Sno
2 CONC rre, Magnus Barefoot's Saga, 12]. 
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#PaulIdied1098A
