1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT Donnsleibhe, who was said to be a descendant of an Irish prince of the O'Neill dynasty named Ánrothán Ua Néill, who left Ireland for Kintyre in the 11th century. He was a son of Áed, son of Flaithbertach Ua Néill, King of Ailech a
2 CONC nd Cenél nEógain, died 1036. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Aodh Aonrachan Ó Néill (1004 – 1036), widely recorded in classical Irish genealogies as Ánrothán Uí Néill and known colloquially as Hugh the Solitary or The Exile, was a pivotal high-medieval Irish prince of the Cenél nÉogain dyna
2 CONC sty. His life marked a monumental geographic and political fracture within the O'Neill royal line. Following a bitter, violent succession dispute with his older brother in Ulster, Aodh permanently abandoned his ancestral Irish lan
2 CONC ds, crossing the Irish Sea to establish the foundational bloodlines for several of the most dominant seafaring clans in western Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aodh’s departure from Ireland is preserved in early narrative accounts, including the historic text Leabhar Chlainne Suibhne (The Book of the MacSweeneys). Upon the death of their father, King Aedh Athlamhan, in 1033, a fierce dyn
2 CONC astic rivalry erupted between Aodh and his older brother, Domnall an Togdhamh. Although Aodh was highly charismatic and possessed significant support among local warriors, his brother held the legal priority of birthright. To avoi
2 CONC d a prolonged, blood-soaked civil war that would weaken the Northern Uí Néill against southern dynasties, or following a formal curse and ritual excommunication leveled against him by his brother, Aodh chose exile. He collected a 
2 CONC heavily armed fleet of loyal warriors, ships, and material wealth, permanently sailing away from Lough Foyle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aodh navigated across the North Channel to the Gaelic maritime territory of Argyll and the Western Isles. Arriving in the kingdom of Scotland, he sought immediate strategic alliances to secure his displaced followers. He entered i
2 CONC nto a highly prestigious matrimonial alliance by marrying a Princess of Scotland, a daughter of the ruling house of King Malcolm II. This union integrated the exiled Irish prince directly into the highest echelons of Scottish nobi
2 CONC lity, gaining him extensive lands across the rugged districts of Cowal and Knapdale, and anchoring his fleet at the strategic maritime hub of Kintyre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aodh passed away around 1036, only a few years after establishing his new sovereign enclave in the Hebrides. Unlike his ancestors, who were entombed in the royal dirt of Ulster, Aodh was buried within his newly claimed Scottish te
2 CONC rritories, likely on the Isle of Barra where his descendants consolidated power. His decision to leave Ireland permanently fractured his lineage, splitting the family between the continental O'Neills of Tyrone and the new seafarin
2 CONC g dynasties of the Scottish Highlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aonrachan's lineages completely dominated the medieval politics of western Scotland, serving as the common ancestral patriarch for several major historical clans:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Aedh Alain O'Neill (Hugh the Splendid) – His primary son and direct genealogical successor.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Clan MacNeil of Barra – Direct patrilineal descendants who claimed his lineage as their supreme sovereign root.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Clan Lamont, Clan MacLachlan, and Clan MacEwen of Otter – Prominent Highland clans who explicitly trace their origins to Aodh's arrival in Cowal.
2 CONT
2 CONT #The MacSweeneys (Clann Suibhne) – The elite gallowglass warrior elite who eventually returned to Ireland as mercenary commanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the official historical traditions of Clan MacNeil of Scotland, Aodh Aonrachan is venerated as the 20th Ancestral Chief. He is the absolute cornerstone of the clan's origin history—the foundational migrant prince who physic
2 CONC ally transported the royal, ancient lineage of Niall of the Nine Hostages across the sea from the Grianan of Ailech straight into the Western Isles, permanently planting the seed that became the Lordship of Barra.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
