1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Tadhg mac Cathail''' (d. 956), a highly powerful medieval King of Connacht from the Uí Briúin Aí dynasty. He ruled his regional kingdom from 925 until his death in 956.
2 CONT
2 CONT He earned the epithet "an Tuir" (meaning "of the Tower") because he fortified his royal seat with stone structures to defend against rival regional kingdoms and Viking raiders.
2 CONT
2 CONT Recorded Wives
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaelic genealogical tracts and individual county histories identify his marriage alliances as purely domestic arrangements within the province of Connacht to secure his borders
2 CONT
2 CONT *Creassa ingen Urchadha: His primary, historically documented wife. She was a princess of West Connacht (Iar Connacht), being the daughter of King Urchadh mac Murchadh. Through Creassa's family tree, Tadhg became the brother-in-la
2 CONC w of Cennétig mac Lorcáin (King of Thomond). This made Tadhg an Tuir the direct maternal uncle of High King Brian Boru.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Other Claims: Some secondary provincial tracks hint at a lateral marriage alliance with the neighboring Uí Fiachrach maritime lords to protect his northern coastline, though her name remains unrecorded in the major national chron
2 CONC icles.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historical Fact 
2 CONT
2 CONT The contemporary annals contain absolutely zero record of any marriage or blood alliance between Tadhg an Tuir's household and the rival Uí Briúin Bréifne (O'Ruairc) line during his lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT The O'Ruaircs were his bitter, territorial enemies. Tadhg spent his entire reign fighting to keep them out of central Connacht. He kept his family alliances locked strictly within his own provincial loyalists and the southern O'Br
2 CONC ien lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://homepages.rootsweb.com/~cousin/html/p322.htm#i14594
2 CONT
