2 GIVN Niall
2 NSFX 1st of Barra, 21st Chief of Clan MacNeil
2 _MARNM MacNeil
1 NAME Niall MacNeil 1st of Barra, 21st Chief of MacNeil
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1029
2 PLAC Fort, Grianan of Aileach
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Ireland
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1047
2 PLAC Isle of Barra 
2 ADDR
3 CITY Inverness
3 STAE Inverness-shire
3 CTRY Scotland
1 OCCU
1 TITL Prince
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Niall,Prince of Argyll and the Norse Council of the Isles, was a descendent of Aodh O'Neil, a king of Northern Ireland at the beginning of the eleventh century. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Niall came to the island of Barra in the Outer Hebrides around 1094. Barra itself is thought to take its name either from St. Fionnbharr, the founder of Cork, or from St. Barr, the great-grandson of Niall of the Nine Hostages - a 
2 CONC famous king of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the 21st Chief of Clan MacNeil, Niall of the Castle, coming to Barra around 1030, when he began construction of Kisimul Castle in Castlebay. Kisimul Castle is the ancestral seat of the MacNeils. Skene says "Tradition unques
2 CONC tionably points to Barra as now chief of the clan, and in this family the right to the chiefship probably exists, although the extreme distance of his possessions, which he appears from the first charter of Barra to have obtained 
2 CONC in consequence of a marriage with an heiress of the Macleans from the rest, led many of them to follow the Macneills of Gigha, and made the latter family almost independent." 
2 CONT
2 CONT For centuries the MacNeil clan based on the Hebridean island of Barra have proudly claimed to be descendants of Ireland's "greatest" King, Niall of the Nine Hostages.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Recent DNA samples show that the Barra MacNeil / McNeil / MacNeill Clan does not have/never had a genetic link to Neil of the Nine Hostages or the O'Neill's of Ireland. It is important that all reputed Barra men wherever they are 
2 CONC living in the world....have their DNA tested and be prepared for the results. All previous generations until now had been led to believe that ancient connection to the past is through Ireland.....and DNA shows otherwise. It was a 
2 CONC stunning revelation when my MacNeill DNA test came back. Every Barra MacNeil descended from the old line of chiefs who has taken the test as prescribed by the clan has been the same. We are Viking Scots...not Celtic Scots. Any Cla
2 CONC nsmen wishing to know more about DNA testing can contact Vince MacNeil of the Canadian Clan MacNeil assoc. in Cape Breton, Canada. [E-mail from Iain Mcneill, Canada rec: 7 Jan 2017]
2 CONT
2 CONT But a check on hundreds of modern day MacNeils has revealed their roots actually lie with the Vikings and not the Irish.
2 CONT
2 CONT DNA swabs taken from Barra MacNeils as far away as Canada and Australia have proved that the blood of fierce Norse raiders runs through their veins.
2 CONT
2 CONT The finding comes from the MacNeil Surname Y-DNA project run by genealogists Vincent MacNeil and Alex Buchanan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clansmen from all over the world including Scotland, the US, Canada, New Zealand and Australia have provided DNA samples.
2 CONT
2 CONT MacNeil remains the main surname on Barra on the southern tip of the Outer Hebrides with a population of just 1,000.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clansmen believed they descended from Niall of the Nine Hostages through an 11th century Irish prince who emigrated to Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT But the DNA project has not found a single match to Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT "We can say we can re-write the history of the Clan MacNeil," said genealogist Vincent MacNeil, from Nova Scotia, Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT "We don't have one participant from Barra that matches the O'Neills of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT "If you look at the history of the Clan MacNeils we are probably of Norse descent. We have legends and myths that have been passed through generations.
2 CONT
2 CONT "But mother nature knows who we are. Oral history is wonderful and often there is truth in it. But everybody's family history is in their DNA."
2 CONT
2 CONT The clan was infamous throughout Scotland and beyond for its Viking-style pirating and great seamanship.
2 CONT
2 CONT MacNeils raided the seas from their base at Kisimul Castle in Birlinn vessels - boats similar to the Viking longships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Western Isles MP, Angus MacNeil, who also lives on Barra, said: "The MacNeils were a notoriously pirating clan. It's no surprise we have Norse DNA.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Maybe we are the last Vikings."
2 CONT
2 CONT The MP added: "'Conquer or die' is the clan motto. Given the size of the island we ended up on we must have been better at the dying then the conquering."
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul McNeil, a 70-year-old clansman, from Washington state, said he was devastated when he got his DNA results.
2 CONT
2 CONT He said: "I nervously awaited the results, and was emotionally devastated when we received them."
2 CONT
2 CONT The college teacher added: "A heavy workload and a bottle of whiskey after work, helped me to get over it in a matter of weeks."
2 CONT
2 CONT "I found solace in the fact that, if not a Celt, I am nevertheless a Gael.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael MacNeil, 62, from Nova Scotia, Canada, said: "It wasn't what I expected."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Aerospace engineer, whose family emigrated from Barra seven generations ago, continued: "I'm pretty good with being of Viking descent. I have no problems at all. You are what you are."
2 CONT
2 CONT Calum MacNeil, a retired fisherman who lives near Castlebay on the Isle of Barra, said: "I knew that anyway but I didn't want to tell anybody."
2 CONT
2 CONT Niall of the Nine Hostages, whose dynasty dominated Ireland between the 5th and 10th Centuries, received his name from taking hostages as a strategy against his opponent chieftains.
2 CONT
2 CONT The King, who died in 405AD, was the founder of the longest and most powerful Irish royal dynasty and known by some as the greatest king that Ireland ever knew.  
2 CONT
