1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Flaithbheartach Locha Feadha O'Neill (also recorded as Flathartach "an Locha Feadha", Flahertach, or Flaithbertach Ua Néill) was born circa 1050–1075. His traditional death date is recorded in genealogical tracks a
2 CONC s 1122. His epithet "Locha Feadha" translates to "of the Lake of the Rushes" or "of Lough Foyle", which signifies the territorial water stronghold, fortress, or crannog where his branch of the family maintained their primary seat 
2 CONC of power in Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Domhnall an t-Ógdhamh O'Neill (also known in bardic poetry as Domhnall na ndamh), the anchor of the Royal Ulster line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Wives: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the father of Conchabhair na Fiadhbhuidhe O'Neill (Conchobar "of the Yellow/Deer Woods"), who was his direct biological son, successor, and future King of Ulster (d. 1170).
2 CONT
2 CONT Flaithbheartach's historical significance is defined by his father's geopolitical decision. While his uncle Aodh Anrothan migrated to Scotland to anchor the Highland Clans (MacNeil, Lamont, MacSweeney), Flaithbheartach’s line rema
2 CONC ined firmly rooted in Ireland, preserving the supreme patrilineal trunk of the Northern Uí Néill. He lived during a massive transitional period in Irish history. His generation saw the traditional kingship of Ailech begin to fract
2 CONC ure and shift south, paving the way for his descendants to firmly establish the localized Kingdom of Tír Eoghain (Tyrone)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
