1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of the wife of William de Burgh is Unknown, but she was said to be the daughter of Domnall Mór Ua Briain, King of Limerick. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ Richardson, Douglas. Royal Ancestry. Vol. II, 2013, p. 14.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mór_N%C3%AD_Briain
2 CONT
2 CONT Mór Ní Briain was Queen of Connacht, and died 1218. She was a daughter of King Domnall Mór Ua Briain of Thomond (died 1194). Domnall's wife was Órlaith Ní Murchadha, Princess of Leinster, daughter of Queen Mor Ui Thuathail and Kin
2 CONC g Diarmaid Mac Murchadha of Leinster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mór's siblings included:
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess ('''Ní Briain''') of Thomond who married William de Burgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT DOI: https://doi.org/10.3318/dib.001146.v1
2 CONT Originally published October 2009 as part of the Dictionary of Irish Biography
2 CONT Last revised October 2009
2 CONT
2 CONT Burgh, William de (a.1164–1206), magnate, brother of Hubert de Burgh, earl of Kent and justiciar of England, was one of the new wave of Anglo-Normans to come to Ireland with John (qv), lord of Ireland, in 1185. In some earlier bio
2 CONC graphies he is confused with William fitz Audelin (qv), the king's agent in Ireland. …
2 CONT
2 CONT His main task was to guard against the O'Briens; indeed he is generally believed to have married the daughter of Domnall Mór Ua Briain (qv) sometime before 1193. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Referenced on her father's Wikipedia 
2 CONT
