1 NOTE RBS : Also called Dermot MacMurrough. <li>KINSELLA.ORG : Dermot MacMurrough (or Dermod naNGhall, meaning Dermot, king of the Foreigners) was born in 1110 AD. At an early age he was fostered out to a minor family on the border of L
2 CONC einster, in the neighboring state of Ossory and here he grew to manhood. At age 16, upon the unexpected death of his older brother (the king of Leinster), he was elected king of Ui Cinnsealaigh. What followed was the turning point
2 CONC  in Irish history. <p> Upon receiving the kingship, Dermot also became king of Leinster, like his brother before him. This the High King of Ireland, Turlough O'Connor of Connaught, opposed, so he sent a neighboring chieftain to su
2 CONC bjugate Leinster--Tiernan O'Rourke, a man who loved battle. Among the three sacred laws of Ireland, the one called Daire's Law specifically forbade the killing of cattle by an enemy for by killing cattle, you were forcing the peop
2 CONC le of that land to starve because dairy products were their sole food source. O'Rourke killed the cows of Leinster. <p> It took years for Dermot to regain the throne of Leinster, but finally, by 1133 he had succeeded and now began
2 CONC  to expand his power. He raided in Ossory, then sacked Waterford, like his great-grandfather, Dermot Mac Mal namBo, before him. The following two decades were mostly peaceful for Dermot, he avoided many of the wars the other four 
2 CONC provinces were waging on one another, but in 1152, he helped Turlough O'Connor raid Tiernan O'Rourke's land. <p> After O'Rourke's land was destroyed and his castle burned, the armies left for their respective provinces. As Dermot 
2 CONC was traveling through Meath to return to Leinster, the King of Meath told him that Dervorgilla, O'Rourke's wife and also the King of Meath's sister, would run away with him. Dermot turned around and picked up Dervorgilla with all 
2 CONC her furniture and cows, then returned home. When O'Rourke discovered his wife had been taken, he was furious. <p> This 'abduction' made Dermot Tiernan O'Rourke's most bitter enemy. After only a year, Dermot was forced to give Derv
2 CONC orgilla back, but O'Rourke never forgave. In 1166, when Ireland was ablaze in war and Dermot's ally, the High King Muirchertach O'Lochlainn, had fallen, O'Rourke joined together a number of other chieftains and raided Leinster...O
2 CONC 'Rourke for revenge, the rest for plunder. Dermot barely escaped with his life and sailed for England. Dermot was not through. <p> MacMurrough gathered a force of Norman and Welsh fighting men and returned to Ireland. In quick suc
2 CONC cession he defeated Ossory, Waterford, and then Dublin, so reclaiming the kingship of Leinster, but he was not satisfied. He marched on the High King of Ireland, Rory O'Connor (Turlough's son), and demanded the High King's submiss
2 CONC ion. Dermot gambled that Rory would not hurt the Leinster hostages he had, Dermot's son and nephew. Rory hesitated, then O'Rourke forced his hand. <p> The bodies of Dermot's son, Conor, and nephew were delivered to him in a sack l
2 CONC ike a bullock would be delivered to market. Dermot lost the will to fight. His army disbanded and he returned to Ferns where, a few months later, he died. <p> There is much written about the cruelty and savageness of Dermot, yet I
2 CONC  have looked at the original sources. The sources that vilify Dermot were all written years after the Norman invasion, when Ireland had learned to fear and hate the foreigners. Those written while Dermot was alive portray him as a
2 CONC  peaceful, just king (in comparison with others of his time).
1 NOTE !Title; King of Leinster.
1 NOTE !Title; King of Leinster.
1 NOTE !Title; King of Leinster.
1 NOTE !Title; King of Leinster.
1 NOTE Diarmaid Mac Murchadha, King of Leinster, by whom a trembling sod was made of all Ireland, -after having brought over the Saxons, after having done extensive injuries to the Irish, after plundering and burning many churches, as Ce
2 CONC anannus, Cluain-Iraird, &c.,-died before the end of a year after this plundering, of an insufferable and unknown disease; for he became putrid while living, through the miracle of God, Colum-Cille, and Finnen, and the other saints
2 CONC  of Ireland, whose churches he had profaned and burned some time before; and he died at Fearnamor, without making a will, without penance, without the body of Christ, without unction, as his evil deeds deserved.
2 CONT
2 CONT  - Annals of the Four Masters (1171)
1 NOTE OR "DIARMAIT MACMURCHADA"; KING OF LEINSTER 1135; HAD SEVERAL WIVES
1 NOTE 27th great grandfather
1 NOTE He asked Richard Strongbow de Clare (and others including William Gerard, etc) to help him in Ireland.  That was the beginning of the Norman Conquest of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT slain by Donal O'Brien and the Danes at Dublin, Ire.
1 NOTE Source: "The Rufus Parks Pedigree" by Brian J.L. Berry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 80 Chart:  Dermont MacMurrough
2 CONT
