2 CONT He appears in all the old genealogies as "Y Gwion," but this is essentially meaningless; Darrell Wolcott considers it to be a corruption of "y wyrion," of "grandson."  In that case, it would mean that a grandfather was still alive
2 CONC  when he was born and named (an unusual occurrence), and since his paternal grandfather was Ithel, Wolcott believes that to be his name.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded to Ial and Ystrad Alun, but lost most of his lands to Gruffudd Maelor ap Madog ap Maredudd, king of northern Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Darrell Wolcott, "The Pedigree of Cynddelw Gam," http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id94.html for untangling and clarification of these lines. (January 17, 2016, Anne Brannen, curator)
