1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The notes in this section make it clear how problematic this profile is.  There was indeed a Llewelyn ab Owain, but he could not have been the son of Owain ap Cadwgan and Nest verch Rhys, the parents he became iden
2 CONC tified with.  Darrell Wolcott, working with land accounts, genealogies, and histories, identifies him as Llewelyn ab Owain ap Trahaearn.  Please see the links below.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, https://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/5210/Bleddyn%20ap%20Cynfyn%2046.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (June 19, 2017; Anne Brannen, curator) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blinded in 1130 by Maredudd ap Bleddyn: Please see Darrell Wolcott: The "Sons" of Owain ap Cadwgan ap Bleddyn; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id159.html. (Steven Ferry, August 15, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrel Wolcott: The Family of Trahaearn ap Caradog; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id240.html. (Steven Ferry, August 29, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Father certain (at least as far as given name goes), mother uncertain. Believed to have been got on Nest verch Rhys, then the wife of Gerald of Windsor, while Owain ap Cadwgan had her in captivity.
2 CONT
2 CONT He did not have a brother named Einion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancientwalesstudies.org thinks he is either Fictional or misidentified - possibly a son of Owain ap Trahearn. http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id159.html (The descendants of Trahearn had a bad case of fratricide, and every singl
2 CONC e one was either killed or blinded by 1132. Llywelyn ab Owain ap Trahearn needed a seeing-eye dog after 1130, assuming he survived the "operation".)
2 CONT
2 CONT The reason for blinding someone was that it took him out of the succession - the ancient Welsh, like the ancient Irish, required physical perfection in their leaders. (The early Rurikids had something similar going on, with the tw
2 CONC ist that the blind man was generally clapped into a monastery to make sure of him.)
