1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Richard Mór de Burgh (c. 1194 – 1243) ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Mór de Burgh, an Anglo-Norman aristocrat, was the eldest son of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Burgh William de Burgh] (d. 1206), who had been a central figure in the [https://yourirish.com/history/medieval/norm
2 CONC an-invasion Anglo-Norman conquest of Ireland].
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard served as the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seneschal seneschal] of Munster and the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_governor_of_Ireland Justiciar of Ireland]  from 1228 to 1232 and held the title [https://en.wikipedi
2 CONC a.org/wiki/Lord_of_Connaught Lord of Connaught] (Irish: ''Tighearna Connacht''). He was also the founder of the towns of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballinasloe Ballinasloe], [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loughrea Loughrea], a
2 CONC nd [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galway Galway].
2 CONT
2 CONT === Historical Title and Status ===
2 CONT
2 CONT While some genealogical sources refer to him as the "1st Baron of Connaught," this is an anachronistic title that does not reflect 13th-century peerage structures. Richard’s power was derived from land grants and royal authority r
2 CONC ather than a formal baronial patent. He is accurately identified in contemporary annals and modern prosopography simply as the '''Lord of Connaught''', a title reflecting his actual territorial influence and authority in the regio
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Claim and Conquest of Connacht ===
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1224, Richard asserted a claim to the land of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Connacht Connacht], which had been granted to his father but never formally transferred. He argued that the grant to the native king, Cathal Crobdearg
2 CONC  Ua Conchobair, was conditional on faithful service, and that his son, Aedh mac Cathal Crobdearg Ua Conchobair, had forfeited it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Supported by the Justiciar, Hubert de Burgh, Richard was officially awarded Connacht in May 1227. He summoned a feudal host of Norman barons in 1235 to expel the native king, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felim_O%27Connor_(d._126
2 CONC 5) Felim mac Cathal Crobderg Ua Conchobair].  Following the conquest, he became the de facto Lord of Connacht, while Felim was forced into a subordinate position, renting the five Royal cantreds of Athlone from the Crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Political Spheres: Ireland and Britain ===
2 CONT
2 CONT While Richard Mór is defined by his administrative and military governance in Ireland, his life was inherently tied to the Anglo-Norman political sphere in Britain. Following his father’s death in 1206, he spent his youth as a war
2 CONC d of the Crown in England, gaining exposure to the royal court. Throughout his career, he frequently traveled between his Irish estates and the English court to secure royal favor, renew his land grants, and attend to political ob
2 CONC ligations with the Justiciar, Hubert de Burgh. His final military commitment in 1242, where he was summoned by King Henry III to join a campaign in Poitou, underscores his active, ongoing presence within the broader political and 
2 CONC military framework of the Angevin Empire beyond the borders of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Death and Burial ===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death:''' Richard Mór de Burgh died in early 1243 while traveling by sea to France to join King Henry III’s military campaign in Poitou. While February 17, 1243, is often cited as his death date, this is actually the date the E
2 CONC nglish Crown issued an official order to take his lands into royal custody, meaning he had died shortly before this date.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Burial:''' There is no reliable historical record stating where he was buried. Some modern sources suggest he was laid to rest at the Dominican Priory in Athenry, but this is a retrospective attribution. There is no proof he wa
2 CONC s buried there, and his final resting place remains unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Family and Successors ===
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy, Richard and his wife, Egidia de Lacy, (married before 21 April 1225), had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Daughter''' (married Hamon de Valognes)
2 CONT * '''Alice de Burgh'''
2 CONT * '''Margery de Burgh''' (married Theobald Butler)
2 CONT * '''Daughter''' (married Gerald de Prendergast)
2 CONT * '''Richard de Burgh''' (died 1248, successor)
2 CONT * '''Walter de Burgh''' (1st Earl of Ulster)
2 CONT * '''William de Burgh''' (died 1270)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Maternal Lineage:''' The identity of Richard’s mother is not established in contemporary archival records. While late medieval genealogies—such as those by Cú Choigcríche Ó Cléirigh—suggest she was a daughter of Domnall Mór Ua 
2 CONC Briain, King of Thomond, this remains unverified.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birth Information:''' Neither the date nor the location of Richard’s birth is documented in primary 12th or 13th-century sources. Claims placing his birth at Galway Castle are historically impossible, as the site was not under 
2 CONC his control until 1232.
2 CONT
2 CONT >''Curatorial Note'': Claims associating Richard’s mother with an illegitimate daughter of the English King Henry II or Richard I, or those identifying an individual named [https://www.geni.com/people/Hodierna-de-Gernon/6000000008
2 CONC 248298733 "Hodierna de Burgh/Gernon"] as his wife, lack primary source support. Such profiles are considered genealogical fabrications or anachronistic constructs. They have been detached from this lineage in accordance with the G
2 CONC enealogical Proof Standard to maintain archival integrity.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been suggested that Richard had an earlier wife, "Agnes or Una O'Connor".[33]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
