2 CONT * Wikipedia: ''William de Burgh'' (Reference for paternal lineage context). < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Burgh link] >
2 CONT * WikiTree contributors, "Richard (Burgh) de Burgh (abt.1193-abt.1243)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Burgh-18 : accessed 18 June 2026).
2 CONT * “The Anglo-Norman Invasion of Ireland: Conquest and Transformation” < [https://yourirish.com/history/medieval/norman-invasion yourirish.com/history/medieval] > …. Many of the Anglo-Norman lords, such as the Butlers of Ormond, th
2 CONC e Fitzgeralds of Kildare, and the Burkes of Connacht, became dominant figures in Irish politics. Over time, these families established their own power bases, often adopting Irish customs, intermarrying with the Gaelic elite, and r
2 CONC uling their lands with significant autonomy from the English Crown …
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_governor_of_Ireland “Chief governor"] is an umbrella term favoured by eighteenth-century historians Walter Harris and John Lodge and subsequently used by many historians and statutes.[1] It w
2 CONC as occasionally used before then.[nb 1] Chief governors were appointed under various titles, the most common of which were: (Chief) justiciar (13th–14th centuries), Latin: capitalis justiciarius[4]
