1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Career info below is about  Hughs 2nd  father, Hugh the First. Hugh 2nd was First Earl of Ulster, assisted in capturing the great John de Courcy, and was also part of the Albigensian Crusade in the Languedoc agains
2 CONC t the Cathars with his brother. Irish Annals state he was hiding in a monastery instead of beseiging towns in Languedoc southern France area. This fact is recent rediscovered history. He was born in Meath, not in Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT From The Cathar Wars website:
2 CONT Comte (Earl) Hugues de Lacy [Ireland] (1176-1242)
2 CONT Hugh de Lacy, Earl of Ulster from 1205.
2 CONT Matthew Paris gives a different blazon: "vert, a bordure or".
2 CONT Hugh fought at Béziers 1209, Carcassonne 1209, Castel 1211, Beaucaire 1216, Toulouse 1218. Simon de Montfort granted him the lordships of Castelnaudary and Laurac.
2 CONT Source: Canso de la crozada - Guilhem [Guillaume or William] de Tudèle
2 CONT
2 CONT Duffy, P. 2014 Ung Sage et Valent Home – Hugh de Lacy and the Albigensian Crusade. Journal of the Royal Society of Antiquaries of Ireland 141 (2011), 66–90.
2 CONT By Paul Duffy
2 CONT 2014, Journal of the Royal Society of Antiquaries of Ireland vol 141
2 CONT Medieval History,
2 CONT Medieval French Literature,
2 CONT Albigensian Crusade
2 CONT Following his expulsion from Ireland by King John in 1210, Hugh de Lacy spent thirteen years in exile before returning to invade parts of Meath and his former earldom of Ulster. During his absence he is known to have been in Franc
2 CONC e. This period of de Lacy’s life has largely been ignored by Irish historians. Examining the career of Hugh de Lacy in the years of his exile from Ireland, this paper focuses on his pivotal role in the
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternative Birthplace:  Ulster, Eire, Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_de_Lacy,_1st_Earl_of_Ulster
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hugh de Lacy, 1st Earl of Ulster''' (~1176 – after December 26, 1242) was the younger '''son of Hugh de Lacy, Lord of Meath.''' He was created Earl of Ulster in 1205 by King John of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Career===
2 CONT
2 CONT He erected a motte in the 1180s in Carlow, on the site of which Carlow Castle was built in the 13th century.[1] Excavations at Carlow Castle in 1996, found the remains of a series of post-holes inside a curving ditch, running unde
2 CONC r the walls of the towered keep and therefore pre-dating it. The remains of a corn-drying kiln were found to the north of this. These features were interpreted as representing the remains of the first castle here, whose defences a
2 CONC nd buildings seem to have been constructed of earth and timber. A reinterpretation of the historical sources suggests that this primary timber castle was built in the early 1180s by Hugh de Lacy for John de Clahull.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1199, King John of England authorized de Lacy to wage war on John de Courcy. Hugh captured de Courcy in 1204.[3] An account of his capture appears in the Book of Howth. He granted Drogheda its charter. He continued the conquest
2 CONC  of the north-eastern over-kingdom of Ulaid as part of the Norman invasion of Ireland in the 12th century, following de Courcy's success of De Courcy. The Earldom of Ulster was based around the modern counties of Antrim and Down.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Emmeline de Riddlesford, the daughter of Walter de Riddlesford about 1242. They had no issue. It was Emmeline's second marriage. Her first husband was Stephen Longespee, grandson of Henry II of England, by whom she had 
2 CONC two daughters.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT The earldom became extinct at de Lacy's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References===
2 CONT
