2 CONC ocuments/view?doc_id=6000000173393013907 & Cynddelw ab Einion 1; https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173383294894 & Llawrodd Dyfed; https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173393259857 & Rhydderch ap Tewdwr
2 CONC  1 https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173392689930 (Steven Ferry, August 16, 2025.)
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhys_ap_Gruffydd 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was under fourteen in 1136, so was left home when Gwenllian rode out to war, and therefore survived, as his older brothers did not.  He became a skilled warrior and diplomat; Henry II made him the justiciar of South Wales in 11
2 CONC 72.  The title which translates to "The Lord Rhys" was not used in his lifetime, and does not appear in the Brut.
2 CONT
2 CONT The "greatness" of his rule can in large part be attributed to his ability to not only fight the Anglo-Normans, but make treaties with them, an unusual gift.  His agreement with King Henry II (1171), which affirmed his independent
2 CONC  status under the Crown, lasted until Henry's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1197 Rhys died unexpectedly and was buried in St David's Cathedral. The chronicler of Brut y Tywysogion records for 1197: “ 	... there was a great pestilence throughout the island of Britain ... and that tempest killed in
2 CONC numerable people and many of the nobility and many princes, and spared none. That year, four days before May Day, died Rhys ap Gruffydd, Prince of Deheubarth and unconquered head of all Wales." Jones, T., ed. 1941. Brut y Tywysogi
2 CONC on: Peniarth MS. 20. University of Wales Press, p. 138.
2 CONT
2 CONT *********************************************
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Lord Rhys''', in Welsh '''Yr Arglwydd Rhys'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhys had at least nine sons and eight daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three of the sons were named Maredudd 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd ap Rhys II (died 1201) was the eldest legitimate son. He married Matilda de Braose, the daughter of Maud [or William] de Braose.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maelgwn ap Rhys (died 1231), who was the eldest son but illegitimate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhys Gryg (died 1233) married a daughter of the Earl of Clare. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hywel ap Rhys (died 1231) became known as Hywel Sais (Hywel the Saxon, i.e. Englishman). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maredudd ap Rhys (died 1239) became known as Maredudd Ddall (Maredudd the Blind). He ended his days as a monk at Whitland Abbey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Another Maredudd (died 1227) became Archdeacon of Cardigan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwenllian ferch Rhys married Rhodri ab Owain, prince of the western part of Gwynedd. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Another Gwenllian (died 1236) married Ednyfed Fychan
2 CONT
2 CONT Angharad ferch Rhys married William FitzMartin, lord of Cemais. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other daughters married the Welsh rulers of Gwrtheyrnion and Elfael.
2 CONT --------------------
