1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard de Burgh was born about 1230 in Galway Castle, County Mayo, Ireland, the son of Richard de Burgh and Egidia Lacy. In 1232, his father, Richard de Burgh, had captured the original castle built by Turlough ‎Mór O'Conor, and 
2 CONC rebuilt it in the Norman style. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard was a minor when his father died. In consequence Richard was made a Ward of Court. He succeeded his father as Lord of Connaught. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Richard de Burgh, was Constable of Montgomery Castle in Powys, Wales, built in 1223 by Hubert de Burgh, Justiciar, and Earl of Kent.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Alice, said to be a relative of Eleanor of Provence. Following Richard's death, Alice received a dower of all the lands her mother-in-law, Egidia, had been endowed. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard died in 1248. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death of Richard de Burgh 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Burgh-18
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annals of Connacht record that in the year 1243, A great hosting by the King of England against the King of France. Messengers came from the King desiring the Irish Galls to attend him. Richard son of William Burke went to him
2 CONC  and died on that eastern expedition.[22] Richard de Burgh and other Irish Lords had joined Maurice Fitzgerald to reinforce Henry III in Poitou. Their expedition was engaged by French ships and then separated by a storm and landed
2 CONC  in Aquitaine. [23] Richard de Burgh fell ill on the sea journey to Poitou and died in France in 1243.[3][24]
2 CONT
2 CONT Date uncertain: Inquisition Post Mortem of Richard de Burgo and assignment of dower to Egidia late the wife of the said Richard.[25]
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 March 1242/1243: Record of the extent of manors which had belonged to Richard de Burgh in Munster, made by the sheriff of that province, and lands held in dower by the Lady Egidia.[26]
2 CONT
2 CONT 12 August 1243: Mandate to Maurice Fitz Gerald, justiciary of Ireland, to take into the King's hand the castles, lands, and tenements whereof Richard de Burgh was seised when he died, and to safely keep them till further orders; s
2 CONC aving to Egidia, who was Richard's wife, her estover until dower be assigned to her.[27]
2 CONT
2 CONT 28 May 1244: Mandate to the justiciary of Ireland to cause Egidia, who was the wife of Richard de Burgh, to have her dower out of her husband's lands in the justiciary's custody, and to administer justice to her regarding her dowe
2 CONC r out of lands in the hand of others.[28]
2 CONT
2 CONT 13 June 1244 and 25 June 1245: His son Richard was under age and in the custody of the King.[29]
2 CONT
2 CONT A charter dated 22 February 1246/1247 recorded the homage of Richard son and heir of Richard de Burgh for all his father´s lands … in Ireland … saving the dower of Egidia, who was Richard´s wife.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 November 1248:[30] Alice, who was the wife of Richard son and heir of Richard de Burgh, who died in the King's service at Bordeaux, to have dower out of all the lands whereof Egidia, who was the wife of the latter, was endowed.
2 CONT
2 CONT 27 August 1249: The manor of Tristelaurent had been given as dower to Alice, widow of Richard de Burgh, by virtue of the King's mandate to assign to Alice the lands which Egidia "her mother" held in dower.[31]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/IRELAND.htm#_Toc389126191 A charter dated 22 Feb 1247 records the homage of 'Richard son and heir of Richard de Burgh for all his father´s lands…in Ireland…saving the dower of Egidia, who was Ric
2 CONC hard´s wife' FMG quoting Calendar of Documents Ireland, Vol. I, 2865, p. 428.
2 CONT * RootsWeb - Richard Mór De Burgh ( -1242) - Sir Richard de Burgh, Lord of Connaught, Constable of Montgomery Castle who died without issue, 1248.
2 CONT *note: Celt: Annales Hiberniae - the Annals of Ireland. By James Grace, of Kilkenny p:35 - 1243 Death of Gerald Fitz Maurice and of Richard de Burgh.
