1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Gilbert Marshal, 4th Earl of Pembroke''' (1194 – 27 June 1241) was the third son of William Marshal, 1st Earl of Pembroke and Isabel de Clare, 4th Countess of Pembroke, the daughter of Richard de Clare.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert acceded to the title of Earl of Pembroke on 11 June 1234, on the death of his elder brother Richard who had died childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married on 1 August 1235 in Berwick-upon-Tweed to Marjorie of Scotland (1200 - 17 November 1244), daughter of King William of Scotland. Their marriage was childless. By an unknown mistress he had one illegitimate daughter, 
2 CONC Isabel, who was married to Rhys ap Maeldon Fychan.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was accidentally killed on 27 June 1241 while in a tournament, which King Henry III had expressely forbidden, as he did not want any of his subjects killing one another in sport.] Gilbert was thrown from his horse and his foot 
2 CONC was caught in the stirrup, thus he was dragged for some distance on the ground and died from the injuries he had received. He was buried at Temple Church next to his father. His title was passed to his younger brother Walter a yea
2 CONC r after his death. Walter was not immediately confirmed as earl and Earl Marshal due to the King's anger at Walter's disobedience of royal orders as he had also attended the tournament which had killed Gilbert.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert Marshal, 4th Earl of Pembroke
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert Marshal, 4th Earl of Pembroke (1194 - 27 June 1241) was the 3rd son of William Marshal, 1st Earl of Pembroke (1146-1219) and Isabella de Clare (1172-1240), the daughter of Richard de Clare.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert acceded to the title of Earl of Pembroke on 11 June 1234, on the death of his elder brother Richard who had died childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married on 1 August 1235 in Berwick-upon-Tweed to Marjorie of Scotland (1200 - 17 November 1244), daughter of King William of Scotland. They had one daughter; Isabel Marshal, who was married to Rhys ap Maeldon Fychan.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed on 27 June 1241 while in tournament, and was buried at Temple Church next to his father. His title was passed to his younger brother Walter.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilbert_Marshal,_4th_Earl_of_Pembroke
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert Marshal, 4th Earl of Pembroke (1194 – 27 June 1241) was the third son of William Marshal, 1st Earl of Pembroke and Isabel de Clare, 4th Countess of Pembroke, the daughter of Richard de Clare.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert acceded to the title of Earl of Pembroke and Lord Marshal on 11 June 1234, on the death of his elder brother Richard who had died childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married on 1 August 1235 in Berwick-upon-Tweed to Marjorie of Scotland (1200 – 17 November 1244), daughter of King William of Scotland. Their marriage was childless. By an unknown mistress he had one illegitimate daughter, 
2 CONC Isabel, who was married to Rhys ap Maeldon Fychan.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was accidentally killed on 27 June 1241 while in a tournament at Ware, which King Henry III had expressely forbidden, as he did not want any of his subjects killing one another in sport. Gilbert was thrown from his horse and hi
2 CONC s foot was caught in the stirrup, thus he was dragged for some distance on the ground and died from the injuries he had received. He was buried at Temple Church next to his father. His title was passed to his younger brother Walte
2 CONC r a year after his death. Walter was not immediately confirmed as Earl of Pembroke and Lord Marshal due to the King's anger at Walter's disobedience of royal orders, as he had also attended the tournament which had killed Gilbert.
