2 NICK Sween
1 SEX M
1 OCCU Progenitor of Clan McSweeney
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Suibhne mac Duinnshléibhe was a late 12th-century, and early 13th-century, lord in Argyll. He does not appear in contemporary records, although his name appears in the patronymic names of two of his sons. Suibhne a
2 CONC ppears in the 16th century Leabhar Chlainne Suibhne, which documents the early history of Clann Suibhne. This account claims that he is the ancestor of Irish clan, and that he was the builder of Castle Sween in Knapdale, Argyll. S
2 CONC uibhne is said to be identical to the "Swineruo", or Suibhne Ruadh ("Suibhne the Red"), recorded in the Ane Accompt of the Genealogie of the Campbells, a 17th-century Clan Campbell genealogy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Suibhne is thought to have flourished in the late 12th century, and early 13th century.[1] He does not appear in any contemporary records, but the records of patronymic names of his sons and descendants prove his existence.[2] His
2 CONC  name, Suibhne, has been stated by some commentators to be Gaelic in origin, and by others to be Norse in origin.[3] The name itself is Gaelic, meaning "pleasant". The name was sometimes used as a Gaelic form of the Old Norse Svei
2 CONC nn, meaning "boy", "servant".[4] He is stated to have been Thane of Glassrie (Glassary) and Knapdale.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Leabhar Chlainne Suibhne is a traditional account of Clan Sweeney. It was written in Ireland, starting in about 1513. The Leabhar Chlainne Suibhne states that Suibhne was the son of Donnshléibhe, son of Aodh Aluinn, son of Anr
2 CONC adhán. The account claims that Anradhan was the younger son of https://www.geni.com/people/Aedh-%C3%93-N%C3%A9ill-Lord-of-Aileach/324782572270006412 When his brother succeeded their father, the two brothers quarrelled with each ot
2 CONC her, and Anradhán sailed to Scotland. There he conquered half of the country before making peace with the King of Scots, by marrying his daughter. Suibhne is credited within the account to have built Castle Sween. His son is named
2 CONC  as Maolmhuire an Sparáin ('Maolmhuire of the Purse').[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sellar noted that Suibhne's supposed ancestor in the Leabhar Chlainne Suibhne, Aodh Athlamhan, appears in contemporary (and independent) records, such as the Annals of Ulster. Aodh Athlamhan was King of Aileach, and succeeded his 
2 CONC father Flaithbhertach. The Annals of Ulster record that Aodh Athlamhan's died in the year 1033.[2] However, Suibhne's supposed ancestors Anrothan, Aodh Alainn and Donnshléibhe, do not appear in any contemporary sources.[2][6] Sell
2 CONC ar noted that Anradhán's supposed elder brother,[2] Domhnall an tOgdhamh ('Domhnall the Young Ox'),[5] is an ancestor of the O'Neills. According to W.D.H. Sellar, there is no reason to doubt the Leabhar Chlainne Suibhne's claim th
2 CONC at Suibhne was the eponymous ancestor of the Sweeneys, or the builder of Castle Sween.[2] Sir Iain Moncreiffe of that Ilk proposed that Anradhán married a daughter of either a King of Argyll, or a sub-King of Cowal, rather than a 
2 CONC King of Scots.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
