1 NAME Muircheartach Muighe /O'Neill/ Prince of Ulster
2 GIVN Muircheartach Muighe
2 SURN O'Neill
2 NSFX Prince of Ulster
2 _MARNM O'Neill
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 PLAC Ireland
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1160
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT Muircheartach Muighe O'Neill (also recorded as Muircheartach Muighe Line, Mortogh Muighe, or Murtagh of Moylinny) was born circa 1110–1120. His traditional death date is recorded in genealogical tracks as 1160. His epithet "Muighe
2 CONC " (or Muighe Line) refers to Moylinny, a prominent ancient plain and territorial district located in modern-day County Antrim, Ulster, where his family held paramount sway.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Tadhg Glinne O'Neill, Lord of Tyrone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Wives: Unknown (though later genealogical tracks note he married a daughter of the neighboring O'Floinn of Line lords to secure the border lands).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the father of Aodh an Macaomh Tóinleasg O'Neill (Aedh "The Lazy-Arsed Youth"), who was his direct biological son, successor, and future King of Tyrone (d. 1177).
2 CONT
2 CONT His historical significance is defined by his death in battle and the subsequent revenge it triggered. He acted as a vital pretender to the northern throne but was slain at the Battle of Magh-Luadhat in 1160 along with four of his
2 CONC  sons by the rival Lochlann Mac Lochlainn. This devastating loss temporarily broke the clan's momentum, until his surviving son Aodh grew up and assassinated his father's killer in 1160 to restore the O'Neill supremacy over Ulster
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
