2 CONT Mistresses and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT While some sources suggest that the mother of most of Llywelyn's illegitimate children was his mistress, Tangwystl Goch (c.1168-1198), Cawley [14] reports only Gruffudd as a child of Tangwystl, with the other illegitimate children
2 CONC  the issue of Llywelyn's other mistreses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gruffydd ap Llywelyn (c.1196-1244) eldest son. Mother: Tangwystl. m. Senena, Dau. Caradoc ap Thomas of Anglesey. Issue: 4 sons:
2 CONT         Llywelyn ap Gruffydd. 
2 CONT     Tegwared ap Llywelyn[36]
2 CONT     Marared ferch Llywelyn (c.1198-after 1263) m.1John de Braose of Gower, nephew of Reginald de Braose; m.2 Walter Clifford of Bronllys and Clifford.
2 CONT     Gwenllian ferch Llywelyn m. William de Lacey
2 CONT     Angharad ferch Llywelyn, Angharad may have been Joan's dau. Her illegitimacy is uncertain. See Wikipedia: Angharad ferch Llywelyn m. Maelgwn Fychan 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marital problems
2 CONT
2 CONT 1230 William de Braose, 10th Baron Abergavenny, was executed for sleeping with Llywelyn's wife. They were caught in Llwelyn's bedroom.[26][37]
2 CONT
2 CONT     Previously ... Braose allied with Llywelyn during his capture, and arranged a marriage between his daughter Isabella and Llywelyn's heir, Dafydd ap Llywelyn. This was upheld after his execution. Shortly after Braose' death, a 
2 CONC letter from Llywelyn to William's wife, Eva asks if she still wants their kid's wedding to take place ... which did happen. Meanwhile, Joan was put on house arrest, but restored to her the next year.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of Spouse
2 CONT
2 CONT 1237: Joan died, "in February, at the court of Aber, and was buried in a new cemetery on the side of the strand ... Howel, bishop of Llanelwy, had consecrated. And in honour of her, Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, built there a monaste
2 CONC ry for barefooted monks, which is called Llanvaes in Mona. And then Ieuan, earl of Caerleon, and Cynvrig, son of the lord Rhys, died. That year, there came again a cardinal from Rome to England, sent, as his legate, by pope Gregor
2 CONC y the ninth."[26] Strata Florida Abbey — council of 1238 & son Dafydd
2 CONT
2 CONT 1238
2 CONT
2 CONT     "The ensuing year, on the morrow after the feast of St. Luke the Evangelist [Oct. 19], all the princes of Wales sware fidelity to David, son of Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, at Strata Florida. And then he took, from his brother G
2 CONC ruffudd, Arwystli and Ceri and Cyveiliog and Mawddwy and Mochnant and Caereinion; leaving to him nothing but the cantrev of Lleyn itself. And then Maredudd, son of Madog, son of Gruffudd Maelor, slew his brother Gruffudd; and imme
2 CONC diately Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, divested him of his territory on that account."[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT 1240:
2 CONT
2 CONT     "Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, prince of Wales, died ... and was buried at Aberconway, after taking the habit of religion. And after him David, his son, by Joan, the daughter of king John ... reigned. The month of May following, 
2 CONC David, son of Llywelyn, having with him the barons of Wales, went to Gloucester, to do homage to the king his uncle, and to receive from him his territory lawfully. And then the English sent Walter Marshall, and an army with him, 
2 CONC to fortify Aberteivi."[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial of Llywelyn the Great
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Lee (1893), Llywelyn died 11 April 1240 at the Cisterician Abbey of Aberconwy, Arllechwedd Isaf, Caernarvonshire, Wales, where he had "taken on the habit of religion." He is buried at the abbey.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn ap Iorwerth, Prince of Aberffraw, Lord of Snowdon, died at Aberconway Abbey 11 April 1240, where he was buried.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was later reburied at Llanrwst Parish Church. Gwydir Chapel next to the Church of St. Grwst in Llanrwst, Conway, Wales, contains the empty coffin of Llywelyn the Great. He was originally buried at Aberconwy Abbey, but his coffi
2 CONC n was moved to the new abbey at Maenan when the Cistercian monks were forced to move there by Edward I.
2 CONT
2 CONT The whereabouts of Llywelyn's corpse remains a mystery. (http;//www.walesdirectory.co.uk/Towns_in_Wales/Llanrwst_Town_2.htm)
2 CONT
2 CONT         Aberconway Abbey was also known as Aberconway Abbey Parish Church and is now known as St. Mary and All Saints Church, in Conwy, Conwy County, North Wales. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes on a Name
2 CONT
2 CONT Why "ap Iorwerth-26" as the WikiTree ID? Richardson[7] and FMG/MedLands[14] both show him as Llywelyn ap Iorwerth; the Cymru project's naming guide calls for including ab, ap, or ferch in the Last Name at Birth (LNAB) field; and "
2 CONC ap" because of the way Iorwerth is pronounced:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Llywelyn ap Iorwerth
2 CONT     [ɬəˈwɛlɨ̞n][4] ap [ˈjɔrwɛrθ][38] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 	Himself 	Parents ap Llywelyn & ferch Llywelyn (see list above) 	Llywelyn ap Iorwerth 	Iorwerth ab Owain ap Gruffudd Margred ferch Madog ap Maredudd
2 CONT
2 CONT ab, ap or ferch[39]
2 CONT
2 CONT     "Ferch" actually means "daughter of", so if a woman was named "Elen ferch Llywelyn ap Gwilym", this would mean that her name was Elen, her father's name was Llywelyn, and her father's father's name was Gwilym. Properly, none o
2 CONC f these words, "ap", "ab", or "ferch", are capitalized. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     The terms come from the fact that those are the words in the Welsh anguage which mean "son of" and "daughter of". Kind of like "filius" in Latin or "...sson" in Scandinavian languages. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     The difference between "ap" and "ab" is the sound which follows it. We do the same in the English language with "a bicycle" and "an orange". The spelling and pronunciation of the indefinite article changes depending on whether
2 CONC  it preceeds a vowel sound or a consonant sound. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     In Welsh, "ap" comes before a consonant sound, thus "Einion ap Llywelyn", "Jenkin ap Gruffudd", "Llywarch ap Bran". "Ab", on the other hand, comes before a vowel sound, thus "Maredudd ab Einion", "Huw ab Owain". The reason I s
2 CONC aid "vowel sound" and not "vowel" is that in certain circumstances the initial letter "I" in Welsh can sound like a consonant. Thus "Rhys ap Iorwerth" and "Dafydd ap Ieuan". Kind of like in English where an initial "U" can sound l
2 CONC ike a consonant. We say "a university", not "an university". 
2 CONT
2 CONT     As you would expect, not everybody knows enough Welsh to handle patronyms properly, so you'll find lots of variations in the forms, even among people who write on Welsh genealogies. But the above is the correct Welsh way of do
2 CONC ing things. 
2 CONT
2 CONT What's in a name? The following "Meaning & History" information is from Behind the Name:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Iorwerth: Means "handsome lord" from Welsh ior "lord" and berth "handsome". This name is used in the Mabinogion, a collection of tales from Welsh myth, where it belongs to a son of Maredudd. This name is sometimes used as a We
2 CONC lsh form of Edward.
2 CONT     Llywelyn: Possibly a Welsh form of the old Celtic name Lugubelenus, a combination of the names of the gods LUGUS and BELENUS. Alternatively it may be derived from Welsh llyw "leader". This was the name of several Welsh rulers,
2 CONC  notably the 13th-century Llywelyn the Great who fought against England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
