0 @I6000000006984972119@ INDI
1 NAME William Liath Oughter /De Burgh/
2 GIVN William Liath Oughter
2 SURN De Burgh
2 _MARNM De Burgh
2 NICK Warden of Ireland
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 FEB 1323
2 PLAC St. Francis,Galway,Ireland
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Custos of Ireland. He won The Battle of Athenry, 1316., Warden/Custos of Ireland
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William Liath de Burgh, magnate and deputy Justiciar of Ireland, died 1324.
2 CONT
2 CONT Background[edit] De Burgh was a son of William Og de Burgh, who was killed at the Battle of Áth-an-Chip or Athankip in 1270, and a nephew of Walter de Burgh, 1st Earl of Ulster (died 1271). He was nicknamed liath, Gaelic for grey,
2 CONC  though the reasons are unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career[edit] De Burgh spent much of his life fighting on behalf of his cousin, the 2nd Earl of Ulster, first coming to notice in 1290 when he was defeated in a skirmish with Mac Coughlan.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was deputy justiciar from 1 October 1308 under Piers Gaveston, relinquishing office on 15 May 1309.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bruce Invasion of Ireland[edit] He was captured at the Battle of Connor in Ulster in 1315, when an army led by his cousin the Earl of Ulster was defeated by an Irish-Scots army led by Edward Bruce. He was sent to Scotland, his
2 CONC  release only been obtained by the earl in summer 1316, in exchange for his son, Edmund, as hostage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Athenry
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning from Scotland he was in Connacht by July 1316 and assembled "a motley army of Anglo-Norman colonists and Irish chieftains who had remained loyal to the earl and marched against Fedlimid O'Connor, who had taken advantage 
2 CONC of the chaos to lay waste to the province. On 10 August, after a particularly bloody battle at Athenry, William was victorious."
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He had married Finola Ni Briain, daughter of Brian Ruad or The MacJordan, by whom he had three sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Sir Edmond Albanach de Burgh (died 1375)
2 CONT *  John Burgh, (1350–1398) Chancellor of the University of Cambridge 
2 CONT * Sir Ulick de Burgh (died 1352)
2 CONT * Sir Walter Liath de Burgh, died February 1332 
2 CONT
2 CONT He may also have had other children- legitimate or illegitimate:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Raymond, ancestor of the Mac Raymond Burkes of Muinter Murchadha
2 CONT * Richard, ancestor of the Clan Henry, Mac Walter of Lackagh, Mac William Duinn, Mac Tibbot and Mac Meyler
2 CONT * Theobald, died 1336
2 CONT * Thomas
2 CONT * Gylle de Burgh (fl. 1332)
2 CONT * Mor, married Ruaidhri O Cellaigh who died 1339
2 CONT * Two unnamed sons, killed in Leinster in 1311
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Summation
2 CONT
2 CONT Ronan Mackay summed him up as a loyal and capable lieutenant of the Earl. "From 1305 onwards he was lord of connacht in all but name, allowing Richard to concentrate on the rest of his sprawling domains. The fact that Connacht did
2 CONC  not collapse during the Bruce invasion was primarily due to William's ability and his strong ties to many of the leading Irish families of the province. Ironically his success in building a local power base there was to lead to c
2 CONC onflict between his heirs and the next earl of Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1323 and was interred in the Dominican priory at Athenry. He is the ancestor of the Bourkes of County Mayo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Annalistic references
2 CONT
2 CONT  From the Annals of the Four Masters:
2 CONT
2 CONT M1322.11. William Liath Burke, son of William More, died. M1324.3. William Burke, son of William More, died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Added by; HRH Prince Kieren de Muire von Drakenberg
