2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT  mac Raghnaill was a 13th-century Scottish magnate. The son of Raghnall, son of Somerled, he appears to have spent his career fighting, in both Ireland and in Scotland. It has been argued that he became hostile to both the Scottis
2 CONC h and English crowns, fighting the Scottish crown in the MacWilliam revolts and dying against the English at the Battle of Ballyshannon in 1247.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT Kintyre, the probable heartland of Ruaidhri before the intrusion of Donnchadh of Argyll into the area
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh MacDonald of Sleat's 17th-century History of the Macdonalds reported a tradition that Ruaidhri's father Raghnall was married to a daughter or sister of the early 14th-century hero Thomas Randolph, Earl of Moray. Sellar sugges
2 CONC ts that his mother may have been a daughter of William fitz Duncan, on the basis that there is a possibility the tradition had confused a later and famous Earl of Moray with an earlier one.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His father Raghnall, carrying the legacy of his own father Somerled, was a powerful Argyll and Hebridean magnate who, depending on context, bore the titles "King of the Isles", "Lord of Argyll and Kintyre", and "lord of the Hebrid
2 CONC es (Inchegal).[2] His father's legacy was such that he became the ancestor figure of both the Clan Ruaidhri and the Clan Donald.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sketch of his known career
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruaidhri's heartland appears to have lain initially in Kintyre, as he bore the title "Lord of Kintyre" (dominus de Kyntire).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruaídhrí appears on record for the first time when, in 1214, he accompanied Tomás Mac Uchtraigh, son of Lochlann of Galloway, on a raid upon the Irish city of Derry:
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomás mac Uchtraigh & Ruaidhri mac Raghnaill plundered Daire completely and took the treasures of the Community of Daire and of the North of Ireland besides from out the midst of the church of the Monastery.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1212, Tomás had raided Derry with a fleet with seventy-six ships, and it is probable that Ruaídhrí was the unnamed "son of Raghnall" who had accompanied Tomás then.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT For the rest of Ruaidhri's life, there is little unambiguous evidence of his activities.[5] R. Andrew McDonald argued that Ruaidhri mac Raghnaill was the 'Roderick' who fought in the MacWilliam rebellion against King Alexander II 
2 CONC of Scotland between 1223 and 1230, a suggestion Alex Woolf called "attractive".[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT The River Erne at modern Ballyshannon, possible location of Ruaidhri's death
2 CONT
2 CONT Alex Woolf suggested further that Ruaidhri's motivations stemmed from Ruaidhri's hostility to Scottish support for Óláfr, son of Goðrøðr Óláfsson; Ruaidhri may have been allied through marriage to Goðrøðr Óláfsson's other son, Ólá
2 CONC fr's half-brother and rival Rögnvaldr, king of Mann; more to the point, Óláfr appears to have repudiated Ruaidhri's kinswoman for a daughter of Ferchar, Earl of Ross, all while King Alexander II was promoting the power of Donnchad
2 CONC h mac Dubhghaill in the west-coast of Scotland, Ruaidhri's base
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Assuming he survived the final defeat of the MacWilliams in 1230, the remainder of Ruaidhri's life is obscure. However, reporting the year 1247, the Annals of Loch Cé related that:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mac Somhairle, king of Airer-Gaeidhel, and the nobles of the Cenel-Conaill besides, were slain.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Woolf has argued that Ruaidhri was the Mac Somhairle who died in this battle, fighting the English at Ballyshannon.[9] Sellar also thought, for other reasons, that the "dead man at Ballyshannon" was Ruaidhri.[10] Macdonald believe
2 CONC d that it referred to Donnchadh mac Dubhghaill, while Duffy suggested Domhnall mac Raghnaill.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruaidhri's son Dubhghall continued his father's legacy, hostile to the Scottish crown while fighting the English in Ireland, notably killing Jordan d'Exeter off Connaught in 1258.[12] Ruaidhri's ancestors held Garmoran and much of
2 CONC  the north-west Scottish coast until the early 14th century, when the MacRuaidhri heiress Christiana married Eoin of Islay, who took Christiana's lands and became the first MacDonald Lord of the Isles.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ruaidhri Reignaldson Of Garmoran was a ruler over North Kintyre, Bute and Isles. He went by the name Roderick. Parents: King Reignald Somerledsson and Fonia Of Moray.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children were: King Dugal Ruaidhrison Of Kintyre.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ruaidhri mac Raghnaill was a 13th-century Scottish magnate. The son of Raghnall, son of Somerled, he appears to have spent his career fighting, in both Ireland and in Scotland. It has been argued that he became hostile to both the
2 CONC  Scottish and English crowns, fighting the Scottish crown in the MacWilliam revolts and dying against the English at the Battle of Ballyshannon in 1247.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT Kintyre, the probable heartland of Ruaidhri before the intrusion of Donnchadh of Argyll into the area
2 CONT
