2 CONT Roger Bigod (c. 1209 – 1270), was 4th Earl of Norfolk and Marshal of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son and heir of Hugh Bigod, 3rd Earl of Norfolk (1182-1225) by his wife Matilda, a daughter of William Marshal, 1st Earl of Pembroke (1147-1219), Marshal of England. His younger brother was Hugh Bigod (1211-1266)
2 CONC , Justiciar. After the death of his father in 1225, the young Roger became the ward of William Longespée, 3rd Earl of Salisbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1228, although still under-age but by now married and in a second wardship to the William the Lion, King of Scotland, he succeeded to his father's estates including Framlingham Castle. He did not, however, receive his father's 
2 CONC title until 1233.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death without male heirs of the last of his mother's brothers, in 1246 Roger inherited the office of Marshal of England. Together with his younger brother Hugh Bigod (1211-1266), Justiciar, he was prominent among the bar
2 CONC ons who wrested control of government from the hands of King Henry III and assisted Simon de Montfort in the Second Barons' War.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first warder married him to Isabella of Scotland, daughter of William the Lion, King of Scotland, whereupon still under-age he became a ward of his new brother-in-law, Alexander II of Scotland until 1228.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger had no children, and was succeeded by his nephew Roger Bigod, 5th Earl of Norfolk (1245-1306).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References===
2 CONT
2 CONT M. Morris, The Bigod Earls of Norfolk in the Thirteenth Century (Woodbridge, 2005)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Roger Bigod, 4th Earl of Norfolk was born on 1209 in England to Hugh Bigod, 3rd Earl of Norfolk and Maud Marshal Bigod.  Roger married Isabella of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger Bigod, 4th Earl of Norfolk is my 26th great uncle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT Roger Bigod (c. 1209-1270) was 4th Earl of Norfolk and Marshal of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son and heir of Hugh Bigod, 3rd Earl of Norfolk (1182-1225) by his wife Matilda, a daughter of William Marshal, 1st Earl of Pembroke (1147-1219), Marshal of England. His younger brother was Hugh Bigod (1211-1266)
2 CONC , Justiciar. After the death of his father in 1225, the young Roger became the ward of William Longespée, 3rd Earl of Salisbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1228, although still under-age but by now married and in a second wardship to the William the Lion, King of Scotland, he succeeded to his father's estates including Framlingham Castle. He did not, however, receive his father's 
2 CONC title until 1233.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death without male heirs of the last of his mother's brothers, in 1246 Roger inherited the office of Marshal of England. Together with his younger brother Hugh Bigod (1211-1266), Justiciar, he was prominent among the bar
2 CONC ons who wrested control of government from the hands of King Henry III and assisted Simon de Montfort in the Second Barons' War.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first warder married him to Isabella of Scotland, daughter of William the Lion, King of Scotland, whereupon still under-age he became a ward of his new brother-in-law, Alexander II of Scotland until 1228.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger had no children, and was succeeded by his nephew Roger Bigod, 5th Earl of Norfolk (1245-1306).
2 CONT
