1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Welsh Legends of Castell Dinas Bran-
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the end of the war in October 1282 and the death of Llywelyn, most of Powys Fadog including the castle was granted to John de Warenne, Earl of Surrey. Rather than rebuild Dinas Brân, De Warenne choose instead to build a 
2 CONC new castle by the River Dee at Holt on the Flintshire-Cheshire border. 
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castell_Dinas_Br%C3%A2n
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Medlands: [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL.htm#_Toc388773409 England, Earls Created 1067-1122]==== 
2 CONT >'''Note:''' The content someone copied and pasted below differs somewhat from a recent FMG update as it may in future updates. It's not appropriate to copy and paste entire swaths of content from updating websites.
2 CONT >— — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''JOHN de Warenne''' (1231 or after-Kennington [Nov] 1304, bur Lewes Priory).  The Chronicle of Tintern Abbey, Monmouthshire, names (in order) ”Johannes de Garren comes de Garren et Isabella de Aubeni soror eius et comitissa de A
2 CONC rundel” as the children of “Johanni de Garrene comiti de Surrey” and his wife Matilda Marshal of the Earls of Pembroke[1444].  He succeeded his father in 1240 as Earl of Surrey.  Henry III King of England agreed that “unam filiaru
2 CONC m filiæ...comitis [Sabaudiæ]” would marry “vel Johanni de Warenna qui si vixerit comes erit Warennæ, vel Edmundo de Lacy qui si vixerit comes erit Lincolniæ” by charter dated 1246[1445].  He was one of the guardians of the realm o
2 CONC n the death of King Henry III, until the return of Edward I from crusade.  He was appointed keeper of the realm of Scotland 3 Sep 1296, but never assumed the post as he was defeated by the Scots at the battle of Stirling[1446].  T
2 CONC he Annales Londonienses record the death "circiter festum Exaltationis Sanctæ Crucis…apud Newentone" in 1304 of "comes Warenniæ" and his burial "in ecclesia Sancti Pancratii Lewensi"[1447].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m [firstly] (Aug 1247) '''ALIX de Lusignan''', daughter of HUGUES [XI] "le Brun" Sire de Lusignan, Comte de la Marche & his wife Isabelle Ctss d’Angoulême ([1224]-1256, after 9 Feb).  She is named "Aelesia" by Matthew Paris when h
2 CONC e records her visit to England in 1247 with her brothers to her uterine half-brother King Henry III and her subsequent marriage with "Johanni comiti Warenniæ adolescenti"[1448].  Matthew Paris records her death in early 1256[1449]
2 CONC .  
2 CONT
2 CONT [m secondly ---.  No direct evidence has been found of this second marriage.  However, the Chronicle of Thomas Wykes describes John de Warenne’s daughter Isabel as “adolescentulam” at the time of her marriage in 1279.  If that is 
2 CONC correct, it appears improbable that Isabel was born from John’s known wife Alix de Lusignan who died in 1256.  In that case, she would have been born from an otherwise unrecorded second marriage of her father.  A second marriage a
2 CONC lso appears probable as John survived his known wife by nearly fifty years.]  
2 CONT
2 CONT John Earl of Surrey & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a) '''WILLIAM de Warenne''' (1256-murdered Croydon 15 Dec 1286).  He was ambushed and slain after attending a tournament at Croydon[1450].  Inquisitions following a writ dated 15 Dec "15 Edw I" following the death of "William de W
2 CONC arenna...he died on Sunday before St Lucy in the said year...Sunday after St Lucy” name “Joan his wife...Robert de Veer earl of Oxford father of theJoand joan...John his son born at the feast of the Nativity of St John the Baptist
2 CONC  14 Edw I is his next heir...aged 38 weeks on Tuesday before St Gregory in the said year”[1451].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ([Jun 1285]) JOAN de Vere, daughter of ROBERT de Vere Earl of Oxford & his wife Alice de Sanford (-23 Nov 1293 or before, bur Lewes Priory).  Inquisitions following a writ dated 15 Dec "15 Edw I" following the death of "Wi
2 CONC lliam de Warenna...he died on Sunday before St Lucy in the said year...Sunday after St Lucy” name “Joan his wife...Robert de Veer earl of Oxford father of the said joan...John his son born at the feast of the Nativity of St John t
2 CONC he Baptist 14 Edw I is his next heir...aged 38 weeks on Tuesday before St Gregory in the said year”[1452]. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT b) '''ELEANOR de Warenne''' (1251-before 30 Nov 1282, bur Sallay).  A writ dated 30 Nov "10 Edw I", after the death of "Ellen de Percy late the wife of William de Percy", names "John son of Henry de Percy aged 11 is next heir” of 
2 CONC the manor of “Donegheton” in Sussex which she held “in dower of the inheritance of the heir of Henry de Percy...which dower king Henry assigned to her after her husband’s death”[1467].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (York 8 Sep 1268) as his second wife, HENRY de Percy, son of WILLIAM de Percy & his second wife Ellen de Balliol ([1235]-29 Aug 1272, bur Sallay). 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Earl of Surrey & his [second] wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT c) '''ISABEL de Warenne''' ([1265/69]-).  The Chronicle of Thomas Wykes records the marriage “infra octavus Purificationis” in 1279 of “Johannes de Balhol” and “adolescentulam Isabellam filiam comitis Warennæ”[1468].  The word “ad
2 CONC olescentulam” suggests her birth in [1265/69].  If that is correct, it appears that Isabel must have been born from an otherwise unrecorded second marriage of her father.  This would also be more consistent with the date of death 
2 CONC of her son Edward, who would have been extremely old if born soon after his parents’ marriage.  A charter dated 27 Mar 1281 records a grant of property by "Dervergulla de Balliol" to "her son John de Balliol and the king’s cousin 
2 CONC Isabella daughter of Earl Warrenne his wife"[1469].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (before 7 Feb 1279) JOHN Balliol, son of JOHN de Balliol of Barnard Castle, co Durham & his wife Devorguilla of Galloway ([1250]-in France [either Château Gaillard, Normandy or Bailleut-en-Gouffern, Normandy] [4 Mar 1314/4
2 CONC  Jan 1315], bur [Church of St Waast, Normandy]).  He succeeded in 1292 as JOHN King of Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources''' 
2 CONT [1444] Dugdale Monasticon V, Tintern Abbey, Monmouthshire IV, In Chronicis Abbatiæ Tynterne in Wallia, p. 270.   
2 CONT [1445] Rymer (1745), Tome I, p. 155. 
2 CONT [1446] CP XII/1 503-7. 
2 CONT [1447] Annales Londonienses, p. 133. 
2 CONT [1448] Matthew Paris, Vol. IV, 1247, pp. 627 and 629. 
2 CONT [1449] Matthew Paris, Vol. V, 1256, p. 551. 
2 CONT [1450] CP XII/1 507. 
2 CONT [1451] Inquisitions Post Mortem, Vol. II, Edward I, 633, p. 382. 
2 CONT [1452] Inquisitions Post Mortem, Vol. II, Edward I, 633, p. 382.
2 CONT [1467] Inquisitions Post Mortem, Vol. II, Edward I, 434, p. 248. 
2 CONT [1468] Luard, H. R. (ed.) (1869) Annales Monastici Vol. IV, Annales de Oseneia, Chronicon Thomæ Wykes, Annales de Wigornia (London)Thomas Wykes, p. 284.
2 CONT [1469] Bain, J. (1884) Calendar of Documents relating to Scotland preserved in the Public Record Office (Scottish Record Office) ("Calendar of Documents Scotland (Bain)"), Vol. II, 189, p. 60. 
2 CONT =----------------------------------------------------=
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00015385&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT "John de Warenne, 7th Earl of Surrey (1231 â c. 29 September 1304) was a prominent English nobleman and military commander during the reigns of Henry III of England and Edward I of England. During the Second Barons' War he switc
2 CONC hed sides twice, ending up in support of the king, for whose capture he was present at Lewes in 1264. Warenne was later appointed a Guardian of Scotland and featured prominently in Edward I's wars in Scotland."
2 CONT
2 CONT However, he fought for Scotland in the Battle of Stirling Bridge in September, 1297, along with Sir John "The Red" Comyn (III), Lord of Badenoch
2 CONT
2 CONT ===========
2 CONT
2 CONT Warenne died on 29 September 1304 in Kennington, Kent. He was interred in Lewes Priory at a service conducted by the Archbishop of Canterbury. He was succeeded by his grandson, also called John.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Warenne and Alice de Lusignan had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Eleanor, who married Henry Percy and was the mother of Henry de Percy, 1st Baron Percy of Alnwick; (See Percy Family)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Isabella, who married John Balliol (also called John II Baiol) and was the mother of Edward Balliol;
2 CONT
2 CONT * William, who married Joan, daughter of Robert de Vere, 5th Earl of Oxford, and was accidentally killed at a tournament on 15 December 1286. Their son John succeeded his grandfather as earl of Surrey; their daughter Alice de Ware
2 CONC nne married Edmund Fitzalan, 9th Earl of Arundel.
2 CONT ==================================================================================
2 CONT
2 CONT New: Confusingly, there are a few references at the beginning of the 1300s to John and William, sons of John, earl of Warenne, both of whom had been, according to letters of the pope, ordained priest while still under age. [8] As 
2 CONC our Earl John (the 7th) was only born in 1286, these two must have been the illegitimate sons of his grandfather John de Warenne (1231-1304), the previous earl of Surrey and Warenne, and thus John's uncles. His sons John and Thoma
2 CONC s had both joined the order of the Hospital of St John of Jerusalem by November 1345, and their mother Maud Nerford was dead by then. [9] Neither of them appeared in their father's will.
2 CONT Source: Kathryn Warner, holds a BA and an MA with Distinction in Medieval History and Literature.
2 CONT Link to her long list of resources: http://edwardthesecond.blogspot.com/2009/09/illegitimate-children-of-john-de.html
2 CONT
