1 NOTE William held lands of Earl Richard in Hampshire and Norfolk for the service of a knight's fee.  In June 1258, during a baronial reform program, William was granted custody of Winchester castle.  A month later he died, reportedly b
2 CONC y poison administered by the Earl Richard's seneschal [an official in a medieval noble household in charge of domestic arrangements and the administration of servants; asteward or major-domo, Middle English, from Old French, of Ge
2 CONC rmanic origin], Walter de Scoteny, in supposed collaboration with Henry III's Poitevin half-brothers, who strongly opposed the baronial program and Earl Richard's participation in it.
