2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Nicolas fitzMaurice fitzThomas, 3rd Baron of Kerry
2 CONT
2 CONT Died in A.D. 132486 or 1332 and was buried at the Monastery in Ardfert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolas (3rd Baron) was the son of Maurice FitzMaurice (2nd Baron)79. The fact that Nicolas was not given the name of his paternal grandfather suggests that Nicolas had an older brother (named Thomas) who died in childhood. The mo
2 CONC ther of Nicolas was either Mary, daughter of Sir John MacLeod, or Elena, daughter of William FitzElie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolas was knighted at Adair for assisting John, Lord Offaly, to suppress a rising in Munster. He made several grants of land for religious purposes, and built the Leper House at Ardfert, as well as the castles of Portrinanda and
2 CONC  Ardfert. He also erected a stone bridge at Lixnaw, and made roads to that place86.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolas (3rd Baron) married Mary, daughter of O'Brien of Thomond, and had at least two sons and one daughter79:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Maurice, 4th Baron.
2 CONT     John, 5th Baron.
2 CONT     Eleanor, who married Maurice FitzThomas, Earl of Desmond. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Cusack86, Nicolas (3rd Baron) had three sons and six daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Desideria
2 CONT
2 CONT Desideria was a member of the FitzMaurice family who was buried at the Franciscan Monastery in Ardfert in A.D. 1345 33.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last king of the Lombards (773 or 774) was named Desiderius. As his descendants intermarried with the various aristocratic families of Italy, "Desiderius" and "Desideria" gained wide usage as names in aristocratic families.
2 CONT
2 CONT This leads me to suspect that the Desideria who died in A.D. 1345 was the sister of Sir Nicolas FitzMaurice. A mother who would name her sons (Nicholas and Peter) after almost forgotten Russian ancestors in her Clan MacLeod would 
2 CONC plausibly name her daughter after an almost forgotten Lombard or Tuscan ancestor of her husband. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://fitzmaurice-family.com/irish.html
