1 NOTE Hugh became Earl of Ross in 1322. He was made Sheriff of Cromarty, and received royal charters from King Robert I (Robert the Bruce) to the lands of Rarichies, the Isle of Skye, Strathglass, Strathconon and others.
2 CONT His first marriage (Lady Maud), produced two sons (William the next Earl of Ross and John de Ross d. 1364) and one daughter (Marjory). His second marriage (Lady Margaret Graham) produced a son (Hugh "of Rarichies" from whom the ch
2 CONC iefs of Balnagowan are descended) and two daughters (Euphemia and Janet). [King Robert II married, as his second wife, Euphemia, who was then crowned Queen of Scotland in 1371.]
2 CONT Earl Hugh  was killed in 1333 at the battle of Halidon Hill near Berwick along with many of the leading barons of Scotland. It is said that Earl Hugh wore the shirt of St. Duthac, which would render him invulnerable to the enemy. 
2 CONC The English general, William Douglas, removed the shirt from the Earl's body and returned it to the sanctuary of St. Duthac at Tain.
