1 NOTE {geni:about_me}   
2 CONT
2 CONT Aodh Mac Muirchertaigh O'Neill (historically recorded as Áed in Macáem Tóinlesc or Aodh an Macaoimh Tóinleasg) was born circa 1140–1145. His traditional death date is recorded in genealogical tracks as 1177. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His epithet "an Macaoimh Tóinleasg" translates to "The Lazy-Arsed Youth" or "The Lazy-Boned", a nickname given to him in his youth because he refused to stand up or show deference in the presence of the High King, Muirchertach Mac
2 CONC  Lochlainn.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Muircheartach Muighe O'Neill, Prince of Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Wives: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the father of 
2 CONT
2 CONT #Aodh Méith O'Neill (Aodh "The Fat"), who was his  immediate successor, and King of Tír Eoghain (d. 1230).
2 CONT
2 CONT #Niall Ruadh O'Neill (Niall "The Red"),  later succeeded his brother Aodh Méith (d. 1231)
2 CONT
2 CONT His historical significance is defined by his restoration of the dynasty. After his father was killed by the rival Mac Lochlainns, Aodh grew up to slay his father's killer, seize the throne, and reign as King of Tír Eoghain from 1
2 CONC 166 until his death. He was assassinated by Maolruanaidh Mac Lochlainn in 1177. He was the first ruler of the lineage to fully break the power of the Mac Lochlainns, permanently securing the supreme kingship of Ulster for the O'Ne
2 CONC ill family.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
