1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''COLIN Campbell (-killed in battle String of Lorne, <28 Aug 1296, bur Kilchrenan on Lochawe, church of St Peter)'''
2 CONT s/o GILLESPIC Campbell [Balfour Paul records him as "Sir Gillespic or Archibald Campbell"] & ?Efferick Carrick
2 CONT x NN
2 CONT >1.         NICOLAS Campbell (-[1304])
2 CONT >2.         NEIL Campbell (-[26 Apr 1315/1316])
2 CONT >3.         DUGAL Campbell [of the county of Perth] (->3 Aug 1323)
2 CONT >4.         ?GILLESPIC Campbell 
2 CONT >5.         [daughter x  ANGUS Macdonald Lord of the Isles (-[1296])] 
2 CONT >6.         ? Donald Campbell (-> 6 Apr 1320)
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY%20UNTITLED.htm#ColinCampbelldied1296B
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ====FMG: Scotland Untitled Nobility ''(7 Oct 2025)'' [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY%20UNTITLED.htm#_Toc100910307 "Campbell"]
2 CONT
2 CONT * "No overall reconstruction of the Campbell family in the late 13th century has been attempted, although possible family relationships, shown in Balfour Paul and the Ane Accompt, are highlighted below. As will be seen, Campbells 
2 CONC are recorded in six different areas of Scotland, which suggests the existence of several different branches of the family (assuming that they all descended from a common ancestor, which is not verifiable)."
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         GILLESPIC Campbell [of Stirlingshire] (-[1280]).  Balfour Paul records that "Sir Gillespic or Archibald Campbell or Cambel...received in 1263, or held from the Crown, the lands of Menstrie and Sauchie, then in Stirlings
2 CONC hire"[146].  The Ane accompt says that Gillespic was son of “Dugald” (son of “Duncan”) and his wife “ffindoig daughter to Nachten mcGilmorie...”, and brother of Duncan [I] who is shown below[147].  These family relationships have 
2 CONC not been corroborated.  "Gillespic Cambel" witnessed the charter dated 1266 under which Alexander III King of Scotland erected the Burgh of Newburgh[148].  Balfour Paul says that Gillespic "is said to have died about 1280"[149].  
2 CONC He cites no source, but the date is consistent with his dating of the first charter in which Gillespic’s son Colin is named.  m ---.  No primary source has been found which names Gillespic’s wife.  The Ane accompt records that he 
2 CONC married “Efferick daughter to Coline foresd of Carrick”[150].  Balfour Paul says that "there was no Colin of Carrick known to history, and no corroborative evidence has been for found for [the] marriage"[151].  Sellar, noting that
2 CONC  the marriage “has been generally disbelieved” (citing in particular Balfour Paul), highlights the existence of “Nicholas of Carrick...son of Duncan Earl of Carrick” (named in an undated charter cited in the Carrick chapter of the
2 CONC  document SCOTLAND EARLS-Creations 1162-1398, and generally assumed to be the same person as Neil Earl of Carrick) and suggests that “a mistaken assimilation of the names Nicholas and Neill has led to confusion”.  He indicates tha
2 CONC t the latinised “Nicholas” normally represents the name “Colin” (given to Gillespic’s son) and not “Neil”, which is generally rendered “Nigellus” in Latin.  Sellar concludes that “the story of Gillespic’s marriage then is feasible
2 CONC . More than that, it is probable”[152]: if he is right, he married Efferic of Carrick, daughter of [Nicholas [Colin] of Carrick & his wife ---].  However, Sellar also discusses possible Gaelic origins of the Campbell family, notin
2 CONC g in particular that Gillespic’s son Colin is probably identifiable as “Cailean Mór, from whom the style MacCailein Mór derives”[153].  Once the third language Gaelic is introduced into the problem, it is uncertain how a Gaelic-sp
2 CONC eaking 13th century monk would have translated Gaelic names into Latin.  If that is correct, Sellar’s latinisation pattern for the names Neil, Colin and Nicholas would not necessarily apply.  It is suggested that this possible ori
2 CONC gin of Gillespic’s should be treated with some caution until more source material emerges.  In addition to referring to Efferic, Balfour Paul says that Gillespic’s wife "according to Douglas” was “Margaret, daughter of William Som
2 CONC ervill baron of Carnwath", but also says that “no corroborative evidence has been found” for this marriage[154].  Gillespic & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''COLIN Campbell, son of GILLESPIC Campbell & his wife --- (-killed in battle String of Lorne [1296], before 28 Aug 1296, bur Kilchrenan on Lochawe, church of St Peter).  His parentage is confirmed by the 1293 document cited bel
2 CONC ow.  "...Domino Colino Campbell..." witnessed the undated charter (dated to “about 1281” by Balfour Paul[189]) under which "domino Johanni de Luss" swore homage for "terra de Banwrith..."[190].  Colin supported Robert Bruce’s clai
2 CONC m to the crown of Scotland in 1291[191].  "Colyn Cambel miles filius quondam dni Gylascop Kambel" guaranteed the donation to Newbattle priory made by "dni Willo de Lyndd filio quondam dni David de Lyndd" by charter dated 1293[192]
2 CONC .  Balfour Paul says that Colin Campbell was killed in [1296] "in a contest with his powerful neighbour the Lord of Lorne at…the String of Lorne" and was buried "at Kilchrenan on Lochawe, at the church of St Peter the Deacon, wher
2 CONC e his tombstone may still be seen" (no source citation)[193].  Colin presumably died before the 28 Aug 1296 Ragman Roll, in which he is not named.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT >m ---.  The name of Colin’s wife is not known.  The Ane accompt says that Colin married “Janet daughter to Sir John Sinclair [of Dunglass]”[194].  This information has not been verified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Colin & his wife had [five] children (in addition, Balfour Paul names Donald, died after 6 Apr 1320 see above, as another son): 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>1.         NICOLAS Campbell (-[1304]).  Balfour Paul records “Sir Neil” as the oldest son of Colin, noting the 17 Oct 1282 charter which names "…Nigello filio Colini Campbell…" (see below)[195].  However, that document appears t
2 CONC o refer to Neil Campbell, not Nicolas, Andrew B. W. MacEwen highlighting the separate existence of the two brothers[196].  MacEwen’s suggestion is consistent with the apparent contradiction between “Neil” having defected to Robert
2 CONC  Bruce’s Scottish cause in [1304] (see below) and the rights of Nicholas’s son and heir being respected by the English king in [1304/05].  “...Nicolaus Cambel...” is named among the auditors for Robert Bruce in the proceedings dat
2 CONC ed 5 Jun 1291[197].  Bailie of Lochawe and Ardskeodnish: King Edward I named “Nicho Cambel ballivo R de Leghor et Ardescothyn” in an order dated 10 Sep 1296[198].  A charter dated 10 Oct 1296 confirmed an undated charter (dated to
2 CONC  [1290] by Balfour Paul[199]) under which "Richard de Burgo Earl of Ulster, lord of Connaught" granted "his castle and burgh del Roo" to "James the Steward of Scotland and Egidia his wife (the earl’s sister in frank marriage" witn
2 CONC essed by "…Sir Nicholas de Chambelle"[200].  The Ragman Roll names "...Nicol Cambel...knights" among those who swore allegiance to Edward I King of England at Berwick-upon-Tweed 28 Aug 1296[201]: Nicholas signed jointly with a gro
2 CONC up which included several Scottish earls and other prominent nobles (but no other Campbells), suggesting his seniority in the Campbell family.  The following documents indicate that Nicholas died in [1304]: “John de Dovedale, to w
2 CONC hom the king has given by charter Sir Nichol Cambel’s lands in fee, as his son and heir is under age” petitioned the king to “give him the ward of the infant’s lands till majority, and his marriage”, dated to [1304][202].  An orde
2 CONC r dated 25 Oct 1305 records "Ward of Nicholas Cambel’s land and marriage of his heir committed to John de Dovedala"[203].  m ---.  No primary source has been found which names Nicholas’s wife.  Nicholas & his wife had one child:
2 CONT >>>a)         --- Campbell (-after 25 Oct 1305). This heir presumably died young. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>2.         NEIL Campbell (-[26 Apr 1315/1316]).  "…Nigello filio Colini Campbell…" witnessed the charter dated 17 Oct 1282 under which "Willelmus de Kynmonde et filius et heres Agnetis filie quondam Willelmi dicti de Ketilstoune
2 CONC " donated "terre de Badyndath" to Cambuskenneth priory[207].  As noted above, MacEwen highlights the separate existence of “Nicholas Campbell” and his brother “Neil Campbell”[208].  MacEwen suggests that the latter was illegitimat
2 CONC e (the basis for this suggestion has not been ascertained).  The 17 Oct 1282 charter suggests that Neil would have been about the same age as his brother Nicholas.  However, Neil is not recorded again in the sources for many years
2 CONC .  One explanation for the long gap could be that the earlier Neil died and was later replaced by another son who was given the same name, maybe born to a different mother.  Neil’s absence from the Ragman Roll may be explained by 
2 CONC his youth, which would be consistent with this hypothesis of two sons being named Neil. -        see below. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>3.         DUGAL Campbell [of the county of Perth] (-after 3 Aug 1323).  His parentage is confirmed by the 3 Aug 1323 charter cited below.  [The Ragman Roll names "…Dougal Cambel…Arthur Cambel…Duncan Cambel...del counte de Perth
2 CONC …" among those who swore allegiance to Edward I King of England at Berwick-upon-Tweed 28 Aug 1296, in a mixed group of people from different counties[209].  Presumably the order of their three names reflects their relative seniori
2 CONC ty in the Campbell family.  Their names being listed in the same group suggests a family relationship: if that is correct, maybe they are listed in descending order of age.  “...Dougal Cambel...” could be identified as Dugal son o
2 CONC f Colin: if that is correct, “...Arthur Cambel...Duncan Cambel” were his younger brothers or cousins as discussed above.]  Robertson indexes charters under which "Dougall Campbell" was granted "sundry lands in Argyle", and 24 Jan 
2 CONC 1312 "the Isle of Torsa" in Argyll and "the lands of Menstrie in vicecomit, de Clackamman", by Robert I King of Scotland[210].  Robertson indexes a charter dated 3 Aug 1323 which records an agreement between "Dugallum Campbell fil
2 CONC ium Colin Campbell, militis" and "Dugallum filium Nigelli" concerning land "de Ardscodniche et advocationem ecclesie de Kilmartin"[211]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>4.         [GILLESPIC Campbell .  Balfour Paul names Gillespic as son of Colin Campbell, citing “Roseneath MS” with no other details about his life or the citation[212].] 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>5.         [daughter .  Balfour Paul says that Angus married "a daughter of Sir Colin Campbell of Lochow" but does not cite the corresponding primary source[213].  m ANGUS Macdonald Lord of the Isles, son of DONALD Lord of the I
2 CONC sles & his wife --- Stewart (-[1296]).] 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY%20UNTITLED.htm#ColinCampbelldied1296B
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ====Wikitree's Current Observations ''(1 Mar 2026)'' [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Campbell-864 Colin Campbell: Research Notes] 
2 CONT * The following points are of import to this person-profile:—
2 CONT ** "'''Unsourced wife disconnected:''' Janet Sinclair was originally attached to this profile as Colin's wife. As all historical sources apparently agree that the name of his wife was not known, she has been removed until a reliab
2 CONC le source has been found. Stevens-17832 19:04, 2 September 2024 (UTC) Later edited to add: Janet Sinclair's name does appear in the ''Ane Compt'' as the possible wife of Colin but this is cited as an ancient oral tradition and no 
2 CONC sources have been found yet to back it up. Stevens-17832 21:40, 8 September 2024 (UTC)"
2 CONT ** "'''Possible additional son of Colin:''' Arthur Campbell, who also swore fealty to Edward in 1296,[11] is sometimes listed as one of Colin's sons.[12] ''The Scots Peerage'' calls this relationship "doubtful,"[10] and although o
2 CONC ther historians accept the possibility that Arthur "may" have been one of Colin's sons they stop short of confirming it.[17] To date '''''no''''' source has been found which styles Arthur as a son of Colin, and it remains equally 
2 CONC possible that he may have belonged to some other branch of the family. For that reason (until a reliable source can be found to establish his relationship to this family) Arthur has not been included as one of Colin's children. St
2 CONC evens-17832 15:27, 23 August 2024 (UTC)"
2 CONT *** [10] Paul, Sir James Balfour. ''The Scots Peerage''. Edinburgh: D. Douglas (1904), Vol. 1, [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/page/343/mode/1up p. 321].
2 CONT *** [11] POMS: [https://poms.ac.uk/record/source/7579/doc. 6/2/227] (IP, 144), Letters Patent (fealty). Also: Ragman Roll: TNA, C47/23/3, m.26. 28 Aug 1296. 
2 CONT *** [12] MacGregor, Gordon. ''The Red Book of Scotland''. Scotland: by the author (3rd ed., 2024), vol. 3, pp. 170-171.
2 CONT *** [17] Johnston, George Harvey. ''The Heraldry of the Campbells''. Edinburgh: W & A.K. Johnston (1920), Vol. 1, p. 17. [https://www.familysearch.org/library/books/records/item/344053-the-heraldry-of-the-campbells-with-notes-on-a
2 CONC ll-the-males-of-the-family-descriptions-of-the-arms-plates-and-pedigrees-vol-1?offset= FamilySearch]
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT Book: Lee, Henry. (1920) ''History of the Campbell Family''. New York: R. L. Polk and Company. [https://archive.org/details/historyofcampbel00newy/page/n5/mode/2up Archive.org].
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT Alt. Death  1296  The String Of Cowal, Argyllshire, Scotland  
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried  Church Of St. Peter The Deacon, Kilchrenan, Loch Awe  
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes  ◦1 - The broad lands of Lorne passed into the hands of the Campbells of Lochow, the direct ancestors of the present ducal house of Argyll, about four hundred years ago; and it is remarkable that they were acquired, not by f
2 CONC orce of arms, but by a fortunate marriage. Sir Bernard Burke tells us that Sir Colin Campbell, of Lochow, in recognition of the great additions which he had made to the estates of the house of Campbell, and to his achievements in 
2 CONC war, acquired the name of 'More,' or the Great, and that from him the head of his descendants, down to the present day, is known among his Gaelic' tenantry 'and clansmen as 'MacCallum More.' He received the honour of knighthood in
2 CONC  A.D. 1280, from the hands of Alexander III. of Scotland; and eleven years later was one of the nominees of Robert Bruce in his contest for the Scottish crown. This renowned and gallant chieftain was slain in a contest with his po
2 CONC werful neighbour, the Lord of Lorne, at a place called the 'String of Cowal,' where an ugly obelisk of large size is erected over his grave. This event occasioned feuds for a series of years between the neighbouring Lairds of Loch
2 CONC ow and Lorne, which were terminated at last by the marriage of Colin, second Lord Campbell of Lochow, and first Earl of Argyll, Lord High Chancellor of Scotland at the close of the fifteenth century, with Isabella Stuart, the elde
2 CONC st daughter and heiress of John, Laird of Lorne.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 - Knight, son of Sir Gillespic Campbell of Menstrie (d ca 1280), k ca 1294, fighting Alexander, Lord of Lorne, at the Ath-dearg (red ford) of Lorn (where a cairn marks the site of his grave). Sir Colin m , possibly a dtr of Sinc
2 CONC lair of Roslin (alias St Clair of Roslin), and had issue:
2 CONT ..A1 Neil, his heir
2 CONT ..A2 Donald Campbell, ancestor of the Campbells of Loudon.
2 CONT ..A3 Dugald Campbell of Torsa, Knight, Laird of Menstrie in Clackmannanshire, swore fealty to King Edward I in 1296, but later joined Robert Bruce.
2 CONT ..A4 Arthur Campbell, Knight, Constable of Dunstaffnage, swore fealty to King Edward I in 1296, but later joined Robert Bruce. (Some writers have doubted he was the son of Sir Colin.)
2 CONT ..A5 Duncan Campbell, alive in 1296.
2 CONT ..A6 Gillespie Campbell
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 - Sir CAILEIN MâOR Great Colin 1280-1294
2 CONT From him the patronymic "Mac Cailein Mor" was derived as he added largely to his estates. In 1280 he was nighted by King Alexander III, the early titles were "de Ergadia" and Lords of Lochow. Sir Colin married a lady of the name o
2 CONC f Sinclair, by whom he had five sons. From his island stronghold of Innischonnaill or Inchconnell (Innischonnel) he was already effectively dominating Lochawe and its surroundings in the very heart of Argyll. He died, 1294, slain 
2 CONC in a fight with the MacDougalls of Lorne at the Red Ford in Lorn and lies buried at nearby Kilchrenan. This death is said to have occasioned bitter feuds betwixt the houses of Lochow and Lorn for a long period of years, which were
2 CONC  put to an end to by the marriage of the daughter of the Celtic proprietor of Lorn, with John Stewart of Innermeath about 1386.
2 CONT http://members.fortunecity.com/gaulois/campbell.html '''<—''' Defunct
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 - The website [https://www.clandonald.org.uk/genealogy/d0001/g0000070.html#I0198 Clan Donald] suggests Sir Colin married the heiress of Duncan MacDuibhne, and that his son Neil and the daughter that married Angus Mor MacDonald o
2 CONC f the Isles were from that marriage. This could be more confusion over which generation the MacDuibhne (O'Duibhne) heiress married into. It is unusual that her father should be named as Duncan whereas all other sources name the fa
2 CONC ther of the O'Duibhne heiress as Paul "the Sporran", ie. King's treasurer.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 - Sir Colin Campbell Mor of Lochow, after whom the head of the Clan Campbell (now the Duke of Argyll) is known by the Gaelic title of 'Mac Cailean Mor', was knighted by King Alexander III in 1280, married a daughter of Sinclair 
2 CONC of Roslin and was killed in 1294 at the Red Fort of Lorne fighting Alexander, Lord of Lorne. 
2 CONT www.clancleary.com '''<—''' Defunct
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 - Cailean Mâor (fully, Cailean Mâor Caimbeul) , also known as Sir Colin Campbell (d. 1296 ), is one of the earliest attested members of Clan Campbell and an important ancestor figure of the later medieval Earls of Argyll. Cailea
2 CONC n was the son of one Gilleasbaig, a knight and lord of the estates of Menstrie and Sauchie in Clackmannanshire. It was first suggested in the 1970s that Cailean's mother was Afraig, a daughter of Cailean mac Dhonnchaidh, the broth
2 CONC er of Niall, Earl of Carrick.[2] Although it has also been suggested that this Afraig was the daughter of Niall himself, there is no doubt that Afraig was of the family of the Gaelic Earls of Carrick. This means that Cailean himse
2 CONC lf was the cousin of the future king, Robert I of Scotland, which explains why the Campbells were so attached to the Bruce cause during the Wars of Scottish Independence .Cailean himself took part in the Great Cause, and was one o
2 CONC f the Bruce representative advocates to King Edward I of England in 1291. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He appears as a witness in various documents dating to the 1290s and relating to lordships in south-western Scotland. He appears (c. 1293 ) in the Newbattle Registrum, where he is called the son of "Gylascop Kambel" ("Gilleasbaig 
2 CONC Caimbeul"), obtaining from Sir Robert Lindsay the estate of Symington; the document, which has James Stewart, 5th High Steward of Scotland, Lord of Kyle, as one of Cailean's pledgers, guarantees continued payment of rent to Newbat
2 CONC tle Abbey. In 1295, Cailean appears as a witness in a charter of James Stewart granted to Paisley Abbey , and in 1296 appears again in the Paisley Registrum attesting the marriage of James to the sister of Richard de Burgh, Earl o
2 CONC f Ulster. Cailean also witnessed a charter of Maol Choluim , the contemporary Mormaer or Earl of Lennox , and in another Lennox charter in which he is granted lands in Cowal by John Lamont, one of Maol Chaluim's vassals.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1296, and perhaps by 1293, Cailean held the position of "Ballie" of Loch Awe and Ardscotnish , a position he was granted either by King John Balliol or Edward I of England. It was this position that made him the enemy for Iain 
2 CONC of Lorn, the MacDougall Lord of Lorne . Sometime after September 1296, Cailean was killed by the MacDougalls at the "Red Ford" on the borders of Loch Awe and Lorne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cailean's great fame lies in the fact that the later Earls and Dukes of Argyll claimed descent from him, and styled themselves "Mac Cailein Mâor", son or descendant of "Colin the Great".
2 CONT Wikipedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cailean_M%C3%B3r Cailean Mór]
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 - The Chief of Clan Campbell takes his Gaelic title of 'MacCailein Mor' from Colin Mor Campbell - 'Colin the Great' who was killled in 1296, and was succeeded by his son Sir Neil Campbell, companion and brother-in-law of King Ro
2 CONC bert the Bruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT Website: [https://www.inveraray-castle.com/ Inveraray Castle]
2 CONT
