2 CONT listed on wikipedia.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tegwared_y_Bais_Wen:
2 CONT
2 CONT Tegwared y Bais Wen ap Llywelyn (English: Tegwared with the White Mantle, son of Llewelyn),[1] Lord of Trefdraeth[2] was a natural son of Llywelyn the Great, Prince of Aberffraw, by a woman in some sources given as Crysten.[3] He 
2 CONC was born circa 1210.[4] 'The white mantle' refers to his coat of arms (as shown to the right).
2 CONT
2 CONT His descendants are one of the few surviving male-line descendants of Llywelyn. Tegwared was not formally recognised as part of the House of Aberffraw by the English king, even though his father was the head, due to his illegitima
2 CONC cy. As such, upon the extinction of Llywelyn's legitimate male-line, the theoretical position of the head of the House of Aberffraw returned to a descendant of Owain Gwynedd, today widely considered to be the Anwyl family. This is
2 CONC  complicated, however, by the fact that Llywelyn was the first Welsh king to be formally recognised by the English and signed the Treaty of Worcester in 1218, by which only his legitimate male-line descendants could succeed him. T
2 CONC his was contrary to Hywel Dda's laws that illegitimate children had an equal right of succession, so it is disputed by genealogists who the head of the house and therefore pretender to the Prince of Wales would be. Tegwared served
2 CONC  as a General in his father's army and held the Lordship of Trefdraeth, Angelsey, where his posterity lived for the next 600 years.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is recorded that he was adopted and raised by Ednyfed Vychan from whom the Tudurs were descended, his father's Seneschal - an office comparable to a sort of hereditary Prime Minister today. Tegwared later married his daughter, 
2 CONC Gwenlian ferch Ednyfed Vychan. They had the following children:[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Howel ap Tegwared Angharad ap Tegwared[1] Arddun ap Tegwared Tegwared Vychan ap Tegwared (b. circa 1250) References[edit] ^ Jump up to: a b Kimber, Edward (1771). The Baronetage of England: Volume Three. London. p. 114. Jump up ^ 
2 CONC Bartrum, Peter (1974). Welsh Genealogies AD 300-1400. Jump up ^ Mosley, Charles (2003). Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes. Delaware, USA: Genealogical books. p. 4183. ^ Jump up to: a b Lundy, Darryl
2 CONC . "The Peerage". Jump up ^ Lewis, Dwnn (1613). Heraldic visitations of Wales and part of the Marches between 1586 and 1613.
