1 OCCU Queen of Scotland and 4th Countess of Ulster, 2nd wife of Robert I.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Elizabeth de Burgh''' (c. 1289 – 27 October 1327) was the second wife and the only queen consort of King Robert I of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Life==
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Dunfermline, Fife in Scotland, the daughter of the powerful Richard Óg de Burgh, 2nd Earl of Ulster and his wife Margarite de Burgh (died 1304). Her father was a close friend of King Edward I of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth probably met Robert the Bruce, then Earl of Carrick, at the English court, and they married in 1302 at Writtle, near Chelmsford, Essex, England. Elizabeth would have been about thirteen years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 March 1306, Robert and Elizabeth were crowned as King and Queen of Scots at Scone. The coronation took place in defiance of the English claims of suzerainty over Scotland, and the new King sent Elizabeth, with other family m
2 CONC embers, to Kildrummy Castle for safety under the protection of his brother Nigel (sometimes known as Niall).
2 CONT
2 CONT After the defeat of the Scots at the Battle of Methven on 19 June 1306, Elizabeth had taken her stepdaughter Marjorie and her husband's sisters Mary and Christian to Kildrummy Castle.[1] The English laid siege to the castle contai
2 CONC ning the royal party. The siege finally succeeded when the English bribed a blacksmith with "all the gold he could carry" to set fire to the corn store. The victors hanged, drew and quartered Nigel Bruce,[citation needed] along wi
2 CONC th all the men from the castle. However, the royal ladies under the escort of the Earl of Atholl had already fled.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were taken from the sanctuary of St. Duthac at Tain by the Earl of Ross, a supporter of the Comyns, and dispatched to King Edward. He imprisoned Bruce's sister Mary and Isabella MacDuff, Countess of Buchan, in wooden cages er
2 CONC ected on the walls of Roxburgh and Berwick castles respectively, and then sent Bruce's nine-year-old daughter Marjorie to the nunnery at Watton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth was held under severe conditions of house arrest in England. The Earl of Atholl was hanged and his head displayed on London Bridge.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was imprisoned for eight years by the English, from October 1306 to July 1308 at Burstwick-in-Holderness, Yorkshire and then transferred to Bisham Manor, Berkshire until March 1312. From there, she was moved to Windsor Castle 
2 CONC until October 1312, Shaftesbury Abbey, Dorset until March 1313, Barking Abbey, Essex until March 1314, and Rochester Castle, Kent until June 1314. After the Battle of Bannockburn, she was moved to York while prisoner exchange talk
2 CONC s took place. At York, she had an audience with King Edward II of England. Finally, in November 1314, she was moved to Carlisle just before the exchange and her return to Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's coronation at Scone, she is quoted as having said,
2 CONT
2 CONT >"''Alas, we are but king and queen of the May!''"
2 CONT
2 CONT as though anticipating a defeat by Edward I.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth had three children who reached adulthood: Matilda, Margaret, and David (the future king David II of Scotland).[4][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth died on 27 October 1327 at Cullen, Banffshire and is buried in Dunfermline. King Robert, her husband, died 18 months later.
2 CONT
2 CONT The organs of Elizabeth de Burgh are said to have been buried in the parish church of Cullen after her death. Robert made an annual payment to the village in gratitude for the treatment of his wife's body and its return south for 
2 CONC burial.[6] A recent non-payment of this sum by the government was challenged and settled to the village's favour.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Issue==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Margaret: born between 1315 and 1323, died on March 30, 1346 in childbirth. Married William de Moravia, 5th Earl of Sutherland and had one son, John, who died aged twenty of the Black Plague.
2 CONT * Matilda: born between 1315 and 1323, died on July 30, 1353. Married Thomas Isak/Isaac and had two daughters, Joanna (wife of John of Argyll) and Catherine.
2 CONT * David: born 5 March 1324, died 22 February 1371. King of Scots (1329 – 1372). Married Joan of The Tower, no issue.
2 CONT * John: born and died in October, 1327 in Dunfermline Palace, Fife.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Further Reading==
2 CONT Sharon Bennett Connolly, [https://historytheinterestingbits.com/2015/04/24/elizabeth-captive-queen-of-scots/ ''Elizabeth de Burgh, the Captive Queen''] 24 April 2015, Website: History ... the interesting bits
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_de_Burgh '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia. 
2 CONT
