1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == SIR WALTER STEWART (otherwise Walterum Senescallum Scotie) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sixth Hereditary Steward of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Bannockburn ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Walter the Steward and his cousin James Douglas were knighted on the eve of the Battle of Bannockburn. The Steward had nominal command of a brigade, although, since he was a mere youth, James Douglas was the actual commande
2 CONC r. [G. W. S. Barrow, Robert Bruce & The Community of the Realm of Scotland (Edinburgh University Press, 1988. Reprinted 1994), p. 210]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == The Lordship of Kilbride == 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Following the death and forfeiture of https://www.geni.com/people/Edmund-Comyn-of-Kilbride/6000000037676404999 who was killed fighting the Scots at Bannockburn, Robert I, King of Scots, gave the Lordship of Kilbride in Lanarksh
2 CONC ire to his son-in-law, Walter Stewart, sixth hereditary steward of Scotland. [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.35112105151130&view=1up&seq=551 RMS, 1306-1424, Appendix II, charter number 220]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Walter the Steward of Scotland married Marjorie Bruce, daughter of Robert I, King of Scots. Following her death, Walter gave the Church of Largs to the monks of Paisley and had prayers said for the salvation of her soul , h
2 CONC is own soul and the souls of his ancestors: "pro salute anime mee et Marjorie quondam sponse mee, necnon et pro salute antecessorum meorum ac etiam omnium fidelium defunctorum". [https://archive.org/details/registrummonaste00mait/
2 CONC page/237 Paisley Registrum: 237] According to the Reverend Doctor J. Camerson Lees Walter's gift was inspired by love. [https://archive.org/details/abbeyofpaisleyfr00leesuoft/page/99 The Abbey of Paisley: 99]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Printed Evidence ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # [https://archive.org/details/registrummonaste00mait/page/n6/mode/1up Registrum Monasterii de Passelet etc (Maitland Club, Edinburgh, MDCCCXXXII), 440 pp. plus Appendix and indexes]
2 CONT # [https://archive.org/details/abbeyofpaisleyfr00leesuoft/page/n8/mode/1up The Abbey of Paisley from its Foundation till its Disolution etc. (Alex. Gardner, Paisley, MDCCCLXXVIII), 340 pp. plus Appendicies]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # [http://www.thepeerage.com/p10210.htm#i102096 Darryl Lundy's Peerage]
2 CONT # [http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I1135&tree=CC Clan MacFarlane]
2 CONT # [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Stewart,_6th_High_Steward Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Stewart,_6th_High_Steward Biographical Summary by Wikipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Steward (1293[1] – 9 April 1326 at Bathgate Castle)[2] was the 6th hereditary High Steward of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was son to James Stewart, 5th High Steward of Scotland by his spouse Cecilia, daughter of Patrick Dunbar, 7th Earl of Dunbar and Marjory, daughter of Alexander Comyn, 2nd Earl of Buchan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter fought on the Scottish side at the Battle of Bannockburn in 1314[3] commanding, with Douglas, the left wing of the Scots' Army.[2] According to another version of events, he was the nominal leader of one of the four Scottis
2 CONC h schiltrons, but because of his youth and inexperience, its effective leader was his cousin James Douglas, Lord of Douglas.[4] This is, however, disputed, as it is now claimed that there were only three Scottish schiltrons at Ban
2 CONC nockburn.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the liberation of Robert The Bruce's wife and daughter from their long captivity in England, the High Steward was sent to receive them at the Border and conduct them back to the Scottish Court.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT During The Bruce's absence in Ireland the High Steward and Sir James Douglas managed government affairs and spent much time defending the Scottish Borders. Upon the capture of Berwick-upon-Tweed from the English in 1318 he got com
2 CONC mand of the town which, on July 24, 1319 was laid siege to by King Edward II of England. Several of the siege engines were destroyed by the Scots' garrison and the Steward suddenly rushed in force from the town to beat off the ene
2 CONC my. In 1322, with Douglas and Randolph, he made an attempt to surprise the English King at Biland Abbey, near Melton, Yorkshire. Edward, however, escaped, pursued towards York by The Steward and 500 horsemen.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter, Steward of Scotland, made a charter to John St.Clair, his valet, of the lands of Maxton, Roxburghshire, circa 1320/1326, one of the witnesses being "Roberto de Lauwedir (Robert de Lauder) tunc justiciario Laudonie" (Justic
2 CONC iar of Lothian).[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter married, in 1315, Marjorie, only daughter of Robert I of Scotland by his first wife Isabella of Mar. The Lordship of Largs, forfeited by John Balliol, was bestowed upon Walter, as well as other lands and the feudal barony o
2 CONC f Bathgate, Linlithgowshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marjorie met her death whilst riding a horse from Paisley to Renfrew on March 2, 1316. She was thrown by the horse at a place called 'The Knock', broke her neck, and is said to have died instantly. She was, however, pregnant, and 
2 CONC a "country fellow" is said to have instantly performed a caesarian operation and delivered the child alive, the future King Robert II.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://www.englishmonarchs.co.uk/stewart_16.html Biographical Summary by English Monarchs] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Stewart, the son of James, fought on the side of Robert the Bruce at the great Scottish victory at Bannockburn in 1314, commanding, along with Douglas, the left wing of the Scots' Army. According to another version of event
2 CONC s, he was the nominal leader of one of the four Scottish schiltrons, but because of his youth and inexperience, its effective leader was his cousin James Douglas, Lord of Douglas. This is, however, disputed, as some claim that the
2 CONC re were only three Scottish schiltrons at Bannockburn. Walter was dispatched to the English border to recieve Robert the Bruce's wife and daughter Marjorie from their long captivity in England, and conducted them back to the Scott
2 CONC ish Court. During Robert's campaign in Ireland he and Sir James Douglas managed government affairs and spent much time defending the Scottish Borders. Following the capture of Berwick¬upon¬Tweed from the English in 1318 he was giv
2 CONC en command of the town which, on 24 July 1319 was besieged by King Edward II of England. Several of the siege engines were destroyed by the Scots' garrison and the Steward suddenly rushed in force from the town to drive off the en
2 CONC emy. In 1322, with Douglas and Thomas Randolph, he made an attempt to surprise the English King at Byland Abbey, near Malton, Yorkshire. Edward, however, escaped, pursued towards York by The Steward and 500 horsemen. Walter marrie
2 CONC d Marjorie Bruce, the only daughter of Robert the Bruce by his first wife Isabella of Mar, in 1315. The king granted the Lordship of Largs to Walter, forfeited by John Balliol, he was also given the Farme Castle estate in Ruthergl
2 CONC en, as well as other lands and the feudal barony of Bathgate, Linlithgowshire. Walter and Marjorie had one son, Robert, delivered by ceasarian section, his mother failed to survive the ordeal, dying a few hours later. Following th
2 CONC e death of his childless uncle, the Bruce's only son David Bruce, Robert Stewart succeeded to the throne of Scotland as King Robert II. He was the son of James Stewart, 5th High Steward of Scotland and Giles (or Egidia) de Burgh, 
2 CONC daughter of Walter de Burgh, 1st Earl of Ulster. Walter's mother is sometimes identified as Cecily or Cecilia de Dunbar (his father's first wife), but this identification is erroneous.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Steward (1293[1] – 9 April 1326 at Bathgate Castle)[2] was the 6th hereditary High Steward of Scotland.
