1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Eóghan MacDubhghaill''', Lord of Argyll
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a 13th century Scottish nobleman and warrior who was styled "King of the Isles", "[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_of_Argyll Lord of Argyll]". He was the son of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donnchadh_of_Argyll Donnchadh]
2 CONC , son of Dubhghall, son of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somerled Somhairle mac Gille Brighde].
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Scandinavian sources, after the death of Harald Olafsson in 1248, King [http://www.geni.com/people/H%C3%A5kon-IV-King-of-Norway/6000000000351087701 Haakon IV of Norway] appointed Eóghan as [http://en.wikipedia.org/wik
2 CONC i/Kingdom_of_Mann_and_the_Isles King of the Isles], though within a year that title went to Dubhghall mac Ruaidhri.
2 CONT
2 CONT In response Eóghan's assumption of this title perhaps, in 1249 King [http://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-III-King-of-the-Scots/6000000001745038323 Alexander II of Scotland] launched an expedition against Eóghan after the latter r
2 CONC efused to renounce his homage to King Haakon IV of Norway. Alexander II fell ill and died on this expedition, but Eóghan seems to have been temporarily deprived of his Argyll possessions.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1250, Eóghan tried to obtain rulership of the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isle_of_Man Isle of Man], but was expelled by the inhabitants. He then travelled to Norway, hoping for recognition as King of the Isles. This attempt w
2 CONC as unsuccessful. By 1255, King [http://www.geni.com/people/Henry-III-King-of-England/979118 Henry III of England] had secured a deal for Eóghan whereby the Eóghan regained Lorne and came into full Scottish allegiance.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Haakon campaigned against the Scots in 1263, Eóghan refused the Norwegian king service and remained a Scottish loyalist. After Haakon's defeat, Eóghan regained formal recognition as ruler of the Isles. His last recorded appea
2 CONC rance is in 1268.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eóghan's son [http://www.geni.com/people/Alasdair-MacDubhgaill/6000000013699263179 Alexander] followed him as Lord of Argyll. His daughter [http://www.geni.com/people/Mary-of-Argyll/6000000006804155423 Mary] married [http://www.ge
2 CONC ni.com/people/Magnus-Olafsson/6000000006804348076 Magnus Olafsson, King of Mann], and secondly Maol Íosa II, Earl of Strathearn.
2 CONT
2 CONT He may have been the Mac Somurli responsible for the death of Jordan de Exeter during a pirate raid in Connemara in 1258.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Sources====
