1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Angus (Aonghas) Mor Mac Domhnaill, Lord Of Islay
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus Mor, being the son of Domhnall, took on the surname of MacDomhnall, and was the first to use that surname which later has taken on it's many forms, i.e. McDonald, MacDonald, McDaniel, MacDonnell, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT In February, 1256 King Henry III o England commanded his bailiffs and subjects in Ireland not to allow Angus Mor MacDonnell, or other Scottish male factors to be received in Ireland, and again in 1260 admittance to that country wa
2 CONC s denied to the Scots. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He gained the title of Lord of the Isles.1 Angus 'Mor' Macdonald, Lord of the Isles also went by the nick-name of Angus 'Mor' (or in English, 'the Elder').1 In 1263 Battle of Largs.1 In 1266 he became a vassal of the Scottish Crow
2 CONC n under the 1266 Treaty of Perth between Magnus IV, King of Norway and Alexander III, King of Scotland.1 In 1291/92 he supported Robert Bruce's claim to the Scottish throne
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Angus, Lord of Islay'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''also known as'': '''Angus Mohr of the Sennachies'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===important Historical note===
2 CONT
2 CONT ==A Historical gap will appear here.
2 CONT  Angus, Lord of Islay had a grandson who will be known as: '''John, Lord of the Isles'''
2 CONT  The space between will have to be researched for correct parentage==
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes  ◦1 - He was son of Donald, the first of his family to acknowledge himself a subject of the Kings of Scotland, A letter was addressed, in 1292, to Anegus, the son of Dovenald of the Isles, and Alexander, his eldest son, resp
2 CONC ecting their comporting themselves well and faithfully towards the Edward I of England. Angus died soon afterwards, leaving three sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT  2 - He was succeeded by his son Angus Mor the original "Mac" (meaning "son of") who supported the expedition of Haco, the Norwegian King, but did not suffer from the latter's defeat at Largs in 1263. In 1284, he appeared at the c
2 CONC onvention, by which the Maid of Norway was declared heiress of the crown, and obtained as the price of his support a grant of Ardnamurchan, a part of the earldom of Garmoran, and the confirmation of his father's and grandfather's 
2 CONC grants to the monastery of Saddel.
2 CONT  Angus had two sons, Alexander and Angus Og (ie, the younger).
2 CONT  (http://www.celtic-twilight.com/atlanta/clans/macdonald/index.htm)
2 CONT
2 CONT  3 - Angus Mor MacDonald (or MacDonnell), Lord of the Western Isles of Scotland (Inse Gall), signed a Treaty with King Magnus IV of Norway in 1266, and submitted to the power of the Scottish Crown which he heretofore had been inde
2 CONC pendent of. He supported the Bruce claim to the Crown of Scotland. He married a daughter of Sir Colin Campbell ofLochow, died about the year 1296, and was buried on lona.
2 CONT  (www.clancleary.com)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources  1.[S67] Macdonald genealogy, Roddy Macdonald of the Clan Donald Society of Edinburgh, (http://www.clandonald.org.uk/genealogy.htm), genealogy/d0000/g0000035.html#I0039 (Reliability: 3)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I2310&tree=CC
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/187011930/angus-mor-macdonald
2 CONT =============
2 CONT
