2 CONT >>>*i)          [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-Macdonald-Lord-of-the-Isles/6000000159132044828?through=6000000159132444821 ALEXANDER Macdonald] (-after 1308).  A charter dated 1292 records safe conduct granted to "Anegus f
2 CONC ilius Dovenaldi et Alexander filius eius"[1371].  He succeeded his father as Lord of the Isles.  He opposed Robert Bruce and was appointed Admiral of the Western Isles under the English crown[1372].  A charter dated [Jun 1297] rec
2 CONC ords the crimes committed by "Alexandrum de Ergadia" against the English, signed by "Julianæ sponsæ nostræ"[1373].  m [https://www.geni.com/people/Juliana/6000000159132053839?through=6000000159132044828 JULIANA], daughter of --- (
2 CONC -after [Jun 1297]).  A charter dated [Jun 1297] records the crimes committed by "Alexandrum de Ergadia" against the English, signed by "Julianæ sponsæ nostræ"[1374].  Balfour Paul says that Alexander married "Juliana of Lorn, and 
2 CONC by her had six sons John Dubh, Reginald, Somerled, Angus, Godfrey and Charles", adding that "these sons found their way to Ireland where they left numerous descendants", but does not cite the corresponding primary sources[1375]. 
2 CONT >>>*ii)         [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000159132981861?fsession%5Fid=1603715076205&through=6000000159132981861&from%5Fflash=1 ANGUS Macdonald] (-Finlaggan Castle, Isla 1330).  He succeeded his brother as Lord of t
2 CONC he Isles. 
2 CONT >>>*iii)        [https://www.geni.com/people/John/6000000159132938836?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1603715076205&through=6000000159132938836 JOHN Macdonald] .  Balfour Paul names "John known as Iain Sprangach" as the third son of Angu
2 CONC s and his wife, adding that he was the ancestor of "the Macdonalds of Ardmamurchan", but does not cite the corresponding primary source[1376]. 
2 CONT >>*b)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-Macdonald/6000000159127291993?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1603713910877&through=6000000003097933853 ALEXANDER Macdonald .  "Angus filius Douenaldi" donated revenue to Paisley monas
2 CONC tery by undated charter, witnessed by "Alexandro fratre meo…"[1377].  Balfour Paul says that Alexander was known as "Alastair Mor" and was ancestor of "the Alexanders of Menstrie, Earls of Sterling, and the Alexanders, Earls of Ca
2 CONC ledon, the MacAlisters of Loup, with their cadet families of Strathaird, Glenbarr and Torrisdal", but he does not cite the corresponding primary sources[1378]. www.findagrave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus Mor “Lord of the Isles” MacDonald
2 CONT BIRTH 1249
2 CONT Scotland
2 CONT DEATH 1296 (aged 46–47)
2 CONT Dundonald, South Ayrshire, Scotland
2 CONT BURIAL
2 CONT St. Oran's Chapel Cemetery-the Reilig Ourain
2 CONT Isle of Iona, Argyll and Bute, Scotland 
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID 187011930
2 CONT
2 CONT Aonghus Mór mac Domhnaill, Lord of the Isles, was a son of Domhnall mac Raghnaill, the second Lord of the Isles, and progenitor of Clan Donald. He was the grandson of Reginal, the first Lord of the Isles, and great grandson of Som
2 CONC erled. Aonghus Mór was married to a member of the Caimbéalaigh kindred (the Campbells), a daughter of Colin Campbell of Lochow & his wife. Angus and his wife had three known sons and two daughters. He died at Dundonald Castle,, So
2 CONC uth Ayrshire, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Members
2 CONT Spouse
2 CONT Helen Nic Colin Campbell MacDonald
2 CONT 1252–1296
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT Angus Og MacDonald
2 CONT 1272–1330
2 CONT
2 CONT >>*c)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Murchaid-Macdonald/6000000159128579839?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1603713910877&through=6000000003097933853 MURCHAID Macdonald .] 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Angus Mor''''' married a daughter of Sir Colin Campbell of Lochow, by whom he had three sons:
2 CONT 1. ''Alexander'', his heir. 
2 CONT 2.''' ''Angus''''', called, in contradistinction to his father, '''''Angus Og'''''.
2 CONT 3. ''John'' "Sprangach," progenitor of the Macians of Ardnamurchan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Angus Mor''''' was succeeded by his son,'' Alexander'', who espoused the cause of Edward I. as against Robert Bruce. On Bruce achieving the independence of Scotland, Alexander, on account of his attitude, was forfeited in all
2 CONC  his estate, and his descendants cut off from the succession for ever. He married Juliana of Lorne, and had six sons: Black John, Reginald, Somerled, Angus, Godfrey, and Charles.
2 CONT Alexander died a prisoner in Dundonald Castle, and was succeeded by his brother,''' ''Angus Og'''''.
2 CONT '''
2 CONT ''Source: "The Clan Donald" Vol 3, page 180.'''''
2 CONT ----
