1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://www.geni.com/people/Ragnall-mac-Somhairle-Lord-of-the-Isles/6000000001745043401?through=6000000002043212109 REGINALD], son of SOMERLED Lord of Argyll & his wife --- of Man (-after 1192).  The Chronicon Man
2 CONC niæ et Insularum names “Dulgallum, Reginaldum, Engus et Olavum” as the four sons of “Sumerledo regulo Herergaildel” and his wife the daughter of Olav King of Man[1355].  Balfour Paul states that Reginald inherited "Kintyre and Isl
2 CONC a" but drove his brother Angus out of Bute and Arran, but does not cite the corresponding primary source[1356].  Lord of the Isles.  "Reginaldus filius Sumerled dominus de Inchegal…et uxor mea Fonie" donated cows and other revenue
2 CONC  to Paisley monastery by undated charter[1357].  Balfour Paul dates this charter to [1180], although he does not state the basis for this speculation[1358].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Engus, son of Somerled, d
2 CONC efeated his brother Reginald in 1192[1359].  The Book of Clanranald records the death of Reginald in 1207[1360].  m [https://www.geni.com/people/Fionnghuala-of-Moray-Lady-of-the-Isles/6000000001745043413?through=600000000174504340
2 CONC 1 FONIE], daughter of ---.  "Reginaldus filius Sumerled dominus de Inchegal…et uxor mea Fonie" donated cows and other revenue to Paisley monastery by undated charter[1361].  Reginald & his wife had [three] children:
2 CONT >*1.        [https://www.geni.com/people/Donald-Lord-of-the-Isles/6000000159127535834?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1603711400664&through=6000000159127535834 DONALD] (-Island of Kerrara 1249, bur Iona).  He succeeded his father as Lord
2 CONC  of the Isles.  "Douenaldus filius Reginaldi filii Sumerled…et uxor mea" confirmed his father’s donation of cows and other revenue to Paisley monastery by undated charter[1362].  The Annals of Ulster record that "the sons of Raghn
2 CONC all, son of Somurlech” fought "the men of Sciadh" [Skye] in 1209 "wherein slaughter was inflicted on them"[1363].  The Chronicle of John of Fordun records that King Alexander II led an army into Argyll, dated to 1221 from the cont
