1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Do not confuse with the later Niall Mor O'Neill of Clannaboye.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Niall Mor was styled "Prince of the Irish in Ulster" when Richard II, King of England, visited Dundalk in 1394. He was also styled "le Grand O'Neill" by the Anglo-Norman aristocracy, the "Defender of Ireland," "The Champion of Dig
2 CONC nity and pre-eminence of the Principality," and "The Unyielding Tower against Tyranny" by the Irish. He was a Prince of Ulster and King of Tir Owen (Tyrone) from 1364 until his death in 1397. He married Beanmhidhe, "daughter of Ma
2 CONC c Mahon." He died "at an advanced age." His wife preceded him in death by two years. [1] He was succeeded by his son (and my 17th great-grandfather), Niall Og. Niall Mor had at least two brothers and four sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT [1]  Annals of Ireland by the Four Masters
