1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''A General and Heraldic Dictionary of the Peerages of England, Ireland, and Scotland, Extinct, Dormant, and in Abeyance. England'''  reproduction of John Burke's book published 1831
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 204 '''John Fitz-John''', who had a military summons to march against the Welsh in the 42d Hen.III.
2 CONT
2 CONT To this feudal Lord succeeded his son -
2 CONT * '''John Fitz-John''', who m. Margery, daughter of Philip Basset, Justice of England. Joining Montford, Early of Leicester, and the other turbulent barons, this Fitz-John had a chief command at the Battle of Lewes; after which ma
2 CONC rching towards Wales, he reduced Ricard's Castle (the Chief Seat of  Hugh de Mortimer), and the Castle of Ludlow. He was subsequently constituted, by a grant from the barons, Sheriff of westmorland, and keeper of the Castles in th
2 CONC ose parts; as likewise governor of Windsor Castle; but sharing the fate of his party at the battle of Evesham, he became a prisoner in the hands of the royalists, when the inheritance of his lands appears to have been given, by th
2 CONC e King, to Clare, Earl of Gloucester; but he had permission afterwards to compound for them under the"dictum of kenilworth". he was summoned to Parliament, as a Baron, on the 14th December, 1864, but died without issue the next ye
2 CONC ar, when that dignity expired. His Lordship was succeeded in hus estates by his brother,
2 CONT
2 CONT >* '''Rochard Fitz-John''', who, upon doing homage, and paying his relief, had livery of all his lands in the counties of Norfoilk, Bucks, Deven, Surrey, Wilts, Southampton, Essex, and Northampton. In the 10th Edward I., this feud
2 CONC al Lord was in the Welsh Wars, and he was summoned to parliament, as a Baron, 23rd June 1295, but died the following year in the wars of France, without issue, when the Barony of Fiotz-John became EXTINCT, while his lands devolved
2 CONC  upon his heirs viz.
2 CONT
2 CONT >>* MAUD, Countess of Warwick, his eldest sister.
2 CONT >>* Robert Clifford, son of Isabel de Clifford daughter of Isabell de Vipount, his second sister
2 CONT >>* Idonea de Leyburne, another daughter of Isabell de Vipount, his second sister
2 CONT >>* Richard de Burgh, Earl of Ulster, son of Avette, his third sister.
2 CONT >>* Joann, wife of Theobold le Buteler, his fourth sister.
