1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Isabelle de Mohun  
2 CONT *Birth 1240 / 1255 Somerset, England Born at Dunster Castle
2 CONT *Death 1280 (aged 39–40)  England
2 CONT *Burial  Newenham Abbey  Axminster, East Devon District, Devon, England 
2 CONT *Memorial ID 171875487
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT *Parents: Reynold II de Mohun  1200–1258 & Isabel Ferrers (disputed)
2 CONT *Spouse: Edmund Deincourt  1256–1326  (son of John Deincourt and Agnes Neville)
2 CONT *Siblings: John I de Mohun, Alice de Mohun de Beauchamp  1228–1275, Lucy de Mohun  1232–1297  
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT #John Deincourt, spouse unknown; father of William "2nd Baron Deincourt" (disputed)  (see https://www.geni.com/people/William-Deincourt-2nd-Baron-Deincourt/6000000003232503893?through=6000000156914264845)
2 CONT #Isabel Deincourt.  Wife of William FitzWilliam 
2 CONT #Margaret Deincourt. Wife of Robert Willoughby 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Disputed origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Deincourt-2
2 CONT
2 CONT Douglas Richardson presents the argument that Sir Edmund's wife Isabell Mohun was actually the daughter of Sir Reynold de Mohun's son John (by Sir Reynold's first wife), who married a sister of Sir Reynold's second wife Isabell Fe
2 CONC rrers:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Maxwell Lyte's book, History of Dunster, states that Reynold de Mohun and Isabel de Ferrers had a daughter, Isabel, who married Edmund Deincourt. We know from other sources, though, that Isabel de Ferrers' issue failed in 1324, w
2 CONC hereas it is certain that Edmund Deincourt's issue continued past that date.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Since Edmund Deincourt's wife can't possibly be the daughter of Reynold de Mohun, a good alternative seems to be that she was the daughter instead of Reynold's son, John de Mohun, and his wife, Joan de Ferrers. This would still l
2 CONC eave Edmund's wife as a Mohun and still give her a descent from William de Ferrers, 5th Earl of Derby. Moreover, the chronology works fine. Edmund Deincourt was born about 1250 (as per the Thurgarton Cartulary). As such, we should
2 CONC  expect his wife to have been born in the period, 1250/1255, which is the exact time period when John de Mohun and his wife, Joan, were having children."[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Comments
2 CONT
2 CONT >”Except by the time of her birth John de Mohun had already been dead two years and Joan was remarried to Robert d'Aguillon.”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
