1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Primary Sources
2 CONT 1276 May 13. Westminster.<br>
2 CONT To the sheriff of Wilts, escheator in that county. Order to cause William de Mohun, son and heir of Isabella Basset, to have seisin of the lands that Isabella at her death held in chief of the king, as the king has taken his homag
2 CONC e for the said lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: 'Close Rolls, Edward I: May 1276', in Calendar of Close Rolls, Edward I: Volume 1, 1272-1279, ed. H C Maxwell Lyte (London, 1900), pp. 281-292. British History Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/cal-close-rolls/edw1/v
2 CONC ol1/pp281-292 [accessed 11 October 2017].
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000068800835032& Inquisitions Post Mortem] William de Mohun. Writ. 20 Aug. 10 Edw. I [1282]. His death date is not recorded. He died before 20 Aug. 1282 (date of writ). Reginald his 
2 CONC son, aged 5 at Christmas next, was his next heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1284 Aug. 18 Carnarvon <br>
2 CONT Grant to Eleanor the king's mother, in lieu of the marriage of Reginald son and heir of William de Mohun, tenant in chief, who died before his marriage, of the marriage of Eleanor, Margaret and Mary, sisters and heirs of the said 
2 CONC Reginald. <br>
2 CONT Source: Calendar of the patent rolls preserved in the Public Record Office: Edward I 1272-1307, [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=iau.31858020271684;view=1up;seq=142 Page 128]  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1288 July 13. Westminster. <br>
2 CONT Grant to Beatrice, late the wife of William de Mohun, tenant in chief, for a fine of 100/. whereof she will pay 40/. a year, that she may marry whomsoever she will in the king's fealty. <br>
2 CONT ''Vacated because the fine has no place, the king having before granted the marriage to his mother, queen Eleanor.'' <br>
2 CONT Source: Calendar of the Fine rolls preserved in the Public record office, Vol. I, [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015066344980;view=1up;seq=264 Page 250]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1297 July 1. Westminster.<br>
2 CONT Order to the same to make partition into two equal parts of the lands late of William de Mohun, tenant in chief, between John de Meriet and Mary his wife, younger daughter and heir of William, and John de Carreu and Eleanor his wi
2 CONC fe, the other daughter and heir, and to deliver to John de Meriet and Mary their pourparty, John having done fealty; retaining the other pourparty in the king's hand.<br>
2 CONT The like to Walter de la Haye, escheator of Ireland.<br>
2 CONT Source: Calendar of the Fine rolls preserved in the Public record office, Vol. I, [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015066344980;view=1up;seq=401 Page 387]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1297 July 22. Westminster.<br>
2 CONT Order to the eseheator on this side Trent to deliver to John de Carreu and Eleanor his wife, eldest daughter and heir of William de Mohun, tenant in chief, their pourparty of the lands late of William, she having proved her age an
2 CONC d John having done fealty.<br>
2 CONT The like to Walter de la Haye, eseheator in Ireland.<br>
2 CONT Source: Calendar of the Fine rolls preserved in the Public record office, Vol. I, [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015066344980;view=1up;seq=403 Page 389]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Greywelle.''' <br>
2 CONT The manor sometime belonged to a certain '''Reginald de Mohun''', to him and his heirs in fee, '''who gave it to William, his younger son,''' and the heirs of his body. The said William had two daughters, viz.—Mary and Eleanor; El
2 CONC eanor was espoused to one John de Carru and they had a son, Nicholas, who died without issue. The other daughter Mary was espoused to John de Meryet, and had a son Henry, who died without issue. Through such failure of issue, the 
2 CONC manor ought to revert to one John de Mohun as kinsman and heir of the said Reginald. From the said Reginald the right of reversion of the said manor descended to John his son and heir, and from him to John his son, and from the sa
2 CONC id John son of John son of John to John his son and heir who now claims; he is aged 40 years and more.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: [https://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vol7/pp11-22 Southampton inquisition for John Meriet 1327.]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT See also: [https://books.google.com/books?id=e69CAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA88&dq=genealogy+of+somerset+family+mohun&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjgwf_R-97WAhUR8YMKHZ29Dl4Q6AEIKDAA#v=onepage&q=genealogy%20of%20somerset%20family%20mohun&f=false Geneal
2 CONC ogy of the Somersetshire Family of Meriet, Traced in an Unbroken Line from the Reign of the Confessor, to Its Extinction in the Reign of King Henry V., Comp. from Public Records and Other Authentic Sources, with Notes and Referenc
2 CONC es]
