1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isabelle Aguillon was born on March 25, 1258 at Addington, Surrey, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Hugh Bardolf circa 1281 at Addington, Surrey, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh BARDOLF 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 was born 1139 in Hoo, Kent, England. He died 1176 in Waddington, Lincolnshire, England. Hugh married Isabel de CONDET on 1166 in Hoo, Kent, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel de CONDET [Parents] 1, 2 was born 1141 in South Carlton, Lincolnshire, England. She married Hugh BARDOLF on 1166 in Hoo, Kent, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT       		M 	i 	Hugh BARDOLF 1, 2 was born 1167 in Hoo, Kent, England. He died 1203 in Hoo, Kent, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT       		F 	ii 	Beatrice BARDOLF was born 1169.
2 CONT
2 CONT       		M 	iii 	Robert BARDOLF was born 1171 and died Jul 1225.
2 CONT
2 CONT       		F 	iv 	Juliana BARDOLF was born 1173 and died 1219.
2 CONT
2 CONT       		F 	v 	Isolde BARDOLF was born 1176 and died before 18 Jun 1246.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT     1Maclean, John, Sir, Historical and Genealogical Memoir of the Family of Poyntz (Baltimore: Gateway Press, 1983.), p. 28, Los Angeles Public Library, 929.2 P892 1983.
2 CONT
2 CONT     2Weis, Frederick Lewis, Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America before 1700 (7th ed., Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., 1992.), 132D-28, 184A-10, 234A-29, Los Angeles Public Library, Gen 974 W42
2 CONC 6 1992.
2 CONT
2 CONT     3Cokayne, George Edward, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct, or Dormant (London: St. Catherine Press, 1910.), 5:539, 6:133, 10:671, Los Angeles Public Libr
2 CONC ary, 929.721 C682.
2 CONT
2 CONT     4Weis, Frederick Lewis, The Magna Charta Sureties, 1215 (5th ed., Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., 1999.), pp. 60-2, Los Angeles Public Library, 929.273 W426 1999.
2 CONT
2 CONT     5Richardson, Douglas, Magna Carta Ancestry: A Study in Colonial and Medieval Families (Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., 2005.), p. 605.
2 CONT www.findagrave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel Aguillon Bardolf
2 CONT BIRTH 25 Mar 1258
2 CONT Addington, London Borough of Croydon, Greater London, England
2 CONT DEATH 1323 (aged 64–65)
2 CONT Nottinghamshire, England
2 CONT BURIAL
2 CONT Shelford Priory
2 CONT Shelford, Rushcliffe Borough, Nottinghamshire, England 
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID 177316274 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh married Isabel Aguillon, the daughter of Robert Aguillon and Joan de Ferrers and heiress of Addington. They had one son, Sir Thomas, who married Agnes de Grandison.
2 CONT
2 CONT A delicious ancient recipe for Malepigernout or Dillegrout, a spiced chicken porridge, was historically made by the current Lord of the Manor of Addington to be served upon the Coronation of the Monarch of England. The service was
2 CONC  inherited through the Bardolf's marriage to the Aguillons. The first documented serving was at the coronation of Richard II in 1377 by the fourth Baron Bardolf; the potage was served at the earlier coronation of Edward II since t
2 CONC he duty was referenced at the inquest following the death of the first Baron Bardolf, and at least as early as the coronation of Edward I in 1272. This ancient recipe is listed as "Bardolf" in an Arundel MS (manuscript) of early 1
2 CONC 5th century date. Brought as an honour by the Lord of the Manor of Addington to the Coronation of the King of England. Three dishes of this gruel are served, the first to the new king, the second to the Archbishop of Canturbury, a
2 CONC nd the third to whom the king assigns it.
2 CONT "Take almonde mylk, and draw hit up thik with vernage, and let hit boyle, and braune of capons braied and put therto; and cast therto sugre, claves (cloves), maces, pynes, and ginger mynced; and take chekyns parboyled and chopped,
2 CONC  and pul of the skin, and boyle al ensemble, and, in the settynge doune of the fire, put therto a lytel vynegur alaied with pouder of ginger, and a lytel water of everose, and make the potage hanginge, and serve hit forth." -- Hou
2 CONC sehold Ordinances (Society of Antiquaries), page 466.
2 CONT
2 CONT The common earthenware pot in which it was made is mentioned in 1304 at the Inquest at Addington on October 14th following the death of Hugh Bardolf, first Baron Bardolf (c. 29 September 1259 – September 1304), where his widow Isa
2 CONC bel succeeded him to the two serjeanties (one serjeanty had the duty to make spits in the King's kitchen, the other serjeanty had the duty to make maupygernoun. Upon her death in 1323, the serjeantry of Addington (which required t
2 CONC he service of maupigernoun at the Coronation of the King) was succeeded by Thomas Bardolf, second Baron Bardolf (4 October 1282 – 11 December 1357).
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Members
