1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Margaret-de-Berkeley/6000000003243309472
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/enguntdk.htm#_Toc21080974
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         MATTHEW FitzHerbert (-[13 Jun 1230/Feb 1231]). ,..  m (before Jun 1200) JOAN Patric, daughter of --- & his wife Mabel Patric[619] (-after 21 Feb 1231). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew & his wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN FitzMatthew (-before 19 Jan 1261).  A debt of "Peter son of Matthew" was granted, to be received from "John son of Matthew brother and heir of the said Peter son of Matthew" dated 28 Dec 1256[626].  m[627] as her first husban
2 CONC d, '''MARGARET de Berkeley,''' daughter of THOMAS de Berkeley of Berkeley, Gloucestershire & his wife Joan [de Somery].  She married secondly (before 18 Oct 1265) Ansel Basset of Winford and Saltford, Somerset.  
2 CONT
2 CONT John & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          MATTHEW FitzJohn (-31 May 1309).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.   m ELEANOR, daughter of --- (-after 4 Jul 1325).  The primary source which confirms her marriage has not y
2 CONC et been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://celtic2realms-medievalnews.blogspot.com/2014/07/anselm-basset-hunting-and-his-life.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime before October 1265 Anselm Basset got married. His new wife was a widow, Margaret de Berkeley, daughter of Thomas de Berkeley of Berkeley castle in Gloucestershire. Margaret had previously married Sir John FitzMatthew (di
2 CONC ed 1261) and had two children, Matthew FitzJohn and Joan. But Margaret de Berkeley had married Anselm without a royal licence and so on 18th October 1265 Margaret got a royal pardon for her offence. This pardon was given because o
2 CONC f the good service given by Anselm Basset to the king.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Anselm Basset and Margaret had at least four children. His son, Edmund Basset, succeeded to the family estates. In 1311 Edmund Basset died holding land at Salford and Dundrey in Somerset, and at Uley, Cam and Owlpen in Glouces
2 CONC tershire.[33] Edmund Basset left his three sisters as his heirs, namely, Isabel, wife of John de Punchardon, Margaret, wife of Nicholas de Valeirs and Catherine, wife of John Biset.[34] Isabel Basset Punchardon was said to be fort
2 CONC y years old in 1311 which gives her birth year of about 1271.[35] Isabel and John Punchardon had a son, Sir Richard Punchardon, a valiant soldier in the wars of Edward III in France.[36]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Sir Anselm Basset, Margaret Basset, is widw, came to the itinerant justices of Somerset in 1280 seeking her dower lands. She claimed a third of a messuage of Isolda Bluet at Henton Bluet, a third of a messuage, 
2 CONC mill and some land at Wynford, a third of a messuage and some land at Wydecumbe Wynford, and a third part of three messuages and land at Wynford. These properties were granted by Isolda Bluet and the other tenants.[37]
2 CONT
2 CONT 31] His widow, Margaret Basset, was still living in 1287.[32]
2 CONT
2 CONT (Footnotes [14] & [15])
2 CONT
2 CONT [14] Calendar of the Patent Rolls, Henry III, 1258-1266, p. 467
2 CONT
2 CONT [15] George Edward Cokayne (ed.), The Complete Peerage (Alan Sutton, Gloucester, 1987), Vol. II, pp. 1, 126-7
2 CONT
