1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was documented using the Anglo-Norman spelling Le Chen. Because his family originally came from Normandy (where the name meant "the oak," from the Old French chesne), all primary records from the Wars of Scottis
2 CONC h Independence and early royal land charters used variations of Le Chen or le Cheyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children with Isabel:
2 CONT 1. Sir Henry le Chen (Recorded as Henry le Chen or Henricus de Chene in 14th-century royal land charters).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Reginald le Chen (Recorded as Reginald le Chen in regional land tracking).
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Christian Chene (Recorded as Christian Chene or Christiane de Chene in early marriage records and the military rolls of the Siege of Berwick).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Francis Chene (Recorded as Francis Chene in the early 14th-century administrative rolls of the Diocese of Aberdeen).
2 CONT
2 CONT —-
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_le_Chen
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Francis le Chen or Cheyne of Straloch was a 14th-century Scottish noble.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis was the son of Sir Reginald le Chen (d.1312) and Helen, daughter of Malise, Earl of Strathearn and Joan de Mentieth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family He married Isabel, daughter of John Comyn, Earl of Buchan and Isabella MacDuff, and had the following known issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry of Straloch, died without issue, succeeded by brother Reginald. Christiane, married Alexander Seton (Governor of Berwick). Reginald, married Janet, daughter of William Marischal. Francis, Dean of Aberdeen. References Cheyne,
2 CONC  Archibald: The Cheyne Family in Scotland
