1 NOTE 5TH EARL OF ROSS
1 NOTE William, the eldest son of Earl Hugh and Lady Maud Bruce, became Earl of Ross when he returned from Norway in 1336, three years after his father's death. He assisted Robert, the High Steward of Scotland and Governor of the Kingdom
2 CONC , for which he was made "Justiciar of Scotland benorth the Forth" and held the title "Lord of Skye".
2 CONT Ten years later, King David II assembled the barons of Scotland and their armies to invade England. King David II had recently restored properties in North Argyll to Reginald of the Isles, and Earl William of Ross took the opportu
2 CONC nity to revenge himself over the disputed lands. Deserted by the armies of Earl William and the deceased Reginal, King David II pressed on and was captured during the Battle of Durham in 1346. In 1350, with the approval of his sis
2 CONC ter Marjory, William appointed his brother Hugh as heir to the Earldom of Ross (upon condition of the king's consent).
2 CONT Earl William and his wife Mary (daughter of Angus Og of the Isles) had a son, William, who died in 1357, and two daughters, Euphemia and Margaret.
2 CONT After an eleven-year confinement in the Tower and a further nine-year refusal by the northern lords to pay his huge ransom, King David II caused Earl William to forfeit all of his lands in 1370, and the Earldom of Ross was given t
2 CONC o his daughter Euphemia and her husband Sir Walter Leslie following the death of Earl William in 1372 at the Castle of Delney. The lands of Balnagowan remained in the possession of the designated heir, Hugh of Rarichies, who becam
2 CONC e the first Chief of Clan Ross in the Balnagowan line.
