1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''NICOLAS Campbell (-[1304])'''
2 CONT s/o COLIN Campbell (-killed in battle String of Lorne c1296)
2 CONT x NN
2 CONT *NN son  (->25 Oct 1305) This heir presumably died young. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY%20UNTITLED.htm#ColinCampbelldied1296B
2 CONT ----
2 CONT COLIN Campbell, son of GILLESPIC Campbell & his wife --- (-killed in battle String of Lorne [1296], before 28 Aug 1296, bur Kilchrenan on Lochawe, church of St Peter).  His parentage is confirmed by the 1293 document cited below. 
2 CONC  "...Domino Colino Campbell..." witnessed the undated charter (dated to “about 1281” by Balfour Paul[189]) under which "domino Johanni de Luss" swore homage for "terra de Banwrith..."[190].  Colin supported Robert Bruce’s claim to
2 CONC  the crown of Scotland in 1291[191].  "Colyn Cambel miles filius quondam dni Gylascop Kambel" guaranteed the donation to Newbattle priory made by "dni Willo de Lyndd filio quondam dni David de Lyndd" by charter dated 1293[192].  B
2 CONC alfour Paul says that Colin Campbell was killed in [1296] "in a contest with his powerful neighbour the Lord of Lorne at…the String of Lorne" and was buried "at Kilchrenan on Lochawe, at the church of St Peter the Deacon, where hi
2 CONC s tombstone may still be seen" (no source citation)[193].  Colin presumably died before the 28 Aug 1296 Ragman Roll, in which he is not named. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---.  The name of Colin’s wife is not known.  The Ane accompt says that Colin married “Janet daughter to Sir John Sinclair [of Dunglass]”[194].  This information has not been verified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Colin & his wife had [five] children 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1.         NICOLAS Campbell (-[1304]).  Balfour Paul records “Sir Neil” as the oldest son of Colin, noting the 17 Oct 1282 charter which names "…Nigello filio Colini Campbell…" (see below)[195].  However, that document appears 
2 CONC to refer to Neil Campbell, not Nicolas, Andrew B. W. MacEwen highlighting the separate existence of the two brothers[196].  MacEwen’s suggestion is consistent with the apparent contradiction between “Neil” having defected to Rober
2 CONC t Bruce’s Scottish cause in [1304] (see below) and the rights of Nicholas’s son and heir being respected by the English king in [1304/05].  “...Nicolaus Cambel...” is named among the auditors for Robert Bruce in the proceedings da
2 CONC ted 5 Jun 1291[197].  Bailie of Lochawe and Ardskeodnish: King Edward I named “Nicho Cambel ballivo R de Leghor et Ardescothyn” in an order dated 10 Sep 1296[198].  A charter dated 10 Oct 1296 confirmed an undated charter (dated t
2 CONC o [1290] by Balfour Paul[199]) under which "Richard de Burgo Earl of Ulster, lord of Connaught" granted "his castle and burgh del Roo" to "James the Steward of Scotland and Egidia his wife (the earl’s sister in frank marriage" wit
2 CONC nessed by "…Sir Nicholas de Chambelle"[200].  The Ragman Roll names "...Nicol Cambel...knights" among those who swore allegiance to Edward I King of England at Berwick-upon-Tweed 28 Aug 1296[201]: Nicholas signed jointly with a gr
2 CONC oup which included several Scottish earls and other prominent nobles (but no other Campbells), suggesting his seniority in the Campbell family.  The following documents indicate that Nicholas died in [1304]: “John de Dovedale, to 
2 CONC whom the king has given by charter Sir Nichol Cambel’s lands in fee, as his son and heir is under age” petitioned the king to “give him the ward of the infant’s lands till majority, and his marriage”, dated to [1304][202].  An ord
2 CONC er dated 25 Oct 1305 records "Ward of Nicholas Cambel’s land and marriage of his heir committed to John de Dovedala"[203].  m ---.  No primary source has been found which names Nicholas’s wife.  Nicholas & his wife had one child:'
2 CONC ''
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         --- Campbell (-after 25 Oct 1305).  He is referred to, but not named, as a minor in the [1304] and 25 Oct 1305 documents cited above under his father.  Balfour Paul names Dugal Campbell as son of “Neil Campbell” (see be
2 CONC low)[204].  MacEwen, when highlighting the separate existence of “Nicholas” and “Neil”, records Mariot Campbell (wife of Colin Campbell of Lochow, see below) as “great-granddaughter and sole heiress” of “Nicholas Campbell”[205].  
2 CONC If he is correct, Mariot would be the granddaughter of this unnamed heir of Nicholas who would have been Dugal.  However, the 3 Aug 1323 document cited below records an agreement between "Dugallum Campbell filium Colin Campbell, m
2 CONC ilitis" and "Dugallum filium Nigelli" concerning land "de Ardscodniche et advocationem ecclesie de Kilmartin"[206].  “Dugallum filium Nigelli” was Mariot’s grandfather (see below), but “filium Nigelli” indicates that he was the so
2 CONC n of Neil not Nicholas (assuming that the name Neil/Nigellus is correctly distinguishable in Latin from Nicholas, as discussed above under Gillespic’s wife).  If that is correct, no further reference has been found to the unnamed 
2 CONC heir of Nicholas or to any other named Campbell who could be identified as him.  This heir presumably died young. 
2 CONT
