1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DUGAL Campbell [of the county of Perth] (->3 Aug 1323)
2 CONT s/o COLIN Campbell (-killed in battle String of Lorne c1296)
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY%20UNTITLED.htm#ColinCampbelldied1296B
2 CONT ----
2 CONT COLIN Campbell, son of GILLESPIC Campbell & his wife --- (-killed in battle String of Lorne [1296], before 28 Aug 1296, bur Kilchrenan on Lochawe, church of St Peter).  His parentage is confirmed by the 1293 document cited below. 
2 CONC  "...Domino Colino Campbell..." witnessed the undated charter (dated to “about 1281” by Balfour Paul[189]) under which "domino Johanni de Luss" swore homage for "terra de Banwrith..."[190].  Colin supported Robert Bruce’s claim to
2 CONC  the crown of Scotland in 1291[191].  "Colyn Cambel miles filius quondam dni Gylascop Kambel" guaranteed the donation to Newbattle priory made by "dni Willo de Lyndd filio quondam dni David de Lyndd" by charter dated 1293[192].  B
2 CONC alfour Paul says that Colin Campbell was killed in [1296] "in a contest with his powerful neighbour the Lord of Lorne at…the String of Lorne" and was buried "at Kilchrenan on Lochawe, at the church of St Peter the Deacon, where hi
2 CONC s tombstone may still be seen" (no source citation)[193].  Colin presumably died before the 28 Aug 1296 Ragman Roll, in which he is not named. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---.  The name of Colin’s wife is not known.  The Ane accompt says that Colin married “Janet daughter to Sir John Sinclair [of Dunglass]”[194].  This information has not been verified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Colin & his wife had [five] children 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3. DUGAL Campbell [of the county of Perth] (-after 3 Aug 1323).  His parentage is confirmed by the 3 Aug 1323 charter cited below.  [The Ragman Roll names "…Dougal Cambel…Arthur Cambel…Duncan Cambel...del counte de Perth…" amon
2 CONC g those who swore allegiance to Edward I King of England at Berwick-upon-Tweed 28 Aug 1296, in a mixed group of people from different counties[209].  Presumably the order of their three names reflects their relative seniority in t
2 CONC he Campbell family.  Their names being listed in the same group suggests a family relationship: if that is correct, maybe they are listed in descending order of age.  “...Dougal Cambel...” could be identified as Dugal son of Colin
2 CONC : if that is correct, “...Arthur Cambel...Duncan Cambel” were his younger brothers or cousins as discussed above.]  Robertson indexes charters under which "Dougall Campbell" was granted "sundry lands in Argyle", and 24 Jan 1312 "t
2 CONC he Isle of Torsa" in Argyll and "the lands of Menstrie in vicecomit, de Clackamman", by Robert I King of Scotland[210].  Robertson indexes a charter dated 3 Aug 1323 which records an agreement between "Dugallum Campbell filium Col
2 CONC in Campbell, militis" and "Dugallum filium Nigelli" concerning land "de Ardscodniche et advocationem ecclesie de Kilmartin"[211].''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY%20UNTITLED.htm#ColinCampbelldied1296B
2 CONT ----
