1 NAME Dafydd /ap Gruffydd/
2 GIVN Dafydd
2 SURN ap Gruffydd
2 _MARNM ap Gruffydd
2 NICK ap Gruffudd
1 NAME Dafydd ap Gruffydd, Last Independent Prince of Wales
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 11 JUL 1238
2 ADDR
3 STAE Wales
1 DEAT
2 DATE 3 OCT 1283
2 ADDR
3 CITY Shrewsbury
3 STAE England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Dafydd Goch ap Dafydd - His Real Ancestry; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id116.html. (Steven Ferry, August 22, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Thomas ap Rhodri, Father of Owain "Lawgoch"; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id153.html. (Steven Ferry, August 23, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Dafydd ap Gruffydd:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dafydd_ap_Gruffydd
2 CONT
2 CONT Dafydd ap Gruffydd (or Dafydd ap Gruffudd, angl. David of Griffith; Welsh pronunciation: [ˈdavɨ̞ð ap ˈɡrɨ̞fɨ̞ð]) (11 July (?) 1238 – 3 October 1283) was Prince of Wales from 11 December 1282 until his execution on 3 October 1283 b
2 CONC y King Edward I of England. He was the last independent ruler of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a prince of Gwynedd, a younger son of Gruffudd ap Llywelyn and his wife, Senena, and thus grandson of Llywelyn Fawr. In 1241, he is recorded as having been handed over to Henry III of England as a hostage with his younger b
2 CONC rother, Rhodri, as part of an agreement. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He may have come of age under Welsh law on 11 July 1252, on which date he issued, in front of his mother, Senena, and the Bishop of Bangor, a charter as lord of the commote of Cymydmaen, at the outer reaches of the Llŷn Peninsula.
2 CONC  In 1253, he was called upon to pay homage to King Henry III of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1255, he joined his brother, Owain, in a challenge to their brother, Llywelyn, but Llywelyn defeated them at the Battle of Bryn Derwin. Dafydd was imprisoned, but Llywelyn released him the following year and restored him to fav
2 CONC our. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1263, he joined King Henry in an attack on his brother. After Llywelyn was acknowledged by King Henry as Prince of Wales in 1267, Dafydd was again restored to Llywelyn's favour, but in 1274, he joined King Edward I of England t
2 CONC o challenge Llywelyn once again. In 1277, following the Treaty of Aberconwy, he was reconciled, finally, with his brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Gruffudd ap Llywelyn ab Iorwerth (died 1244) and his wife Senana.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brothers:
2 CONT *1. Owain Goch ap Gruffydd (died c. 1282)
2 CONT *2. Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Prince of Wales (lured into a trap and put to death, 11 December 1282) married Eleanor de Montfort, daughter of Simon de Montfort, Earl of Leicester. They had one daughter, Gwenllian. Eleanor died in chil
2 CONC dbirth at the royal home Abergwyngregyn on 19 June 1282.
2 CONT *3. Rhodri ap Gruffudd (died c. 1315) married 1) Beatrice, daughter of David of Malpas. 2) unknown. He had a son Thomas ap Rhodri (died 1363) by his second marriage, who was the father of Owain ap Thomas (died 1378).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sisters:
2 CONT *1. Gwladus ferch Gruffudd (died 1261) married Rhys Fychan (died 1271) of Ystrad Tywi.
2 CONT *2. Margaret ferch Gruffudd married Madog II ap Gruffydd, Lord of Dinas Bran (died 1277) of Powys Fadog. They had two sons, Gruffudd ap Madog and Llywelyn ap Madog. The two boys died in mysterious circumstances shortly after the o
2 CONC utbreak of war in 1282.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Pennant[1] states that the boys were "drowned in the River Dee" at Holt by their guardians John de Warenne, 6th Earl of Surrey, and Roger Mortimer the younger. D. Powel[2] mentions the "destruction" of the two princes, whos
2 CONC e guardians, Warenne and Mortimer, "so garded their wardes wit so small regard, that they never returned to their possessions. And shortlie after the said guardians did obtaine the same lands to themselves by charters of the king.
2 CONC "
2 CONT
2 CONT On 7 October 1282, John de Warenne was granted the land of Maelor (Bromfield) that had previously held by the two sons of Madoc ap Gruffudd at the beginning of the war.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dafydd ap Gruffudd married (sometime after 1265) Lady Elizabeth Ferrers, daughter of William de Ferrers, 5th Earl of Derby, and the widow of William Marshal, 2nd Baron.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through the marriage Dafydd came into possession of the manor of Folesham, Norfolk. He exchanged Folesham with John Marshal for the manor of Norton, Northamptonshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT September 1278, he accepted a grant for life from Edward l, King of England, of the manor of Frodsham, near Chester.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT After Dafydd's capture his sons were imprisoned for the rest of their lives at Bristol Castle; his daughter was sent to a convent.
2 CONT *1. Llywelyn ap Dafydd (died 1287)
2 CONT *2. Owain ap Dafydd (died c.1325)
2 CONT *3. Gwladys ferch Dafydd (died 1336)
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition, Dafydd is recorded in late genealogical sources as having sired an illegitimate child, Dafydd Goch, though there is no contemporary evidence to support this.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT At Easter 1282, Dafydd ap Gruffudd attacked Hawarden Castle, thereby starting the final conflict with Plantagenet-ruled England, in the course of which Welsh independence was lost. In December Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Prince of Wales
2 CONC , had been lured into what was probably a trap and killed on 11 December 1282 (see corr. of Archbishop John Peckham, Lambeth Palace Archives)[citation needed]. Dafydd was his brother's successor and became the last Tywysog of Gwyn
2 CONC edd and Prince of Wales. Dafydd was leader of his nation only for a few months after his brother's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT By January 1283, Edward I of England had the heartland of independent Wales ringed with a massive army. Dafydd initially operated from Dolwyddelan and was supported by various royal refugees from Powys Fadog and Deheubarth; includ
2 CONC ing Rhys Wyndod, Rhys Ieuanc and the sons of Maredudd ab Owain. With limited resources of manpower and equipment available the passes leading to Dolwyddelan became indefensible and Dafydd moved down to Castell y Bere. In April, Ca
2 CONC stell y Bere was besieged by over 3,000 men and the small Welsh garrison, commanded by Cynfrig ap Madog, surrendered on 25 April. Dafydd escaped the siege and moved north to Dolbadarn Castle, a guardpost in the Peris Valley at the
2 CONC  foot of Snowdon. In May 1283, he was forced to move again, this time to the mountains above the Welsh royal home Garth Celyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Those who survived fled for refuge to the inaccessible rocks of Snowdonia and David with a few followers hid himself for some months at different places and suffered hunger and cold. At last he retreated to a bog (Nanhysglain), n
2 CONC ear Bera Mountain about four miles above Aber with his wife two sons and seven daughters. His place of retreat was known to Einion Bishop of Bangor and Gronw ab Dafydd, who basely betrayed him." [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 June, Dafydd and his younger son Owain ap Dafydd were captured at Nanhysglain, a secret hiding place in a bog by Bera Mountain to the south of Garth Celyn. Dafydd, seriously wounded (graviter vulneratus) in the struggle, was
2 CONC  brought to King Edward's camp at Rhuddlan that same night (Cotton Vesp. B xi, f30). Dafydd was taken from here to Chester and then on to Shrewsbury. Dafydd's wife Elizabeth de Ferrers, their seven daughters, and their infant niec
2 CONC e, Gwenllian ferch Llywelyn, were also taken prisoner at the same time. Whether they were with Dafydd and Owain at Bera is not recorded, but it is likely.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 28 June, Llywelyn ap Dafydd was captured. Edward triumphantly proclaimed that the last of the "treacherous lineage", princes of the "turbulent nation", was now in his grasp, captured by men of his own nation (per homines lingua
2 CONC e suae).[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Welsh resistance to the invasion temporarily came to an end. On 28 June, Edward issued writs to summon a parliament to meet at Shrewsbury, to discuss Dafydd's fate.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 30 September, Dafydd ap Gruffudd, Prince of Wales, was condemned to death, the first person known to have been tried and executed for what from that time onwards would be described as high treason against the King. Edward ensur
2 CONC ed that Dafydd's death was to be slow and agonising, and also historic; he became the first prominent person in recorded history to have been hanged, drawn and quartered, preceded by a number of minor knights earlier in the thirte
2 CONC enth century. Dafydd was dragged through the streets of Shrewsbury attached to a horse's tail then hanged alive, revived, then disembowelled and his entrails burned before him for "his sacrilege in committing his crimes in the wee
2 CONC k of Christ's passion", and then his body cut into four quarters "for plotting the king's death". Geoffrey of Shrewsbury was paid 20 shillings for carrying out the gruesome task on 3 October 1283 (though some sources give the date
2 CONC  as 2 October).
2 CONT
2 CONT Dafydd's daughter Gwladys, like her cousin Gwenllian ferch Llywelyn, was sent to a convent in Lincolnshire – Gwenllian to Sempringham and Gwladys to Sixhills, where she died in 1336. Their sons were both imprisoned at Bristol Cast
2 CONC le; Llywelyn ap Dafydd died at Bristol Castle in mysterious circumstances in 1287 or 1288, while Owain ap Dafydd is last found living in August 1325. Dafydd may have had another (illegitimate) son, Dafydd Goch, who survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT One cadet member of the ruling House of Cunedda also survived, Madog ap Llywelyn, who led a nationwide revolt in 1294-95.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT *Bellamy, J. G. The Law of Treason in England in the Later Middle Ages (Cambridge University Press, 1970)
2 CONT *Maud, Ralph, David the last prince of Wales. The Ten "lost" months of Welsh History.
2 CONT *Pryce, Huw (ed.) The Acts of Welsh Rulers 1120-1283 (Cardiff, 2005)
2 CONT *Smith, J. Beverley Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Prince of Wales (Cardiff, 1998), p. 579
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT *1.^ Tours in Wales (1874), citing a manuscript communicated by the Reverend Mr Price, Keeper of the Bodleian Library.
2 CONT *2.^ History of Cambria (1584).
2 CONT *3.^ Calendar of Welsh Rolls, Calendar of Chancery Rolls, Various, London, 1912, page 240).
2 CONT *4.^ CCR, 1272-79, 317; CPR, 1272-81, 279.
2 CONT *5.^ Hafod Garth Celyn
2 CONT *6.^ Note: Much has been read into this latter statement regarding Llywelyn ap Dafydd's betrayal, but it has to be taken in context with the other events of 1283, the fact that Llywelyn's father and brother had been taken, and the
2 CONC  size of the army that had by now occupied Snowdonia.
2 CONT
