1 NOTE {geni:about_me} “In terms of family strategy, it may even have been a deliberate policy to have kin in both camps. Even for staunchly Montfortian kin groups, the picture is not entirely black and white. Bishop Walter de Cantilupe,
2 CONC  himself a significant figure in the rebel camp, was head of his family at the time due to a series of unfortunate deaths and minorities. Yet the Cantilupes seemed to have been somewhat divided during the war, with Sir Nicholas de
2 CONC  Cantilupe of Greasley, brother of Bishop Thomas and nephew of Bishop Walter, being rather difficult to place on one side or another depending on the jury being asked about his rebellious actions. '''Similarly, while Peter Corbet 
2 CONC was pardoned at his father’s insistence in 1266, there is little evidence to place him directly on the rebels’ side. After all, he had married Joan Mortimer, between whose family and Earl Simon de Montfort there was a bitter enmit
2 CONC y, which culminated with Earl Simon allegedly falling in battle at the hand of Roger Mortimer, and chroniclers concurring that body parts were then ‘barbarously’ hacked from the earl’s corpse and sent to Lady Mortimer as gruesome 
2 CONC trophies.'''[21] Nicholas, of course, seemed to have little choice in the matter of his own political leanings, given that the head of the Cantilupe family was the staunch Montfortian Bishop Walter of Worcester, a childhood friend
2 CONC  of the rebel earl.[22].”
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[21]''' John Sadler, The Second Barons’ War: Simon de Montfort and the Battles of Lewes and Evesham (Barnsley, 2008), 123.
2 CONT '''[22]''' C. H. Lawrence, ‘Cantilupe, Walter de (c.1195-1266)’, Oxford Dictionary of National Biography (Oxford Universtiy Press, 2004), online resource, http://www.oxfordnb.com/view/article/4571 .
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source'''
2 CONT Melissa Julian-Jones, “Family Strategy or Personal Principles? The Corbets in the Reign of Henry III,” in T''hirteenth Century England Authority and Resistance in the Age of Magna Carta: Proceedings of the Aberystwyth and Lampeter
2 CONC  Conference, 2013,'' ed. Janet Burton, Phillipp Schofield, and Björn Weiler, vol. XV (Boydell Press, 2015), p. 73.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Joan de Mortimer''' 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 was born 1240 in Wigmore, Herefordshire, England. She died 1295 in Caus, Shropshire, England.   She was the daughter of Gwladys "Dhu" ferch LLYWELYN and Ralph de Mortimer, Lord Mortimer o
2 CONC f Wigmore
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family===
2 CONT From http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~hwbradley/aqwg737.htm#28914
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married 
2 CONT # Peter I CORBET Baron of Caus on 1260 in Caus, Shropshire, England.  He was the son of Thomas Corbet and Isabel Valletourt; his first wife.  He married Alice (de Orreby?) after 1295.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children:
2 CONT * M	i	 Thomas CORBET 1, 2, 3, 4 was born 1262 in Caus, Shropshire, England. He died 1285 in Caus, Shropshire, England.
2 CONT * M	ii	 Peter II CORBET Baron of Caus was born 1264 and died May 1322.
2 CONT * M	iii	 Reginald CORBET 1, 2 was born 1266 in Caus, Shropshire, England.
2 CONT * M	iv	 Edmund CORBET 1, 2 was born 1269 in Caus, Shropshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT A daughter Sibyl (Sibella) who married William de Baskerville is also seen.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''FMG (below) only has the children Thomas and Peter.'''
2 CONT ----
