2 CONC    Shropshire, the widow of the still greater lord of Powys..."  Her dower      rights were the subject of a landmark case in 1292 regarding possible      infringement of the Magna Charta and the case is cited in studies of
2 CONT       the Magna Charta.  2.  Bartrum, Peter C.  _Welsh Genealogies AD 300-1400_.  University of Wales      Press, 1978; "Bleddyn ap Cynfyn 29 & 30", pages 56 and 57.  Gives a death
2 CONT       date of 1286.  3.  Burke, Sir Bernard, C.B., LL.D., Ulster King of Arms.  _A Genealogical      History of the Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the      British Empire_.  Baltimore, MD: Genealogical Publis
2 CONC hing Company, Inc.,
2 CONT       1988; pages 114-115 XREFS:
2 CONT   *   HYATT: CHR006.12.
2 CONT An ally of Henry III against Llewellyn ap Gruffydd.  Took the anglicized name of De La Pole from his castle of Pool in Wales.
1 NOTE Griffith ap Gwenwynwyn, Prince of Powys-Wenwynwyn was the son of Gwenwynwyn ap Owen Cyfeiliog, Prince of Powys Cyfeiliog and Margaret Corbet of Caus.3 He married Hawyse Le Strange, daughter of John Le Strange, Sheriff of Salopshir
2 CONC e and Staffordshire and Lucy de Tregoz.1 He died before 1218.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~cousin/html/p437.htm#i26763
1 NOTE [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT Sources: Ayers, p376, 848; Dot Clark, who cites four sources for her own chart.   Clark: Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn, Lord of Powys. Married 1242. Died 1291.   Ayers: Gruffyd ap Wennowen, called De La Pole (Welshpool, co. Montgomery), 
2 CONC Prince, or  Lord, of Upper Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn, Prince of Powys-Wenwynwyn.
2 CONT
