2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_de_Ergadia Maria de Ergadia (died 1302) was a fourteenth-century Scottish noblewoman. She was Queen consort of Mann and the Isles and Countess of Strathearn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria was a daughter of Eóghan Mac Dubhghaill, Lord of Argyll (died c.1268×1275), and thus a member of Clann Dubhghaill.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married four times. Her successive husbands were: Magnús Óláfsson, King of Mann and the Isles (died 1265),[3] Maol Íosa II, Earl of Strathearn (died 1271),[4] Hugh, Lord of Abernethy (died 1291/1292),[5] and William FitzWa
2 CONC rin (died 1299).[6] These unions appear to reveal the remarkable wide-ranging connections enjoyed by Clann Dubhghaill.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria's father last appears on record in 1268, when he witnessed a charter of Maol Íosa. It is possible that this could have been about the time when Maria married him.[8] Within the same year, Maol Íosa is recorded to have owed a
2 CONC  debt of £62 to the Scottish Crown,[9] a sum that could have been incurred as a result of the marriage.[10] The Earls of Strathearn were not amongst the Scottish realm's most wealthy magnates, and it is likely that Maol Íosa's mar
2 CONC riage to the widow of the King of Mann and the Isles contributed to his wealth and enhanced his prestige.[11] Throughout much of her life, Maria bore the title Countess of Strathearn.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria and her third husband, Hugh, had several children.[13] One such child of her and Hugh was Alexander.[14] After Hugh's death, Maria was summoned to appear before parliament to answer regarding Alexander's rights to various la
2 CONC nds.[15] In 1292, Maria was indebted to Nicholas de Meynell for 200 marks, part of the tocher of a daughter of hers.[16] When Maria rendered homage to Edward I, King of England in 1296, she styled herself "la Reẏne de Man".[17] In
2 CONC  1302, Maria died in London amongst her Clann Dubhghaill kin,[18] and was buried along with William in London's Greyfriars church.[19] An effigy of her second husband, and perhaps Maria herself, lies in Dunblane Cathedral.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT After becoming a widow, Máire had three successive husbands: Maol Íosa II, Earl of Strathearn (died 1271), Hugh, Lord of Abernethy (died 1291/1292), and William Fitzwarin (died 1299).[89]
