1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Alasdair MacDubhgaill''', Lord of Argyll
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a Scottish magnate from the late 13th and early 14th century, and was chief of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clan_MacDougall Clan MacDougall]. Alexander was the son of [http://www.geni.com/people/E%C3%B3ghan-MacDubhghaill/6
2 CONC 000000013698924698 Eóghan MacDubhghaill, Lord of Argyll]. Although the details of Alexander's early life are largely unknown, he appears to have succeeded to his father's position as Lord of Argyll and Lorne and head of the MacDou
2 CONC gall kindred after the latter's death in 1268. Alexander appears to have been named after King [http://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-III-King-of-the-Scots/6000000001745038323 Alexander III of Scotland]. Under the latter's authorit
2 CONC y, Alexander was involved in a Scottish invasion of the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isle_of_Man Isle of Man] in 1275.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1284 he joined with other Scottish noblemen after the sudden death of the King Alexander, who died in a fall from his horse while riding to visit the queen at Kinghorn in Fife on 19 March 1286, by  acknowledgeding [http://www.g
2 CONC eni.com/people/Margaret-Maid-of-Norway/6000000010201379576 Margaret of Norway] as the heir of Alexander. As the succession crisis resulting from the unexpected deaths of Alexander III (1286) and then his designated successor Marga
2 CONC ret (1290) developed, Argyll took a prominent part in the succession dispute. He was married to the sister of John II Comyn, Lord of Badenoch, key ally and kinsman of the Balliols. Alexander found himself as a firm Balliol support
2 CONC er as the Balliol's vied against the Bruces to take the succession. He served as one of John de Balliol's auditors during the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Cause Great Cause], and after the latter's accession as King, Alexan
2 CONC der was a key ally and helped King John establish his sheriffdoms in the west.
2 CONT
2 CONT The alliance between MacDougall and Balliol developed from and caused an alliance between MacDougall's main regional rival, Alexander MacDonald, and the Bruces. Alexander was captured during the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battl
2 CONC e_of_Dunbar_%281296%29 Battle of Dunbar] by English forces and was imprisoned at Berwick Castle until his release in 1297.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the deposition of Balliol in 1296, MacDougall opposed the power of his new overlord Edward I of England. The failure of Balliol's kingship also helped to fuel conflict between the two west Highland kindreds as part of the ci
2 CONC vil and international conflict known today as the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_War_of_Scottish_Independence First War of Scottish Independence]; in 1299 MacDougall killed his MacDonald namesake.
2 CONT
2 CONT MacDougall became reconciled with King Edward and in 1305 became a member of the King's Scottish council. The murder of Alexander's kinsman John III Comyn, Lord of Badenoch in 1306 by Robert de Brus, Earl of Carrick, hardened MacD
2 CONC ougall's anti-Bruce position, and this became opposition to Robert's kingship as the latter proclaimed himself King of Scots at Scone later in the year. Through 1307 and into 1308 King Robert assaulted the MacDougall-Comyn positio
2 CONC n in the Western Highlands. After Alexander's seat, [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunstaffnage_Castle Dunstaffnage Castle], was captured by Bruce forces in 1308, Alexander entered the King's peace. Although Alexander attended the 
2 CONC St Andrews parliament of 1309, by 1310 Alexander and his son had gone into England to join the service of King Edward II of England. Alexander died in that year, perhaps in English service in Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander's only known wife was a daughter of the John I Comyn, Lord of Badenoch. He had many children, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT * John of Argyll, his son and successor
2 CONT *Donnchadh
2 CONT *Christiana, m. Maol Mhuire Lamont
2 CONT *Juliana, m. Alexander, Lord of Islay
2 CONT
2 CONT Another of his daughters, unknown by name, married [https://www.geni.com/people/Lachlan/6000000002188078742?through=6000000159153445821 Lachlan]MacRuaraidh, son of Alan MacRuaraidh, Lord of Garmoran. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT
2 CONT *English Wikipedia: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_of_Argyll Alexander of Argyll]
2 CONT
