2 CONT Notes
2 CONT  The arms of the Devereux family of Bodenham with tinctures reversed.
2 CONT  Maud de Giffard had children from her first marriage: Baldwin de Freville, Alexander de Freville, Sibila de Freville, and Alexander de Freville. She also had children with William Devereux the Elder: Margaret Devereux, Archdeacon
2 CONC  John Devereux, Master Thomas Devereux, and Sibilla Devereux.
2 CONT  Some sources record a possible second son, Stephen Devereux of Frome Haymond. This Stephen Devereux is probably his grandson, Stephen Devereux of Bodenham and Burghope.
2 CONT  John Devereux was a valet in the retinue of his father's step-brother, Alexander de Freville, during the invasion of Scotland by Edward I and Battle of Falkirk.
2 CONT  John Devereux of Frome should not be confused with his distant cousin, John Devereux, Lord of Munsley.
2 CONT  John de Pycheford had married William Devereux's sister, Margery.
2 CONT  Lyonshall would finally be regained by John Devereux, 1st Baron Devereux
2 CONT  During Easter 1361 (De Banco. Easter. 35 Edward 3, membrane 201. Norfolk). Adam Blome sued Nicholas de Bourne and Cecily, his wife, over the manor of Wilby. The suit established that William Devereux had given the manor to Richar
2 CONC d de Boylande and Matilda (Maud), his wife, to be held by them and the heirs of their bodies after the death of his stepmother, Matilda (Maud), the widow of William de Ebroicis (this William’s father) who held it in dower. The def
2 CONC endants pleaded that Elizabeth, the grandmother of Adam, was the daughter of Richard by a first wife, and not by Matilda.
2 CONT  This may have been part of the resolution of the complaint in 1295 by William Devereux against John de Acton for taking his stock from Lyonshall while Devereux was in Gascony.
2 CONT  William de Grandison and Sibyl, dropped their to right to oppose the claim of William Devereux’s sister Margery, John de Pycheford’s widow, to Pycheford manor in 1301.
2 CONT  John de Acton had married William Devereux's half-sister, Sibyl.
2 CONT  Appointed baron of the Exchequer in 1299, he worked closely with Walter de Langton and would receive a prebend in Lichfield Cathedral in 1304.
2 CONT  The bishopric of Chester at this time was held by Walter de Langton as part of the bishopric of Coventry
2 CONT  William Devereux was identified as a Centarius, an officer who had the government of a village containing 100 freemen. A number of Centenarii served under the command of a Count.
2 CONT  John Devereux’s widow, Constance Burnell, married as her second husband Henry de Mortimer, Lord of Chelmarsh
2 CONT
2 CONT
