1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John De Warenne
2 CONT Birth
2 CONT 24 Jun 1286
2 CONT East Sussex, England
2 CONT Death
2 CONT 30 Jun 1347 (aged 61)
2 CONT East Sussex, England
2 CONT Burial
2 CONT Lewes Priory
2 CONT Lewes, Lewes District, East Sussex, England
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ====FMG: [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL.htm#WilliamWarennedied1286 England, Earls Created 1067-1122]
2 CONT * i) '''JOHN de Warenne''' (30 Jun 1286-29 Jun 1347, bur Lewes St Pancras). Inquisitions following a writ dated 15 Dec "15 Edw I" following the death of "William de Warenna...he died on Sunday before St Lucy in the said year...Sun
2 CONC day after St Lucy” name “Joan his wife...Robert de Veer earl of Oxford father of the said joan...John his son born at the feast of the Nativity of St John the Baptist 14 Edw I is his next heir...aged 38 weeks on Tuesday before St 
2 CONC Gregory in the said year”[1302].  Her succeeded his grandfather in 1304 as Earl of Surrey. He unsuccessfully attempted to divorce his wife to marry his mistress Matilda de Nerford[1303].  The testament of "''Johan Counte de Warenn
2 CONC e de Surrey et de Strathorne Seigneur de Bromfeld et de Yal''" is dated 24 Jun 1347, requests burial "''en l’esgise Saint Pancratz de Lewes''", makes bequests to "…''Monsieur William de Warenne mon filz…ma fille sa compaigne…Edwar
2 CONC d de Warenne mon filz…Johanne de Basyngg ma fille…Katerine ma fille…Isabell ma fille Noneyn de Sempyngham…William de Warenne mon fil…Isabelle de Holand ma compaigne''"[1304].  
2 CONT * '''m''' (25 May 1306) '''JEANNE de Bar''', daughter of HENRI III Comte de Bar & his wife Eleanor of England (-31 Aug 1361). The ''Chronicle of Lanercost'' names "''filium…Edwardum et filiam quam Johannes de Warenna comes duxit i
2 CONC n uxorem''" as the children of "''Alianora filia regis''" and "''Henrico comiti Barienti''"[1305]. The ''Annales Londonienses'' record the marriage "''VIII Kal Jun''" in 1306 of "''dominus Johannes de Warenne''" and "''filiam comi
2 CONC tis de Bar et Elianoræ filiæ…regis Edwardi''"[1306]. Froissart records that "''la contesse de Garanes…estoit suer au conte de Bar''"[1307]. "''Dame Iehanne de Bar comtesse de Garennes''" bought property at "''Gibecourt, Raupont et
2 CONC  Gernencourt''" from “''l’abbé...de l’Isle en Barrois''”, in the presence of “''son...frere...Edouard comte de Bar''”, by charter dated Nov 1333[1308]. "''Consanguinea nostra Yolandis de Flandria comitissa de Barro et domina de Ca
2 CONC ssello relicta defuncti Henrici quondam comitis de Barro''" confirmed that she was the mother of "''Roberti filii sui nunc comitis de Barro...nunc unici filii sui''", and records “''Edvardi primogeniti...tunc viventis''”, by chart
2 CONC er dated 5 Jun 1353 which also records that “''Ioanna de Barro comitissa de Barro''” affirmed that “''defunctus Henricus quondam comes de Barro, proavusque dicti Roberti comitis''” was father of “''comitem Edvardum et ipsam comiti
2 CONC ssam de Garennis''”, that “''quidem comes Edvardus''” was father of “''Henricum comitem Barrensem maritum dictæ comitissæ Barrensis''”, who was father of “''præfati Edvardus comes ultimo defunctus et Robertus comes modernus''”[130
2 CONC 9].  
2 CONT * '''Mistress (1)''': MATILDA de Nerford, wife of S. de Diriba , daughter of ---.  
2 CONT * '''Mistress (2)''': ISABEL de Holand , daughter of ---.  The testament of "''Johan Counte de Warenne de Surrey et de Strathorne Seigneur de Bromfeld et de Yal''" is dated 24 Jun 1347 and makes bequests to "…Isabelle de Holand ma
2 CONC  compaigne"[1310].  
2 CONT * '''Earl John had six illegitimate children by Mistress (1):'''
2 CONT ** (a) '''JOHN Warenne''' (-after Jun 1347). The testament of "''Johan Counte de Warenne de Surrey et de Strathorne Seigneur de Bromfeld et de Yal''" is dated 24 Jun 1347 and makes bequests to "…''Monsieur William de Warenne mon f
2 CONC ilz…ma fille sa compaigne…Edward de Warenne mon filz…Johanne de Basyngg ma fille…Katerine ma fille…Isabell ma fille Noneyn de Sempyngham…William de Warenne mon fil…Isabelle de Holand ma compaigne''"[1311].  
2 CONT ** (b) '''THOMAS Warenne''' (-before 24 Jun 1347).  It is assumed that Thomas predeceased his father in whose testament he is not named.  
2 CONT ** (c) '''EDWARD Warenne''' (-after Jun 1347). The testament of "''Johan Counte de Warenne de Surrey et de Strathorne Seigneur de Bromfeld et de Yal''" is dated 24 Jun 1347 and makes bequests to "…''Monsieur William de Warenne mon
2 CONC  filz…ma fille sa compaigne…Edward de Warenne mon filz…Johanne de Basyngg ma fille…Katerine ma fille…Isabell ma fille Noneyn de Sempyngham…William de Warenne mon fil…Isabelle de Holand ma compaigne''"[1312]. '''Founder of the WARE
2 CONC NN family of Poynton'''.  
2 CONT ** (d) '''JOAN Warenne''' (-after Jun 1347). The testament of "''Johan Counte de Warenne de Surrey et de Strathorne Seigneur de Bromfeld et de Yal''" is dated 24 Jun 1347 and makes bequests to "…''Monsieur William de Warenne mon f
2 CONC ilz…ma fille sa compaigne…Edward de Warenne mon filz…Johanne de Basyngg ma fille…Katerine ma fille…Isabell ma fille Noneyn de Sempyngham…William de Warenne mon fil…Isabelle de Holand ma compaigne''"[1313]. '''m''' (before 24 Jun 1
2 CONC 347) --- de Basing, son of ---.  
2 CONT ** (e) '''CATHERINE Warenne'''. The testament of "''Johan Counte de Warenne de Surrey et de Strathorne Seigneur de Bromfeld et de Yal''" is dated 24 Jun 1347 and makes bequests to "…''Monsieur William de Warenne mon filz…ma fille 
2 CONC sa compaigne…Edward de Warenne mon filz…Johanne de Basyngg ma fille…Katerine ma fille…Isabell ma fille Noneyn de Sempyngham…William de Warenne mon fil…Isabelle de Holand ma compaigne''"[1314]. '''m''' (after 24 Jun 1347) '''ROBERT
2 CONC  Heveningham''', son of ---.  
2 CONT ** (f) '''ISABEL Warenne'''. The testament of "''Johan Counte de Warenne de Surrey et de Strathorne Seigneur de Bromfeld et de Ya''l" is dated 24 Jun 1347 and makes bequests to "…''Monsieur William de Warenne mon filz…ma fille sa 
2 CONC compaigne…Edward de Warenne mon filz…Johanne de Basyngg ma fille…Katerine ma fille…Isabell ma fille Noneyn de Sempyngham…William de Warenne mon fil…Isabelle de Holand ma compaigne''"[1315]. '''Nun at Sempingham.'''  
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "John de Warenne (30 June 1286 – June 1347), 7th Earl of Surrey or Warenne, was the last Warenne earl of Surrey." Summary of John's proven illegitimate children, who are named in his will, or otherwise accounted for in other histo
2 CONC rical documents of the time. The will in its entirety can be found here: http://books.google.com/books?id=c_TCZG2oqdkC&printsec=frontcover&dq=testamenta+eboracensia&hl=en&ei=x4rFTN-KMcaMnQfW0K3wCQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&res
2 CONC num=1&ved=0CCkQ6AEwAA#v=onepage&q&f=false  Page 41
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of William de Warenne, the only son of John de Warenne, 6th Earl of Surrey. His mother was Joan, daughter of Robert de Vere, 5th Earl of Oxford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Joan (Jeanne) of Bar, daughter of count Henry III of Bar and Eleanor of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT John had no legitimate children, but many illegitimate children, who were included in his will. They are listed below.
2 CONT
2 CONT Had at least 3 sons with mistress Maud Nerford. Another mistress was Isabel Holland (sister of Thomas Holland, Earl of Kent). There are no documented children of John and Isabel Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT His heir was his sister Alice's son Richard 'Copped Hat', earl of Arundel, while his Yorkshire lands passed to his godson, Edward III's son, Edmund of Langley. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The followinglist of John's children is excerpted from the blog of historian Kathryn Warner, who holds an MA with Distinction in Medieval History and Literature. Her blog may be found at  http://edwardthesecond.blogspot.com/2009/0
2 CONC 9/illegitimate-children-of-john-de.html:
2 CONT
2 CONT John left bequests to three daughters in his will. I don't know the identity of their mother(s):
2 CONT
2 CONT - '''Joan de Basing''' ('Johanne de Basyngg' in the original spelling) who received a cup of plain silver from her father. Judging by her last name, she was already married. Joan was the name of John's mother, which implies Joan d
2 CONC e Basing was his eldest daughter, as does the fact that she was named first in the list of his daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT - '''Katherine''', who according to several genealogy sites - I don't know what primary source is the basis for this statement, and I can't confirm it - married Sir Robert Heveningham after her father's death. She certainly receiv
2 CONC ed a bequest of ten marks (six pounds, sixty-six pence) in John's will. ('Katerine' in the original, no surname given)
2 CONT
2 CONT - '''Isabel''', a nun at Sempringham, who received twenty pounds (vynt l) from John. ('Isabell') [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT John had six illegitimate sons that I've been able to find. Here's a list of them.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ravlyn'''
2 CONT
2 CONT John's most obscure son and only mentioned, that I've found, in a petition presented to parliament in 1334 by one Ralph le Botiller. This petition calls him Ravlyn fitz al Counte de Garrein, "Ravlyn, son of the earl of Warenne," a
2 CONC nd records le Botiller's complaint that John had sent Ravlyn and some members of his household to attack two of his (le Botiller's) manors in Cheshire and steal or destroy his possessions. Ravlyn is not mentioned either in his fat
2 CONC her's will of June 1347 or in a letter John sent to Edward III in April 1346 naming his other two secular sons, perhaps because he was dead by then. [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John''' and '''Thomas'''
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1316, John de Warenne had two sons by Maud Nerford, and in August that year persuaded Edward II to accept them as his heirs: he surrendered his lands to Edward and received them back "with remainder to John de Warenna son of Ma
2 CONC tilda de Neirford and the heirs male of his body, and failing such issue to Thomas de Warenna, son of the said Matilda..." [7] John evidently was the elder of the two and presumably named after their father; Thomas may have been n
2 CONC amed after Thomas Nerford, one of Maud's brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Confusingly, there are a few references at the beginning of the 1300s to John and William, sons of John, earl of Warenne, both of whom had been, according to letters of the pope, ordained priest while still under age. [8] As our E
2 CONC arl John was only born in 1286, these two must have been the illegitimate sons of his grandfather John de Warenne (1231-1304), the previous earl of Surrey and Warenne, and thus John's uncles. His sons John and Thomas had both join
2 CONC ed the order of the Hospital of St John of Jerusalem by November 1345, and their mother Maud Nerford was dead by then. [9] Neither of them appeared in their father's will.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Edward'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Almost certainly another son of Maud Nerford, as he owned lands in Norfolk which had previously belonged to her. John called him "Edward de Warenne, my son" - plain 'Edward', not 'Sir Edward' - in his 1347 will, and left him twent
2 CONC y pounds. Edward was also mentioned in a letter John wrote to Edward III in April 1346, saying that his sons Edward and William were ready to serve the king abroad. [10] He was presumably born after August 1316 as he was not menti
2 CONC oned in John's land settlements at that time, and before his father "ousted" Maud Nerford from his heart and company in or shortly before 1320. He may have been named in honour of Edward II, or possibly after his father's first co
2 CONC usin Edward Balliol, son of John Balliol, king of Scotland (himself probably named after Edward I). He is named as "Edward de Warenn, knight" in an entry on the Close Roll of 23 February 1349. [11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward de Warenne married Cecily, daughter of Nicholas de Eton, and founded the Warren family of Poynton, Cheshire. His eldest son, named John after his father, was born in 1343 or 1344; he had other sons named Edward and William.
2 CONC  Edward de Warenne had died by 1369, and his son John died in 1392. See this thread for more information.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir William''' and '''Prior William'''
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Warenne had two sons called William, one a prior and the other a knight, by an unknown mother or mothers. William was the name of John's father, Sir William de Warenne, son of the earl of Surrey who died in 1304 and killed
2 CONC  in a jousting tournament in 1286 when John was a baby, so it's not at all surprising that John would use the name for his sons. One of them, probably the knight, had been born by 24 August 1310, when John (then aged twenty-four) 
2 CONC granted "his son William de Warenna and the heirs of his body" the manor of Beeston in Norfolk. Although John gave the manor of Beeston to Earl Thomas of Lancaster in 1318, Sir William de Warenne was holding it in January 1333. [1
2 CONC 2] It is strange, therefore, that William was not mentioned in John's land settlements of 1316, when John named his sons John and Thomas as his heirs. Perhaps this means that William was not Maud Nerford's son and she persuaded Ea
2 CONC rl John to make her own sons his heirs? Or perhaps John had envisaged a career in the Church for William, then changed his mind? I can only speculate. On the other hand, Sir William witnessed a grant of land from his father to his
2 CONC  (Earl John's) lardener Henry de Kelsterne in January 1332 with Thomas Nerford, Maud Nerford's brother - which may imply a relationship between William and Nerford, or may only mean that John de Warenne held onto his connections t
2 CONC o the Nerfords even after his relationship with Maud ended. [13]
2 CONT
2 CONT John's other son William was prior of Horton in Kent and of Castle Acre in Norfolk, and was named in numerous papal letters, warrants, writs etc as the illegitimate son of John de Warenne. I don't know the identity of his mother, 
2 CONC but according to a declaration of 1338 that he was a true-born Englishman and not a foreigner, he was born at his father's Yorkshire castle of Conisbrough. [14] In his will, John left "Master [daunz] William de Warenne, my son, a 
2 CONC Bible which I had made in French." In October 1348 and again in February 1351, Edward III appointed several sergeants-at-arms to arrest William and a fellow monk of Castle Acre on the grounds that they "have spurned the habit of t
2 CONC heir order and are vagabonds in England in secular habit" who were "to be chastised according to the rule of their order." William was, according to a papal letter, still alive in early 1364. [15]
2 CONT
2 CONT John's son William the knight seems to have been a great favourite of his father, judging by the number of things John bequeathed to "Sir William de Warenne, my son" in his will, which included 100 marks (sixty-six pounds), a silv
2 CONC er-gilt helmet and coronet and all his armour for jousting. John also left a gold brooch to William's wife, and although his will didn't give her name, it appears in a papal grant of April 1344: Margaret. [16] Sir William was one 
2 CONC of the three leaders of a company of archers and men-at-arms raised by his father in November 1339, and accompanied his (half-?) brother Edward de Warenne on campaign abroad in April 1346. [17] Like his (half-?) brothers Edward an
2 CONC d William the prior, he was openly and frequently acknowledged as the earl of Surrey's illegitimate offspring and sometimes witnessed John's charters as "the grantor's son," and also received grants of his own from John on occasio
2 CONC n. In June 1364, Edward III granted him an annuity of forty marks "for long service," and William was still active in November 1368, when he and other men were accused of hunting without permission in the lands of Hugh Hastings in
2 CONC  Yorkshire. [18]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
