2 CONT ohn de Warenne m. Joan de Barre in 1306 when she was 10 or 11 y.o. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT On 25 May 1306 Warenne married Joan of Bar, daughter of count Henry III of Bar and Eleanor of England, eldest daughter of king Edward I of England. The two were soon estranged and lived apart, and had no children, though the marri
2 CONC age was never dissolved.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1316 Warenne was excommunicated for adultery with Maud de Nerford, by whom he had several illegitimate children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maude de Skegeton, called Maud de Nerford was alive in 1326, but dead before 22 Nov 1345.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having no issue by his wife, he had for that, or some other reason, conceived a dislike to her, and had cohabited with one Maud de Nerford ...
2 CONT
2 CONT The earl finding that he could not have both a wife and concubine, was determined, if he could, to be without the former, and therefore, partly on the pretence of a prior contract made with this Maud de Nerford, and partly because
2 CONC  Joan de Barr and he were too nearly related ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes de Warren ... sued for a divorce; on account of a former contract with Maud de Nerford, it was necessary that the said Maud should libel against Joan de Barr; and accordingly she did so ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan (being the king's relation, and residing at court) was cited in the king's palace at Westminster.
2 CONT
2 CONT This divorce, being agreeable to both parties, took place*, and the earl settled on the said Joan 740 marks per annum, for life; after which she stayed in England till the 19 Edw. III. when obtaining protection for all her lands i
2 CONC n England, which were assigned for her support, with the stock thereupon, for the better defence, and safe-guard of them in her absence, she went beyond sea, on special employment for the king. 26 Edw. III. she had licence there t
2 CONC o continue, till the 15th of St. Michael that year; after which I know nothing more of her, than that the register book of Lewes tells us, that she died in 1361, and was buried abroad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other sources deny that the divorce was ever granted and it is generally accepted that it was not.
2 CONT • Memoirs of the Ancient Earls Vol 2 - John de Warenne, 8th Earl + Maud de Nerford his mistress
2 CONT
2 CONT It is because none of John's children were "legitimate" and therefore he had no one to legally pass his titles to, he is often styled "the last Earl".
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of Earl John de Warenne: On Sunday the 24th day of June in the year of the Incarnation of our Lord 1347, I, John, Earl of Warenne, of Surrey and of Strathorne. Lord of Bromfeld and of Yal, in good and sound memory, make m
2 CONC y Testament in my castle of Conesburgh ...
2 CONT
2 CONT I bequeath to Sir William de Warenne, my son, 100 marks, my helmet of silver gilt for Strathorne, with the coronet of silver gilt for the same, two fastenings and the pin of silver gilt for the cloak, and all my amour for joust,
2 CONT
2 CONT And to my daughter, his wife [not named], one golden broach,
2 CONT
2 CONT I bequeath to Edward de Warenne, my son, £5,
2 CONT
2 CONT I bequeath to Joan de Basyngg, my daughter, one cup of plain silver,
2 CONT
2 CONT I bequeath to Katherine, my daughter, 10 marks,
2 CONT
2 CONT I bequeath to Isabel, my daughter, a nun at Sempyngham, £5
2 CONT
2 CONT I bequeath to Dan [“Dauns”] William de Warenne, my son, my Bible which I had made in French, and which should remain in the house where he shall be prior in perpetual memory of me.
2 CONT
2 CONT I bequeath to Sir Thomas Beron, 12 dishes, 12 saucers, six ordinary goblets, one plain cup, two basins and two chargers of silver, my old bed of red [English; “worsted”], and the cloth of one robe, partly of striped velvet and par
2 CONC tly of red velvet, with the cloak of red velvet, for a vestment for his church.
2 CONT
2 CONT I bequeath to Isabel de Holand, my compaigne, my gold ring with the good ruby, the five gold rings placed as stars which are in my golden eagle, so that she put other rings in their place, such as she shall please, the complete pr
2 CONC incipal vestments for my chapel, with the complete fittings for the altar, my censer of silver gilt and enamel, my golden cup with a little [English: “Ewer”] of silver gilt, all my beds, great and small, except those which I have 
2 CONC bequeathed to othera [sic, plural], the great dish, the silver pot for alms, three plates for spices, all my vessels of plain silver, as in dishes, saucers, basins, washing dishes, chargers, cups and goblets, except those which I 
2 CONC have bequeathed to others in this Testament,
2 CONT
2 CONT And the half of my stock, as oxen, cows, mares, foals, sheep and other beasts,
2 CONT
2 CONT And after my debts and my bequeaths shall be paid, I bequeath to my said wife, all the residue of all my goods and chattels, in whatever place they should be found,
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Warenne-91
2 CONT https://www.britannica.com/biography/John-de-Warenne-7th-earl-of-Surrey
2 CONT https://www.allabouthistory.co.uk/History/England/Person/John-Warenne-7th-Earl-Surrey-1286-1347.html
2 CONT https://www.royalline.uk/people/john-de-warenne-7th-earl-of-surrey
2 CONT https://www.encyclopedia.com/history/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/warenne-john-de-7th-earl-surrey
2 CONT https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/LRS7-ZBD/earl-john-de-warenne-of-surrey-1286-1347
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/51615273/john-de_warenne
2 CONT
2 CONT English Aristocracy. Born the only son of William de Warenne, eldest son of John de Warenne, 7th Earl of Surrey. His father was killed at Croydon in December 1286, where he is said either to have been killed due to a tournament ac
2 CONC cident or to have been ambushed after attending a tournament and murdered by rivals, leaving his only son heir to the earldom. After his grandfather's death in 1304, he succeeded to the title becoming the 8th Earl of Surrey. Betwe
2 CONC en 1306 and 1315 he married Joan de Bar, but they either separated or divorced shortly thereafter, and there were no children. He was noted for his opposition to Edward II’s favorite, Piers Gaveston, however, he later supported th
2 CONC e king against the Lords Ordainers, a baronial committee seeking to restrict the king’s powers. He remained one of the king's supporters for the remainder of his reign. He was very involved in the Scottish Wars under Edward III. H
2 CONC e joined with his cousin Edward Balliol in his attempt to gain the Scottish throne and received from him the Scottish title of Earl of Strathern in 1332, though he never held those lands. He died at 61 at Conisborough in Yorkshire
2 CONC  without any direct heirs, the titles passed to his nephew Richard Fitzalan.
