1 NOTE "When the Red Earl [Gilbert (3)] married King Edward  [I]'s daughter[Joan
2 CONT of Acre], the inheritance was entailed on their issue; Gilbert'sdaughters
2 CONT by Alice de Lusignan were excluded as potential heiresses in the event of
2 CONT the failure of the male line.  Ironically, the king's provise becameoperative, with serious political consequences for Edward II.  The earl'sonly son Gilbert [the present Gilbert (4)], born in 1291, was styled earlof Gloucester in
2 CONC  1307, shortly after the death of his mother and theconsequent reversion of his stepfather, Ralph de Monthermer, to ordinarybaronial status.  The young Earl Gilbert was also the last. His tenure ofthe inheritance was brief.  On Ju
2 CONC ne 24, 1314, he was killed at the battleof Bannockburn, and with his death the male line of the senior branch ofthe family became extinct.  In 1308 Gilbert had married Maud, daughter ofRichard de Burgh, earl of Ulster, but they ha
2 CONC d no children, or at leastno surviving issue. According to the compiler of the *FloresHistoriarum*, there was a son John who was born in April 1312 and whodied before the end of the year.  The accuracy of the chronicle onmatters o
2 CONC f this sort is often suspect, but the authenticity of thestatement has been accepted by modern peerage writers.  In December,1314, Maud de Burgh was granted dower, but this was intended only as atemporary and precautionary measure
2 CONC , since she claimed to be pregnant.For nearly three years thereafter [!!!], the
2 CONT countess continued to insist on her pregnancy, but in 1317 Edward II, who
2 CONT had hoped that the birth of a child would preserve the inheritanceintact,
2 CONT reluctantly concluded that her claims were spurious.  n November of that
2 CONT year, the great Clare inheritance was partitioned among the husbands ofthe
2 CONT last Earl Gilbert's full sisters, and after the countess' death in 1320,her
2 CONT dower portion was likewise divided."
2 CONT --- Michael Altschul, *A Baronial Family in Medieval  England: The
2 CONT Clares, 1217-1314*, Baltimore MD (Johns Hopkins Press) 1965. p 39-40.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Gilbert served the king better than most of the other great magnatesof the realm.  He quickly distinguished himself in the Scottishcampaigns. But individual efforts displayed by such men as Gilbert orAymer de Valence, the new
2 CONC  earl of Pembroke, were not an adequatesubstitute for a concerted series of campaigns against the Scotsutilizing the full resources of the realm.
2 CONT Between 1307 and 1313, Robert Bruce was able to subdue Inverness, Dundee,
2 CONT and Perth, thus securing the entire region north of the Tay River, and tobegin attacking the center of English power in lothian; and Edward [II],faced with financial crisis and growing political dissension at home, wasunable to co
2 CONC ntain him.  (P) Earl Gilbert maintained a middle ground inthe struggle between king and baronage.  Gilbert's mediating influence,however, had little effect on the program of reform.  The greatestproblem facing the king [Edward II]
2 CONC  after his reconciliation with[Thomas] Lancaster was the steady advance of Robert Bruce who by March,1314, had secured the strategic castle of Roxburgh and Edinburgh and evenattacked Berwick itself.  His main objective was Stirlin
2 CONC g, the lastremaining English stronghold north of the Tweed, but Edward wasdetermined to retain it at all costs.  Accordingly, the king raised alarge army of foot and cavalry and set north, coming within three milesof the castle on
2 CONC  June 23.  The front line of the army was commanded bythe earls of Gloucester and Hereford.  Gilbert engaged in a briefskirmish with the Scots on the 23rd, but although unhorsed, escapedwithout injurt.  The next day he advised Edw
2 CONC ard to order a day's rest forthe army.  The king foolishly spurned his advice as deceitful andtreacherous.  Gilbert retorted sharply and impetuously plunged intobattle.  He led a gallant charge against the Scots line commanded byR
2 CONC obert's brother Edward, but failed to receive adequate support from hisown troops or the English bowmen.  His horse was cut down, and Gilbert,deserted by his followers, was slain.  His body was later recovered fromRobert Bruce and
2 CONC  brought back to Tewkesbury Abbey for burial at the righthand of his father.  Earl Gilbert's death marked the beginning of acomplete rout of the English forces.  Scottish independence was assured.(P) Gilbert de Clare, the last mem
2 CONC ber of the senior branch of the familyin the male line, was also one of its most attractive.  In spite of hisyouth he displayed qualities of
2 CONT leadership and military ability which held great promise.  Had he notbeen
2 CONT killed, he might have continued to exercise a salutary effect on therelations between the king and the barons led by Thomas of Lancaster,which would have mitigated the worst excesses of the period immediatelyfollowing
2 CONT Bannockburn.  As it was, his consistent efforts to effect a moderatesolution in the struggle between the two factions did much to prevent theoutbreak of civil war before his death.  He had served the king valiantlyto the last."
2 CONT  --- Michael Altschul, *A Baronial Family in Medieval  England: The
2 CONT Clares, 1217-1314*, Baltimore MD (The Johns Hopkins Press) 1965, p159-164
2 CONT passim
