2 CONT *'''1. John De BOHUN (1º B. Bohun of Midhurst)
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/BOHUN.htm#James DE BOHUN of Midhurst _____________________________
2 CONT *'''A genealogical history of the dormant, abeyant, forfeited, and extinct ... By Sir Bernard Burke
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=1ysWkXKSrpIC&pg=PA58&lpg=PA58&dq=John+De+Bohun+1362&source=bl&ots=K7nRTxEk8F&sig=V92kd6w-_aU7PXq6BogGOgot_mk&hl=en&ei=VdELTMLsDY_YMMuWybUE&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=9&ved=0CDQQ6AEwCA#v=
2 CONC onepage&q=John%20De%20Bohun%201362&f=false
2 CONT *Pg.58
2 CONT *   ENGHILER DE BOHUN, temp. WILLIAM THE CONQUEROR, was father of SAVARIC DE BOHUN, temp. HENRY I., who had two sons, Savaric, who d. s. p., and JELDEWIN DE BOHUN, whose son,
2 CONT *   FRANCO DE BOHUN (1st RICHARD I.), Lord of Midhurst Ford, Clemping, and Rushington, was father of
2 CONT *   JOHN DE BOHUN, who m. Sibel, dau. of William Ferrars, 2nd Earl of Derby, and d. 12th of EDWARD I., having had two sons and a dau.,
2 CONT **	I. John d. s. p.
2 CONT **'''	II. JAMES, of whom presently.
2 CONT **	I. Elizabeth, m. to Sir John Lesley, and had two daus. and co-heirs,
2 CONT ***		1 Elizabeth, m. to John Bramshott, and had issue, Thomas.
2 CONT ***		2 Eva, m. to Sir John Barford, and was mother of a dau. and heir,
2 CONT ****			Elinor, m. to Thomas Cooke, of Wreckham, and was mother of Richard Cooke, of Rushington, ancestor of the COOKES, of Westburton and Goring. Their descendant, Sir John Cooke had a dau. and heir,
2 CONT *****				Elizabeth, m. to John Covert, of Sullington, in 1558.
2 CONT *'''The 2nd son,
2 CONT *'''   JAMES DE BOHUN (4th EDWARD I.), m. Joan, dau., and co-heir of William de Braose, Lord of Bramber, and was father of
2 CONT *   JOHN DE BOHUN, who was summoned to parliament as Baron of Midhurst, 1364 (37th EDWARD III.) "This is he (say Dugdale) who for his great services in Flanders, and elsewhere beyond sea, in 14th EDWARD III. (when the king first l
2 CONC aid claim to the crown of France), as also in that famous expedition into France, 19th EDWARD III. (shortly after which, the king obtained that glorious victory at Cressy, whereof our historians make ample mention), became afterwa
2 CONC rds one of the BARONS of the realm, being summoned to sit in parliament, in 37th, 38th, and 39th of that king's reign." By his 1st wife, Isabel, he had a dau. Joan, m. to John de Lisle de Gatcombe, in the Isle of Wight, and by his
2 CONC  2nd wife Cicely, dau. and heir of John Filliol, he had a son John. His lordship d. 41st EDWARD III., his son,
2 CONT *   JOHN DE BOHUN (7th RICHARD II.) had two sons, JOHN and Humphrey (Sir), of Midhurst, who d. s. p. 1468. The eldest son,
2 CONT *   JOHN DE BOHUN (10th HENRY VI., 1432), left two daus. and co-heirs, .... ___________________________________
2 CONT *'''Sussex archaeological collections relating to the history and ..., Volume 20 By Sussex Archaeological Society Pg.11-12
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=9DcGAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA12&lpg=PA12&dq=Anne+halsham+bohun&source=bl&ots=oulE2VaIo6&sig=iS1e-LgmZAe9NTYwZTaazaoRRa0&hl=en&ei=f0YMTNOAIYr4MrrQvM0I&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=6&ved=0CCAQ6AEwBTgU#
2 CONC v=onepage&q=Anne%20halsham%20bohun&f=false
2 CONT *Pg.11
2 CONT *After the entire estate had been remitted to the Bohuns, Franco's grandson John died without children, and was succeeded by his brother '''James, who married the heiress of''' Wm. de Braose, of Bramber. '''Their only child was th
2 CONC e most distinguished of the family''', fighting at Cressy, in 1346, endowing the Benedictine Nunnery of Easebourne, and dying
2 CONT *Pg.12
2 CONT *in 1367, after having been summoned to Parliament, from 1363 to 1366, as Lord Bohun of Midhurst. His second wife, Cecilia, was another heiress, and she brought the good estates of the Filiols, of Essex. Their only child, another 
2 CONC John, lived for 57 years after his father's death.
2 CONT *   This John seems to have been a troublesome person, for among the Bills in Chancery, perferred to Thomas Arundel, Archbishop of York, whilst Chancellor (15 to 20 Rich. II.) is one from the Burgesses of Midhurst, praying that he
2 CONC  might find fresh securities in L40, to keep the peace, the sureties already given, viz., John Bramshote and William Tailard, not having tenements of that value.
2 CONT *   When he died, he left Midhurst for his feoffees (men of note in Sussex) to grant to his widow Anne, which they did on 4th January, 1440 [18 Hen. VI.]
2 CONT *   Know all (39) present and future that we, Hugh Halsham, knt., (40) John Lyndesfeld, clerk, William Ryman, (41) Walter Vere, Richard Wakehurst, (42) William Sydeney, John Lelye, and Walter Urry, (43) feoffees of Sir John Bohun,
2 CONC  knight, lately dead, to perform his last will and testament, have given, and by these our charter, have confirmed to Anne, late wife of the said John Bohun, all these our manors of Cowdray, Midhurst, Eseborne, and Farnehurst, wit
2 CONC h all members, &c., to hold to her for her life.
2 CONT *   That he married late in life is clear, for his eldest son, Humphrey, was only 14 when his father died. This Humphrey died about 50 years old, for his will was made on 2nd Nov., 1468, and proved ten days after. .... etc. ______
2 CONC ________________________
2 CONT *William de Braose, (sometimes William de Briouze, William de Breuse, William de Brewes or William de Brewose; c. 1260–1326) was the second Baron Braose, as well as Lord of Gower and Lord of Bramber. He was held as a hostage after
2 CONC  being captured in 1264 during the Second Barons' War and records of some of his childhood expenses survive from his time as a hostage. He first entered royal service in 1286 and, in 1291, he succeeded his father as baron. He cont
2 CONC inued in royal military service, serving in Scotland as well as in Wales. Protracted disputes over his lands embroiled him throughout his life and at the end of his life helped spark a revolt against King Edward II of England's fa
2 CONC vourites, the Despensers. He married twice, and his heirs were his daughter Aline and his grandson John de Bohun.
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *The name of Braose's first wife was Agnes,[16] but her family is not known. His second wife was Elizabeth, the daughter and heiress of Raymund de Sully. He had two daughters with his first wife, but no children with his second wi
2 CONC fe, who outlived him.[1] It appears that there was a son named William, who was the subject of a military summons from King Edward in 1311, but nothing further is mentioned of him after 1315. In 1316 a settlement of William the fa
2 CONC ther's estates made no mention of this son making it likely that the son died before this date.[17]
2 CONT *Braose died not long before 1 May 1326[1] and his heirs were his daughter Aline and his grandson John de Bohun.[18] Aline, the elder daughter,[13] married John de Mowbray and Richard de Peschale. The second daughter, '''Joan, mar
2 CONC ried James de Bohun''' and Richard Foliot. Mowbray received the lands of Gower and Bramber before Braose's death.[1]
2 CONT *Braose was known as a man often in debt and as being unable to manage his cash flow well.[17] Thomas Walsingham stated in his chronicle that Braose was "very rich by descent but a dissipater of the property left to him".[19]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Braose,_2nd_Baron_Braose ______________________________
2 CONT
