1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Verdon-14
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Miles de Verdun''', also known as Milo de Verdon, was a younger son of Thebaud Verdun and Margery Bohun. [1]. He was born about 1280, and died after 14 Feb 1327.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Rebel Robert de Verdun had been slain in the course of the war, but some of his line continued to hold lands in Oriel, while Milo de Verdun, brother of the Theobald who had died in 1316, remained head of the family, and was a
2 CONC ncester of the Verduns of Clonmore in Louth." [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [https://www.libraryireland.com/Pedigrees2/jordan-de-exeter.php Jordan (De Exeter) family genealogy] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Grace’s Annals the following entries of this family are to be found:
2 CONT
2 CONT >A.D. 1312: “Milo De Verdon married the daughter of Richard De Exonia, Dexter, or De Exeter. This great Connaught family of De Exeter assumed at this time the name of Jordan or MacJordan, and Richard De Exonia was Chief Justice in
2 CONC  Banco.” (State Papers, Edward II., page 117.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theobald_de_Verdun,_2nd_Baron_Verdun Theobald de Verdun (1278–1316)] > Wikipedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT >https://www.geni.com/people/Theobald-de-Verdun-2nd-Lord-of-Verdun/6000000001891596714?through=6000000002603763168, (1278–1316) was the second and eldest surviving son of Theobald de Verdun, 1st Baron Verdun, of Alton, Staffordshi
2 CONC re, and his wife Margery de Bohun.[1][2]. …. [he]  lived mainly in Ireland, and held the office of Justiciar of Ireland in 1314-15. '''His three brothers''' rebelled against the Crown in 1312-13, but Theobald himself seems to have
2 CONC  remained loyal. … The Verdun estates in Ireland passed to his four daughters as co-heiresses. Some of the Verdun estates later came to his surviving brothers, '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Nicholas-de-Verdon-Knight/600000000218
2 CONC 8651080?through=6000000002603763168 Nicholas] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Milo-de-Verdon/6000000002603763168 Milo]''', and passed to their sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [https://what-when-how.com/medieval-ireland/verdon-de-medieval-ireland/ VERDON, DE (Medieval Ireland)] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1266, [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-John-le-Botiller-de-Verdun/6000000003168659110?through=6000000003243537190 John de Verdun] began his attempt to regain the full judicial liberties once held by Walter in Meath. This legal 
2 CONC battle was continued by Theobald I (d. 1309), but the de Verdons were unsuccessful, although this privilege had been granted to de Geneville. The failure to secure these rights may have contributed to the reorientation of the main
2 CONC  de Verdon line away from Ireland and toward the Welsh March, where their franchise remained wide. Nevertheless, de Verdon authority over their tenants in Louth remained strong, such that Robert (a younger brother of Theobald II) 
2 CONC was able to lead much of the county in the still unexplained "de Verdon rebellion" in 1312.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gaelic Revival may also have played a part in the reorientation of the main de Verdon line. During John’s absence on crusade in 1271, his sons Nicholas and John were killed defending the family lands in Louth. Another John, th
2 CONC e eldest son of Theobald I, was also killed by the Irish in 1297. Theobald II divided his time between England and Ireland far less equally than his immediate forbears, and he appointed his younger brother Milo as chief guardian o
2 CONC f his lands and fees in Ireland in 1309. Arguably this did not represent a lack of interest in Ireland, but rather a sensible approach to the problem of cross-Irish Sea land-holding in a period of political uncertainty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald de Verdon had no sons. His lands were divided between his widow, Elizabeth de Clare (d. 1360), and his four daughters. The final partition was effected in 1332, custody of the lands in the interim being granted to Theobal
2 CONC d’s brothers, Milo and Nicholas, who actively defended them during the Bruce invasion. The absentee de Verdon co-heirs eventually sold their lands in Ireland between 1366 and around 1378.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Smith, Brendan, '1 False Dawn: 1330–1369', Crisis and Survival in Late Medieval Ireland: The English of Louth and Their Neighbours, 1330-1450 (Oxford, 2013; online edn, Oxford Academic, 26 Sept. 2013), https://doi.org/10.1093
2 CONC /acprof:oso/9780199594757.003.0002, accessed 7 Mar. 2023.
2 CONT
2 CONT > The execution of Roger Mortimer in 1330 promised more stable times ahead for the English of Louth, although it did not end the influence there of the Mortimer family. The most powerful resident lords, Nicholas and Milo Verdon, e
2 CONC njoyed influence in England as a result of the '''marriages of their nieces''', and participated in Edward III’s Scottish campaigns. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Descent 
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [https://www.de-verdon.uk/ The de Verdun family of England, Normandy & Ireland] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Henry's brother was Sir George Frederic Verdon KCMG CB FRS of Queen Street Melbourne and Alton, Upper Macedon, Victoria, Australia, a well known Australian politician, diplomat and (later) banker. Their father Edward Verdon came 
2 CONC from Dublin and was a descendant of  https://www.geni.com/people/Milo-de-Verdon/6000000002603763168,  a younger brother of https://www.geni.com/people/Theobald-de-Verdun-2nd-Lord-of-Verdun/6000000001891596714 of Alton in Staffords
2 CONC hire.   Milo was the founder of the “Verdons of Clonmore in Co. Louth,”  who produced quite a few priests, both Catholic and Anglican.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [https://www.de-verdon.uk/home/verdins-of-darnhall-garnstone Verdins of Darnhall & Garnstone] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT >The Verdin family are descended from Milo de Verdon of Clonmore in Co. Louth, son of Theobald II de Verdon. Milo's son James de Verdon, who died before 1st September 1384, married Isabella Gaydoun (or Gayton), whose second husban
2 CONC d was Thomas Talbot. James and Isabella had, with other children, a son also called James, whose son John Verdon, said to have been born c.1415 and who lived at Painstone, Clonmore, married Eleanor ___.  ….
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Cruys Sir John Cruys or Cruise (died 1407)] > Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT The Verdon inheritance
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1386 the King's Escheator was ordered to convey to Cruys and his wife Matilda Verdon the lands of Clonmore (now Togher) and Mansfieldtown in County Louth.[3] Matilda, whom he married before 1375, was the daughter and co-heiress
2 CONC  with her sister Anna, wife of John Bellew of Bellewstown, of https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Thomas-Verdon-of-Clonmore/6000000189901505826  (died 1375), head of the dominant Anglo-Norman family in County Louth and his wife Joan H
2 CONC artort. Matilda's first husband was Peter Howth.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father was a grand-nephew of Theobald de Verdun, 2nd Baron Verdun (died 1316).[1] Matilda's recovery of Clonmore was the result of a determined and lengthy legal struggle against her male cousins, whom her father had tried to 
2 CONC make his heirs, ignoring the right of his daughters to inherit his lands.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
