1 NOTE {geni:about_me} On the matter of the various numbering systems for the Earls of Arundel, Wikipedia remarks, “ The Earls of Arundel have been numbered differently depending on whether the claims of the first seven to have been Earl
2 CONC s by tenancy are accepted. Richard FitzAlan was the first member of the FitzAlan family to be definitely styled Earl of Arundel. He is therefore counted variously as the 1st, 6th or 8th Earl.” See https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/R
2 CONC ichard_Fitzalan,_1st_Earl_of_Arundel. For a full list of the Earls of Arundel, see https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earl_of_Arundel
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Richard FitzAlan, 8th Earl of Arundel (7th Earl of Arundel per Ancestral Roots) (3 February 1266/7 – 9 March 1301/2) was an English Norman medieval nobleman."
2 CONT
2 CONT === Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/FitzAlan-606
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard FitzAlan, 8th or 1st Earl of Arundel[1][2][3][4][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son and heir of John FitzAlan.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 3 February 1266/7.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT He married about 1285 Alasia Saluzzo, daughter of Tomas, Marquis of Saluzzo and Luisa Ceva.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She died 25 September 1292 and was buried at Todingham Priory.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT # Edmund Fitzalan, 9th Earl of Arundel
2 CONT # John (priest)
2 CONT # Alice m. Stephen de Segrave, 3rd Lord Segrave.
2 CONT # Margaret m. William le Botiller (Butler)
2 CONT # Eleanor m. Henry de Percy, 1st Baron Percy
2 CONT Career
2 CONT
2 CONT He had seizin of his lands, 8 December 1287.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1272–1302: Earl of Arundel (after attaining majority in 1289 became Earl of Arundel, summoned to Parliament as such) (7th Earl of Arundel).
2 CONT
2 CONT Feudal Lord of Clun and Oswaldestre
2 CONT
2 CONT Fought in Welsh Wars, 1288; in Gascony 1295-97; and in Scottish Wars, 1289-1300.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT He died 9 March 1301/2 and was buried with his ancestors.[1][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT "The bodies of both Richard and Alesia were at Haughmond Abbey, Shropshire, by 1341, when provision was made for 12 candles to burn in the church of Haughmond around their tombs[7]"
2 CONT
2 CONT FitzAlan or de Arundel
2 CONT
2 CONT Richardson[8] states: Earl Richard Fitz Alan (FitzAlan) above, his father and grandfather all employed the surname Fitz Alan . In the 1270's, Earl Richard's father was styled John "Fitz Alan de Arundel" in several records [see C.P
2 CONC .R. 1272-1281 (1901): 11, 96, 161, 331]. In 1291 Earl Richard received a grant addressed to him as "Richard de Arundel, Earl of Arundel" [see C.P. 1 (1910): 241]. In the same period, 1291-1302, Earl Richard's brother, John, was li
2 CONC kewise styled John de Arundel [see Rees Cartulary of Haughmond Abbey (1985): 227]. Following Earl Richard's death in 1302, the family dropped the surname Fitz Alan in favor of de Arundel (or simply Arundel). The last known use of 
2 CONC the name Fitz Alan by any member of this family dates c.1312-3, when Earl Richard's son, Edmund, brought a writ as "Edmund Fitz Alan" [see Year Books of Edward III 12 (Rolls Ser. 31b) (1905): 518-521]. Thereafter, all further refe
2 CONC rences to this family employ the surname Arundel to the complete exclusion of the surname Fitz Alan. Specifically, Earl Richard's son Edmund (died 1326), both of his brothers, two of his sons and all four of his grandsons all empl
2 CONC oyed the Arundel surname. Edmund's sister Alice is likewise styled "de Arundel" in an ancient Segrave Family ped. VCH Surrey 1 (1902): 348, footnote 1 observes that Richard's son and heir, Edmund, is "commonly called Fitz Alan but
2 CONC  the real designation of the family was then de Arundel." Nicholas, a well known antiquarian, states: "This family presents a singular instance of adopting the name of their title as the surname of the family, for after the marria
2 CONC ge of John Fitz-Alan, Lord of Clun, with Isabel, the sister and co-heir of Hugh D'Albini, Earl of Arundel, all the descendants called themselves Arundel instead of Fitz-Alan" [see Nicolas Testamenta Vetusta 1 (1826): 105]. "J.G.N.
2 CONC " in Gentleman's magazine 103 Oxfordshire, etc., .......
2 CONT
2 CONT
