1 NAME John /White/
2 GIVN John
2 SURN White
2 _MARNM White
1 NAME John White of Martock
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1340
2 PLAC Martock, Somerset, England
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He is probably the son of the Thomas White who was assessed in Martock in 1333, but might be a grandson, (great-)nephew, or cousin of a close degree.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''If it weren't for John White of Martock getting into the Gascon Rolls, his line might have been erased for all time.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ====================================
2 CONT
2 CONT C 61/84
2 CONT Gascon Roll for the 45th year of the reign of Edward III
2 CONT
2 CONT     1 February 1371. Westminster . For protection.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To all bailiffs and faithful people.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Letters of protection with clause volumus for one year granted to John White of Martock in Somerset , his men, lands, rents, things and all possessions, as he is going in the king's service to Aquitaine, to stay there in the c
2 CONC ompany of John Shakell. They are to be maintained, protected and defended from injury, molestation, damage and harm, and amends should be made for any forfeitures they have suffered without delay.
2 CONT
2 CONT     By bill of p.s.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT There turns out to be a dramatic story behind this casual note. The mentioned John Shakell, along with Robert Hawley, had during an earlier campaign captured a Spanish grandee, the Count of Denia, at the battle of Najera (3 April 
2 CONC 1367). The Count had been released on condition that he leave his eldest son, Alfonso, as a hostage. Ten years went by before a ransom was declared to be ready. Hawley and Shakell were ordered to produce their hostage, who was bei
2 CONC ng kept in secret, in Parliament; they refused, and were sent to the Tower. Some months later, they escaped and took sanctuary at Westminster - but the sanctuary was violated *''during the celebration of High Mass''*(!!!) and Hawl
2 CONC ey, along with an innocent bystander, was killed. Shakell saved himself by surrendering without resistance. It was a major scandal, and all the perpetrators were publicly and repeatedly censured from the pulpit; but thereafter san
2 CONC ctuary protection for debtors was eliminated. Shakell was returned to the Tower and remained there until 1379, when he made terms with the government and revealed his hostage as his supposed body servant, who had been allowed to a
2 CONC ttend him in prison. -- Reference:  ''Genesis of Lancaster'', Volume 2,  By Sir James Henry Ramsay, pp. 121-122 ff.
1 FAMC @F6000000019861928132@
1 RFN geni:6000000019861928127
1 SUBM @S6000000018860914918@
