1 NOTE {geni:about_me} >'''His precise place''' in Montgomery ancestry '''and''' his exact ancestry are disputed. 
2 CONT >'''His title''' "10th Baron of Eagllesham" cannot be adequately proven with extant sources.
2 CONT >'''Please review''' the attached document in Sources titled: Stirnet - Montgomery Pedigree (2015)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~mainegenie/genealogy/MONTGMRY.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT 15I. JOHN (ROGER 1, WILLIAM 2, HUGH 3, ROGER 4, ROGER 5, ARNULPH 6, PHILIP 7, ROBERT 8, ALAN 9, JOHN 10, ALAN 11, JOHN 12, JOHN 13, ALEXANDER 14)
2 CONT b.c.1338
2 CONT m.c.1360 Otterburn, ELIZABETH de EGLINTON, d. of Sir Hugh Eglinton and Agnes Mores
2 CONT d. before July 1401
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Montgomerie of Eaglesham''' is the first member of the family whose position can be ascertained with certainty, and from whom the descent is clear.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage with Elizabeth de Eglinton, sole heiress of Sir Hugh de Eglinton of Eglinton, justiciary of Lothian in 1361, he obtained the baronies of Eglinton and Ardrossan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1388 he accompanied his brother-in-law, Sir James Douglas, second earl of Douglas, in an expedition to England. At the battle of Otterburn, where Douglas was slain, Montgomerie, according to the Scots version of the ballad on t
2 CONC he battle, fought 'handle to handle right valyauntly' with Sir Henry Percy, known as 'Hotspur,' the commander of the English, in single combat and took him prisoner.(1) It is said that, in lieu of ransom, Sir Henry was required to
2 CONC  build at Eaglesham the castle of Polnoon, now in ruins, but long the chief seat of the Eglinton family. In 1391 Montgomerie, for service to the king and the Duke of Rothesay, received an annuity from the customs of Edinburgh and 
2 CONC Linlithgow (2)
2 CONT
2 CONT On 9 December 1389 Sir James Lindsay granted to his 'cosyng' John Montgomerie of Eagleshame an obligation not to deprive him of the lands of Dunbulg and Carney. He styles himself John of Montgomerie, Lord of Eaglesham, in a charte
2 CONC r of the lands of Little Benan, dated at Eagleshame on 8 October 1392. He is said to have died between that date and 1398, but is found receiving payment of a pension for attendance on the King and Duke of Rothesay for the year Ma
2 CONC y 1399 to May 1400. He, however, deceased before July 1401. (3)
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue-
2 CONT
2 CONT # I. Hugh- b.c.1360. Hugh was shot through the heart at the battle of Otterburn. “ Another son, Hugh, is said to have been killed at Otterburn, but no mention of him is found except in the ballad, and ''Sir William Fraser doubts h
2 CONC is existence''.1
2 CONT # 16. II. JOHN- b.c.1362, m.1. Agnes MacDonald, 2. MARGARET MAXWELL, d. 22 Nov. 1429 Androssan.  SIR JOHN MONTGOMERIE, who styles himself Lord of Ardrossan in his charters.
2 CONT # III. Alexander- Lord of Bonnington. Alexander, in a grant to Alan Lander of an annualrent of 4 merks sterling from his lands of Platt, Westhall, and Northraw in Ratho, speaks of Sir Hugh Eglintoun as his grandfather. The annualr
2 CONC ent was in repayment of a loan to release his lands from Sir James Douglas of Dalkeith, and his wife Egidia Stewart, widow of Sir Hugh. Elizabeth of Eglinton, in a charter of the same lands to Alan Lauder, not dated, refers to Joh
2 CONC n of Montgomerie her son and heir, and Alexander Montgomerie her son.(4)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref:
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) Chronicles of England, France, Spain and the adjoining countries, from the latter part of the reign of Edward II to the coronations of Henry IV- Jean Froissart, translated by Thomas Jhones, Esq, 1857- Vol. I, p. 428
2 CONT (2) Exchquer Rolls of Scotland, iii. 280 et seq.; Memorials- Sir William Fraser, Vol. I, pp.15,17; II, 17
2 CONT (3) Scots Peerage- Vol. III, pp. 427-9 < [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft/page/429/mode/1up Archive.Org] > ALEXANDER MONTGOMERIE is the next on record, and may have been the son of
2 CONC  John, but of him little is known, as the only notice of him is in two safe-conducts, dated on 20 ^ May and 24 October 1358, permitting him to pass througli England on his way to visit holy places.4 According to the Memorials he m
2 CONC arried * a daughter of William, first Earl of Douglas, and his wife Margaret, daughter of the Earl of Dunbar and March,' but as William, Earl of Douglas (see that title), had no such wife nor daughter, the name of Alexander Montgo
2 CONC merie's wife remains unknown. He apparently had issue : — 1. JOHN, who succeeded.  JOHN MONTGOMERIE of Eaglesham is the first member of the family whose position can be ascertained with certainty ….
2 CONT (4) Ibid- p.428
2 CONT (5) The Battle of Otterburn- from "The Border Minstrelsy" by Sir Walter Scott- go to: http://www.musicanet.org/robokopp/scottish/itfellab.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT John Montgomery, 10th Baron of Eaglesham was the distinguished warrior who captured Henry Percy, called Hotspur, at the Battle of Otterburn in 1388. With Percy's ransom he built the castle of Polnoon as well as acquiring the lands
2 CONC  of Eglinton and Ardrossan through his marriage to Elizabeth of Eglinton.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT * History from the Clan Montgomery Society
2 CONT * "Origin and History of the Montgomerys" by B.G. Montgomery of Sweden
2 CONT * Correspondence with Brent Friedrichs, Box 34, Charter Oak, IA 51439, 712-678-3720, email: blf@ia.frontiercomm.net, Dec. 1996.
