2 CONT * '''Sir John Montgomery1
2 CONT * '''M, #3452, d. July 1401
2 CONT * Father	Sir Alexander Montgomerie d. c 1370
2 CONT * Mother	Margaret Douglas
2 CONT * '''Sir John Montgomery was born at of Eglesham and Androssan. He married Elizabeth Eglington, daughter of Sir Hugh Eglinton, Justiciar of Lothian and Agnes Mure. Sir John Montgomery died in July 1401.
2 CONT * '''Family: Elizabeth Eglington
2 CONT * '''Children'''	
2 CONT ** Hugh Montgomerie d. Aug 1388
2 CONT ** (Miss) Montgomery+
2 CONT ** Sir John Montgomerie+ d. b 22 Nov 1429
2 CONT ** Alexander Montgomerie
2 CONT * '''Citations'''
2 CONT * 1. [S403] Unknown author, Europaische Stammtafeln by Isenburg, chart 640, Vol. 3; Magna Charta Sureties, 1215, by F. L. Weis, 4th Ed., p. 16.
2 CONT * From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p115.htm#i3452
2 CONT * '''John Montgomery1
2 CONT * M, #260382
2 CONT *Last Edited=24 Sep 2012
2 CONT * '''John Montgomery married Elizabeth Eglinton, daughter of Sir Hugh Eglinton of that Ilk and Egidia Stewart.1
2 CONT * '''He obtained the territorial Baronies of Eglinton and Ardrossan through his wife.1 He fought in the Battle of Otterburn in 1388, where he captured Sir Henry Percy.1
2 CONT *'''Children of John Montgomery and Elizabeth Eglinton
2 CONT ** 1. Sir John Montgomerie of Ardrossan+1 d. fr 1426/27 - Nov 1429
2 CONT ** 2. unknown daughter Montgomery1
2 CONT ** 3. Alexander Montgomery1
2 CONT ** 4. Hugh Montgomery1 d. 5 Aug 1388
2 CONT * '''Citations'''
2 CONT *1. BP2003 volume 1, page 1279. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT * From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p26039.htm#i260382 
2 CONT
2 CONT ====WIKISOURCE: [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1885-1900/Montgomerie,_John Dictionary of National Biography], Vol. 38 Montgomerie, John
2 CONT * '''MONTGOMERIE, Sir JOHN, ninth of Eaglesham and first of Eglinton and Ardrossan (d. 1398?), was the only son''' of Sir Alexander de Montgomerie, eighth of Eaglesham and first of Eglinton and Ardrossan, by a daughter of William,
2 CONC  first earl of Douglas. The Montgomeries of Scotland trace their descent from Robert de Montgomerie (d. 1177), a supposed descendant of Roger of Montgomery (d. 1094) [q. v.], who was created Earl of Shrewsbury, and was father of H
2 CONC ugh, earl of Shrewsbury (d. 1098) [q. v.] Robert de Montgomerie accompanied Walter, son of Alan, first high steward of Scotland, from Wales to Scotland, and received from him the manor of Eaglesham, Renfrewshire.
2 CONT *'''Sir John Montgomerie, ninth of Eaglesham, succeeded his father about 1380, and by his marriage with Elizabeth de Eglinton, sole heiress of Sir Hugh de Eglinton of Eglinton, justiciary of Lothian in 1361, obtained the baronies 
2 CONC of Eglinton and Ardrossan. In 1388 he accompanied his brother-in-law''', Sir James Douglas, second earl of Douglas [q. v.], '''in an expedition to England. At the battle of Otterburn, where Douglas was slain, Montgomerie, accordin
2 CONC g to the Scots version of the ballad on the battle, worsted Sir Henry Percy, surnamed Hotspur, the commander of the English, in single combat and took him prisoner. With the ransom of Percy he built at Eaglesham the castle of Poln
2 CONC oon, now in ruins, but long the chief seat of the Eglinton family. In 1391 Montgomerie, for service to the king and the Duke of Rothesay, received an annuity from the customs of Edinburgh and Linlithgow (Exchequer Rolls of Scotlan
2 CONC d, iii. 280 et seq.) He died about 1398, leaving three sons'''—Sir John, who succeeded him, and was father of Sir Alexander, first lord Montgomerie [q. v.]; Alexander of Bonnington, and Hugh, shot with an arrow through the heart a
2 CONC t Otterburn.
2 CONT *[Froissart's Chronicles; ancient ballad on the battle of Otterburn; Exchequer Kolls of Scotland, vol. iii.; Sir William Fraser's Earls of Eglinton; Douglas's Scottish Peerage (Wood), i. 494.]
2 CONT '''————————————————————————————————'''
2 CONT ====BOOK
2 CONT * '''''A Genealogical History of the family of Montgomery: including the Montgomery'''''... By Thomas Harrison Montgomery. [﻿﻿https://www.google.com/books/edition/_/vWs6AAAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA52&dq=john+de+montgomery+1429+Maxw
2 CONC ell Googe Books]
2 CONT * '''Pg.46'''
2 CONT *  XIV. Sir ALEXANDER de MONTGOMERIE of Eaglesham and Eastwood, succeeded his father. In a charter by David II., dated 1357, he is designed "Alexander de Montgomery de Eglisham, filius Johannis de Montgomery." "He seems to have be
2 CONC en a man of ability and trust, .... etc.
2 CONT * '''Pg. 47'''
2 CONT * married a daughter of William, first Earl of Douglas, by his second wife, Margaret, daughter of the Earl of Dunbar and March, by whom he had a son who succeeded him prior to the year 1388."(1)
2 CONT * '''XV. SIR JOHN de MONTGOMERIE of Eaglesham and Eastwood, and afterwards of Eglinton and Ardrossan, was the son of Sir Alexander. "He married in 1361, Elizabeth, the daughter and heiress of Sir Hugh Eglinton of Eglinton, and by 
2 CONC her obtained the large possessions of that family on the death of her father, which occurred about  the year 1374, together with Ardrossan, the heiress of which estate one of her ancestors had married. Sir John quartered with his 
2 CONC own the Eglinton arms, which were gules, three rings or, gemmed azure."(2)
2 CONT * '''The EGLINTONS, whose name furnished a title to a branch of Sir John de Montgomerie's descendants, was a family of much antiquity in Scotland. The first one recorded is
2 CONT * EGLIN,(3) Lord of Eglintoun, who assumed the name from the lordship and castle of which he was the possessor. He lived during the reign of Malcolm III. (1057-1093), and was the father of
2 CONT * BRYCE  .... etc.
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT * Sir HUGH DE EGLINTOUN, his son, lived in the reign of David II. and Robert II. He was variously styled, "Hugone Domino de Eglintoun," and "Hugo de Eglintoun, miles." He was Justiciary of Lothian in 1362; and one of the conventio
2 CONC n, held September 1, 1367, at Muirhouslaw, relative to the affairs of the Marches. He appears to have been twice married; first to the heiress of Ardrossan,(4) possibly the daughter of Sir Fergus of Ardrossan, who was one of the S
2 CONC cottish nobles who accompanied Edward Bruce on his expedition into Ireland, in 1315; which opening with many successes, terminated in disaster to most of those who were concerned in it.(5) Sir Hugh married, secondly Egidia, the da
2 CONC ughter of Walter the high Steward, by his second wife, and half-sister of king Robert II., and who was widow of Sir James Lindsay of Craufurd. He had one daughter, '''Elizabeth, who was the wife of Sir John de Montgomerie. As she 
2 CONC is mentioned as the heiress of Eglinton and Ardrosson, it is presumed she was the daughter by the first wife, and that Sir Hugh had no other children; the date also of her marriage is not in favor of the supposition, which has bee
2 CONC n generally maintained, of her relationship to the king'''. In 1372, Sir Hugh had a charter from Robert of the lands of Bonnington and Nortoun, and of Westhall and Cotraw; "Dilecto fratri nostro Hugone de Eglyntoun, militis et car
2 CONC issime sorori nostre Egidie de Lyndesay, sponse ejus **** apud Perth. 28 Junii, an reg. 2nd." He is said to have died about the year 1374; and his is supposed to .... etc.
2 CONT *Pg. 48
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT * '''Sir John de Montgomerie of Eaglesham, greatly distinguished himself at the battle of Otterburne, where James Earl Douglas, who was his uncle, was slain.2  .... etc.  Hugh, the eldest son of Sir John, lost his life there, as t
2 CONC he ballad has it: 
2 CONT ** .... etc. 
2 CONT * '''Sir John granted on October, 1396, to William de Blakeford, the lands of Ardbennane, within two years from which time he died; he had by his wife, Elizabeth, who survived him, four sons,
2 CONT ** I. Sir HUGH, who died at the battle of Otterburne, in August 1388, from the good aim of an English archer, in revenging the death of Earl Douglas. . . . 
2 CONT ** '''Pg. 49'''
2 CONT ** II. Sir JOHN, who succeeded his father.
2 CONT ** III. ALEXANDER, to whom his mother gave a charter of the lands of Bonnington, in the barony of Ratho. .... etc.
2 CONT ** '''Pg.50''' 
2 CONT ** IV. HUGH, who was -- it is presumed-- born after the death of the eldest son. He was tutor to Hugh, the third Lord Montgomerie, and lived to a very advanced age, having survived four generations of the chief of his family.
2 CONT *  XVI. Sir JOHN de MONTGOMERIE, "Dominus Ejusdem, or of the Ilk," which title shows he was the male heir and chief of the French house of Montgomerie.
2 CONT * "This ancient and noble title-- That Ilk -- is peculiar to the Scots, and has never been hitherto defined well. It is the title which denotes -- not the gentleman, as Johnson's degrading appellation, (viz., whose surname and the
2 CONC  title of his estate are the same,) but also the chief of all the clan of his own surname. It does not necessarily or essentially refer to the estate. McFarlan, McIntosh, Brisbane, Tweedie, &c., of that Ilk, have never had lands o
2 CONC f the name of their surnames. .... etc. 
2 CONT * '''Pg. 51'''
2 CONT * Sir John de Montgomerie " succeeded his father before 1398. In 1402 he formed on the chiefs of the Scotch army which invaded England, and was taken prisoner at the disastrous battle of Halidon Hill." He was not long a captive, h
2 CONC owever, for two years after he was in .... etc.
2 CONT * Sir John married Margaret, daughter of Sir Robert Maxwell of Caerlaverock, (ancestor of the Earls of Nithsdale,) and dying previous to November, 1429, left by her three sons and three daughters.
2 CONT ** I. ALEXANDER, .... etc.
2 CONT ** '''Pg. 52''' _______________________
2 CONT * '''The Magna Charta Sureties, 1215: The Barons Named in the Magna Charta, 1215 ... By Frederick Lewis Weis, Walter Lee Sheppard, William Ryland Beall. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Magna_Charta_Sureties_1215/59XcwoRK
2 CONC 9jkC?q=eglinton&gbpv=1#f=false Google Books]:—
2 CONT * '''Pg.16, Line 15A'''
2 CONT * 4. AVELINA FITZ JOHN (15-4); m. Walter de Burgh, Earl of Ulster.
2 CONT * 5. EDIGIA (or Jill) de BURGH; m. James Stewart, 5th High Steward of Scotland, b. c. 1243, d. 1309. (SP I, 13-14).
2 CONT * 6. WALTER STEWART, 6th High Steward of Scotland, b. 1292, d. 9 Apr. 1326; m. (1) Marjory Bruce, by whom Robert I, King of Scots; m. (2) Isabel Graham, sisster of Sir John Graham of Abercorn. (SP I, 14-15).
2 CONT * 7. EGIDIA (or Jill) STEWART, by 2nd wife, widow of Sir James Lindsay of Crawford; m. as (2) wife, after Oct. 1357, Sir Hugh Eglinton of that Ilk. (SP I, 15).
2 CONT * '''Elizabeth No. 8 was child of Sir Hugh by his first wife Agnes Mure, not dau. of Egidia No. 7.
2 CONT * ''' 8. ELIZABETH EGLINTON; m. Sir John Montgomery, d. bef. July 1401, captured Sir Henry "Hotspur" Percy at the battle of Otterburn, 1388. (SP III, 427-428).
2 CONT * 9. SIR JOHN MONTGOMERY of Eglinton and Ardrossan, d. bef. 22 Nov. 1429; m. (1) Agnes of the Isles, d. bef. Mar. 1413/4. (SP III, 428-430).
2 CONT * 10. .... etc. ____________________________
