2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Scots Peerage''''', Vol. 2, by J.B. Paul, [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun02paul/page/445/mode/1up?q=carrick pp. 446-447]. 
2 CONT >'''John Kennedy married a lady named Mary, r'''eferred to in his charter of 29 November 1371, '''but who she was is not certain.''' ''It has been variously stated that she was the Marjorie Montgomerie from whom or with whom he ac
2 CONC quired Cassillis, but if so, she must have been his second wife''; '''that she was Mary de Carrick, or a Mary Douglas,' but no evidence on the point has yet been found.''' — '''NOTE:''' The Corrigenda and Errata volume made no cha
2 CONC nges to this quoted matter.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The lands of Castlys, or Cassillis, were first owned by the Montgomery family and it may be they who built the first castle at Cassillis as a Neil de Montgomerie is said to have been born there around 1276. It is thought that ther
2 CONC e may have been a castle at Cassillis as early as the 12th century. Around 1360 the then owner of the lands, Sir John Montgomery de Castlys, died, and Cassillis seems to have passed jointly to his daughter, Marjory de Montgomery, 
2 CONC and his cousin, also Marjory de Montgomery. In 1373, upon the marriage of the younger Marjory to Sir John Kennedy of Dunure, Cassillis passed into the Kennedy family. The ancestors of the Kennedys had lived in the area since at le
2 CONC ast the mid-12th century, the first of the family to be mentioned in a charter being a Duncan de Carrick. 
2 CONT See more at: http://www.stravaiging.com/history/castle/cassillis-house#sthash.Tj7PTAqe.dpuf '''<—''' This provides no genealogical information about Sir John Kennedy's wife.
2 CONT
