1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =John V Le Strange=
2 CONT son of '''John IV le Strange''' and '''Joan de Somery''' Coat of Arms: https://books.google.com/books?id=lfIxAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA368&lpg=PA368&dq=coat+of+arms+le+strange&source=bl&ots=iueSvvqgTp&sig=Vg_LGSOMXY6X2kI6GyaejMIg0XE&hl=en&sa=
2 CONC X&ved=0CE0Q6AEwDmoVChMItcCXy4qLxwIVgY8NCh3rVwtE#v=onepage&q=coat%20of%20arms%20le%20strange&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT FMG Medieval Lands database:
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN [V] le Strange ([1252/53]-8 Aug 1309 or before).  
2 CONT * The Chronicle of Peterborough names "dominus Rogerus le Estrange capitaneus…dominus Johannes le Estrange" among those sent to fight Llywelyn Prince of Wales in 1282[299].  
2 CONT * Eyton gives some details about his descendants in Shropshire[300].  
2 CONT * He was summoned to Parliament in 1299 whereby he is held to have become Lord Strange.
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ELEANOR, daughter of --- & his wife Joan [de Somery].   m secondly MAUD, daughter of --- (-after 30 Oct 1309).  LORDS STRANGE[301].
2 CONT
2 CONT [299] Stapleton, T. (ed.) (1849) Chronicon Petroburgense (London) ("Peterborough Chronicle"), p. 57.  [300] Eyton (1859), Vol. IX, pp. 26-7.  [301] CP XII.1, pp. 352-7. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/gen-medieval/1997-06/0865180184:
2 CONT
2 CONT From: Ed Mann <edmann@MAIL2.COMMNECTIONS.COM>  Subject: LeStrange of Knockyn  Date: Sun, 1 Jun 1997 10:49:44 -0500
2 CONT
2 CONT GE>I have two questions re. the Lestrange's of Knockyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT GE>Who were the ancestors of John III LeStrange (d.1269)? The refs. given in GE>Weis" "Ancestral Roots. . ." (255:30), where he is mentioned are: Topo et GE>Geneal. II 130; CP XII (1) 350-351, Eyton, Antiquities of Shropshire X 26
2 CONC 2; GE>Ewen, Observations on the LaStranges, chart, p.1.
2 CONT
2 CONT Per Burke's Dormant & Extinct Peerages, p. 515:
2 CONT
2 CONT John le Strange, d. 1233.
2 CONT
2 CONT >From somewhere, I have a note that the line starts with:
2 CONT
2 CONT Roland le Strange, d. 1158 = Maud Hunstanton
2 CONT John le Strange, d. 1178 then John le Strange, d. 1233.
2 CONT
2 CONT Again, no source for the first two generations.
2 CONT
2 CONT GE>The son of the above mentioned John III was John IV (d.1257). I have a Sir GE>Roger of Knockyn (d.1382), who m. Aline Fitzalan, mentioned in Weis" GE>"Ancestral Roots. . ." (27:32), (71:33), (93A:31). I would expect him to GE>b
2 CONC e a descendant of John IV. Can anyone tell me how so?
2 CONT
2 CONT Gleaned from Burke's: John III le Strange, d. bef 1270 = Lucy de Tregoz John IV le Strange, d. 1279 = Joan de Somery, d. 1282 John V le Strange, b. abt 1253, d. abt 1309 = Maud de Deyville John VI le Strange, d. 1312 = Isolda de W
2 CONC alton, d. aft 1311 Roger le Strange, 1301 - 1349 = Maud, bef 1305 - bef 1344 Roger le Strange, abt 1327 - 1381, 5th Baron Strange of Knockyn
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/gen-medieval/1999-11/0942592574:
2 CONT
2 CONT From: Sutliff <sssbo@earthlink.net>  Subject: Re: Mysterious Maud who married John le Strange  Date: Sun, 14 Nov 1999 15:16:14 +0000
2 CONT
2 CONT Kotliar wrote:
2 CONT
2 CONT > Does anyone have any reliable information on the wife of the 1st Baron
2 CONT > Strange of Knokyn, John le Strange? Possibilities I have seen:
2 CONT >
2 CONT > Maud de Montibus-(Burke (EP):515; and Complete Peerage XII 1:353 )
2 CONT > Maud Deiville of Walton-Deiville?-Complete Peerage VI:342)
2 CONT > Maud Walton (Weis-Ancestoral Roots of Sixty Colonists Line 249)
2 CONT > Maud Walton of Wauton-Deyville (D'Eiville)? ( no source given)
2 CONT >
2 CONT > -Jay
2 CONT
2 CONT This can be found in the archives. John V le Strange of Knockin was
2 CONT married twice which may be part of your confusion. CP XIV: 596 gives first wife as Alienore de Montz and second wife as Maud de Walton of Little Wellesbourne, Warw. The archive discussion considers the placement of children for ea
2 CONC ch wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hope this helps.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Sutlif
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/gen-medieval/2003-04/1050065653:
2 CONT
2 CONT From: ADRIANCHANNING@aol.com  Subject: Re: Origin of Strange (was: How much fact in the fiction?)  Date: Fri, 11 Apr 2003 08:54:13 EDT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Duchess of Cleveland's (_Battle Abbey Roll_, 1889) account of this family  is as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT ...John, the common ancestor of the two great baronial families that bore the  name, was the first of seven John Le Stranges, who followed each other in  lineal succession as chiefs of a house "remarkable for longevity, activity, 
2 CONC  and loyal steadfastness." They had a castle and park at Cheswardine; but the  head of their Honour was the frontier fortress of Knockyn, traditionally said  to have been founded by Guy the Viscount. Round this, their principal  s
2 CONC tronghold, "the Stranges gradually amassed an extent of territory which made  them formidable even to their own suzerains the Fitz Alans, and constituted  the Chatelleny or Fee of Knockyn." They were enterprising and energetic  Ba
2 CONC rons Marcher. John II., who died in 1237, an old man of more than eighty,  had spent fully fifty years of his life in the active discharge of the duties  of his station. King John greatly favoured and trusted him, and he never  sw
2 CONC erved from his loyalty, but proved himself a faithful liegeman to the very  end. In 1226, Henry III. acknowledged his "great services, large outlay, and  losses," by the remission of some arrears due to the Crown. John III. was  i
2 CONC nvested with even wider authority. In 1232 he was Constable of the three  castles of Shrewsbury, Montgomery, and Bridgenorth, with "the greater trust  or custody of the counties of Salop and Stafford: " and in 1240 had the  furthe
2 CONC r charge of the castle and county of Chester conferred upon him by a  patent, "equivalent," says Eyton, "to appointing him to the high office of  Justiciar of Chester." He was in arms against the Welsh even in advanced old  age, s
2 CONC ummoned to parliament as a baron in 1260, and stood fast to the Crown  throughout the brunt of the Barons' War. His younger son Hamo was equally  and zealously loyal; but he had the mortification of seeing the elder, John  IV., br
2 CONC eak away from the honoured traditions of his house, and join Simon de  Montfort. During the brief supremacy of the barons, this younger John held  his father's office of Constable of Montgomery, but "had small joy of his  possessi
2 CONC on. In a midnight march through Kari, he was attacked by the Welsh,  and two hundred of his men slain." He was not, with the other insurgent  barons, compelled to compound for his estate after the battle of Evesham,  being "probab
2 CONC ly shielded from punishment by the name he bore," but peaceably  succeeded his father as second Lord Strange of Knockyn in 1269. He added  materially to his influence and possessions by his marriage with Joan de  Somery, daughter 
2 CONC of Roger, Baron of Somery, and Nichola, sister and coheir of  Hugh de Albini, the last Earl of Arundel of his line; and John V., following  his father's example, again espoused an heiress, Maud, the only child of  Roger D'Eivill o
2 CONC f Walton D'Eivill in Warwickshire. Yet none of his  successors ever attained the position in the county that had been held by the  first Lord Strange. They were not slack of service in the field; nor  backward in doing their duty 
2 CONC there and one, at least, of them made another  great alliance. This was John VIII., nephew and heir of John VII., with whom  the direct line of descent closed in 1323; and his wife was one of the  coheirs of the last Lord Mohun of
2 CONC  Dunster, and sisrer of Philippa Duchess of  York, to whose share of the lands the Le Stranges in process of time  succeeded. Their grandson, who died in 1461, was the last Lord Strange of  Knockyn. He had been selected by Edward 
2 CONC IV.'s up-start Queen as an eligible  husband for one of her many portionless sisters, and married Jaquetta  Widville, by whom he left an only child, Joan, the wife of George, son and  heir-apparent of Thomas Stanley, the first Ear
2 CONC l of Derby of that name. The  Stanleys thus became representatives of the elder line of Le Strange, and  held the barony till it lapsed into abeyance on the death of the fifth Earl.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Lord Strange of Knockyn 1eft, besides his heir and successor, John  IV., three younger sons, Hamo, Roger, and Robert. Hamo (already mentioned)  was the loyal Sheriff of Shropshire who stood fast for the King when his  el
2 CONC der brother joined Simon de Montfont and was rewarded by splendid grants,  comprising Stretton and the fortalice and hundred of Ellesmere. He went with  Robert to the Crusade of 1270, in the train of Prince Edward, and died in  Pa
2 CONC lestine. "The elder brother," says Eyton, "perished in the expedition; the  younger barely survived it." Hamo left no children, and Ellesmere passed, by  Royal grant, to the next brother, Sir Roger, summoned to parliament as  Domi
2 CONC nus de Ellesmere in 1294; but he, too, was without an heir, and it  reverted to the Crown on his death in 1311.
2 CONT --------------------
