0 @N6000000003949663468@ NOTE Carol (Seejay51) stated that Sitric (she named him "Halfdansson" implying that he was son of Halfdan?) was father of Sitric King of Dublin & York. Todd Farmerie stated that the name of his father was not known, and naming one's so
1 CONC n after the father was not common custom. Todd did state that Sitric, King of Dublin & York, was grandson of Inguar of Dublin (who I take to be another name for Ivar the Boneless).
1 CONT Stewart Baldwin, reviewing a book "Scandinavian Kings in the British Isles, 850-880" by Smyth, stated that Smyth said Sitric's father was also named Sitric (d. 896). Stewart Baldwin did not agree with the name (it was not the prac
1 CONC tice to name one's son after one's self), but did verify that Sitric King of Dublin & York was grandson of Ivar.
1 CONT Stewart Baldwin, on his website entitled "The Kings of the Isle of Man", in the notes under "Table 1: Kings of Dublin" states that Ivar had a son Sitric, who died in 896. However he does not connect that Sitric with Sitric King of
1 CONC  Dublin & York; EXCEPT to say that Harald King of Limerick, if not son of one Sitric, could be the son of the other Sitric, which seems to imply a relationship between the two Sitrics.
1 CONT Note: The sources seem to agree that there was one generation between Ivar (the father) & Sitric (the son). Obviously this generation was named something, and "Sitric" is the only name given (although the name is questioned by som
1 CONC e), so I am using it, even if possibly/probably wrong.
0 @N6000000004533031921@ NOTE Acceded 989. Deposed 1036. King of Northumberland, King of
1 CONT Denmark,King of York.
0 @N6000000002188035625@ NOTE Sigtrygg Silkbeard
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigtrygg Silkbeard Olafsson (known also as "Sitric" in Irish texts) was the son of King Olaf Cuaran and Gormflaith. He was the leader of the Vikings at the Battle of Clontarf in 1014, though he himself did not take part.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 989, he succeeded his brother, Glun Iarainn ("Iron-knee"), as King of Dublin. In 998, his cousin, King Mael Mordha of Leinster, rebelled against the High King of Ireland, Brian Ború. In 999, Sigtrygg was defeated alongside his 
1 CONC cousin at the Battle of Glen Mama. To negotiate peace, Brian married one of his daughters to Sigtrygg and took Gormflaith as wife. However, when Brian divorced Gromflaith, she began plotting with Sigtrygg against him. Sigtrygg gai
1 CONC ned the support of Earl Sigurd of Orkney and Brodir of the Isle of Man, and the conflict came to its climax at the Battle of Clontarf. Though the Irishmen were victorious, Brian was killed while Sigtrygg was safe behind the walls 
1 CONC of Dublin. He died in 1036.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Glun Iarainn King of Dublin Succeeded by:
1 CONT Echmarcach mac Ragnaill 
0 @N6000000003299260823@ NOTE Blinded and slain by the Danes near Dublin
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Annals of the Kingdom of Ireland, also known as the Annals of the Four Masters (See year 1580, p. 1747), the O'Byrne clan are descended from and take their name from this Bran. In the Irish tongue, his lineage bec
1 CONC ame became O'Broin, or O'Byrn.
