1 CONT King RICHARD FitzGilbert "Strongbow" de CLARE of Leinster
1 CONT ABT 1130 - 20 Apr 1176
1 CONT ID Number: I2996
1 CONT TITLE: King
1 CONT OCCUPATION: Acknowledge The Suzerainty Of HENRY II Of England
1 CONT RESIDENCE: ENG; IRE
1 CONT
1 CONT BIRTH: ABT 1130, Tunbridge, Kent, England 
1 CONT DEATH: 20 Apr 1176, Dublin, IRE 
1 CONT BURIAL: Holy Trinity (Christ Church Cathedrall), Dublin, IRE 
1 CONT RESOURCES: See: (LDS) [S11] [S590] [S790] [S1993] 
1 CONT Father: GILBERT de CLARE 1st Earl of Pembroke
1 CONT Mother: ISABEL de BEAUMONT of Leicester
1 CONT
1 CONT  Family 1 : AIFE (Eva) MACMURRAGH of Leinster 
1 CONT MARRIAGE: 26 Aug 1171, Waterford 
1 CONT  GILBERT de CLARE 3rd Earl of Pembroke 
1 CONT +ISABEL de CLARE of Pembroke 
1 CONT +ALINA de CLARE 
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT Richard Strongbow of Pembroke invaded Ireland, married Isabel (typo-error he married Eva! RJH) , and when King Dermot died he crowned himself King of Leinster. 
1 CONT "From STRONGBOW comes the name of County Clare in Ireland and since his time there has been continual strife between Ireland and England." 
1 CONT Strongbow FitzGilbert, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, Earl of Striguil, Justiciar of Ireland. 
1 CONT de Clare, Richard Strongbow FitzGilbert, Earl of Pembroke 2nd Born: 1130 Acceded: 1148 
1 CONT Died: 20 APR 1176, Dublin, Ireland Interred: Holy Triniy, Dublin. Lord of Leinster 1171. 
1 CONT
1 CONT ==========================================================================================================
1 CONT
1 CONT Internet Genealogy  URL:  http://www.gulbangi.com/d0001/g0000132.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Strongbow Fitzgilbert de Clare Earl of Pembroke
1 CONT 1130 - 20 Apr 1176
1 CONT NAME: Strongbow 
1 CONT BIRTH: 1130, Tunbridge Wells, Kent, England 
1 CONT DEATH: 20 Apr 1176, Dublin, Dublin, Ireland 
1 CONT Father: Gilbert Strongbow FitzGilbert de Clare Earl of Pembroke
1 CONT Mother: Isabel de Beaumont 
1 CONT
1 CONT Family 1 : Eve MacMurrough of Leinster, Countess Of Ireland 
1 CONT MARRIAGE: 26 Aug 1171, Waterford, Ireland 
1 CONT +Isabel de Clare Countess of Pembroke 
1 CONT
1 CONT ===========================================================================================================
1 CONT
1 CONT Internet Genealogy  URL:  http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~cousin/html/d0002/g0000228.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabel de Clare 
1 CONT Countess of Pembroke
1 CONT ( b1172, d1220 )
1 CONT
1 CONT birth 
1 CONT 1172, in Pembroke, Pembrokeshire, Wales. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT death 
1 CONT 1220, in Pembroke, Pembrokeshire 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ==================================================================
1 CONT
1 CONT A Grandchild's Heritage URL:  http://www.reach.net/~roanne/macmurrough2.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Generation No. 10
1 CONT
1 CONT Aoife MacMurrough, Princess of Leinster was born Abt 1145 in Leinster, Ireland[10], and died Aft 1186. MH LINK - LINK
1 CONT She married Richard de Clare, 2nd Earl of Pembroke 26 August 1171 in Waterford[11], son of Gilbert de Clare and Elizabeth Beaumont.
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT Aoife (Eve)
1 CONT Countess of Ireland - Acceded: 1185
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Aoife MacMurrough and Richard de Clare are:
1 CONT 19 i. Gilbertde11 Strigoil, 3rd Earl of Pembroke, born 1173.
1 CONT 20 ii. Isabel de Clare, Countess Strigoil, born Abt 1174[12]; died 1220. She married William Marshal, 4th Earl of Pembroke August 1189 in London, England[13].
1 CONT 21 iii. Alina de Clare.
1 CONT
1 CONT 18. Gilla Comgaill10 O'Toole died 1127.
1 CONT
1 CONT Child of Gilla O'Toole is:
1 CONT 22 i. Mouirchertach11 O'Toole, King Ui Muiredaig, died 1164. He married Cacht ingen Loigsig O'Morda.
1 CONT
1 CONT ===================================================================
0 @N6000000002247926969@ NOTE Isabel was heiress of Pembroke, Leinster, Bienfaite and Orbec.
1 CONT ********************
1 CONT Acceded: 1185 -Source:  Brian Tompsett <http://web500gw.hull.ac.uk:8888/o%3dUniversity%20of%20Hull%2c%20c%3dGB?%3FS=Tompsett>
1 CONT
1 CONT ********************
1 CONT
1 CONT otherwise Countess of Pembroke. The Complete Peerage vol.X,pp.358-364- Source: same as above
0 @N6000000003615554958@ NOTE Imported from &quot;The Tillotson Project&quot;: tilston@pe.net
0 @N6000000004303398612@ NOTE Heir of her brother Gilbert.
0 @N6000000004533082020@ NOTE EARLDOM OF PEMBROKE (IV) 1185 and 1189 or 1199?
1 CONT
1 CONT ISABEL, suo jure COUNTESS OF PEMBROKE, sister and heir. She marriedWilliam MARSHAL, 4th son of John FITZGILBERT, styled also JOHN THEMARSHAL, being 2nd son by his 2nd wife, Sibyl, sister of Patrick DESALISBURY, 1st EARL OF WILTSHI
1 CONC RE, and daughter of Walter DE SALISBURY,hereditary sheriff of Wilts and constable of Salisbury Castle, bySibyl, da. of Patrick DE CHAOURCES [CHAWORTH]. He was born probably in1146. In 1152 his father gave him as hostage to Stephen
1 CONC  at the siegeof Newbury, but in spite of the Marshal's bad faith, the King sparedthe child. At a later date, his father sent him to William deTancarville, hereditary Master Chamberlain of Normandy, with whom heremained for 8 years
1 CONC  as a squire. In 1167 he returned to England andattached himself to his uncle, the Earl of Salisbury, with whom heproceeded early in 1168 to Poitou, where Patrick was ambushed andslain and William was wounded and captured. Eventua
1 CONC lly he was ransomedby Queen Eleanor and returned to England, where he was chosen by Henryas a member of the Young King's household. In 1173 he was knighted atDrincourt by Tancarville, and supported the Young King in hisrebellion a
1 CONC gainst Henry II. Young Henry chose William to knight him,and on his deathbed (11 June 1183) he charged William to carry hiscross to the Holy Sepulchre. When William returned to England circa1187, the King made him a member of his 
1 CONC household. In 1188 and 1189William was with Henry in France, and in the retreat toFresnay-sur-Sarthe he checked the pursuit by the King's rebel son andheir by killing Richard's horse with his spear. He was at Henry'sdeathbed in Ch
1 CONC inon, and escorted the body to Fontevrault. Richard atonce took him into favour and gave him in marriage Isabel, suo jureCOUNTESS OF STRIGOIL or PEMBROKE, whereupon, according to contemporarychroniclers, William was styled EARL OF
1 CONC  STRIGOIL. At the Coronation (3September 1189) he bore the gold sceptre with the cross; and shortlyafterwards he was appointed one of the subordinate justiciars underLongchamp. Before September 1190 he had fined for 2,000 marks fo
1 CONC r amoiety of the lands of Walter Giffard, sometime Earl of Buckingham,whereby he became Lord of Longueville and obtained a great addition tohis wife's vast estates. When the Archbishop of Rouen supersededLongchamp in October 1191,
1 CONC  William became his chief assistant; andwhen John revolted in 1193, William besieged and took Windsor Castle.In March 1193/4 he succeeded his brother as hereditary Master Marshal,and in the modest family estates; and hurrying from
1 CONC  the funeral ritesto join the King, he took part in the siege of Nottingham Castle. From1194 to 1199 he was mostly with Richard in Normandy. One of Richard'slast acts was to appoint him custodian of Rouen and the royal treasurethe
1 CONC re; and after the King's death William persuaded Archbishop Walterto support John, who sent him to England, where he obtained thesupport of the magnates at a meeting in Northampton. He was present atthe Coronation of John on 27 Ma
1 CONC y 1199, on which day the King girt himwith the sword of the Earldom of Pembroke, whereby he became (if hewas not already) EARL OF PEMBROKE. On 20 April 1200 he was confirmedin his office of Marshal; and in May he was a surety for 
1 CONC the peacewith France. He served John actively in Gascony, England, andNormandy; and on 22 April 1202 he was appointed constable of thecastle of Lillebonne. In 1204 he invaded Wales and captured Kilgerran.Returning to Normandy, he 
1 CONC did homage to the King of France for hislands in the Duchy. In June 1205 the Marshal joined the Archbishop ofCanterbury in forcing the King to abandon his projected expedition toPoitou. From 1207 to 1213 he spent most of his time 
1 CONC in Ireland. InApril 1213 John's desperate position drove him to recall William, andon 15 May 1213, he witnessed John's, charter of resignation to thePope. He was given charge of the castles of Haverfordwest, Carmarthen,Cardigan an
1 CONC d Gower, and Dunamase in Ireland. In 1215 he supported Johnand was one of his representatives at Runnymede. He was one of theexecutors of John's will, was present when Henry III was crowned atGloucester, and was unanimously chosen
1 CONC  Regent at a Council held inBristol on 11 November 1216. On 20 May 1217 he routed the French andthe rebel Barons at Lincoln. Some months later he besieged London, andon 11 September he concluded the treaty of Lambeth with Louis. I
1 CONC n May1219, on his death-bed, he committed the young King to the care of thePapal Legate. He founded an Austin priory at Cartmel (Lancs), aCistercian abbey on the shore of Bannow Bay in Ireland, and prioriesat Wexford, Duisk and Ki
1 CONC lkenny. He was a benefactor to the chapter ofLisieux, the abbeys of Foucarmont, Gloucester, Tintern, Nutley and St.Thomas, Dublln, the priories of Longueville, Bradenstoke, Pembroke,Pill, and Stanley, Holy Trinity and St. Kevin, D
1 CONC ublin, the Templarsand other religious bodies.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married, in August 1189, in London, Isabel, only legitimatedaughter and heir (after her brother's death in childhood) of Richard(FITZGILBERT), 2nd EARL OF PEMBROKE, by Eve, daughter of DermotMACMURROUGH, King of Leinster. He di
1 CONC ed 14 May 1219, at Caversham, andwas buried in the Temple Church, London. His widow died 1220, and wasburied at Tintern Abbey.  [Complete Peerage X:358-64, (transcribed byDave Utzinger)]
