1 CONT  ___
1 CONT In south Wales a major development in the 12th century was the establishment of Rhys ap Gruffydd - known as The Lord Rhys - as the ruler of a reinvigorated kingdom of Deheubarth. After Gruffydd's death in 1137 his four sons worked
1 CONC  closely together to defend and consolidate their territory. Each son, Anarawd, Cadell, Maredudd and Rhys took the lead in succession, and there seems to have been no discord between them. Their raids extended from west Wales to t
1 CONC he lordship of Glamorgan. Anarawd was assassinated by men from north Wales in 1143; Cadell and his younger brothers achieved the reconquest of Ceredigion, making it once more part of Deheubarth, before Cadell was wounded so severe
1 CONC ly by Normans from Tenby that he was effectively removed from the political scene. Maredudd died in 1155, leaving Rhys as ruler of the southern kingdom. 
1 CONT
1 CONT It was a remarkably peaceful and bloodless succession. Between 1158 and 1165 Rhys was under heavy pressure from Henry II. He was persuaded to submit, despite the loss of territory involved when Ceredigion and Cantref Bychan were r
1 CONC estored to their Norman lords. Immediate retaliation by Rhys and his kinsmen, an attempt to take Carmarthen in 1159, and a successful attack on Llandovery in 1162 pointed for Henry II a recurrent danger: the Welsh prince would not
1 CONC  accept an enhanced Anglo-Norman presence in West Wales. For Rhys, submission and defiance were part of the dynamic border conflict. In the late 1160s the political climate in south Wales changed and Henry had to reassess the plac
1 CONC e of the Welsh prince and the marcher lords in his polity. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The change sprang from Richard Strongbow's interest in Ireland. Ireland was a magnet for men from west Wales, and in particular for the sons and grandsons of Nest, daughter of Rhys ap Tewdwr. To check their independence in Ireland
1 CONC , Henry embarked on his own expedition in 1171; he and his successors retained a close interest in the Irish lands conquered by Anglo-Norman settlers, and royal officials played an important part in their affairs. In Wales, it was
1 CONC  a valuable expedient for the king and the prince of Deheubarth to make common cause; how soon this was mooted is hard to say, but by the autumn of 1171 the terms of their rapprochement had been agreed. For Rhys, the first and mos
1 CONC t important result was the confirmation of his tenure of the lands he had conquered, Ceredigion, Cantref Bychan, Emlyn, and two commotes near Carmarthen. The final confirmation on his standing came with the style justiciar for sou
1 CONC th Wales; an English title which carried the clear delegation of royal authority was used to cover jurisdiction over Welsh leaders and communities which looked to Rhys from the lordships of Gwynllwg, Usk, Caerleon, Glamorgan, Elfa
1 CONC el and Maelienydd. 
1 CONT
1 CONT While Henry lived, Rhys was a trusted agent and ally. For his part, Rhys was content to make the most of his relationship with the king, but he continued to think and act as an independent Welsh prince. He rebuilt Cardigan Castle 
1 CONC for his own use, and he used marriage alliances to consolidate his position. As the 12th century drew to a close, Rhys was once again engaged in campaigning against the crown and the greater lords of the southern march, and at the
1 CONC  same time he was deeply implicated in internal feuds among his kindred. These struggles presaged the decline of his dynasty and the eclipse of his kingdom. By the time of his death in 1197 he had been an active participant in war
1 CONC  and politics for sixty years, and he had been the dominate ruling prince in Wales for more than forty years. 
1 CONT
1 CONT David Walker, Medieval Wales. Cambridge University Press, 1990. 
1 CONT
1 CONT __________________________________________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT Section Navigation  
1 CONT Introduction ~ Owain ap Gruffudd ~ Rhys ap Gruffudd ~ Llywelyn ap Iorwerth ~ Llywelyn ap Gruffudd ~ Owain Glyndwr 
1 CONT
1 CONT Page One ~ Page Two  
1 CONT
1 CONT It was in a chamber of the castle of Dinefwr on a early cold winter's morning in 1132 that the first cry of Rhys ap Gruffudd could be heard. One of four sons born to Gwenllian a daughter of Owain-Gwynedd, young Rhys had a traumati
1 CONC c childhood even for medieval times. 
1 CONT
1 CONT While only three his mother Gwenllian was killed by Norman forces as she and her men sought to defend the Tywi valley whilst her husband was away in north Wales visiting her father. When the battle was over she was decapitated, he
1 CONC r head paraded among the Norman troops then thrown into the river Tywi. Three years later young Rhys became an orphan when his father was also killed while attempting to drive the Normans out of Deheubarth. 
1 CONT
1 CONT With his eldest brother Anarawd also having been killed, young Rhys now came under the guidance and control of his brother Cadell, however it was the ladies of Dinefwr who taught him the finer things such as poetry and verse. Desp
1 CONC ite their influence the young prince soon began to learn the ways that would be successful when he began to wage war. With his pack of wolfhounds he learnt how to track and kill deer and boar, which abounded in the forest close to
1 CONC  the castle, and Cadell on returning from his many patrols to confront the Normans often found Rhys practicing with a wooden sword against the guards in the outer courtyard. 
1 CONT
1 CONT It came as no surprise to anyone at Dinefwr, when at the age of twelve Rhys became involved in his first attack against the Norman enemy. Guarded by his own troop of warriors, he accompanied Cadell and his other brother Maredudd f
1 CONC or an attack on the powerful castle of Llanstephan. Indeed had it not been for him leading his troop into the affray, I doubt if the castle would have been overrun
1 CONT
1 CONT  By his twenty first birthday Rhys had become proficient enough in the ways of war for Cadell to allow him to conduct his own campaigns on behalf of the kingdom of Deheubarth. Within days of his birthday celebrations, he joined hi
1 CONC s troop with that of the one belonging to his brother Maredudd and led the combined force in an attack against the castle of Aberafon. When it had been overrun the glow of the burning castle in the night sky, caused by Rhys himsel
1 CONC f torching it, could be seen by the men of Maredudd garrison as they guarded Llanstephan castle; some twenty miles to the north across the waters of Swansea bay. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The year of young Rhys's twenty first was a particular good one, for the Normans suffered humiliating losses, as the princes of Deheubarth struck here, there, and everywhere. It appeared that slowly but surely that they were stemm
1 CONC ing the tide of Norman penetration into their lands.  
1 CONT
1 CONT Christmas 1152 was one of the major turning points in the life of Lord Rhys. His eldest brother announced he was departing on a pilgrimage to Rome and that he was entrusting his authority to rule the kingdom to both Maredudd and R
1 CONC hys jointly. In carrying out Cadell's wishes the two brothers were hard but fair in their rule. They continued to attack Norman installations and burn them, then to slip quietly back to Dinefwr castle before setting out on the nex
1 CONC t raid. In fact they were carrying out the same modus operandi that Cadell had found so rewarding and they saw no need to chang
1 CONT
1 CONT The situation may have remained the status quo, had not destiny declared her hand once again. First Maredudd was killed when he approached poachers during the hours of darkness on his estate at Llanstephan, then Cadell returned fr
1 CONC om his pilgrimage crippled by horrific injuries: having been attacked by Norman forces in Normandy while he and his companions were on their way to Rome. 
1 CONT
1 CONT After a few months following his return, because of his injuries, Cadell renounced his rights to the Deheubarth throne. Rhys was persuaded to take over his crown. It was a testing time, now with all his brothers dead, he was left 
1 CONC on his own to to defend south Wales and Deheubarth in particula
1 CONT
1 CONT The young Lord soon began to have major bloody encounters with the invading Normans, no quarter was asked and certainly none was being given. His tactics in the field were nothing short of brilliant, however, he could also be stub
1 CONC born and arrogant; it was these last points that at times caused friction between him and his own sons. Many times he went to war against them, as indeed did they against him; again they were bloody encounters with brother fightin
1 CONC g brother and father fighting against his own sons. 
1 CONT
1 CONT As Rhys became increasingly powerful, so twice did Henry II, King of England invade Deheubarth in an attempt to curtail his ambitions. Each time on leaving, Henry believed that Rhys would wage war no more. How wrong the English mo
1 CONC narch was to believe a man who had a way with words. Each time, Henry had no sooner crossed the Welsh/English border, than Rhys was once again exerting military pressure upon his enemies. 
1 CONT
1 CONT By the end of 1165, with one exception, that of the large and powerful castle of Pembroke, the whole of west Wales and Ceridigion was under the control of the Lord of Deheubarth. It had been a hard and bitterly fought endeavour to
1 CONC  achieve what many had considered the unachievable. Never before had there been such a powerful ruling house in the whole of south Wales. It was about this time that Rhys turned his thoughts to the religious happenings in his king
1 CONC dom. He granted land to the monks of the Cistercian order for the building of an abbey at Strata Florida, and many of his relatives were to be buried there. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The tide of change had swept over north Wales too. With the help of the weather Rhys had combined his forces with those of his uncle 'Owain Gwynedd' at Corwen, before setting out to cause the English King to abandon his attempt to
1 CONC  defeat the Welsh force on the Berwyn mountains. 
1 CONT
1 CONT It was this defeat that was to have serious consequences for those Normans who resided in west Wales, for when Henry returned to Shrewsbury castle, he had a hostage son of lord Rhys blinded by his blacksmith, Rhys after his return
1 CONC  to Dinefwr struck at Cardigan castle and town with such fervour and hate that it was centauries before people stopped talking of his feat. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The castle and town ran red with blood as Rhys and his men took revenge for his son and killed men, women and children. Then having torched the town himself, the flames of the fire storm could be seen from the little hamlets which
1 CONC  dotted the coastline north across Cardigan bay, Rhys observed that there was a sole survivor, blood soaked, dazed and in a total state of shock. The survivor proved to be a cousin, born on the Norman side, Rhys left him there to 
1 CONC do what he would. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys again returned to Cardigan in the summer 1171, but this time he set himself the task of rebuilding both the castle and town. As the new castle rose stone by stone from the rubble of the past on high ground overlooking the Tie
1 CONC fy estuary, so it became a visual emblem of the power of Rhys. Relation with the English crown had improved too, for Henry had apologised for the wrong doings to his son.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Although he frequently journeyed south east to return to the castle of Dinefwr, it was from Cardigan that Rhys set out for the mighty castle of Pembroke when summoned by Henry II. The occasion to attend Henry's embarkation for th
1 CONC e invasion of Ireland. Having marched south protected by a large force of bowmen, Rhys was greeted by a magnificent sight on reaching Pembroke. From atop of the castle's ramparts he could see hundreds of ships of the king's fleet 
1 CONC assembled in the estuary. 
1 CONT
1 CONT It proved to be a disastrous campaign among the peat bogs of Ireland for Henry, his army was almost totally destroyed. His return to Pembroke on Good Friday in 1172 was not much better, for his ship had been battered by mountainou
1 CONC s seas and gale force winds in the Irish sea. When it finally docked in the king's dock below the castle, he came ashore and took to his bed, ill from sea sickness. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Rising from his bed on Easter Sunday Henry began planning his return to London. Within the day he was ready to march, but before doing so he sent a courier to Cardigan with orders that Rhys was to attend him at the castle of Laugh
1 CONC arne. With mistrust of the king in his mind and thinking that it may be a trap, Rhys traveled south in the company of a considerable force of archers and fighting men. However, when he arrived he found Henry to be in a convivial m
1 CONC ood and they met in the great hall on Easter Monday. Rhys was more than a little surprised, when Henry told him that he wished him to become the Justicar ( High Sheriff ) for the whole of west Wales. 
1 CONT
1 CONT With total support of Lord Rhys religion began to make important advances throughout Deheubarth too. He founded the Premonstratensian order and built Talley abbey, north east of the castle of Dinefwr. 
1 CONT Talley 
1 CONT Talley 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT He gave lands and helped the monks to build a Cistercian abbey at Strata Florida, and many of his family are buried there. He also gave funds to the Cistercian parent Abbey at Whitland. He established a community of nuns at Llanll
1 CONC yr and despite the fact that it was a Norman order he supported the Benedictine monks at their priory at Cardigan. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys was soon involved in one more devious event, however it was never proved that he was involved but his men certainly were. Under the pretext of both hearing a royal ordinance as to the bearing of arms in Gwent and in answer to
1 CONC  an invitation to a banquet given by William de Broase, the leading Welshmen of Gwent were lured to Abergavenny castle. Included in the company were Seisyll ap Dyfnwal and his son Geoffrey, brother-in-law and nephew of Lord Rhys. 
1 CONC Disarmed and seated around the table in the great hall, all the assembled guests were murdered in cold blood when William de Broase men burst in and put them to the sword. Not content with this heinous crime, the followers of de B
1 CONC roase then mounted their swiftest horses and sped to the home of Seisyll ap Dyfnwal. Once there they captured his wife and slew his youngest son Cadwaladr in her arms. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Before the warm spring air caused the lush grass to grow in the meadows of the Twyi valley in the spring of 1176, warriors from the castle of Dinefwr crossed the mountainous region of the upper reaches of the river Usk, then march
1 CONC ed the forty mile down the valley and visited Abergavenny castle. It was not a place to be when they broke out of the forest, overran it then torched it. Rhys sometime later was heard to say 'how unfortunate de Broase wasn't home'
1 CONC . 
1 CONT
1 CONT 1176 however will be remembered not for the attack on Abergavenny, but for a far more salubrious occasion which occurred at Cardigan castle that year. The event, a National Eisteddfod (the forerunner of today's successful event ) 
1 CONC had been proclaimed the previous year to which competitors from Scotland, Ireland, England and Wales were invited. Such was the good time had by all that no one noticed when Lord Rhys presented a Welshman from the House of Gwynedd
1 CONC  in north Wales with the chair for poetry, while the other for harpists went to a man in his own court. Nation building and diplomacy at its very best, then Rhys was rather good at that on times. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Once again however destiny had risen her head. The House of Gwynedd had fallen apart on the death of the great 'Owain Gwynedd'. in 1170 and a state of war existed between his wife and two of her sons, against the others. There was
1 CONC  bloodshed and mayhem everywhere throughout the north. In a bid to settle the tensions and uncertainties in the north and throughout Wales the king ordered all the Princes in Wales to gather at Oxford to settle the issue. The sons
1 CONC  of Owain Gwynedd however, were conspicuous by their absence. 
1 CONT
1 CONT During the time at Oxford all grievances were settled, lands and hostages were restored to their rightful families and Rhys was recognised as the prominent prince in Wales. Thereby earning himself the title Prince of Wales, for He
1 CONC nry was content to allow him to control the rebellious ways of some of the princes. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Or perhaps it was that Henry remembered the reply a wise old Welshman had given him during his expedition to Pencadder in west Wales during the year of 1163, when he asked him."what do you think of the royal campaign, will you reb
1 CONC el against me again?. The Welshman had replied thus:- " I doubt not that this race of mine will be brought low and broken by any might of English arms, for the wrath of man, if God's anger be not added, will never utterly destroy 
1 CONC it. For I am persuaded that no race other than mine and no tongue other than mine, come what may, will answer on the Day of Judgment for this little corner of mother earth." 
1 CONT
1 CONT God bless that Welshman, for he spoke as a true son of this Nation of mine. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1188, as it does for all mortals, time was running out for Lord Rhys, even so he was again at war. However, this time it was against his own sons. Bloodshed and death were everywhere as time and again one son sided with his fat
1 CONC her, then against him. Carreg Cennen was captured and held against the forces of Lord Rhys, then Dinefwr was destroyed. It was a disastrous time as slowly but surely the House of Deheubarth fell apart at the seams. 
1 CONT
1 CONT For nine years until the 4th of May 1197 the blood letting continued, then the golden age of the House of Deheubarth came to an end. Lord Rhys, “Rhys the Great”, “Rhys the Good”, died in his sleep in the south tower of Cardigan ca
1 CONC stle. He was buried in St. Davids Cathedral Pembrokeshire with all the pomp and ceremony deserving of a king. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon after the House of Gwynedd rose again to became the ruling house of Wales, and full scale war was to rage throughout the land. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
