0 @N6000000003051263562@ NOTE She inherited from her father the homestead manor of Hempstead-Marshall in Berkshire, with the office of Marshall of England.
0 @N6000000003949733205@ NOTE Maud Marshal m. 1st to Hugh Bigod, Earl of Norfolk; 2ndly, to William de Warren, Earl of Surrey; and 3rdly, to Walde de Dunstanville. This lady, upon the decease of her youngest brother, Anselm, Earl of Pembroke, s. p., in 1245, a
1 CONC nd the division of the estates, obtained as her share the manor of Hempsted-Marshall, in Berks, with the office of marshal of England, which was inherited by her son Roger Bigod, 4th Earl of Norfolk, and surrendered to the crown b
1 CONC y her grandson, Rogert Bigod, 5th Earl of Norfolk. Maud, Countess of Norfolk, had likewise the manors of Chepstow and Carlogh. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1
1 CONC 883, p. 358, Marshal, Earls of Pembroke]
