1 CONT He was a brilliant battle commander & long range strategist. He seized Gwynedd for his paternal uncles when only 14. He united all of Wales under his tumultuous rule between 1194 and 1187. He broke Welsh tradition by naming his so
1 CONC n DaFydd his sole heir to his titles. Rhys Gryg joined him in an alliance against King John. He was captured at Shrewsbury, Shropshire in May 1215. He
1 CONT refused King John's call for support to defend his throne against Louis Capet in 1216. His son Gruffydd rebelled after his father chose his younger half brother, Dafydd as his sole heir. However,
1 CONT he refused to fight his father at Craig Aderyn. When he knew he was dying he took holy vows.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America before 1700" by Frederick Lewis Weis, Genealogical Publixhing Co., Inc., edited by Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., 7th Edition.
1 CONT
1 CONT Information obtained from http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~delatorre/d0020/g0000013.html#I19650.
1 CONT
1 CONT Web Site: "The Paternal Ancestry of Homer Beers James".
1 CONT
1 CONT Information obtained from Paul McBride, http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~pmcbride/rfc/gw109.htm#I3559.
1 CONT
1 CONT Occupation: Prince of Wales (1194-1240).
1 CONT
1 CONT Cause of death: A Stroke as a monk.
1 CONT
1 CONT At first Llewellyn was a friend of King John, but their friendship soon ended and in 1211 John reduced him to submission. However, in the following year, Llewellyn recovered all his losses in North Wales and, in 1215, he took Shre
1 CONC wsbury. His rising had been encouraged by the Pope, by France and by the English Barons. Throughout his reign John and Llewellyn were friends or foes according to the dictates of intelligent self-interest. Llewellyn aimed at a uni
1 CONC ted Wales under his rule and resisting the threat to local independence offered by the increasing royal power of the kingdom of England. Later Llewellyn managed to make alliances with the Anglo-Normanlords of the Marches, not only
1 CONC  with his old friend the Earl of Chester, but also with the De Mortimers and the De Braoses. With these families Llewellyn had personal links, as his daughters married members of all of them.
1 CONT
1 CONT ********************************************************************************88
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn Fawr "the Great" ap Iowerth, Prince of Wales who defeated his uncle Dafydd in 1194 and was acknowledged as overlord by other Welsh princes. At first Llywelyn was on good terms with King John of England and he married King
1 CONC  John's daughter Joan in 1206. However, after King John attacked him in 1210, Llywelyn allied hinself with the barons who opposed King John. When the barons pressured King John to sigh the Magna Charta in 1215, the rights of Llywe
1 CONC lyn and the Welsh were recognised. 
1 CONT Born in 1173 probably at Dolwyddelan Castle, shown in the photograph below. 
1 CONT Died on November 4, 1240 at Aberconwy 
1 CONT Llwelyn married first to Gwenllian.
1 CONT
1 CONT Llwelyn married second to Joan, a daughter of King John of England and they had the following children: 
1 CONT Dafydd ap Llywelyn Fawr who succeeded his father as Prince of Gwynedd from 1240 until his death in 1246. The throne then passed to his nephew Llywelyn ap Gruffydd ap Llywelyn Fawr. 
1 CONT Elen (Helena, Helen) who married first to John le Scot, Earl of Huntington and second to Robert de Quincy, son of Magna Charta Surety Saher de Quincy. For details, see Robert de Quincy <http://www3.sympatico.ca/robert.sewell/dequi
1 CONC ncy.html>. 
1 CONT Angharad 
1 CONT Margaret verch Llywelyn 
1 CONT Tegwared-y-Bais Wen 
1 CONT Llwelyn married third to Tangwystyl Goch and they had the following children: 
1 CONT Gruffydd ap Llywelyn Fawr 
1 CONT Llywelyn ap Gruffydd ap Llywelyn Fawr, also known as "Llywelyn the Last", Prince of Wales from 1246 to 1282). Llywelyn paid homage to King Henry III in 1247, but after rebelling several times he was killed in battle on December 11
1 CONC , 1282 during the reign of King Edward I. Llwelyn was the last of the Welsh princes to rule over an independent Wales. 
1 CONT Gwladus Ddu "the Dark" 
1 CONT Helen
0 @N6000000002907048715@ NOTE He hanged the Anglo-Norman baron, William de la Braose, for having an affair with his wife
0 @N6000000003086921495@ NOTE In Gwynedd, Llywelyn ap Iorwerth had come to power in the classic way of Welsh princes bedeviled by the dividing rule of Welsh inheritance - he seized it from his uncle. He proved to be the greatest and most constructive Welsh sta
1 CONC tesman of the Middle Ages. In his long career he succeeded, by constant warfare, by tactful yielding under pressure and by masterly resilience the moment that pressure was relieved, in bringing under his control most of Pura Walli
1 CONC a. When he died in 1240, full of honor and glory, he left a principality which had the possibility of expanding into a truly national state of Wales. There was a moment when an independent Wales seemed about to become a reality.
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn deliberately set out on a policy of reconstructing the whole basis of Welsh political life, and not every Welshman was happy about it. Llywelyn lived in an age which saw the emergence of the centralized feudal state. Both
1 CONC  France and England presented the spectacle of societies elaborating their administrative machinery, putting their taxation on a new and sounder footing and systematizing their codes of justice, but Llywelyn's principality was sma
1 CONC ll and lacking resources. Hostile English observers could wax satirical about its pretensions to international status.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gwynedd  had always been the core of the power of the princes, and the expansion of Llywelyn's territory gave him the ability to do many things beyond the power of previous Welsh rulers. We find Llywelyn ap Iorwerth (the Great) an
1 CONC d Llywelyn ap Gruffydd  (the Last) developing castle building on a considerable scale. The remains of Castell y Bere  or of Ewloe , Dolbadarn  and Dolwyddelan even show distinctive Welsh style. The princes gave charters to the sma
1 CONC ll towns growing in their domains. They supported the abbeys and the friaries. We sense a new Wales coming into being, and, at the moment, it was basically an independent Wales. The great question was, would this new Wales be able
1 CONC  to develop to its full potential without interference from without or protests from within? Looming over it was the king of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT For over all this hung the vexing yet vital question of the exact terms of Llywelyn's homage to the king. The king was always acknowledged as being at the head of the pyramid and by the 13th century Welsh rulers also accepted the 
1 CONC principle that homage should be paid to the King of England. Hywel Dda  had done do, far back in the 10th century, and both Owain Gwynedd and the Lord Rhys  had done homage to Henry II. The problem was that Llywelyn claimed that t
1 CONC he status in relation to the King of England was the same enjoyed by the King of Scotland - that barons were to pay their homage directly to him and not the king, but King John took a different view. He felt that the barons should
1 CONC  also do their homage to him. This gave him the right of continual interference in Welsh affairs. At times the relationship between Llywelyn and the king were mutually supportive, in part because Llywelyn managed to marry Joan, th
1 CONC e illegitimate daughter of King John, in 1205. But even this family tie soon broke down over the question of homage and disputed territories.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the Welsh princes were strong they could enforce a grudging acknowledgement of their position from the king. When they were weak, the king granted treaties firmly maintaining his view of homage. Llywelyn the Great had sought 
1 CONC to solve the problem before his death. He had two sons, Gruffydd  by a Welsh lady and the younger, David, by his wife Joan. Welsh law at the time said that both sons should inherit - a law which had been the cause of so many of th
1 CONC ose disputed successions which had brought ruin to Wales in the past. Llywelyn made a bold and successful attempt to put this dangerous Welsh law aside in favor of the English system, and finally got the consent of King Henry III 
1 CONC - or his advisors - to agree to the succession of David as his sole heir. Then, shortly before his death, he called all the princes of Wales together at Strata Florida Abbey  in 1238 and made them swear allegiance to David. Henry 
1 CONC III allowed him to succeed, but refused David the direct homage of the barons, eventually leading to war between the king and the new Welsh prince. David and his forces had no change against Henry's large army and withdrew. David 
1 CONC died childless shortly thereafter, and it would be up to Llywelyn's grandson, Llywelyn ap Gruffydd , to assert Wales' independence once again.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wales - A History, Wynford Vaughan-Thomas, Michael Joseph Ltd. Publishing, London WC1, 1985.
