0 @N6000000001876715009@ NOTE http://www.british-history.ac.uk/report.aspx?compid=47891&strquery=Gwenwynwyn
1 CONT The castle of Powis/Powys, (Welshpool, Montgomery, North Wales) which was begun by Bleddyn(Cadwgan ab Bleddyn ab Cynvyn), was continued by Gwenwynwyn, and on completion appeared as a low-roofed edifice of red stone. The castle was
1 CONC  captured by the English in 1191, but retaken by Gwenwynwyn in 1197, it contains a vast store of art collections, paintings and statuary, including heroic busts of the twelve Caesors brought from Italy. This Prince, and the Castle
1 CONC  of Powis so finely portrayed in the "Betrothed" by Sir Walter Scott, recovered his castle from Archbishop Hubert, who commanded the armies of Richard I against the Welsh. In 1198, ambitious of achieving the independence of his co
1 CONC untry, he raised a large army, and besieged William Braose in his castle of Payn in Radnor, but was defeated in a battle near the castle. In 1208 he was in arms with Llewellyn, Prince of North Wales, and with other great men of Wa
1 CONC les and the two princes drove King John from the country. He later returned his allegiance to King John, and in 1210 Gwenwynwyn was pursued by Llewellyn and driven within the walls of Chester. He was dead by 1218, his widow Margar
1 CONC et living, and his children under age. He married Margaret, daughter of Robert Corbet, feudal baron of Caus. Griffith resumed the arms of his line: Or, a lion Rampant Gules. He had Madoc Goch and his elder son [Griffith]
0 @N6000000003615588707@ NOTE Gwenwynwyn, who is so finely portrayed by Sir Walter Scott in his "Betrothed." He married Margaret, daughter of Robert Corbet, feudal baron of Caus. They resumed the arms of his line: Or a lion rampant gules. In 2nd of Henry III, 
1 CONC 1218, he appears to have been dead, his widow Margaret living and his heirs under age.
0 @N6000000003949663251@ NOTE Prince of South Powys. Prince of Powys. He was one of the most ambitious of the princes of Powys, but his career showed that Powys, of all the ancient Welsh kingdoms, was the most vulnerable to English power. Succeeding his father
1 CONC  Owain ap Gruffudd as ruler of southern Powys in 1197, he at once took the role of national deliverer but was rapidly defeated in battle at Painscastle (Radnors) in 1198. He tried again whenever opportunity offered, but was humili
1 CONC ated by King John in 1208, allowing Llywelyn ap Lorwerth to step in, to seize both territory and national leadership. In 1216 he was evicted by Llywelyn and died in exile. Source: Who's Who in British History. Collins & Brown. Lon
1 CONC don. 2000.
