0 @N6000000002187761243@ NOTE Richard Og de Burgh, 2nd Earl of Ulster
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Og de Burgh (1259 –1326, Athassil monastary), also known as Richard de Burgh, was the 2nd Earl of Ulster, 3rd Lord of Connacht. His name, "Richard Og" meant Richard the Young, probably a reference to his youth when he beca
1 CONC me earl in 1271, or to differentiate him from his grandfather, Richard Mor. He was also known as the Red Earl.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Og was the most powerful of the de Burgh Earls of Ulster. He was a friend of King Edward I of England, and ranked first among the Earls of Ireland. Richard's wife Marguerite de Guines was the cousin of King Edward's queen.
1 CONC  He pursued expansionist policies that often left him at odds with fellow Anglo-Irish lords; he successfully defeated the King Felim mac Aedh Ua Conchobair of Connacht at the Second Battle of Athenry in 1316.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT His sister, Egidia de Burgh, was wife of James the Stewart of Scotland. His second son, John de Burgh, was husband to Elizabeth de Clare, a granddaughter of Edward I of England. Of his daughters, Catherine married the Maurice Fitz
1 CONC gerald, 1st Earl of Desmond; Joan married the Maurice FitzThomas FitzGerald, 4th Earl of Kildare; Avelina married John Earl of Louth; Matilda married Gilbert de Clare, 8th Earl of Gloucester; and Elizabeth de Burgh married Robert 
1 CONC the Bruce, later Robert I of Scotland. His son John died in 1313, leaving the succession to his infant son, William.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Literature
1 CONT Burke People and Places, Eamonn Burke, Dublin, 1995. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Walter de Burgh Earl of Ulster Succeeded by:
1 CONT William Donn de Burgh 
