0 @N6000000002187742442@ NOTE Roger Bigod, 4th Earl of Norfolk
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Roger Bigod (c. 1209 - 1270), was 4th Earl of Norfolk and Marshal of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Hugh Bigod, and Matilda, a daughter of William Marshal, Earl of Pembroke and Marshal of England. On his father's death in 1225 he became 4th Earl of Norfolk. Still a minor, he was a ward of William de Longespee, 
1 CONC 3rd Earl of Salisbury. After his marriage to Isabella, daughter of William the Lion, King of Scotland, he was a ward of his new brother-in-law, Alexander II of Scotland until 1228, when, although still under-age, he succeeded to h
1 CONC is father's estates. He did not, however, receive his father's title until 1233.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death without male heirs of the last of his mother's brothers, Roger obtained the office of Marshal of England in 1246. He was prominent among the barons who wrested the control of the government from the hands of Henry 
1 CONC III, and assisted Simon de Montfort.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger had no children, and was succeeded by his nephew, also named Roger.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: M. Morris, The Bigod Earls of Norfolk in the Thirteenth Century (Woodbridge, 2005)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT The Earl of Pembroke Lord Marshal
1 CONT 1245–1269 Succeeded by:
1 CONT The Earl of Norfolk 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Hugh Bigod Earl of Norfolk Succeeded by:
1 CONT Roger Bigod Roger Bigod, 4th Earl of Norfolk
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger Bigod (c. 1209 – 1270), was 4th Earl of Norfolk and Marshal of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Hugh Bigod, and Matilda, a daughter of William Marshal, Earl of Pembroke and Marshal of England. After the death of his father in 1225 Roger became the ward of William de Longespee, 3rd Earl of Salisbury. After h
1 CONC is marriage to Isabella, daughter of William the Lion, King of Scotland, he was a ward of his new brother-in-law, Alexander II of Scotland until 1228, when, although still under-age, he succeeded to his father's estates (including
1 CONC  Framlingham Castle). He did not, however, receive his father's title until 1233.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death without male heirs of the last of his mother's brothers, Roger obtained the office of Marshal of England in 1246. With his younger brother Hugh Bigod (Justiciar), he was prominent among the barons who wrested the c
1 CONC ontrol of the government from the hands of Henry III and assisted Simon de Montfort, in what became the Second Barons' War.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger had no children, and was succeeded by his nephew, also named Roger Bigod, 5th Earl of Norfolk.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * M. Morris, The Bigod Earls of Norfolk in the Thirteenth Century (Woodbridge, 2005)
1 CONT
1 CONT Political offices
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT The Earl of Pembroke Lord Marshal
1 CONT 1245–1269 Succeeded by
1 CONT The Earl of Norfolk
1 CONT Peerage of England
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Hugh Bigod Earl of Norfolk Succeeded by
1 CONT Roger Bigod
