0 @N6000000003949718161@ NOTE Eve, the daughter of the youngest de Briouze brother, married c 1238 William de Cauntelo. Their son George held Abergavenny for some 18 years, but without issue. His heirs were his sister Millicent, who married as her second husba
1 CONC nd Eon la Zouche and with him was the ancestor of the Lords (Barons) Zouche of Haryngworth, and his nephew by another sister, Joan. [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT Eve de Briouze, daughter and coheir, heiress of Abergavenny. She m. after 25 July 1238 (when his father, William de Cantelou, obtained her wardship and marriage together with the custody of Abergavenny and the other lands falling 
1 CONC to her share), and bef. 15 Feb 1247/8, William de Cantelou, of Calne, Wilts, and Aston Cantlow, co. Warwick. He d. at Calstone, Wilts, 25, and was buried 30 Sep 1254, at Studley Priory, co. Warwick. Writ of extent 15 Oct 1254. She
1 CONC  d. in 1255, about 20 and bef. 28 July. [Complete Peerage I:22-3
1 CONT Heiress of Abergavenny (her father) and of the town and castle of  Haverford (her mother)[5],[8] also inherited two thirds of the borough and manor of Bridgwater,   Somerset[9]
1 CONT market recorded 1233 [mercatum], held by Eva de Breus. In 1233, it  was alleged that the market of Eva de Breus was being damaged by that  at Dartmouth, Devon. Eva also complained that ships with merchandise,  which should have do
1 CONC cked at Totnes, were docking at Dartmouth and taking the customs which belonged to Totnes. In 1234–5, twelve  knights came to judge whether the market was being damaged by that   at Dartmouth. Judgement went against the market at 
1 CONC Dartmouth (CRR,   xv, nos. 235, 1285). In 1242, it was judged that the market at  Dartmouth was damaging William Cantilupe’s market at Totnes (CRR,  xvii, no. 375). '[10]
