1 CONT OOW son of Llywelyn ap Iorwerth, Prince of Wales; m. Gwenllian ferch Ednyfed Fychan; father o f Angharad who m. Gruffudd of Abercain.  [Graham Milne <graham@gmilne.demon.co.uk]
0 @N6000000001220381108@ NOTE [2753682.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT WAITE, FOSTER LINE
1 CONT
1 CONT !Son of Llywelyn the Great of Gwynedd by his first union.  Llywelyn declared him his successo r before the assembled Welsh lords in 1238, but he was set aside 15 May 1240 when his younge r half-brother Dafydd was created paramount
1 CONC  prince in Wales by King Henry III of England.  A t the time Gruffudd was imprisoned with his son Owain at Criccieth. [Chronicle of the Royal F amily, p. 58]
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Llywelyn the Great; father of Llywelyn, Dafydd, and Rhodri. [A History of Wales, p. 81 ]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1220, King Henry III supported Llywelyn in his efforts to ensure that his son by Joan, Hen ry's nephew Dafydd, should be heir to Gwynedd. That dispensation involved disinheriting Llywe lyn's eldest son, Gruffudd, who had been b
1 CONC orn to Tangwystl before the marriage with Joan. I n 1222, the arrangement was granted the blessing of the Pope. Gruffudd m. Senana, a descendan t of Rhodri ab Owain Gwynedd. [History, p. 139]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1241 Henry III compelled Dafydd to turn over his elder bro., Gruffudd, a man who could b e used to undermine the unity of Gwynedd. Gruffudd was imprisoned in London's White Tower an d he fell to his death on St David's Day, 124
1 CONC 4, while lowering himself from his prison on a r ope of sheets. [History, p. 143]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1239 Dafydd, Llywelyn ab Iorwerth's legitimate son, imprisoned his half-bro, Gruffudd, an d Gruffudd's son, Owain at Criccieth Castle. In 1241 Dafydd was forced to turn his prisoner s over to King Henry III. Gruffudd died while
1 CONC  trying to escape from the Tower of London thre e years later. [Criccieth Castle, pp. 2-3]
1 CONT
1 CONT d. 1244. Son of Llywelyn ap Iorwerth, prince of Wales. Gruffyd rebelled against his father an d in 1223 headed an army against William Marshal, 5th earl of Pembroke. In 1239 he was seize d by his brother Dafydd, who handed him ove
1 CONC r to Henry III of England in 1241. In 1244 Gruffy d attempted to escape from the Tower of London but fell and died. [The Plantagenet Encycloped ia, p. 88]
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Tangwystl verch Llywarch and Llewelyn 'the Great' ap Iorwerth, Prince of Wales; m. Sen ena of Man. [Larry Overmire <larryover@worldnet.att.net, 18 Jun 2003]
0 @N6000000001744977287@ NOTE From http://www.castlewales.com/gruffyd.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT Imprisonment or exile was not an infrequent fate of those members of the royal house of Gwynedd who were seen as a threat to the authority of its ruler. Gruffydd ap Llywelyn (d.1244), the illegitimate son of Llywelyn ap Iorwerth a
1 CONC nd Owain Goch's father, spent much of his life in prison and was eventually to
1 CONT die whilst attempting to escape from incarceration.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gruffydd was born sometime before Llywelyn's marriage to Princess Joan. The first historical reference to him is as one of the hostages his father had to hand over to John, following Llywelyn's defeat by the English king in 1211. 
1 CONC Gruffydd was to remain a prisoner until 1215. Despite subsequently being granted land by his father, Gruffydd was to prove troublesome, and he was seen as a threat to Dafydd's clear path as Llywelyn's successor. Llywelyn chose to 
1 CONC imprison him in Degannwy Castle from 1228 until 1234.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1239, towards the end of their father's life, Dafydd confined both Gruffydd and his son Owain Goch in Criccieth Castle. In 1241, Dafydd suffered a humiliating defeat at the hands of King Henry III, and as one of the terms of th
1 CONC e resulting settlement he was forced to hand over Gruffydd. The unlucky prince was to spend his last three years as a prisoner at the Tower of London, where he fell to his death in an attempted escape in 1244 (depicted in a mediev
1 CONC al manuscript). Despite his seemingly bad luck, Gruffydd's son Llywelyn, was later successful in reuniting much of Wales under his rule, earning unprecedented recognition as "Prince of Wales" from Henry III under the terms of the 
1 CONC Treaty of Montgomery in 1267.
0 @N6000000002187962739@ NOTE Gruffydd ap Llywelyn Fawr
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gruffydd ap Llywelyn (c. 1200 – March 1, 1244) was the illegitimate son of Llywelyn the Great ("Llywelyn Fawr"). As far as is known, he was Llywelyn's eldest son.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Hostage
1 CONT     * 2 Imprisonment
1 CONT     * 3 Death
1 CONT     * 4 Succession
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Hostage
1 CONT
1 CONT As a boy, Gruffydd was one of the hostages taken by King John of England as a pledge for his father's continued good faith. On his father's death in 1240 he would under Welsh law have been entitled to consideration as his father's
1 CONC  successor. Llywelyn however had excluded him from the succession on the grounds of his illegitimacy and had declared his only legitimate son by his wife Joan, Dafydd, to be his sole heir. Llywelyn went to considerable lengths to 
1 CONC strengthen Dafydd's position, probably aware that there would be considerable Welsh support for Gruffydd against the half-English Dafydd.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Imprisonment
1 CONT
1 CONT Gruffydd was held a prisoner by his brother Dafydd when the latter took over Gwynedd. Following a successful invasion of the Welsh borders by King Henry III of England in 1241, Dafydd was obliged to hand over Gruffydd into the kin
1 CONC g's custody whence he was taken to London and imprisoned in the Tower of London. Gruffydd's wife, Senena (possibly a daughter of Caradoc ap Thomas of Anglesey), agreed to pay Henry 600 marks for the release of her husband and thei
1 CONC r eldest son, Owain, and to hand over her two youngest sons, Dafydd and Rhodri, to the king as hostages to ensure that she kept her part of the bargain. Henry did not keep his part however, and kept Gruffydd and his son imprisoned
1 CONC  as "guests" because this continued to give him the possibility of using Gruffydd as a weapon against his brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT However, the headstrong and impatient Gruffydd died while attempting to escape from the Tower in 1244. He is said to have used an improvised rope made from sheets and cloths to lower himself from his window, but as he was a heavy 
1 CONC man the rope broke and he fell to his death. His body was discovered by the Yeoman of the Guard the next morning at the foot of the White Tower where he had fallen some ninety feet. The window from which he made his descent is on 
1 CONC the south side of the Tower on the top floor. It was bricked up afterwards and can still be seen today.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1248 the abbots of Strata Florida and Aberconwy arranged for the return of his body to Wales where he was buried at Aberconwy with his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Succession
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death Gruffydd's four sons—Owain, Llywelyn, Dafydd and Rhodri—would come into their own, and after much fraternal discord, Llywelyn ended up ruling most of Wales.
0 @N6000000003086921514@ NOTE Imprisonment or exile was not an infrequent fate of those members of the royal house of Gwynedd who were seen as a threat to the authority of its ruler. Gruffydd ap Llywelyn (d.1244), the illegitimate son of Llywelyn ap Iorwerth  
1 CONC and Owain Goch's  father, spent much of his life in prison and was eventually to die whilst attempting to escape from incarceration.
