0 @N6000000001220382808@ NOTE [2753682.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT NEWLIN LINE
1 CONT
1 CONT !Widow of John le Scot, Earl of Huntingdon; married Robert de Quincey, the younger. [Magna Ch arta Barons, p. 122]
1 CONT
1 CONT !Widow of John le Scot, and got Fotheringay and other large estates for her life, from the si sters of her first husband. [The Bruces and the Cumyns, p. 519]
1 CONT
1 CONT Dau. of Llywelyn; her marriage to John of Scots was childless, a misforture of importance i n the history of Wales and of Scotland. When John died in 1237, his territories passed to th e crown, and Edward I's campaigns in Wales wo
1 CONC uld be greatly facilitated by the fact that he h eld the earldom of Chester. [A History of Wales, p. 141]
1 CONT
1 CONT !Genealogical Society of Utah; Gareth Rice[2753682.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Dau of Llewelyn 'the Great' ap Iorwerth, Prince of Wales, and an unknown mistress; m. Malcol m MacDuff, 8th Earl of Fife. [Larry Overmire <larryover@worldnet.att.net, 2 Apr 2003][2753682.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Dau. of Joan Plantagenet and Llywelyn ap Iorworth; m. William Caentwn. [Ed Mann]
0 @N6000000003051212257@ NOTE [Weis 201] received manors of Bidford, County Warwick, and Suckley, County Worcester, as maritagium.
0 @N6000000004533909964@ NOTE Helen, daughter of Llewellyn ap Iorwerth, Prince of Wales.  [MagnaCharta Sureties, Line 41-5]
1 CONT
1 CONT Helen or Elen, illegitimate daughter of Llewellyn ap Iorwerth, Princeof Wales, widow of Malcolm, Earl of Fife.  [Magna Charta Sureties,Line 41b-5]
1 CONT
1 CONT This Helen was illegitimate and born very late- shortly beforeLlewellyn's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT -----------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT His [Donald 6th Earl of Mar's] wife, and mother of Isabel, was Helen,widow of Malcolm, 7th Earl of Fife, d. 1266, and daughter ofLlewellyn, Prince of North Wales.  Helen's first husband must havebeen an old man when she married hi
1 CONC m, for he succeeded his uncle in1228.  When he died, his son and heir was Colban, the 8th Earl, thenunder age, who had been knighted in his teens in 1264.  Colban wasmarried in his nonage, for when he died in 1270, when he could n
1 CONC othave been more than 24, his heir was his son Duncan, aged 8.  (Mr.Balfour Paul believes Colban's wife Alice was one of three daughtersand co-heirs of Sir Alan Durward.  If so, his issue shared with theSoulis family the descent f
1 CONC rom Alexander II of Scotland.  However,since the line of his heir Duncan has died out, remaining descendantsof this line would stem from younger children of Colban and Alice, ifthere were any.)  The Helen, daughter of Llewellyn, w
1 CONC ho wassuccessively the wife of Malcolm and of Donald, and mother of thechildren of both, appears clearly the daughter of Llewellyn apIorwerth but must not be confused with his daughter Helen,successively the wife of John le Scot, 
1 CONC Earl of Chester, and of Robertde Quincy, whose mother was Princess Joan.  [Ancestral Roots, Line252-30]
0 @N6000000000337436267@ NOTE Source #1:  Marcellus Donald Alexander R. von Redlich, "Pedigrees of Some of the Emperor Charlemagne's Descendants" (Baltimore:  Genealogical Publishing Co, Inc, 1988 reprint of 1941 edition), pp. 133-134
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  Frederick Lewis Weis, "Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700" - Seventh Edition, with additions and corrections by Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., assisted by Davis Faris (Baltimore, M
1 CONC D:  Genealogical Publishing Co, 1995), pp. 42;  63
