0 @N6000000003484298227@ NOTE (Research):>Death note: Death Surety:2
0 @N6000000003615871729@ NOTE Gilbert, 10th Earl de Clare was only 23 years old when he was killed at Ba 
1 CONT nnockburn in 1314. Gilbert did not have any children and so his death brou
1 CONT ght an end to the male line of the Clare family. The estates should have b
1 CONT een immediately divided between Gilbert's three sisters, Eleanor, Margar
1 CONT et and Elizabeth.
1 CONT At first, Edward II refused permission for the sisters to inherit this lan
1 CONT d. The king knew that these estates would give great power to the women
1 CONT 's husbands. It was only when Edward was convinced that these men would su
1 CONT pport him in his struggle with his barons, that he gave permission for t
1 CONT he women toinherit the estates.
1 CONT In the 14th century it was common for the king to be involved in selecti
1 CONT ng husbands for the daughters of his most powerful barons. Eleanor, the el
1 CONT dest daughter, was only 14 years old when she was forced to marry the king
1 CONT 's main adviser, Hugh Despenser, in 1306. As Eleanor was married too
1 CONT ne of his most loyal subjects, Edward was willing to allow her to inher
1 CONT ither share of the Clare estates.
1 CONT Margaret was more of a problem as she was a widow when Gilbert was kill
1 CONT ed at Bannockburn. In 1307 Edward II had arranged for 14 year old Margar
1 CONT et to marry his favourite knight, Piers Gaveston. Five years later Gavest
1 CONT on was murdered by the king's opponents. Before Margaret wasallowed to in
1 CONT herit her share of the Clare estates in 1317 she had to agree to marry Hu
1 CONT gh de Audley, one of the king's loyal knights.
1 CONT Elizabeth, the youngest daughter, was also a widow in 1314. Elizabeth marr
1 CONT ied John de Burgh, sonof the Earl of Ulster, when she was 14 but he di
1 CONT ed five years later. While Elizabeth was waiting for her inheritance, t
1 CONT he marcher lord, Theobald Verdun kidnapped her and took her to his cast
1 CONT le at Alton where he married her againsther will. However, Theobald Verd
1 CONT un died six months after the wedding.
1 CONT Edward II decided to keep Elizabeth in custody at Bristol Castle. The foll
1 CONT owing year, she was granted her share of the Clare inheritance when she ag
1 CONT reed to marry Roger Damory, another one of the king's supporters.
1 CONT In 1322 Roger Damory changed sides and fought for the Earl of Lancast
1 CONT er at Boroughbridge. Damory wascaptured during the battle and was later e
1 CONT xecuted for treason. Elizabeth hadremained loyal to Edward and she was al
1 CONT lowed to keep her estates. The king now decided it would be better if Eliz
1 CONT abeth remained a widow.
0 @N6000000001956401313@ NOTE
1 CONT --also called The Red Earl Welsh nobleman whose belated support of King Henry III of England was a major factor in the collapse of the baronial rebellion led by Simon de Montfort.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gilbert married Alice of Angoulême, niece of King Henry III, succeeded his father (Richard de Clare) in July 1262, and joined the baronial party led by Simon de Montfort. With Simon, Gloucester was at the battle of Lewes in May 12
1 CONC 64, when the king himself surrendered to him, and after this victory he was one of the three persons selected to nominate a council. Soon, however, he quarreled with Simon. Leaving London for his lands on the Welsh border, he met 
1 CONC Prince Edward (afterward King Edward I) at Ludlow, just after his escape from captivity, and contributed largely to Edward's victory at Evesham in August 1265. But this alliance was as transitory as the one with Simon. Gloucester 
1 CONC championed the barons who had surrendered at Kenilworth in November and December 1266 and, after putting his demands before the king, secured possession of London (April 1267). Gloucester quickly made his peace with Henry III and 
1 CONC with Prince Edward. Under Edward I he spent several years fighting in Wales, or on the Welsh border. He was succeeded by his son, also named Gilbert de Clare (1291–1314), who was killed at the Battle of Bannockburn.
0 @N6000000002240778960@ NOTE He was the 9th earl of Clare, the 7th earl of Hertford, and the 6th earl of
1 CONT Gloucester. Known a Red Earl, he was the leader of the Baron's War against
1 CONT the English king Henry III who was forced to accept a negotiated settlement.
1 CONT His sister was Elizaberth de Clare (1291?-1360), founder of Clare College at
1 CONT the University of Cambridge, England.
0 @N6000000002248195092@ NOTE When Gilbert married Joan of Acre, he gave up the inheritance of all his castles and manors in England and Wales to her father, Edward I, for him to dispose of them as he saw fit. Edward settled them on the issue of the marriage.
1 CONT Gilbert built Caerphilly Castle, in Glamorgan, which can still be seen and visited.
