1 CONT Source: www.eb.com
0 @N6000000003658774206@ NOTE GILBERT DE CLARE, Knt., 6th Earl of Gloucester, 6th Earl of Hertford, Baron of Clare, Suffolk, Steward of St. Edmund's Abbey, son and heir, born at Christchurch, Hampshire 2 Sept. 1243.  He married (1st) in the spring of 1253 ALIC
1 CONC E DE LUSIGNAN (or DE LA MARCHE), daughter of Hugh XI le Brun, Count of La Marche and Angoulême, seigneur of Lusignan in Poitou (uterine brother of Henry III, King of England), by Yolande, daughter of Pierre de Braine styled Maucle
1 CONC rc, Knt., Duke of Brittany, Earl of Richmond.  They had two daughters, Isabel and Joan.  Earl Gilbert played an important role in the constitutional crisis and the Barons' War, 1258-1267.  Initially he supported the Baronial cause
1 CONC .  IN 1264 he led the massacre of the Jews at Canterbury, Kent.  He and Simon de Montfort were denounced as traitors by the king 12 May 1264.  He commanded the 2nd line at the Battle of Lewes in May 1264, and took the king prisone
1 CONC r.  In May 1264 Prince Edward managed to ecape from Montford and met Gilbert as Ludlow, where he swore to observe the "good old laws" and to remove aliens from the royal councils.  The following month, Prince Edward and Gilbert we
1 CONC re denounced as rebels by Montford.  He shared Prince Edward's victory at Kenilworth 176 July 1264, and, at the Battle of Evesham, 4 August 1265, he commanded the 2nd division and contributed largely to the victory.  He and his wi
1 CONC fe, Alice, were legally separated at Norwich, Norfolk 18 July 1271.  In 1271 he had license to enditch his new castle of Caerphilly in Wales.  At the death of King Henry III 16 Nov. 1272, the Earl took the lead in swearing fealty 
1 CONC to Edward I, who was then in Sicily returning from the Crusade.  He was Joint Guardian of England during the King's absence.  In 1275 he went to France to negotiate for peace.  He was summoned to serve against the Weslh in 1277 an
1 CONC d 1282.  In 1278 he was among those selected to escort King Alexander III of Scotland to the king.  In May 1283 he was contracted to marry the king's daughter, Joan, provided he be formally divorced from his wife, Alice, and be fr
1 CONC ee to marry where he will, and also that he obtain a dispensation from the Pope to marry Joan, a first cousin once removed of Alice.  In 1285 Gilbert was "wholly absolved" from the contract of marriage between him and his former w
1 CONC ife, Alice, he granting her various manors including Thaxted, Essex, and Wells and Warham, Norfolk for her support.  In 1286 and 1287 he was beyond seas with the King, and, in June 1287, he was again in Wales on the King's service
1 CONC .  He married (2nd) at Winchester Abbey 23 April 1290 (by dispensation dated 16 Nov. 1289, they being related in the 2nd & 3rd degrees of affinity) JOAN OF ENGLAND [sometimes styled JOAN OF ACRE], daughter of Edward I, King of Eng
1 CONC land, by his 1st wife, Eleanor of Castile.  She was born at Acre in Palestine in Spring 1272.  They had one son ... and three daughters. ... In 1291 his quarrels with the Earl of Hereford about Brecknock culminated in a private wa
1 CONC r between them.  Both earls were imprisoned by the King, Gilbert being fined 10,000 marks as the aggressor.  In 1291 his wife, Joan, received a papal indult to enter Cisterian monasteries accompanied by eight honest matrons. In 12
1 CONC 93 he was appointed Captain of the forces in Ireland, and resided tehre until some time in 1294.  SIR GILBERT DE CLARE, Earl of Gloucester and Hertford, died at Monmouth Castle 7 Dec. 1295, and was buried at Tewkesbury Abbey, Glou
1 CONC cestershire.  His widow, Joan, married (2nd) early in 1297 RALPH DE MONTHERMER, Knt., in right of his wife, of Tonbridge, Kent, Keeper of Cardiff Castle, Guardian and Lieutenant in Scotland, Warden of the Royal Forests south of Tr
