1 CONT !Constable of Gloucester Castle in 1266; constituted governor of the city of London by Edwar d I upon his accession, 1274; d. 1287, in Ireland; son of Richard de Clare, Earl of Hertfor d and Gloucester, and Lady Maud de Lacie; m. 
1 CONC Amy Fitz-Maurice; father of Lady Maud de Clare w ho m. Robert de Clifford of Appleby. [Magna Charta Barons, p. 421]
1 CONT
1 CONT Brian Ruadh and his son Donach proceeded to Cork to Thomas de Clare, son of the Earl of Glouc ester, then at the head of all the Anglo-Norman forces of Munster, and sought his assistanc e against his nephew Turloch O'Brien, offeri
1 CONC ng him anample remuneration for his services.  Th ey offered him all the land lying between the city of Limerick and the town of Ardsallas, i n Clare.  De Clare gladly accepted those terms, and both parties met by agreement at Lim
1 CONC erick , from which they marched into Clare; where, before any successful opposition
1 CONT could be offered them, the castle of Bunratty was built and fortified by the Norman leader.
1 CONT
1 CONT A short time afterwards, however (1277), de Clare put the unfortunate Brian Ruadh to death; h aving had him drawn between horses and torn limb from limb, notwithstanding that the fidelit y of their mutual alliance had been ratifie
1 CONC d by the most solemn oaths on all the ancient reli cs of Munster.  And it was then that the great wars of Thomond commenced in earnest; for, not withstanding the treacherous death of their father, the infatuated sons of Brian Ruad
1 CONC h stil l adhered to De Clare, and the warfare was kept up with varying success until 1318, when Robe rt de Clare and his son were at last killed in the battle of Disert O'Dea. [Ancient Irish His tory, p. 236]
1 CONT
1 CONT b. 1248 [Judy Martin]
1 CONT
1 CONT Gov. of London; m. Juliane Fitz Maurice; father of Margaret and Richard. [Charlemagne & Other s, Chart 2539]
1 CONT
1 CONT b. 1245, 2nd son of Sir Richard de Clare, 8th Earl de Clare. [Charlemagne & Others, Chart 291 9b]
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Sir Richard de Clare, 8th Earl of Clare, and Maud de Lacy. [Charlemagne & Others, Char t 2918b]
1 CONT
1 CONT 2nd son of Sir Richard de Clare and Maud de Lacy; d. Ireland, 1287/8; Governor of London; Lor d of Inchequin and Youghae; m. Juliane Fitz Maurice; father of Margaret de Clare. [Ancestra l Roots, p. 59]
1 CONT
1 CONT 1276 - Hereditary lordship of all Thomond granted to Thomas de Clare [Oxford Illustrated Hist ory of Ireland, p. 357]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Cantred of Tradraighe were granted to Thomas de Clare by King Edward I, along with 'all t he land he could conquer in Munster'.
1 CONT    It was de Clare who built the first stone castle at Bunratty. He drove out the native inha bitants and moved in his fellow Normans. Naturally he was resented by both the O'Briens and t he MacNamaras, the most powerful clans in 
1 CONC this part of Thomond or North Munster. They launche d regular attacks on the castle and town. [Bunratty Castle, p. 4]
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Richard de Clare and Maud de Lacy; m. Juliana FitzMaurice of Offaly; father of:
1 CONT 1. Thomas
1 CONT 2. Maud who m.1 Robert de Clifford and m.2 Robert de Welles
1 CONT 3. Margaret who m.1 Gilbert de Umfreville; m.2 Bartholomew Badlesmere
1 CONT 4. Gilbert who m. Isabella
1 CONT 5. Richard who m. Joanna
1 CONT [Dave Utz <utz@aol.com]
