0 @N6000000002188648157@ NOTE Aymer de Valence, 2nd Earl of Pembroke
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Aymer de Valence, 2nd Earl of Pembroke
1 CONT c. 1275—23 June 1324
1 CONT
1 CONT Arms of de Valence Earls of Pembroke.
1 CONT Place of birth Unknown
1 CONT Place of death Picardy
1 CONT Place of burial Westminster Abbey
1 CONT Allegiance England
1 CONT Battles/wars First War of Scottish Independence
1 CONT • Battle of Methven (1306)
1 CONT • Battle of Loudoun Hill (1307)
1 CONT • Battle of Bannockburn (1314)
1 CONT • Battle of Old Byland (1322)[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Aymer de Valence, 2nd Earl of Pembroke (c. 1275 – 23 June 1324) was a French-English nobleman. Though primarily active in England, he also had strong connections with the French royal house. One of the wealthiest and most powerful
1 CONC  men of his age, he was a central player in the conflicts between Edward II and his nobility, particularly Earl Thomas of Lancaster. Pembroke was one of the Lords Ordainers appointed to restrict the power of the king and his favou
1 CONC rite Piers Gaveston. His position changed with the great insult he suffered when Gaveston, as a prisoner in his custody whom he had sworn to protect, was removed and beheaded on the instigation of Lancaster. This led Pembroke into
1 CONC  close and lifelong cooperation with the King. Later in life, however, political circumstances combined with financial difficulties would cause him problems, driving him away from the centre of power.
1 CONT
1 CONT Though earlier historians saw Pembroke as the head of a 'middle party', between the extremes of Lancaster and the king, the modern consensus is that he remained essentially loyal to Edward throughout most of his career. Pembroke w
1 CONC as married twice, and left no legitimate issue, though he did have a bastard son. He is today remembered primarily through his wife's foundation of Pembroke College in Cambridge, and for his splendid tomb that can still be seen in
1 CONC  Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Family and Early Years
1 CONT     * 2 The Ordinances and Piers Gaveston
1 CONT     * 3 Later years
1 CONT     * 4 Legacy
1 CONT     * 5 Notes
1 CONT     * 6 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and Early Years
1 CONT
1 CONT Aymer was the son of William de Valence, son of Hugh X, Count of La Marche and Isabella of Angoulême.[2] William was Henry III's half-brother through his mother’s prior marriage to King John, and as such gained a central position 
1 CONC in the Kingdom of England.[3] He had come to the earldom of Pembroke through his marriage to Joan de Munchensi, granddaughter of William Marshal.[2] Aymer was the third son of his family, so little is known of his birth and early 
1 CONC years. He is believed to have been born some time between 1270 and 1275.[4] As his father was on crusade with the Lord Edward until January 1273, a date towards the end of this period is more likely.[5] With the death in battle in
1 CONC  Wales of his remaining brother William in 1282 (John, the elder brother, was dead in 1277), Aymer found himself heir to the Earldom of Pembroke.[5] William de Valence died in 1296, and Aymer inherited his father's French lands, b
1 CONC ut had to wait until his mother died in 1307 to succeed to the Earldom.[6] Through inheritance and marriages his lands consisted of – apart from the county palatine in Pembrokeshire – property spread out across England primarily i
1 CONC n a strip from Gloucestershire to East Anglia, in south-east Ireland (Wexford), and French lands in the Poitou- and Calais areas.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1297 he accompanied Edward I on a campaign to Flanders, and seems to have been knighted by this time.[8] With his French connections he was in the following years a valuable diplomat in France for the English King.[9] He also s
1 CONC erved as military commander in Scotland, and won an important victory over Robert Bruce in 1306 at the Battle of Methven, only to be routed himself by Bruce at Loudoun Hill the next year.[10]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Ordinances and Piers Gaveston
1 CONT
1 CONT King Edward I died in 1307 and was succeeded by his son Edward II. The new King at first enjoyed the good will of his nobility, Valence among them.[11] Conflict soon ensued, however, connected especially with the enormous unpopula
1 CONC rity of Edward's favourite Piers Gaveston.[12] Gaveston's arrogance towards the peers, and his control over Edward, united the Baronage in opposition to the King. In 1311 the initiative known as the Ordinances was introduced, seve
1 CONC rely limiting Royal powers in financial matters and in the appointment of officers.[13] Equally important, Gaveston was expelled from the realm (as Edward I had already done once before). Pembroke, who was not among the most radic
1 CONC al of the Ordainers, and had earlier been sympathetic with the King, had now realised the necessity of exiling Gaveston.[14]
1 CONT
1 CONT When Gaveston without permission returned from exile later the same year, a Baronial council entrusted Pembroke and John de Warenne, earl of Surrey, with the task of taking him into custody.[15] This they did on May 19, 1312, but 
1 CONC not long after Thomas of Lancaster, acting with the Earls of Warwick, Hereford and Arundel, seized Gaveston and executed him on June 19.[16] This criminal act had the effect of garnering support for the King, and marginalising the
1 CONC  rebellious earls.[17] As far as Pembroke is concerned, the seizing and execution of a prisoner in his custody was a breach of the most fundamental chivalric codes, and a serious affront to his honour. The event must therefore be 
1 CONC seen as pivotal in turning his sympathies away from the rebels and towards the King.[18]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Later years
1 CONT Pembroke Castle, Pembrokeshire, Wales.
1 CONT Pembroke Castle, Pembrokeshire, Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the following years Pembroke worked closely with the King. He was appointed the King’s lieutenant in Scotland in 1314, and was present at the disastrous English defeat at the Battle of Bannockburn, where he helped lead Edward a
1 CONC way from the field of battle.[5] In 1317, however, while returning from a papal embassy to Avignon, he was captured by a Jean de Lamouilly, and held for ransom in Germany.[19] The ransom of £ 10,400 was to cause Pembroke significa
1 CONC nt financial difficulties for the remainder of his life.[20]
1 CONT
1 CONT Although ostracized by the murder of Gaveston, Thomas of Lancaster had regained virtual control of Royal government in the period after Bannockburn.[21] Proving himself as incapable to rule as Edward, however, he soon grew unpopul
1 CONC ar.[22] Pembroke was one of the magnates who in the years 1316-18 tried to prevent civil war from breaking out between the supporters of Edward and those of Lancaster, and he helped negotiate the Treaty of Leake in 1318, restoring
1 CONC  Edward to power.[5] Peace did not last long, however, as the King by now had taken on Hugh Despenser the younger as another favourite, in much the same position as Gaveston.[23] Pembroke's attempts at reconciliation eventually fa
1 CONC iled, and civil war broke out in 1321. In 1322 Lancaster was defeated at the Battle of Boroughbridge, and executed. Pembroke was among the Earls behind the conviction.[24]
1 CONT
1 CONT After Boroughbridge Pembroke found himself in a difficult situation. The opponents of Hugh Despenser and his father had lost all faith in him, but at the same time he found himself marginalised at court where the Despensers' power
1 CONC  grew more and more complete.[5] On top of this came his financial problems. On June 24, 1324, while on an embassy to France, he suddenly collapsed and died while lodging somewhere in Picardy.[25]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT T.F. Tout, in 1914 one of the first historians to make a thorough academic study of the period, considered Pembroke the one favourable exception in an age of small-minded and incompetent leaders.[26] Tout wrote of a 'middle party'
1 CONC , led by Pembroke, representing a moderate position between the extremes of Edward and Lancaster. This 'middle party' supposedly took control of royal government through the Treaty of Leake in 1318.[27] In his authoritative study 
1 CONC of 1972, J.R.S. Phillips refutes this view. In spite of misgivings with the King’s favourites, Pembroke was consistently loyal to Edward. What was accomplished in 1318 was not the takeover by a 'middle party', but simply a restora
1 CONC tion of royal power.[28]
1 CONT
1 CONT Aymer married twice; his first marriage, before 1295, was to Beatrice, daughter of Raoul de Clermont, Lord of Nesle in Picardy and Constable of France.[29] Beatrice died in 1320, and in 1321 he married Marie de St Pol, daughter of
1 CONC  Gui de Châtillon, Count of St Pol and Butler of France.[30] He never had any legitimate children, but he had an illegitimate son, Henry de Valence, whose mother is unknown.[5] Pembroke's most lasting legacy is probably through hi
1 CONC s second wife, who in 1347 founded Pembroke College in Cambridge.[31] The family arms are still represented on the dexter side of the college arms.[32] Aymer de Valence was buried in Westminster Abbey, where his tomb can still be 
1 CONC seen as a splendid example of late gothic architecture, elaborating on the design of the near by tomb of Edmund Crouchback.[33]
1 CONT
