0 @N6000000003043431748@ NOTE succeeded to the lands.  He wa sevidently a man of great competence, and although he never seems to have been called "Sir Alexander', his descendents referred to him as 'Lord Montgomerie', apparently a courtesy title.   In 1357 he
1 CONC  had a charter from King David II to assum the lands of Egglesham and Thorntoun.  In 1358 he was one of the barons of Scotland sent to the English court to treat for the release of King David Ii.  Either he or his son Alexander ha
1 CONC d letters of permission to pass through England on his way abroad, accompanied by his body-guard of sixty men on horse and food.  He seems to have been married to a daughter of the Earl of Douglas.   He died in 1388, leaving two s
1 CONC ons, William who succeeded and Alexander.   About Sir William Montgomerie nothing ssems to be known except that he left a son, John.
