0 @N6000000002188001069@ NOTE Uilleam III, Earl of Ross
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Uilleam III of Ross, or William III of Ross, was the fourth successor of Ferchar mac in tSagairt, as Mormaer of Ross (1333/6-1370).
1 CONT
1 CONT Uilleam came into his inheritance at a torrid time, his father Aodh dying at the Battle of Halidon Hill. Uilleam temporarily lost many of his lands. However, he returned from Norway in 1336 and regained them. Uilleam cultivated th
1 CONC e friendship of the Stewarts, and was rewarded by being appointed Justiciar of Scotia. Uilleam's reign was marked by continued strife with his nominal vassal, Ranald MacRuadridh (Lord of Skye). In 1348, Uilleam killed Ranald with 
1 CONC eight of his men at the nunnery of Elcho.
1 CONT
1 CONT Uilleam married Màiri, the daughter of Aonghus Óg MacDomhnaill. They had an heir, Uilleam, but he died in 1357. Uilleam's lands were forfeited by King David II in 1370. Uilleam died in 1372, and the Mormaerdom was given to his dau
1 CONC ghter Euphemia with her husband Sir Walter Leslie, thus ending the dynasty started a century and a half before by the great Ferchar. The dominions eventually passed to the MacDonald Lords of the Isles.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT Roberts, John L., Lost Kingdoms: Celtic Scotland in the Middle Ages, (Edinburgh, 1997) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Genealogy Page 
1 CONT Great Clan Ross (N/B Unfortunately, much info depends on this unreliable site) 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Aodh Mormaer of Ross
1 CONT 1333/6-1370/2 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Euphemia m. Walter Leslie 
0 @N6000000004851188781@ NOTE might have been a Mackay from another branch, conflicting information (M'Ge)
0 @N6000000003299447880@ NOTE His bard's song recounts that his delights were reading the law, playing the crwth and following the greyhounds.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nannau, which is in Llanfechreth parish, stands 700 feet above the level of the sea, and has been for centuries the home of one of the most powerful families in the county. The 'sprouting root', according to the bards, was YNYR HE
1 CONC N, who flourished 1200-50; his son, YNYR FYCHAN, took the credit of helping to capture the rebel MADOG AP LLYWELYN (q.v.) in 1295 and of handing him over to the victors; his son, MEURIG FYCHAN has a sepulchral effigy in Dolgelley 
1 CONC church, with a lion carved on his shield.
1 CONT
1 CONT "In 1293, the largest township in terms of population was Nannau, covering a compact area of some eighteen square miles between the Afon Mawddach to the west and north, and the Afon Wnion to the south, while its eastern limits lay
1 CONC  in the upland margins of Penllyn. The township was all the more remarkable because it was occupied by only one free gwelygordd or clan, rather than several, though the existence of at least 112 families by the close of the centur
1 CONC y suggests that the population increase that had occurred since the gwely foundation was partly the result of migration as well as natural increment, particularly since it is known from family pedigrees that Tyddyn Cefn Llanfair, 
1 CONC situated between Foel Cynwch and Foel Offrwm, was the creation in the early Twelfth century of Cadwgan ap Bleddyn ap Cynfyn, a direct descendant of the ruling house of Powys."
0 @N6000000004533931909@ NOTE knighted c mid 1280's, joined Robert the Bruce 1296 in struggleagainst the English, one of the great Barons of Parliament which metat Ayr 26 April 1315 and fixed the succession to the Crown.  [Burke'sPeerage]
0 @N6000000004571333376@ NOTE Sir Neil, Colin More's son and successor, was knighted in the latter end of the reign of King Alexander III and was in the same Commission with his father in the arbitration over the successor for the crown of Scotland, and so far
1 CONC  acquiesced in the Decision of Edward, in favour of John de Baliol, that he remained faithful to that weak and unhappy Prince, until by an inglorious surrender of the independancy of Scotland, and acknowledging to hold the Soverei
1 CONC gnity in Vassalage of Edward of England, he had forfeited that Title his Blood gave him, and declared himself unworthy of that Crown he was born to.
1 CONT Upon this Emergency, and so soon as Robert de Bruce had formed a rational scheme to make good his prentitions to the throne, this Sir Neil, whom Bushanan calls Sir Neil Campbell, joined heartily with those few patriots who had Cou
1 CONC rage and Honesty to assert the Liberty and Independency of that Ancient Kingdom, against the usurpation of the English Edward, owned King Robert's title, and raised a considerable number of his vassals to make head against McDouga
1 CONC ld of Lorn, who had a personal Pique at and was declared Enemy of Robert. 
1 CONT He soon reduced McDougald and all his Adherents, and brought his Country, and all Shire of Argyle to submit to the new King. From this Action he went to Scoon, and assisted at the Solemnity of King Robert's Coronation, in Anno 130
1 CONC 6; after which he continued stedfast in his Loyalty, and gave many signal Marks of his Attachment to that Monarch, even in the lowest Edd of his Fortune, and when he was yet tottering on his throne, he enterred into that famous As
1 CONC sociation with Sir Gilbert Hay and Sir Alexander Seton; where, in a most solemn manner, they bound themselves to defend, with their lives and fortunes the liberty of their country and the right of Robert Bruce their King against a
1 CONC ll Mortals, French, English or Scotch. To this Association they apended their Seals at the Abbey of Cambuskeneth, Sept 9, 1308. So early this family appearing in the Interest of their Country, against the Usurpation of foreigners,
1 CONC  and the weak Concessions of an hereditary Prince.
1 CONT A spirit which seems hitherto intailed upon the Successors of this great man.
1 CONT This Sir Neil was one of the Commissioners sent from Scotland to England to treat of a  Peace, Anno 1314, which did not then take effect. The ensuing year he was one of the Barons of the Parliament held at Air, which intailed the 
1 CONC Crown upon King Robert, and his Heirs: and in the close of the year he died.
