0 @N6000000001744977339@ NOTE From http://www.castlewales.com/llywel2.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT The years that followed the death of Llywelyn ap Iorwerth saw a major reversal in the fortunes of Gwynedd, culminating in the Treaty of Woodstock,                          concluded with King Henry III in 1247, a year after the de
1 CONC ath of Llywelyn's son Dafydd. Under the terms of the treaty, Gwynedd lost all its lands to the east of the River Conwy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1255, Llywelyn ap Gruffydd defeated his brothers Owain (d.1282) and Dafydd (d.1283), in battle at Bryn Derwin. Owain was imprisoned, and Llywelyn set about reasserting the authority of Gwynedd and thereafter extending it into a
1 CONC  supremacy over much of the rest of Wales. In 1267 his position as overlord was recognized by Henry III in the Treaty of Montgomery when the English king accepted Llywelyn's homage as prince of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ten years after his recognition as the prince of Wales by Henry III, Llywelyn was to suffer a humiliating defeat at the hands of the new King Edward I. From the outset, Llywelyn seemed almost to go out of his way to court Edward's
1 CONC  anger. In particular, he refused to yield the homage and money payments owing to the king under the terms of the Treaty of Montgomery. He tempted fate further by arranging to marry Eleanor, daughter of rebel baron Simon de Montfo
1 CONC rt, and act destined to strain Edward's patience to the limit.
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn began his own castle campaign by strengthening his grandfather's castles at Criccieth, Ewloe, and Dolwyddelan. Moreover, in 1273 he started to build a new castle at Dolforwyn, high above the Severn valley, posing a challe
1 CONC nge to the royal frontier post at Montgomery. The prince's refusal to abandon this project was just one incident in an eventual catalogue of disagreements with the new king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Enough was enough, and in 1276-77 Edward had decided to settle accounts with the recalcitrant Welsh prince. Edward himself took the field at Chester in
1 CONT July 1277, and by August he had some 15,600 troops in his pay. Against these odds, Llywelyn had no choice but to sue for peace. The ensuing Treaty of
1 CONT Aberconwy represented a comprehensive humiliation for the prince of Wales. Stripped of his overlordship he had won ten years earlier, Gwynedd was again
1 CONT reduced to its traditional heartland to the west of the River Conwy.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 21 March 1282, Llywelyn's brother, Dafydd, attacked Hawarden Castle and sparked off the war of 1282-83. Llywelyn was faced with an almost
1 CONT impossible dilemma. Torn between his fealty to the king and his loyalty to to his brother Dafydd and his people, Llywelyn was to side with his brother and led the Welsh resistance to the inevitable invasion by Edward I. By the end
1 CONC  of
1 CONT the year Llywelyn was dead, having been killed on 11 December in a brief engagement with English forces at Irfon Bridge near Builth Wales. A memorial stone was placed near Llywelyn's final battleground.
0 @N6000000003658762406@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT ELEANOR DE MONTFORT, born about 1258.  In 1275 she married by proxy LLYWELYN AP GRUFFYDD, Prince of Wales, lord of Snowdon, 2nd son of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, by his wife, Senena.  At the close of 1275, she set out with her brother,
1 CONC  Amaury, for WAles, but their ship was captured in the Bristol Channel.  Eleanor was subsequently imprisoned at Bristol, and afterwards at Windsor Castle.  She was released in 1278, when Llywelyn submitted to King Edward I.  She m
1 CONC arried Llywelyn at Worcester in a public ceremony 13 Oct. 1278 in King Edward I's presence.  They had one daughter, Gwenllian (nun at Sempringham).  Between 1256 and 1257 Llywelyn reunited North Wales from the Dovey to the Dee, an
1 CONC d annexed extensive territories in the middle marches.  In 1258 he declared himself Prince of Wales.  He was defeated in the war of 1277, which left him only Gwynedd west of Conway.  His wife, Eleanor, died in childbirth 19 June 1
1 CONC 282.  LLYWELYN AP GRUFFYDD, Prince of Wales, was killed 11 Dec. 1282, while campaigning in the Builth region.  His body was interred by the Cistercians of Cwn Hir.
