0 @N6000000002188011653@ NOTE Guy de Beauchamp, 10th Earl of Warwick
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Guy de Beauchamp, 10th Earl of Warwick (1278 – August 10, 1315) was an English nobleman, and one of the principal opponents of Edward II's favourite Piers Gaveston.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Lineage
1 CONT     * 2 Victor in Scotland and marriage
1 CONT     * 3 Opposition to Gaveston
1 CONT     * 4 Death by Poisoning?
1 CONT     * 5 References
1 CONT     * 6 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Lineage
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of William de Beauchamp, 9th Earl of Warwick and Maud FitzGeoffrey, and succeeded his father as Earl in 1298.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Victor in Scotland and marriage
1 CONT
1 CONT Later that year he distinguished himself at the Battle of Falkirk, and he subsequently served in other campaigns in Scotland. He also received grants of land in Scotland and, in 1309, he married Alice de Toeni, a Scots heiress.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Opposition to Gaveston
1 CONT
1 CONT Warwick had no great liking for Piers Gaveston, who had called Warwick "the black cur of Arden" (an allusion to Warwick's dark complexion and to the Forest of Arden in Warwickshire). Not long after Edward II's accession, he helped
1 CONC  arrange for Gaveston's banishment, and refused to be reconciled with Gaveston when Gaveston returned the next year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Warwick was one of the Ordainers put in charge of reform of government in 1310. The peers were successful in getting Gaveston banished again, and when he returned to England in 1312, Warwick was one of the five nobles who arrested
1 CONC  him. Gaveston was placed in the custody of Aymer de Valence, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, but then on June 10 Warwick, with a force of 140 men, seized him and carried him off to Warwick Castle. After the arrival of the confederate noble
1 CONC s, Gaveston was executed, though Warwick declined to be present.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death by Poisoning?
1 CONT
1 CONT Warwick, along with his allies, was eventually pardoned for the Gaveston affair in 1313. He and the others, nevertheless, refused to serve in the King's Scottish campaign of 1314. The next year Warwick suddenly fell ill and died. 
1 CONC As was inevitable in such cases, there were rumours of poison.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Lines: 86-30, 87-30, 98-31.
1 CONT     * Round, J. H. (1885). "Guy de Beauchamp, Earl of Warwick". Dictionary of National Biography 4. 28. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * thePeerage.com on Guy de Beauchamp
1 CONT
1 CONT Peerage of England
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT William de Beauchamp Earl of Warwick
1 CONT 1298–1315 Succeeded by
1 CONT Thomas de Beauchamp
