0 @N6000000000337383500@ NOTE 8th Earl of Hereford and 9th Earl of Essex
1 CONT
1 CONT Constable of England. See Complete Peerage V6.p469 & Vol.V,p135.
1 CONT Killed trying to force Boroughbridge
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  T. Anna Leese, "Blood Royal:  Issue of the Kings and Queens of Medieval England, 1066-1399:  The Normans and Plantagenets" (Heritage Books, Inc, 1996)
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #3:  Weis, p. 93
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #4:  Douglas Richardson, "Plantagenet Ancestry:  A Study in Colonial and Medieval Families" (Baltimore, Maryland:  Genealogical Publishing Company, Inc., 2004), pp.118-122, 201-203
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #5:  Ian Mortimer, "The Greatest Traitor:  The Life of Sir Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March, Ruler of England, 1327 - 1330" (London:  Jonathan Cape, 2003), pp. 123 - 124.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Hereford and the others persuaded [Lancaster] to make for Boroughbridge, a crossing over the River Ure in Yorkshire, about twenty miles north-east of York.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was a fatal mistake.  Both armies were riddled with spies, and a man in Lancaster's ranks informed Sir Andrew de Harclay, Sheriff of Carlisle, about the plan to retreat by way of Boroughbridge.  De Harley roused the men of the 
1 CONC counties of Cumberland and Westmoreland, and, by forcing them to march through the night, reached the bridge first.  As Hereford and Lancaster approached on 16 March 1322, they realized that they would have to fight their way acro
1 CONC ss.  With the king's army to the south, they were trapped.
1 CONT
1 CONT The earls surveyed the battlefield.  Besides the bridge there was also a ford, where de Harclay had stationed a number of pikemen.  They decided that the Earl of Hereford and Lord Clifford would attack the bridge, while Lancaster 
1 CONC would attack the ford with his cavalry.  It must have seemed a relatively straightforward task, but they underestimated the redoubtable de Harclay.  From lomg experience fighting the Scots, he knew well how to defend a strategic p
1 CONC oint, and the rebels were acting in haste, and consequently did not prepare their attack on the bridge with sufficient care.  De Harclay's men were drawn up in Scottish schiltrom formations, preventing the knights from charging.  
1 CONC Hereford led the fight on the bridge, but he and his men were caught in the arrow fire.  Then one of de Harclay's pikemen, concealed beneath the bridge, thrust upwards between the planks and skewered the Earl of Hereford through t
1 CONC he anus, twisting the iron pike into his intestines.  His dying screams turned the advance into panic.  Lancaster, amazed, and finding no easy way to cross the river with the arrows coming at him thick and fast, called off the att
1 CONC ack, and withdrew from the river, promising to return the next morning, either to do battle or surrender.
1 CONT
1 CONT That night, the men of the dead Earl of Hereford deserted, many of them leaving their armour in their tents and creeping away in borrowed or stolen old clothes, pretending to be peasants or beggers.
1 CONT
1 CONT At first light de Harclay, whose scouts had kept an eye on the desertions throughout the night, seized the initiative.  He crossed the bridge and moved towards Lord Lancaster.  In a short while the Battle of Boroughsbridge was all
1 CONC  over.  Most of the leading lords who had opposed the king [Edward II]  including the Earl of Lancaster, were led to gaol in York."  [Lancaster soon beheaded]
