0 @N6000000003098108903@ NOTE Hollie's 20th Great GrandParents and ancestors of President Abraham LINCOLN through their daughter Maud.
0 @N6000000001745022851@ NOTE Queen Isabella (wife of Edward II) and her lover Roger Mortimer imprisoned King Edward II in Berkeley Castle. He was murdered there in 1327 and his young son was declared king in his place (though Mortimer actually ruled for a num
1 CONC ber of years).  Edward was 14 when he took the throne. For the first 3 years of his reign, England was ruled by his father's murderer (his mother's lover), Mortimer. But Edward had Mortimer executed in 1330 and took control.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Nicholas Best, "The Kings and Queens of England" (Boston: Bullfinch, 1995).
1 CONT
1 CONT Ironically, Edward II's granddaughter Philippa of Ulster would marry Roger Mortimer's great-grandson Edmund (Earl of March III). They were the parents of Elizabeth Mortimer who married Sir Henry Percy.
1 CONT
1 CONT  http://www.castlewales.com/mortimer.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger Mortimer  (1287-1330)
1 CONT
1 CONT David Lloyd from the Ludlow Castle guidebook
1 CONT
1 CONT The Mortimers were descended from Roger de Mortemer of Mortemer-sur-Eaulane in Normandy, a supporter of William the Conqueror. Their
1 CONT main castle was at Wigmore, eight miles west of Ludlow. They had lands mainly in Herefordshire and Shropshire, including Cleobury (Mortimer) on the edge of the hunting forest of Wyre. By marriage to Joanna de Geneville, a later Ro
1 CONC ger Mortimer (1287-1330) secured possession of Ludlow Castle. This became the family's principal power base for the next six generations.
1 CONT
1 CONT Right: Roger Mortimer & Queen Isabella
1 CONT (foreground)
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger Mortimer was a very powerful and ambitious Marcher Lord. He was the first of several members of his family to attempt to seize the throne of England.
1 CONT He fought the Scottish Wars and made attempts to remove the King's favorites, at first with some success. In 1323 he was imprisoned in the Tower of
1 CONT London, but escaped to France, an event he later commemorated by building St Peter's chapel in the outer bailey of Ludlow Castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT In France, Mortimer formed an alliance with Queen Isabella, who had deserted her effeminate husband, King Edward II of England. They raised an army,
1 CONT invaded England and forced Edward to abdicate in favor of his youngest son, the future Edward III. Mortimer entertained Isabella at his castles on the Welsh borders and they became famous lovers. Meanwhile, Edward II was
1 CONT cruelly murdered at Berkeley Castle in 1327.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following Edward's death, Mortimer, acting as regent, was the virtual ruler of England, but he over-reached himself and aroused the anger of other barons. In October 1330 he was arrested at Nottingham and sentenced to death. He wa
1 CONC s executed at Tyburn in London.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later, the ambitions of the Mortimers became part of the great dynastic struggles of the mid-15th century which became known as the "War of the Roses." English Magnate.  He led the baronial opposition to Edward II's favourites (13
1 CONC 20-22) and was imprisoned before fleeing to France.  There he became the lover of Edward's Queen Isabella with whom he secured Edward's deposition and murder in 1327.  He then ruled England in the name of Edward's son Edward III, 
1 CONC until the latter caused him to be executed.  Lord of Leix.
0 @N6000000002248207377@ NOTE 1282: Roger was succeeded by Edmund who later died in 1304. Edmund was followed by another Roger who was just 17 years old. In 1301 Roger had married Joan de Grenville, who was heiress of the Laceys. This brought extensive estates
1 CONC  in Ireland and Shropshire into the Mortimer family.
1 CONT 1316: Roger de Mortimer was forced to return to Wigmore to secure the March and his Welsh possessions against Llewellyn Bren. Roger was also appointed Lieutnenant of Ireland in this year. In 1318 – 1321 Roger was also made the roy
1 CONC al justiciar.
1 CONT 1321: Roger becomes very influential over Edward II’s wife Isabella and in this year led an armed rebellion against the King. In the summer of this year he joined all the other Marcher Lords in the ravaging of the lands of Hugh De
1 CONC spencer, a favourite of King Edward II. This infighting soon turned into open war and resulted in a defeat of the rebels at the Battle of Boroughbridge. Roger de Mortimer submitted to the king rather than run the risk of facing hi
1 CONC m in battle. He spent 2 years as a prisoner at the Tower of London
1 CONT 1322: Following Roger’s rebellion a record was made of all the weapons at Wigmore Castle, they included: 3 Springholds, Crossbows of horn and wood, helmets for jousting and real war, suits of armour and chain mail as well as six t
1 CONC ents and pavilions. Also detailed were a large chessboard and a table for playing draughts. 
1 CONT 1327: Edward II was deposed and murdered by Roger and Isabella at Berkley Castle. Roger and Isabella ruled through the young Edward III. Within a year Roger had been made Earl of March and openly continued his relationship with th
1 CONC e Queen.
1 CONT 1330: Once Edward III had come of age he arranged for Roger to be arrested at Nottingham and in the same year Roger was hanged at Tyburn. Wigmore Castle was granted to the Earl of Salisbury. Edward III was lenient in his treatment
1 CONC  of his mother Isabella and had her pensioned off to live in comfortable retirement. Nor did Edward lay blame at Roger's sons for the sins of their father. Edmund died soon after the hanging of his father, leaving his brother Roge
1 CONC r - a young boy - as heir, to whom Edward III later reinstated the earldom of the March when he came of age.________________________________________________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT Baron Roger De Mortimer <d75095.htm> was born on 25 Apr 1287 in Netherwood, Thornbury, Herefordshire, England. (203) <fowsrc.htm> Some sources states that he was born on May 3, 1287. He was christened in 1330 in Netherwood, Thornb
1 CONC ury, Herefordshire, England. He was buried in 1330 in Church of Grey Friar, Shrewsbury, Shropshire, England. He died on 29 Nov 1330 in Elms, Tyburn, Warwickshire, England. Roger Mortimer, 2nd Baron of Mortimer, was summoned to Par
1 CONC liament 1306-1326. This nobleman, notorious in our histories as the paramour of Isabel, Queen Consort of Edward II, was in his sixteenth year at the death of his father. He married Jaone, daughter of Peter de Genville, Lord of Tri
1 CONC m, in Ireland. In 34th of Edward I, about 1306, he received the honour of Knighthood. He aided in the Scottish wars, and in 3rd of Edward II, 1310, he was made Governor of the Castle of Buelt, and later was Lord Lieutenant of Irel
1 CONC and. During the latter part of Edward II's reign he attached himself to the Queen, and at length fled with her and Prince Edward to France. He later returned and was made Earl of March soon after the accession of Edward III. He he
1 CONC reupon became proud beyond measure (so that his son Geoffrey called him the King of Folly) and assumed royal authority. His career was not however of long continuance, for King Edward III, becoming sensible of his folly and vices,
1 CONC  had him seized in the Castle of Queen Isabel in Nottingham and was convicted under various charges, the first was complicity in the murder of Edward II, and receiving sentence of death was hanged in 1330. He left by Joan de Genev
1 CONC ille 4 sons and 7 daughters. 
1 CONT
1 CONT By marriage to Joanna de Geneville, a later Roger Mortimer (1287-1330) secured possession of Ludlow Castle. This became the family's principal power base for the next six generations. 
1 CONT Roger Mortimer was a very powerful and ambitious Marcher Lord. He was the first of several members of his family to attempt to seize the throne of England. He fought the Scottish Wars and made attempts to remove the King's favorit
1 CONC es, at first with some success. In 1323 he was imprisoned in the Tower of London, but escaped to France, an event he later commemorated by building St Peter's chapel in the outer bailey of Ludlow Castle. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In France, Mortimer formed an alliance with Queen Isabella, who had deserted her effeminate husband, King Edward II of England. They raised an army, invaded England and forced Edward to abdicate in favor of his youngest son, the f
1 CONC uture Edward III. Mortimer entertained Isabella at his castles on the Welsh borders and they became famous lovers. Meanwhile, Edward II was cruelly murdered at Berkeley Castle in 1327. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Following Edward's death, Mortimer, acting as regent, was the virtual ruler of England, but he over-reached himself and aroused the anger of other barons. In October 1330 he was arrested at Nottingham and sentenced to death. He wa
1 CONC s executed at Tyburn in London. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Later, the ambitions of the Mortimers became part of the great dynastic struggles of the mid-15th century which became known as the "War of the Roses." 
1 CONT
1 CONT From Encyclopedia Britannica Online, article titled: "March, Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of, 8th Baron Of Wigmore" 
1 CONT
1 CONT "lover of the English King Edward II's queen, Isabella of France, with whom he contrived Edward's deposition and murder (1327). For three years thereafter he was virtual king of England during the minority of Edward III. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "The descendant of Norman knights who had accompanied William the Conqueror, he inherited wealthy family estates and fortunes, principally in Wales and Ireland, and in 1304 became 8th Baron of Wigmore on the death of his father, t
1 CONC he 7th baron. He devoted the early years of his majority to obtaining effective control of his Irish lordships against his wife's kinsmen, the Lacys, who summoned to their aid Edward Bruce, brother of King Robert I of Scotland, wh
1 CONC en he was fighting to become king of Ireland. In 1316 Mortimer was defeated at Kells and withdrew to England, but afterward, as King Edward II's lieutenant in Ireland (November 1316), he was largely instrumental in overcoming Bruc
1 CONC e and in driving the Lacys from Meath. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "In 1317 he was associated with the Earl of Pembroke's "middle party" in English politics; but distrust of the Despensers (see Despenser, Hugh Le and Hugh Le) drove him, in common with other marcher lords, into opposition and viol
1 CONC ent conflict with the Despensers in South Wales in 1321. But, receiving no help from Edward II's other enemies, Roger and his uncle Roger Mortimer of Chirk made their submission in January 1322. Imprisoned in the Tower of London, 
1 CONC Roger escaped in 1323 and fled to France, where in 1325 he was joined by Queen Isabella, who became his mistress. The exiles invaded England in September 1326; the fall of the Despensers was followed by the deposition of Edward II
1 CONC  and his subsequent murder (1327), in which Mortimer was deeply implicated. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "Thereafter, as the queen's paramour, Mortimer virtually ruled England. He used his position to further his own ends. Created Earl of March in October 1328, he secured for himself the lordships of Denbigh, Oswestry, and Clun, form
1 CONC erly belonging to the Earl of Arundel; the marcher lordships of the Mortimers of Chirk; and Montgomery, granted to him by the queen. His insatiable avarice, his arrogance, and his unpopular policy toward Scotland aroused against M
1 CONC ortimer a general revulsion among his fellow barons, and in October 1330 the young king Edward III, at the instigation of Henry of Lancaster, had him seized at Nottingham and conveyed to the Tower. Condemned for crimes declared to
1 CONC  be notorious by his peers in Parliament, he was hanged at Tyburn as a traitor, and his estates were forfeited to the crown." 
1 CONT He was married to Countess Joan de Geneville (daughter of Lord Piers de Geneville and Joan "Le Brun" De Lusignan) before 6 Oct 1306.(204) <fowsrc.htm> Countess Joan de Geneville was born on 2 Feb 1285/86 in Ludlow, Shropshire, Eng
1 CONC land. She died on 19 Oct 1356. (205) <fowsrc.htm> She was the Countess of March. Baron Roger De Mortimer and Countess Joan de Geneville had the following children: 
1 CONT
1 CONT  +1028 i. Edmund de Mortimer. <d 1 Jan 1331-1332
1 CONT  1029 ii. Beatrice de Mortimer died on 16 Oct 1383. 
1 CONT  1030 iii. Agnes de Mortimer died on 25 Jul 1368. 
1 CONT  1031 iv. Roger de Mortimer. 
1 CONT  1032 v. Geoffrey de Mortimer. Geoffrey was arrested with his father in 1330. 
1 CONT  1033 vi. John de Mortimer. On August 27, 1328 John was granted castles and lans in Ireland by his parents. He was killed in a tournament at Shrewsbury. 
1 CONT  1034 vii. Joan de Mortimer. 
1 CONT  1035 viii. Katherine de Mortimer died between 4 Aug 6 and Sep . 
1 CONT  +1036 ix. Margaret de Mortimer. <d 5 May 1337 
1 CONT  1037 x. Maud de Mortimer. 
1 CONT  +1038 xi. Blanche de Mortimer. <d 1347
