0 @N6000000004969812695@ NOTE Sir Thomas Gerard of Bryn, had an eventful life. He was a minor in 1380 when his father died. He was fined for marrying when he was a minor in 1383 when he should have been the King's ward. His wife’s maiden name is unknown but he
1 CONC r first name was Matilda. He received a royal pardon (for marrying too young) and was knighted for his efforts in the Scottish wars in 1393 and became the knight of the shire of Lancashire.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was appointed sheriff in November 1399 when the deposition of Richard 11 and the accession to the throne of Henry of Bolingbroke brought him to the forefront in the period of political unrest which marked the Lancasterian reign
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT He was commissioned on January 28, 1401 in Lancashire to “enquire into the seizure of goods from the house of Friars Minor of Lanvas, in Wales, and to restore them on the supplication of the ministers and friars of the Order of En
1 CONC gland”.
1 CONT
1 CONT At his death, he (Sir Knight Thomas Gerard) was in possession of:
1 CONT
1 CONT A moity (one half) of the Manor of Kirkby
1 CONT the Manor of Melling held of the King as Duke of Lancaster;
1 CONT the Manor of Ashton held by Henry de Langton, Baron of Newton;
1 CONT the Manors of Brindle and Anderton;
1 CONT the advowson (the right in English ecclesiastical law of presentation to a vacant
1 CONT benefice) of the Church of Brindle;
1 CONT the Manor of Skelmersdale held of Matilda Lovel, Lady Holland;
1 CONT a moity of the Manor of Rainhill held of the heir of Henry de Eccleston;
1 CONT two messuages (houses) and 40 acres of land in Eccleston;
1 CONT messuages and land in Sutton, Goldburne, and Newton, Grimsargh, Rainford.
1 CONT
1 CONT From modern day maps, I judge that all of the above properties were within a 70 mile radius of Kingsley.
1 CONT Sir Knight Thomas Gerard died on March 27, 1416.
0 @N6000000004969858839@ NOTE Knighted in 1393
1 CONT OCCUPATION: Lord of the manors of Kingsley and Bryn, 1416-1431; knight of the shire 1384, 1388, 1394.ID: I3435
1 CONT Sir Thomas Gerard of Bryn, son and heir of Sir Peter Gerard and grandson of William Gerard of Kingsley near Frodsham in Cheshire and of Bryn in the Manor of Ashton in Makerfield, was a minor in 1380 when his father died. He was fi
1 CONC ned for marrying in his minority in 1383 when he should have been the King's ward. His wife Matilda was named in the fine. He received a royal pardon and was knighted for his efforts in the Scotch wars in 1393 and became the knigh
1 CONC t of the shire of Lancashire. 
1 CONT Sir Thomas remained in relative obscurity from the official point of view until appointed sheriff in November 1399 when the deposition of Richard 11 and the accession to the throne of Henry of Bolingbroke brought him to the forefr
1 CONC ont in the period of political unrest which marked the Lancasterian reign. 
1 CONT He was commissioned 28 January 1401 in Lancashire to enquire into the seizure of goods from the house of Friars Minor of Lanvas, in Wales, and to restore them on the supplication of the ministers and friars of the Order of England
1 CONC . Died 27 March 1416. 
1 CONT At his death, Thomas was in possession of a moity (one half) of the Manor of Kirkby and the Manor of Melling held of the King as Duke of Lancaster; the Manor of Ashton held by Henry de Langton, Baron of Newton; the Manors of Brind
1 CONC le and Anderton; with the advowson of the Church of Brindle; the Manor of Skelmersdale held of Matilda Lovel, Lady Holland; a moity of the Manor of Rainhill held of the heir of Henry de Eccleston; two messuages (houses) and 40 acr
1 CONC es of land in Eccleston; messuages and land in Sutton, Goldburne, and Newton, Grimsargh, Rainford. (See map.) 
1 CONT On the 22 April, it was estimated his lands produced some 150 pounds income per year. With the exception of the Cheshire Manor of Kingsley, most of his lands were exclusively in the West Derby Hundred in Lancaster Co. The territor
1 CONC ial link with the Botiliers (Butler) of Warrington was strengthened in 1402 by the marriage of his son Sir John with Alice, daughter of Sir John Boteler (also Botiller).
0 @N6000000004533931120@ NOTE Sir Alexander Fraser of Touchfraser and Cowie; Chamberlain ofScotland 1319, Sheriff of Kincardine and Stirling, granted extensivelands for loyalty to Robert I (The Bruce); marred 1316 Lady Mary Bruce(died c1323), sister of Robert 
1 CONC I and widow of Sir Neil Campbell ofLochow, and was killed at defeat of Scots by an invading host ofEnglish Lords at Dupplin 12 Aug 1332.  [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is from "Scotland and her Tartans" by Alexander Fulton.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first known Fraser in Scotland was Simon Fraser, who in about 1160donated the Church of Keith to Kelso Abbey. The name came from thelordship of LaFraseliere in Anjou, and a descendant of Simon Fraser,Sir Gilbert Fraser, establ
1 CONC ished the main line of the family in about1250 at Touch-Fraser, Stirlingshire.  His direct descendant, AlexanderFraser was knighted by King Robert I (the Bruce) before the battle ofBannockburn in 1314.  After the battle he married
1 CONC  the Bruce's sister,Lady Mary - who had been strung up in a cage for four years by KingEdward I of England in reprisal for the Bruce's coronation - and hewas later Chamberlain of Scotland.  Their grandson gained the lands ofPhilor
1 CONC th in Buchan by his marriage in 1375.
